\mathbb{\tilde{F}}_{X_{i}}[\cdot]\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ is tight. Then there
exists a subsequence $\{X_{i_{j}}\}_{j=1}^{\infty}$ which converges in distribution. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.412261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ea8022c903796f236888620071eef4e0e125ea43adceecbceb7d7dbd26c295e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Closed blocks of arXiv:2601.07808, 'Supercritical long-range percolation on graphs of polynomial growth: the truncated one-arm'] \label[lemma]{lem:closed_blocks}
    Let $b \in \mathbf{N}$ and let $A_0,\ldots,A_{b-1}$ be a $1$-disconnected sequence of $1$-connected sets.

    Then there exists $\ell \leq b$ and $(B_0,\ldots,B_{\ell-1}) \in \mathcal{A}(\ell)$ such that $\cup_{i \leq b} A_i \subset \cup_{j \leq \ell} B_j$ and $\cup_{j \leq \ell} \partial B_j \subset \cup_{i \leq b} \partial A_i$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.902047+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ea9f92e4e8ac971ff2b0ca28d5c304e50cdcdd6779cd09929665295b7ad73a2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm wellposed of arXiv:2412.05698, 'Controlled rough SDEs, pathwise stochastic control and dynamic programming principles'] {FHL21}}] 
Let $\mathbf{X} \in \mathscr{C}^\alpha, \alpha \in (1/3,1/2]$ and $p \in [2,\infty)$.
    Assume\footnote{For simplicity, take \(\beta=\alpha\) at first reading.}  that $\beta \in [0,\alpha]$ and $\gamma \in \left(\frac{1}{\alpha},3 \right]$ such that $\alpha + (\gamma-1)\beta > 1 $. Let $b$ and $\sigma$ be random bounded Lipschitz vector fields.
    Let $(f, f')$ be a stochastic controlled vector field in \(\mathbf{D}_X^{2\beta} L_{p,\infty} {\mathcal{C}}_b^\gamma\) such that \((D_yf,D_yf')\) belongs to \(\mathbf{D}_X^{2\beta} L_{p,\infty} {\mathcal{C}}_b^{\gamma-1}\).
    Then, for any $\xi \in L^p(\Omega,{\mathfrak F}_0;\mathbb{R}^d)$, there exists a unique \(L_{p,\infty}\)- integrable solution $Y$ to (equation eq:dRDE of the paper).



    Moreover, 
    \begin{align}
      \|(Y,Y')\|_{\mathbf{D}_X^{\alpha,\beta}L_{p,\infty}} \lesssim_{T,p,\alpha,\beta,\gamma} \left(1+\|b\|_\infty + \|\sigma\|_\infty + M \nn{\mathbf{X}}_\alpha \right)^{\frac{1}{\beta}},
    \end{align}
    where \(M>0\) is any constant such that \(\| (f, f') \|_{\gamma-1;\infty} + \llbracket(f, f') \rrbracket_{X;2\beta; p,\infty} \leq M\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.290614+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6eaa7a5617db4701bf17ff1da79806272b52f6e862c2bcca4a2dd9bf55fb8320","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2608.30166, 'Square Functions and Rectifiability\\ under Monotone Transformations of the Density'] Let $\mu$ be an $n$-AD-regular measure in $\mathbb{R}^d$ with constant $c_0$ and
$\mu(\mathbb{R}^d)=\infty$. Let $F\colon(0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ be a Borel function and
bi-Lipschitz on $J_0$ with constant $\lambda$. Then $\mu$ is uniformly $n$-rectifiable if and only if
there exists a constant $c$ such that, for any ball $B(x_0,R)$ centered at $\operatorname{supp}(\mu)$,
\begin{equation}
\int_0^R\!\!\int_{x\in B(x_0,R)} |\Delta^F_\mu(x,r)|^2\, d\mu(x)\,\frac{dr}{r}
\;\le\; c\,R^n.
\end{equation}
Quantitatively: if (equation eq:carleson of the paper) holds with constant $c'$ then
(equation eq:carlesonF of the paper) holds with constant $\lambda^2 c'$, and if (equation eq:carlesonF of the paper)
holds with constant $c$ then (equation eq:carleson of the paper) holds with constant $\lambda^2 c$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.240430+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6eae30a8932a68853fbbb7319f39869ac94c1faa43aa19a4ae821867cf14c52f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition balancing_l1 of arXiv:2506.20932, 'Thinning to improve two-sample discrepancy'] Consider any vectors $v_1,\ldots,v_m \in \mathbb{R}^{N}$. There is an online algorithm 
to choose \(\varepsilon_1, \ldots, \varepsilon_m = \pm 1\) so that the running sums satisfy
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E \sup_k \left\| \sum_{i = 1}^{k} \varepsilon_i v_i \right\|_{\infty} 
\leq 2 \sup_i \|v_i\|_1 \left[ 
3 + \log_2 \left(
1 + \frac{\sum_i \|v_i\|_1}{2 \sup_i \|v_i\|_1}
\right)
\right]
= O\bigl(\sup_i \|v_i\|_1 \log m\bigr).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:04:06.978912+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6eb136558931065bf574c3b01185fd978ad3da125e6bc1d9524c5780b0d71bfa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:MD-trick of arXiv:2607.26169, 'Scaling and partial universality of the height zero probability in the 2D Abelian sandpile'] \ \\
(i) We have 
\eqn{e:MD-trick}
{ p_\Lambda(0; o)
  = \det ( I + G_\Lambda B ), }
where $B$ is the matrix defined by $\Delta_{\mathcal{G}^{(g)}_\Lambda} = \Delta_\Lambda + B$. \\
(ii) The above can also be written in the form
