{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.665956+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ed38fdba98ede25574e0db4d31f75b73250cebb98fa37339de433401ea151bd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop finite-graph-window-tools of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] The following estimates hold.

\emph{(i) Greedy bounds.} For every fixed $p\geqslant1$, there is $C_p<\infty$ such
that, for all $\ell\geqslant2$ and all integers $r\geqslant0$,
\begin{align}
\left\|
 \mathfrak{a}_\ell
 T_{[0,r+\ell]}(0,[r,r+\ell])
\right\|_p
&\leqslant
C_p\left(\frac r\ell+1\right),
\end{align}
and, for all integers $r\geqslant\ell$,
\begin{align}
\left\|
 \mathfrak{a}_\ell
 T_{[0,r]}(0,r)
\right\|_p
&\leqslant
C_p\left(\frac r\ell+\log\ell+1\right).
\end{align}

\emph{(ii) One-sided short-window mean.} With $h_\ell$ as in
(equation eq:h-ell-def of the paper),
\begin{align}
 \mathfrak{a}_\ell
 \E \Big( T_{[0,\lfloor h_\ell\ell\rfloor+\ell]}
 \left(0,[\lfloor h_\ell\ell\rfloor,\lfloor h_\ell\ell\rfloor+\ell]\right) \Big)
 \leqslant \frac{h_\ell}{\mathsf{v}^\star}+o(h_\ell).
\end{align}

\emph{(iii) Freshness after a stopped window crossing.} Fix $x,H\in\mathds{Z}$ with $H\geqslant1$ and set
\begin{align*}
 I_x:=[x+H,x+H+\ell]\cap\mathds{Z} .
\end{align*}
Let $\pi^\star=\pi^\star(x,I_x)$ be the shortest path from $x$ to $I_x$ strictly inside $[x, x+H+\ell]$, stopped upon its first entrance at a terminal point $Y$. Then, conditionally on $\pi^\star$, its passage time, and $Y=y$,
\begin{align*}
 \bigl\{\go_{\{u,v\}}:\ 1\leqslant |u-v|\leqslant \ell,\ u\geqslant y,\ v\geqslant y\bigr\}
\end{align*}
are fresh, \emph{i.e.,}~conditionally independent and identically distributed as $\mathrm{Exp}(1)^{1/\theta}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.290113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ed499879c8521d1c4fefe5e9de0a905a24e70119bc0c27582b415afe8568979","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 27 of arXiv:2604.25725, 'What is The Probability That A Random Graph With A Given Degree Sequence is Connected?'] Let $H$ be a graph on $[n]$  such that $H$ is a possible subgraph on a graph with degree sequence $\cD$, and   $uv\not\in  E(H)$. Then,
