{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:58:09.903179+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_708998b13b4d354cc4fa7a9f2c33a1cf91d645e64c529e883ed6ab96aaf5c5fb","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def CPS of arXiv:2607.19511, 'A Conditional Probability Hierarchy for Stochastic Choice\footnote{We thank Samson Abramsk'] A \textbf{conditional probability space} (\textbf{CPS}) (relative to $\cal G$) is a map $p : {\cal G} \rightarrow \Delta(\Omega)$, which we will also write as $G \mapsto p_G(\cdot)$ for $G \in {\cal G}$, satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $p_G(G) = 1$ for every $G \in \cal G$;
\item $p_G(E) = p_G(F) \times p_F(E)$ for every $E \subseteq F \subseteq G$ with $E \in \cal F$ and $F, G \in \cal G$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.931965+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_708b2971d19e792b7bf170096be1dccb453a6eb86f90c62cc6db74f506d0adb3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 24 (The black-golden coupling) of arXiv:2506.19123, 'The longest increasing subsequence of Brownian separable permutons'] We say that the decreasing chains $\DMC$ and $\DMC'$ have the \textbf{black-golden coupling} when the dynamics of $\DMC$ (and $\DMC'$) are described for all $t\geq 0$ as follows:\footnote{We stress that here we are using a \emph{unique} set of black and golden instructions for \emph{both} chains $\DMC$ and $\DMC'$.}
    \begin{equation}
    	\DMC_{t+1} = \left\lbrace \begin{aligned}
    	& G_{\DMC_t} \qquad \text{if } \DMC_t \in \intervalleentier{5\ell}{7\ell} \text{ for some good scale $\ell$  and } B_{\DMC_t}  \in \intervalleentier{\ell}{4\ell}; \\
    	&B_{\DMC_t} \qquad \text{otherwise.}
    \end{aligned}
	\right.
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, when two chains $\DMC$ and $\DMC'$ are started from $k$ and $k'$ respectively, we denote their joint law under this coupling by $\mathbb{P}_{k,k'}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:48:30.932188+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_708e6a8e5fd44dd27cf8f0fab7ca6e917307c3fdbf6032167d69bd8d0e9bf5d1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2609.01020, '2609.01020'] Assume that $f=(f_1,\ldots , f_d)\colon X\longrightarrow \CC_s^d$ 
