{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.300750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70cd0646786e7a6fd7f71aff7302b503255d8a28a70f3d646154a073fdc9a50f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:exactcov of arXiv:2411.08848, 'Stationary random measures: Covariance asymptotics, variance bounds and central limit theo'] Let ${\mathcal X}$ be a stationary random measure on $\mathbb{R}^d$ and let $f,g:\mathbb{R}^d\to \mathbb{R}$. Assume that
\begin{enumerate}
\item  $\int |z|^m |{\bf K}|(\mathrm{d} z) < \infty$ for all  $m\ge 0$.
\item $g$ is real analytic. Further, $f,g$ and   partial derivatives of $g$ of all orders are in $L^1\cap L^2$.
\item $\sum\limits_{\gamma\in \mathbb{Z}_+^d}\frac{1}{\gamma!}\|\partial^{\gamma}g\|_2\int |z^{\gamma}| \ |{\bf K}| (\mathrm{d} z) < \infty$.
\end{enumerate}
Let $ I(\gamma):=\int z^{\gamma} {\bf K}(\mathrm{d} z)$ for $\gamma\in \mathbb{Z}_+^d$.   Then

\begin{equation}

 \mathrm{Cov}({\mathcal X}(f),{\mathcal X}(g))  = \sum_{p\ge 0}Q_{2p}^{{\bf K}}(f,g),
 \end{equation}
 where 
 \begin{align} 
 Q_m^{{\bf K}}(f,g)=\begin{cases}
 (\lambda_D + I(0))\ \langle f,g\rangle  &\mbox{ if }m=0, \\
 \sum\limits_{\gamma \in \mathbb{Z}^d_+:\ |\gamma|=m}\frac{1}{\gamma!}I(\gamma)\langle f,  \partial^{\gamma}g \rangle &\mbox{ if }m\ge 1.
 \end{cases}
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.893088+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70d5c0abcf3811162564762198e8c5c673edd097e416023c290579a97586fc71","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma thm13.12 of arXiv:2411.11393, '\fontsize{16}{19} \selectfont On differentiability of reward functionals corresponding to '] Let $\mathfrak A$ denote the characteristic operator of $X$. Moreover, let $J\subset I$ be an open interval with $\mathbb{E}_x[\tau^J]<\infty$ for all $x\in J$, $\psi:J \to [0,\infty)$ bounded and measurable and $h:J\to \mathbb{R}$ bounded and measurable. Assume that $\mathbb{E}_{\mathpalette\mathpalette\mathpalette\bigcdot@{.6}@{.6}@{.6}}[\psi(X_{t\wedge \tau^J})]\vert_J\to \psi$ weakly for $t\searrow 0$ and $\mathbb{E}_{\mathpalette\mathpalette\mathpalette\bigcdot@{.6}@{.6}@{.6}}[h(X_{t\wedge \tau^J})e^{-\int_0^{t\wedge\tau} \psi(X_s)ds }]\vert_J\to h$ weakly for $t\searrow 0$ respectively. We extend the functions $\psi, h$ to $I$ by 0 outside of $J$, set $\tau:=\tau^J$ and
    \begin{align*}
        F(x):= \mathbb{E}_x\left[\int_0^\tau e^{-\int_0^s \psi(X_s) ds} h(X_s) dt \right]
    \end{align*} for all $x\in I$. Then $F$ is continuous with
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak A F(x) - \psi(x) F(x) = - h (x)
    \end{align*} and in particular $F\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathfrak A}(y)$ for all $x\in J$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:27.904070+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70d789f24182c0edc1fa993b034e7f6a7645e07f432a644fd3a1d23f0635d1cb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 (Theorem~4.1 of \cite{FK06}) of arXiv:2607.12666, 'Sample-path Large deviations for \\Scheduled Arrival Processes with Unpunctuality'] Let $\{X^{(n)}\}$ be a sequence of processes in $D([0,T])$. Suppose that
