    \sup_{(x,y)\in [0,1]^2}|w(x,y)|<1.
\end{align*}
Then, the free Fisher information of $\mu_{w}$ is finite. In particular, $\mu_{w}$ is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure, and its density is in $L^3(\mathbb{R})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.350569+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72bc0b98f8fc23c9410b88f627ed94cb762750d36c50a0a02ac0f1378182a905","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:entropy of arXiv:2412.06420, 'Accuracy, Estimates, and Representation Results'] Define the \emph{entropy}\index{entropy} of $\mathsf{acc}$ as:
