{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.296779+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72c07209f4b399c487471c7587e490ce0a138d819417467ed47449835c50cbc8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:infconv of arXiv:2605.20587, 'Persistence and entropic repulsion of stationary Gaussian fields with spectral singularity'] Let $\mu$ be a spectral measure in $\mathbb{R}^d$ and let $\delta > 0$.  
 \begin{enumerate}[\rm (1)]

 \item There exists $T_0=T_0(\mu,\delta)>0$ such that, for all $T>T_0$,
 \[ \inf_{\mu' \le \mu} \mathbb{E} \inf_{B(T)} f_{\mu'} \ge -\sqrt{(2d\|\mu_{a.c.} + \mu_{s.c.}\|-\delta)\log T} . \]

\item As $T \to \infty$
\begin{equation*}
     \inf_{\mu' \le \mu} \mathbb{P} \Big[  \frac{\inf_{x \in B(T)} f_{\mu'}(x) }{ \sqrt{\log T}} \ge - \sqrt{2d\|\mu_{a.c.} + \mu_{s.c.}\|- \delta } \Big] \to 1. 
     \end{equation*}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.985980+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72c1194cbe07f23a43df4a2b491cdc30f55a1a322716da10838529840e7a46a4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:thick_quantum_wedge of arXiv:2412.04005, '$\SLE_{\kappa}(\rho)$ processes in the light cone regime on\\ Liouville quantum gravity'] Fix $\gamma \in (0,2)$, $W > \gamma^2 / 2$, and set $\alpha = \frac{\gamma}{2} + Q - \frac{1}{\gamma}W$.  A quantum wedge of weight $W$ is the quantum surface together with two marked boundary points at $-\infty$ and $+\infty$ (when parameterized by ${\mathscr{S}}$) described by the distribution $h$ on ${\mathscr{S}}$ whose law can be sampled from as follows.  Let $X_s$ be the process defined for $s \in \mathbf{R}$ such that
\begin{itemize}
\item For $s \geq 0$,  $X_s = B_{2s} + (Q - \alpha)s$ where $B$ is a standard Brownian motion with $B_0 = 0$ conditioned so that $B_{2u} + (Q - \alpha)u > 0$ for all $u > 0$ and
\item For $s < 0$,  $X_s = \widehat{B}_{-2s} + (Q - \alpha)s$ where $\widehat{B}$ is a standard 	Brownian motion independent of $B$ with $\widehat{B}_0 = 0$.
\end{itemize}

Let $\mathcal{H}_1({\mathscr{S}})$ be the subspace of $H({\mathscr{S}})$ which consists of those functions which are constant on vertical lines of the form $u + [0,i\pi]$ for $u \in \mathbf{R}$ and let $\mathcal{H}_2({\mathscr{S}})$ be the subspace of $H({\mathscr{S}})$ which consists of those functions which have a common mean on all such vertical lines.  Then $h$ is the field with projection onto $\mathcal{H}_1({\mathscr{S}})$ given by the function whose common value on $u + [0,i\pi]$ is $X_u$ for $u \in \mathbf{R}$ and its projection onto $\mathcal{H}_2({\mathscr{S}})$ is given by the corresponding projection of a free boundary GFF on ${\mathscr{S}}$.  We fix the additive constant for the projection of $h$ onto $\mathcal{H}_1({\mathscr{S}})$ (resp.\ $\mathcal{H}_2({\mathscr{S}})$) so that its average on $[0,i\pi]$ vanishes.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.399743+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72c402546628da88236f8ca8accbe1bcbe2f885861dee726e3103cd4ab4999b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary C: open or short circuit of arXiv:2511.11036, 'Analysis of a class of Recursive Distributional Equations Including the Resistance of the '] Assume that $p = 1/2$ and let $R^{(0)}$ be any random variable taking values in $[0,+\infty]$.  Let $R^{(n)}$ be the resistance of the series-parallel graph at stage $n$, in which each edge has i.i.d.\ resistances with the same law as $R^{(0)}$, and let $q = \mathbb{P} \{ 0 < R^{(0)} < + \infty \}$.  Then $\mathbb{P} \{ 0 < R^{(n)} < + \infty \} = q$ for each $n$ and the law of $R^{(n)}$ conditional on this event has the following limit:
		\begin{align*}
			\lim_{N \to \infty} \mathbb{P} \{ (36 a)^{-\frac{1}{3}} N^{-\frac{1}{3}} \log R^{(N)}  \leq x \, \mid \, 0 < R^{(N)} < + \infty \} = F_{\text{Beta}(2,2)} ( q^{-\frac{1}{3}}x + \frac{1}{2}  ).
		\end{align*}
	Here $F_{\text{Beta}(2,2)}$ is the CDF of the $\text{Beta}(2,2)$ distribution and $a = 2 \zeta(3)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.568722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72c7e66236c1fe799ba094914a7cd7fb6b0d9abb8ed58e981ce487491451a4e1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cox of arXiv:2412.04589, 'Inverting the Markovian projection for pure jump processes'] Let $\lambda$ be a nonnegative progressively measurable process, and denote $\Lambda_t \coloneqq \int_0^t \lambda_s \,ds$. Suppose that $\Lambda_t < \infty$ for all $t \geq 0$ and $\Lambda_\infty = \infty$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. Moreover, suppose that $\Omega$ supports an $\operatorname{Exp}(1)$ random variable $E$ which is independent of $\mathcal{F}_\infty$. Define the random time
	\begin{equation*}
		\tau \coloneqq \inf\{t > 0: \Lambda_t \geq E\},
	\end{equation*}
	and define the filtration $\mathbb{A} = (\mathcal{A}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ by $\mathcal{A}_t \coloneqq \sigma(\{\tau \leq s\}; s \leq t)$. Let $\mathbb{G} \coloneqq \mathbb{F} \newtriangledown \mathbb{A}$. Then, the process $(\bm{1}_{\{\tau \leq t\}} - \Lambda_{\tau \land t})_{t \geq 0}$ is a $\mathbb{G}$-martingale, and we have $\mathbb{F} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{G}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.984722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72c842776c1df7bf3d358c55c219ebd9bc03060bec4917d0a82c7729614c915a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 45 (cf.\ {\cite[Lemma 4.3) of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] {MSS24}}] 
Suppose that $\epsilon  = o(1)$ and 
\begin{align*}
    p &= \frac{1 +  \epsilon}{n}, \\
    q &= \frac{1 +  t \epsilon}{n},
\end{align*}
for $t \ll \epsilon^{-1}$. For $u,v \in V_n$, define the event
\begin{equation*}
    F(u,v) := \big\{ u \xleftrightarrow{p} v, \,\gamma_{\cT}(u,v) \text{ contains an edge $e$ with }  \omega_e > q \big\}.
\end{equation*}
Then there exists a constant $c_g = c_g(\alpha, \rho, \mu) > 0$ such that for $n$ large enough
\begin{equation}
    \widehat{\P} \bigg( \bigcup_{u,v \in V_n, u \neq v} F(u,v) \bigg) \leq n^5 \exp \big( -c_g (t-1) \frac{\beta \epsilon }{n^\alpha}\big). 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.654304+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72ca2283c8d3add95836a4cd7c4899dba524844066464ce911e14d2b4c9c71bb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 15 of arXiv:2605.07184, 'Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with mini-batching and infinite variance'] Let $\{X_{i,n},{1\leq i\leq m_n,n\geq 1}\}$ be a triangular array of rowwise independent
  random vectors such that $\esp[\|X_{i,n}\|]<\infty$. Define
  \begin{align*}
    S_n =\sum_{i=1}^{m_n}\left\{X_{i,n}-\esp[X_{i,n}]\right\}\;.
  \end{align*}
  Let $\nu$ be an $\alpha$-homogeneous Borel measure on $\Rset^d\setminus\{0\}$. Assume
  that
  \begin{align}

    \sum_{i=1}^{m_n} & \ \pr\!\left({X_{i,n}}\in \cdot \right) \convvague \nu \; , \\

    \lim_{\epsilon\to 0} \ \limsup_{n\to\infty}   & \ \esp\left[\|X_{i,n}\|^2
    \ind{\|X_{i,n}\|\le \epsilon}\right]  = 0 \; 
  \end{align}
  as $n\to\infty$ and for every $\epsilon>0$,
  \begin{align}

    \lim_{n\to\infty} \max_{1\le i\le m_n} & \pr(\|X_{i,n}\|>\epsilon )  = 0 \; .
  \end{align}
  Then $S_n$ converges weakly to a stable random vector $\Lambda$ with characteristic
  function given by
  \begin{align}

    \log\esp\left[e^{i u^\top \Lambda}\right]=\int_{\Rset^d\setminus\{0\}}
    \left\{e^{iu^\top x}-1-iu^\top x\right\}\,\nu(\rmd x) \; , \qquad u\in\Rset^d \; .
  \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.476850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72cb57c0c5b1b4fa1d20ab3ce250f376e6bb21a72e6779e7161106691292f79c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $G,H$ be compact Lie groups, and let $X$ be a $G$-free and Hausdorff $(G\times H)$-space. Then the quotient map $\eta\colon X\to X/G$ (i.e.\ the unit of the adjunction $(-)/G\dashv{\textup{triv}}_G$) is a $\mathcal G_{H,G}$-equivariant fibration.
    \begin{proof}
        Let $K\subset H$ be any closed subgroup and let $\varphi\colon K\to G$ be any continuous group homomorphism. Using the homeomorphism $((eq:splitting-of-fp of the paper))$, we may identify $\eta^\varphi$ with the quotient map $X^\varphi\to X^\varphi/C(\im\varphi)$ followed by the inclusion of a disjoint summand. The latter is clearly a Serre fibration, while the former is so by Lemma~(lemma:quotient-map-is-fib of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.467544+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72cb843b93d330a5af499ca8a48014c685b325fe626ab4636c3099b92819f7ac","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:hypoqms of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let QMS $\mathcal{P}_t = \exp(t \mathcal{L})$ be ergodic with an invariant state $\sigma \in \mathcal{D}_+(\mathcal{M})$, and let $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{L})$ be the fixed-point algebra defined in (equation def:fixedpointspace of the paper). 
Then we have $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{L}) \subset \ker(\mathcal{L} + \mathcal{L}^\star)$, and 
$\mathcal{P}_t$ is hypocoercive, i.e., $C > 1$ in (equation ex:expdecay of the paper), if and only if 
\begin{align*}
    \dim \ker(\mathcal{L} + \mathcal{L}^\star) > \dim \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{L})\,,
\end{align*}
that is, $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{L})$ is a strict subspace of $\ker(\mathcal{L} + \mathcal{L}^\star)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.335303+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72cd3df2219a072d701e9853148a78b1b7ad85dfe20f7593f7cb8750444e314f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 of arXiv:2511.06414, 'Non-uniform Edgeworth expansions for weakly dependent random variables and their applicati'] Under Assumption \ref{AssStat}
there are polynomials $\textbf{H}_j$ whose coefficients are polynomial functions of  $\al_j=q_{j+2}-\frac{q_2p_{j+2}}{p_2}$ and $\beta_j=\frac{p_{j+2}}{p_2}$ for $j\leq m-2$ so that
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{n,m}(x)=\Phi(x)-\varphi(x)\sum_{j=1}^{m-2}\sig_n^{-j}\textbf{H}_{j}(x)+e^{-x^2/2}R_n(x)\sig_n^{-(m-1)}
