instead of the function $\Phi_{m,n}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.379047+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72ce0212db90b545c4d8e7063c70dadf13798546b4b02bb80c7f039aa05932ee","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The integrals over the symmetric group $S_N$ are given by
$$\int_{S_N}u_{i_1j_1}\ldots u_{i_kj_k}=\begin{cases}
\frac{(N-|\ker i|)!}{N!}&{\rm if}\ \ker i=\ker j\\
0&{\rm otherwise}
\end{cases}$$
where $\ker i$ denotes as usual the partition of $\{1,\ldots,k\}$ whose blocks collect the equal indices of $i$, and where $|.|$ denotes the number of blocks.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.743446+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72ce592e9ca93f6348f1fb6c72e1c2251e71a4ddcc78b9b3ace3389512d82e26","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem surftype of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\lambda$ be a connected surface (i.e., $2$-manifold), possibly non-compact and possibly with boundary. Then at least one of the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\pi_1(\lambda)$ remains nontrivial after capping off all the closed boundary components of $\lambda$.
        \item $\lambda$ is a compact genus 0 surface.
        \item $\lambda$ becomes homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^2$ after filling the closed boundary components with disks and deleting the open boundary components. In that case, $\pi_1(\lambda)$ is freely generated by its closed boundary components.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.588041+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72d9e648386aaf2af43273034d8107490b8856d912a96f641e1bb14c64fef306","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition solution definition (u P) of arXiv:2605.16963, 'Stochastic Euler Equations with Pseudo-differential Noise: Continuous and Discontinuous Pe'] Let $\tau^*$ be a stopping time with $\mathbb P(\tau^*>0)=1$, and let $(u,\tau^*) \triangleq (u(t))_{t\in [0,\tau^*)}$ be a progressively measurable process on $H_{\rm div}^s$.
		The pair $(u,\tau^*)$ is called a maximal $H_{\rm div}^s$ classical solution to (equation Target problem (u P) damp of the paper) if ${\mathbb P}\text{-}{\rm a.s.}$ the following conditions are satisfied:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item (equation Target problem (u P) damp of the paper) holds as an equality in $C(\mathbb K^d)$ for all $t\in[0,\tau^*)$;
			\item  $\sup_{[0,T]}\|u(t)\|_{H^s}<\infty$ for all $T<\tau^*$;
			\item $\limsup_{t\uparrow\tau^*}\|u(t)\|_{\mathbb H^s}=\infty$ a.s. on $\{\tau^*<\infty\}.$
		\end{itemize}
		In particular, if $\mathbb P(\tau^*=\infty)=1$, the solution is called global.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:43:45.398498+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72da5752a9cca9945e048b12b0e42c62668c70ef77df98d551defeb0677c0886","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2609.04143, 'The Geometry of Real Anisotropic Bohnenblust--Hille Constants'] Let $\mathbf q=(q_1,\ldots,q_m)$ be a Bohnenblust--Hille exponent, fix $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$, and put $\mathbf q^+=(q_1,\ldots,q_m,2)$. Then
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathbf q^+}^{(m+1)} \ge2^{1/q_i-1/2}C_{\mathbf q}^{(m)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.875079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72db16a332ba16586e897eb7575cd4faece405be5c34e5870b44305a65e90f78","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 10 (Finite directional coefficient module) of arXiv:2609.02449, 'Finite Directional Coefficient Modules over Euler Fibers of $A$-Hypergeometric Systems'] The \emph{directional relation submodule} is
\[
U_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{\mathrm{dir}}
:=
U_{\Lambda}^{\mathrm{Eul}}
+
U_{\Lambda}^{\mathrm{tr}}
+
U_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{\mathrm{van}}
\subset
F_{\Lambda},
\]
and the \emph{finite directional coefficient module} is
\begin{equation}

M_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{\mathrm{dir}}
:=
F_{\Lambda}/U_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{\mathrm{dir}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.832543+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72dc5f9c5aac6b5650e3644ac29117a0a9630930f84a1b177011c089d3dd9c76","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 25 (Smooth conditional fiber weights) of arXiv:2604.10792, 'Variable-Length Markov Chains on Finite Quivers: Boundary-Window Identifiability, Exact De'] Assume exact depth $r$ at $\theta_0$ together with \cref{ass:regularity}, and fix $m<r$. Let $y\in\cS_m$ and $z\in F_y$. Then, for $\theta$ near $\theta_0$, the stationary conditional weight
\begin{equation}
\alpha_z(\theta):=P_{\theta,\mathrm{stat}}(Z_t^{(r)}=z\mid Z_t^{(m)}=y)
\end{equation}
is well defined and $C^1$ in $\theta$.
More explicitly,
\begin{equation}
\alpha_z(\theta)=\frac{\pi_\theta(z)}{\sum_{w\in F_y}\pi_\theta(w)},
\end{equation}
where $\pi_\theta$ is the stationary law of the depth-$r$ chain.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.432847+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e2190de48bbfbc18d4c852a2dd5b1a7b60bdc27f6fdbc9457205575b1c8e04","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9--lemma6.61 of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] Assume that $A_n$ satisfies the assumptions in Theorem (Theorem1.2 of the paper) and $X\sim\operatorname{Col}_n(\zeta)$. Fix any $\kappa>0$, $\Delta>0$ and some $\sigma\in(0,1)$. Then there exists an event $\mathcal{E}_\sigma(A_n)$ with $\mathbb{P}(A_n\in\mathcal{E}_\sigma(A_n))\geq 1-\exp(-\Omega(n^{\sigma/2}))$ such that for any $A_n\in\mathcal{E}_\sigma(A_n)$, the following holds:

   \begin{enumerate}
       \item Fix any given $(\theta_1,\theta_2)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ satisfying $|\theta_1\theta_2|=1$.   Then there exists some $J\in\{1,2\}$ depending on $A_n$ and $|\theta_1/\theta_2|$ such that, for any $1\leq k\leq {c_0}_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}n^\sigma$ and for any \begin{equation}s\in\left(e^{-cn}, \frac{C_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}\cdot \mu_k(\lambda_J)}{{\left(\prod_{i=1}^2\mu_1(\lambda_i)\right)^{1/2}}}\right),\end{equation} we have the estimate
    \begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}_{\widetilde{X}}\left(\left\|\sum_{i=1}^2 \frac{\theta_i}{\mu_1(\lambda_i)}(A_n-\lambda_i I_n)^{-1}\widetilde{X}\right\|_2\leq s
\right) \lesssim s e^{-ck},
    \end{equation} where ${c_0}_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}>0$ and $C_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}>0$ are two constants that depend only on $\kappa,\Delta$ and $B$. The constant $c>0$ depends only on $B$.
    \item  Fix any given $(\theta_1,\theta_2)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ satisfying $\max(|\theta_1|,|\theta_2|)=1$. Then there exist $I,J\in\{1,2\}$ depending on $A_n$ and $|\theta_1/\theta_2|$ such that, for any $1\leq k\leq {c_0}_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}n^\sigma$ and any 
    \begin{equation}
s\in(e^{-cn},\frac{\mu_k(\lambda_J)}{\mu_1(\lambda_I)})
    \end{equation} we have the same estimate (equation 2122122121122 of the paper).
     \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.452248+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e49df4d383aeacfb260151a16fcb758c4217e521189441ee3ccab7b797c09b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 24 of arXiv:2604.03213, 'Asymptotic expansion for transport maps \\ between laws of multimatrix models'] With the notations of Theorem \ref{chainrule},
\begin{equation}

    \partial \cg = \sum_{i=1}^d \left( (\partial_i \mathbf{J})\circ \cf \right)\sh_1 \left(\partial \mathbf{f_i} \right).
\end{equation}
Besides, if given $ \cX = A\otimes B \otimes \tr(R_1)\otimes \cdots\otimes \tr(R_m)$  and $ \cY = h\otimes C_1 \otimes C_2 \otimes \tr(R_{m+1})\otimes \cdots\otimes \tr(R_n)$, we set
$$ \cX\sh_4\cY = h\otimes C_2AC_1 \otimes B \otimes \tr(R_1)\otimes \cdots\otimes \tr(R_n),$$
then
\begin{equation}

