{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.119400+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72f61a41b4beac0dd3dbbb93e0389e91ac974f228a2d14cad3963443175a13ff","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma ManyToOneLemma of arXiv:2608.17031, 'A shape theorem for periodic branching diffusion'] Let $(Y_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the weak solution introduced above, let $\mathcal{F}^Y_t=\sigma(Y_s; s\in [0,t])$, and $F:C([0,t])\rightarrow [0,\infty)$ be a given function. Recall that for $\zeta \in \mathbb{R}^d$, $\gamma(\zeta)$ denotes the principal eigenvalue of the eigenvalue problem (equation eq:Intro:EigenvalueProblem of the paper), and $\psi(\cdot;\zeta)$ its associated eigenfunction. Recall that $X_s(v)$ denotes the position of the particle $v$ at time $s$. Then, if $F((Y_s)_{s \in [0,t]})$ is $\mathcal{F}^Y_t$-measurable,
