Moreover, when \(Y\) is independent of \(x\), \(\sup_{x\in \mathbb{R}^m}|u(x,y)|\le C(1+|y|)\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.316274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7335d471e09cee598ca9a517e7affbfff2484c982ce054a909d5a5e52653de58","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:triple of arXiv:2601.10359, 'An Operator It\^o Formula for Volterra Gaussian Processes:\\ The Intrinsic Bracket via Cau'] A \emph{causal derivation--divergence triple} is $(D,\delta,\Pi)$ where:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item $D: \Dom(D) \subset L^2(\Omega) \to L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H})$ is a densely-defined closed linear operator (the \emph{derivation}) with $\mathbf{1}\in\Dom(D)$ and $D\mathbf{1}=0$.
\item $\delta: \Dom(\delta) \subset L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H}) \to L^2(\Omega)$ is the Hilbert space adjoint of $D$ (the \emph{divergence}):
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[F\delta(u)] = \mathbb{E}[\inner{DF}{u}_\mathcal{H}], \qquad F \in \Dom(D),\; u \in \Dom(\delta).
\end{equation}
\item $\Pi: L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H}) \to L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H})$ is a bounded projection (``causal'' or ``predictable'') with $\Pi^2 = \Pi$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.825297+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7336970761cf8a7bb364f09225fe4a08313267250fa58c23a21ad4be09fb129f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:optimal-four-mark of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] For every $h\geq2$, put
