{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.923107+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73513799dc64998f9f20079f671ebc0dc5187e494c853443a379e9164a468e70","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma equivlemma of arXiv:2412.02087, 'The spectral distribution of random graphs with given degree sequences'] We assume that for arbitrarily large $C > 0$ and arbitrarily small $\varepsilon > 0$, the degree sequence satisfies that $\sum_{i = 1}^n \mathbbm{1}\{ d_i^{(n)} < C \sqrt{D^{(n)}/n} \} \leq \varepsilon n$ for all large $n > N(C, \varepsilon)$. Then, with probability $1 - o(1)$, we may remove at most $2 \varepsilon n$ vertices and edges adjacent to them, so that the following holds:
