{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.133664+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7451058c04c241005088ef28ec5905ce1b917909dc444752a96d90e20227ad01","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:unimodularity of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Let $\Gamma$ be a transitive subgroup of automorphism of the graph $G$. If we have the {\bf mass transport principle (MTP)} 
	\begin{equation}

		\sum_{y \in V(G)} f(y,x) =
		\sum_{y \in V(G)} f(x,y)
	\end{equation}
	for any diagonally $\Gamma$-invariant $f  :  V(G) \times V(G) \longrightarrow [0, \infty]$ function and for any $x \in V(G)$, then we say that the action of $\Gamma$ is {\bf unimodular}.	If the action of $\mathrm{Aut}(G)$ is unimodular on $G$, then we simply say that $G$ is unimodular.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.720618+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7455da7d160fc4919b7bf35ddac397fa8a5a14a0ee030c3af1b76c1b20fd6783","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 of arXiv:2511.13404, 'Asymptotic stability and mean ergodicity of Feller processes on Polish spaces'] Let $\mathfrak{F}$ be a sub-family of $\mathcal{A}$.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item $\Phi$ is said to be \emph{equicontinuous w.r.t $\mathfrak{F}$} at $x\in \X$ if 
		\begin{equation}
			\limsup\limits_{x'\rightarrow x}\sup\limits_{t\ge0}|P_tf(x')-P_tf(x)|=0,\quad \forall\,f\in \mathfrak{F}.
		\end{equation}
        \item $\Phi$ is said to be  \emph{uniformly equicontinuous w.r.t $\mathfrak{F}$} at $x\in \X$ if 
		\begin{equation*}
			\limsup\limits_{x'\rightarrow x}\sup\limits_{t\ge0}\sup_{f
            \in\mathfrak{F}}|P_tf(x')-P_tf(x)|=0.
		\end{equation*} 
    \end{enumerate}
    If $P_t$ is replaced by $Q_t$, then we refer to these properties as  \emph{Cesàro equicontinuous w.r.t.\ $\mathfrak{F}$} and \emph{uniformly Cesàro equicontinuous w.r.t.\ $\mathfrak{F}$} at $x \in \X$. If the property holds for all $x \in \X$, then we replace ``at $x \in \X$" by ``on $\X$" and sometimes omit this phrase for convenience.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.045428+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_745ee4ce0564f9f75992323c3aeb589907580c775c1af510751549b0c0fd5239","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_SBOspec of arXiv:2411.13972, 'The Stochastic Bessel operator at high temperatures'] {DiffusionAtTheRandomMatrixHardEdge}}] ~\\
