{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.877318+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_764dd974d0993841127a02ad81b6f03b2bdc486f6d053be25d3fe8571438891c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2608.29152, 'Uniform Statistical Convergence of Empirical Sinkhorn Potentials\\ with Exponential and Po'] Under Assumptions~\ref{ass:poly-complexity} and \ref{ass:poly-stability}, there is a universal constant $C$ such that for any $\delta\in(0,1)$, if the sample size $n$ satisfies:
\begin{equation}
 C\left[ A_0\eps^{-p}\sqrt{H_\eps} + E_0\eps^{-a}\sqrt{\log(4/\delta)} \right] n^{-1/2} \le 1/2, 
\end{equation}
then with probability at least $1-\delta$, we obtain the bound:
\begin{equation}
 d_\infty([\widehat f_{\eps,n}],[f_\eps]) \le \frac{C K_0}{\sqrt n} \left[ A_0\eps^{1-r-p}\sqrt{H_\eps} + E_0\eps^{1-r-a} \sqrt{\log\frac4\delta} \right].
\end{equation}
Provided $0<\eps\le1$, $p\ge a$, and $H_\eps\ge1$, this implies the simplified rate:
\begin{equation}
 d_\infty([\widehat f_{\eps,n}],[f_\eps]) \lesssim \eps^{1-r-p} \sqrt{\frac{H_\eps+\log(1/\delta)}{n}}. 
\end{equation}
If $H_\eps \lesssim \eps^{-h}$, the simplified exponent becomes $\eps^{1-r-p-h/2}$. All displayed dependence on $\eps^{-1}$ is polynomial (ignoring logarithms inside $H_\eps$). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

