{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.373483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78b84487e4947654ca849e5896600f6890d559253475513de4451cc9f0d61a87","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 5 (Stopping Time Criterion) of arXiv:2606.04245, 'Properties of a Special Type of Filtration and its Martingale Criteria'] A random variable $T$ is a stopping time with respect to $\FF=(\cF_t)_{t\in\RR_+}$ if and only if the following property holds: if the set $\{T<\gamma\}$ is non-empty, then there exists an $\cH$-measurable stopping time $S$ such that
