{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:55.027893+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78df097c878dd7b6d24dfbc3900e6368ba7bc22d7fc16960b6a44dd015282617","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:separation-nnb of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] $\mathcal{V}^e_\Box \subsetneq \mathcal{V}^e \cap \mathscr{L}_\Box$. In particular:
  (1) $\neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot \in \mathcal{V}^e$, and
  (2) $\neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot \notin \mathcal{V}^e_\Box$.
  \begin{proof}
    (1) Take any $\Diamond$-p model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$,
    then take any $x, x'$ such that $x \leq x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot$.
    Then $x' \leq x'$ implies $x' \nVdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \Box \bot$, so there is $x''$ such that $x' \leq x'' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Box \bot$,
    which implies there is no $y''$ such that $x'' \sqsubset y''$.
    Now suppose, by way of contradiction, that there is $y'$ such that $x' \sqsubset y'$,
    then $x'' \geq x' \sqsubset y'$, so by the $\Diamond$-p condition, there must be some $y''$ such that $x'' \sqsubset y'' \geq y'$, which is a contradiction.
    Now that there is no $y'$ such that $x' \sqsubset y'$, we obtain $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Box \bot$.
    Therefore, $x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot$.

    (2) Consider the model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, where $W = \set{ x, x', x'', y, y' }$,
    $\mathord{\leq}$ is the reflexive and transitive closure of $\set{ (x, x'), (x', x''), (y, y') }$,
    $\mathord{\sqsubset} = \set{ (x, y), (x', y')}$,
    and $V$ is arbitrary.
    The model $\mathfrak{M}$ is visualized in Figure (fig:nnb-countermodel of the paper).

    \begin{figure}[ht]
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}
      \fill (0, 0) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 0) node[left]{$x$};

      \fill (2, 0) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 0) node[right]{$y$};

      \fill (0, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 2) node[left]{$x'$};

      \fill (2, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 2) node[right]{$y' \nVdash \bot$};

      \fill (0, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 4) node[right]{$x'' \Vdash \Box \bot$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0,0) to (0,2);
      \draw (0,1) node[left]{$\leq$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel, squig] (0,0) to (2,0);
      \draw (1, 0) node[below]{$\sqsubset$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (2,0) to (2,2);
      \draw (2,1) node[right]{$\leq$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel, squig] (0,2) to (2,2);
      \draw (1, 2) node[above]{$\sqsubset$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0,2) to (0,4);
      \draw (0,3) node[left]{$\leq$};

      \end{tikzpicture}
      \caption{The model $\mathfrak{M}$.}

    \end{figure}

    It is easy to see that $\mathfrak{M}$ is a $\Box$-p model.
    Also, we can easily check that $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot$,
    $x' \nVdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Box \bot$,
    and so $x \nVdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:58:24.503482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78df0a9bf756808cbbc3321f835bafb1749b2f6ea865028365bd5818fd577b45","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:poisson-dirichlet of arXiv:2601.09537, 'Gene genealogies in haploid populations evolving according to sweepstakes reproduction'] Let $0 < \alpha < 1$ be fixed,  write
$x := (x_1, x_2, \ldots)$ for $x \in \Delta_{+}$ (recall
~(equation eq:simplex of the paper)), and $(x,x) := \sum_{j=1}^\infty x_j^2$. Let
$F_\alpha$ be a probability measure on $\Delta_{+}$ associated with
the Poisson-Dirichlet$(\alpha,0)$-distribution, and $\Xi_\alpha$ a
measure on $\Delta_{+}$ given by
\begin{displaymath}
\Xi_\alpha({\rm d}x) =   (x,x)F_\alpha( {\rm d}x)
\end{displaymath}
A Poisson-Dirichlet$(\alpha,0)$-coalescent is a discrete-time
$\Xi$-coalescent with $\Xi$-measure $\Xi_\alpha$ and no atom at zero.
The transition probability of merging blocks in $r$ groups of size
$k_{1}, \ldots, k_{r} \ge 2$ with current number of blocks
$b \ge k_{1} + \cdots + k_{r}$ and $s = b - k_{1} - \cdots - k_{r}$ is
(recall~(equation eq:1 of the paper) in Definition~(def:notation of the paper)),
\begin{equation}

p_{b; k_{1}, \ldots, k_{r}; s} =  \frac{\alpha^{r+s-1}(r+s-1)! }{(b-1)!}\prod_{i=1}^{r} {(k_{i}-1 - \alpha)}_{k_{i}-1}
\end{equation}
so that $p_{2;2;0} = 1-\alpha$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.449739+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78df9aea398900d1e318e5310639135dd3c16d41225ee95bdb554e12a9d7ff44","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition d:trigonometric polynomials of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] A function $p:\R\to\C$ is called a \textit{trigonometric polynomial} of degree $m\in\N_0$ if there are $a_{-m},...,a_m\in\C$ such that $a_{-m}\neq 0$ or $a_m\neq 0$, and
    \begin{equation}
        p(x) = \sum_{k=-m}^m a_k e^{ikx},\quad x\in\R.
    \end{equation}
    Similarly, a function $p:\R^l\to\C$ is called a \textit{multivariate trigonometric polynomial} of degree $(m_1,...,m_l)\in\N_0^l$ if there are $a_{k_1,...,k_l}\in\C$, $k_1,...,k_l\in\Z$ with $|k_j|\leq m_j$ for $j\in\{1,...,l\}$, such that
    \begin{equation}
        p(x_1,...,x_l) = \sum_{\substack{k_1,...,k_l\in\Z,\\|k_j|\leq m_j \textnormal{ for } j\in\{1,...,l\}}} a_{k_1,...,k_l} e^{ik_1x_1+...+ik_lx_l},\quad x\in\R^l,
    \end{equation}
     and such that, for all $j\in\{1,...,l\}$, there are $k_1,...,k_l\in\Z$ with $a_{k_1,...,k_l}\neq 0$ and $|k_j|=m_j$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.776781+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78e107fbb9aa4f7871c97f7526a63502201d04de3ff6460e97892618f3c23bdf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma cover_to_green of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $A, B \subseteq \mathbb Z^d$ and $\ell \in \mathbb N^+$, then we have 
\begin{align*}
	\sum_{y \in B} \mathbf P_y(\tau_{A} \le \ell) \ge \frac{G_{\ell}(A, B)}{\max_{x \in A} G_{\ell}(x, A)}.
\end{align*}
Similarly, for any $\ell_1, \ell_2 \in \mathbb N^+$ we also have 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{y \in B} \mathbf P_y(\tau_{A} \le \ell_1) \le \frac{G_{\ell_1 + \ell_2}(A, B)}{\max_{x \in A} G_{\ell_2}(x, A)}.
\end{align*}
Moreover, the same inequalities hold on the torus $\mathbb{T}^d_L$ by replacing $G_\ell$ with $G^{(L)}_\ell$ and $\mathbf P_y$ with $\mathbf P^{(L)}_y$ for any $L \in \mathbb N^+$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.228791+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78e30251574e5d56fc07f6ca8a025ae05f20872054acc7126ecc5a98698842a7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition replace-M-by-explicit-expression-in-tightness-for-critical of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] Let $\Pi$ be a fixed point of BBM with drift $\lambda =\sqrt{2}$ and $\theta \in Dom_{\lambda}(\Pi)$. Then, the random variables $\{\widetilde{Y}_t^b\}_{t\ge 1}$ given by 

\begin{equation}
\widetilde{Y}_t^b := \int_{-\infty}^{\frac{1}{4} \log t }\frac{1}{t^{3/2}}(-x + \log t)e^{\sqrt{2}x} e^{-\frac{x^2}{2t}} \theta(dx)     
\end{equation}
is tight.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:01:25.573901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78e741ac3707687a67f34fcf2476ad842d0464fc46a376b1a4507af39c978a3b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem TH1-* of arXiv:2605.15742, 'Turbulent stretching of FENE dumbbell polymer model via special stochastic scaling and sin'] \textbf{(Existence with cut-off.)} There exists  at    least   one     solution $f^\varepsilon_N$ to  (equation Ito-FP_eps-V2 of the paper) in the sense of  (Def-sol-QR-* of the paper). Moreover,  it holds 
\begin{align}
 \ensuremath{\mathbb{E}}\Big[\sup_{v\in[0,t]} \Vert f^\varepsilon_N(v)\Vert_H^2  \Big]&+\dfrac{4}{\beta}\ensuremath{\mathbb{E}}\int_0^t [f^\varepsilon_N(s)]_V^2ds\leq 2\Vert f_{0}\Vert_H^2\exp{(4\mathcal{K}_\varepsilon t)} \text{ for all } t\in [0,T],
