		\]
		Here, $\mathsf{d}(x,y)$ denotes the chemical distance on $\mathscr{C}_\infty$, i.e., minimum length of the open paths connecting $x$ and $y$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:19.153856+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78fce7468d27e90e1884efaceb80a6e9cb15211e60c3ef9cf1dc9369b65634cf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma3.4 of arXiv:2609.02607, 'Spatial inhomogeneity for a three-dimensional doubly degenerate nutrient system with indir'] Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^3$ be  a smoothly bounded domain, $k>-1$ and $\beta\in (k+2,k+\frac{8}{3})$. Then for all $m^*>0$, one can find $C_1=C_1(k, \beta)>0$ and $C_2=C_2(m^*,k,\beta)>0$ with the property that whenever $\varphi\in C^1(\overline \Omega)$ and $\psi \in C^1(\overline \Omega)$ are positive 
in $\overline \Omega$ fulfilling  $\int_{\Omega}\varphi\leq m^*$,
  \begin{align}
    \int_{\Omega}\varphi^{\beta}\psi\leq  2\int_{\Omega}\varphi^{k}\psi|\nabla \varphi|^2+C_1\int_\Omega\frac{|\nabla \psi|^{\frac{2\beta}{\beta-k-2}}}{\psi^{\frac{2\beta}{\beta-k-2}-1}}+C_2\int_{\Omega}\varphi \psi
