{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.715035+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a506cb3301fed4511c96832d21d63bdc43212b6e5ea69d65362059d449671a6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:truth-lemma-imp-left of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] Let $L \supseteq \textbf{FIK}$, $x = (\Gamma_x, \Delta_x) \in W_L^*$, $\varphi, \psi \in \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$.
  If $(\varphi \to \psi) \notin \Gamma_x$, then there is $x' = (\Gamma_x', \Delta_x') \in W_L^*$ such that
  $x \leq^* x'$, $\varphi \in \Gamma_x'$, and $\psi \in \Delta_x'$.
  \begin{proof}
    Suppose $(\varphi \to \psi) \notin \Gamma_x$, then $(\varphi \to \psi) \in \Delta_x$ by maximality.
    Here, $(\Gamma_x \cup \set{\varphi}, \set{\psi})$ is $L$-consistent;
    for otherwise, there is a finite $X \subseteq \Gamma_x$ such that $L \vdash \left(\bigwedge X \land \varphi\right) \to \psi$,
    then $L \vdash \bigwedge X \to (\varphi \to \psi)$, so $(\Gamma, \Delta)$ would be $L$-inconsistent since $X \subseteq \Gamma_x$ and $\varphi \to \psi \in \Delta_x$.
    Now by the Lindenbaum lemma, there is $x' = (\Gamma_x', \Delta_x') \in W_L^*$ such that $\Gamma_x \cup \set{\varphi} \subseteq \Gamma_x'$ and $\psi \in \Delta_x'$.
    Here, $x \leq^* x'$ by the definition of $\leq^*$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.294457+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a51bd5eea9de76e1fceb9360bd347666061dd42cbf2959e4b8f71d7990810ae","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Tm item:characterization of arXiv:2411.05441, 'Sticky diffusions on star graphs : characterization and It\^o formula'] Let $X(t) = (i(t), x(t))$ be a sticky diffusion on a star graph $\Gamma$ with stickiness parameter $\eta > 0$. Then,
	\begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\roman*)}]
	 \item  there exists a nonsticky diffusion $Y$ on $\Gamma$ and a strictly increasing continuous function $V^{-1}$ such that $X$ and $Y(V^{-1}(\cdot))$ are indistinguishable. Moreover, there exists an increasing process $\ell_X$, adapted to the completion of the natural filtration of $X$ with respect to the collection of probability measures $\left ( \mathbf P_\mathsf{x} \right)_{\mathsf{x} \in \Gamma}$, such that $\ell_X$ almost surely increases only when $X = \mathsf{v}$ and $V^{-1}(t) = t - \eta \ell_X(t)$;

	 \item  up to an extension of the filtered probability space, there exists a one-dimensional Brownian motion $W$ such that, almost surely, we have
	 \begin{equation} 
		\begin{cases}
		dx(t) = \sigma_{i(t)}(x(t)) \mathbf{1}_{\{x(t) \ne 0\}}\, d W(t) + b_{i(t)}(x(t)) \mathbf{1}_{\{x(t) \ne 0\}}\, dt + d\ell_X(t), \\
		\eta \ell_X(t) = \int_0^t \mathbf 1_{\{X(s) = \mathsf{v} \}} ds;
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}

	\item  for every $f \in C_b^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}_+ \times \Gamma)$, almost surely, we have the It\^o formula
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
	& f(t,X(t)) = f(0,X(0)) + \int_0^t \left ( \partial_s f(s,X(s)) +  Lf(s,X(s)) \right ) \mathbf 1_{\{ X(s) \neq \mathsf{v} \}}  ds \\
	& \quad + \int_0^t \sigma(X(s)) \partial_x f(s,X(s)) \mathbf{1}_{\{X(s) \neq \mathsf{v} \}} d  W(s)
	 + \int_0^t \left ( \eta\, \partial_s f(s,\mathsf{v}) +  \sum_{i=1}^N \rho_i \partial_x f_i(s,0) \right ) d \ell_X(s).
	\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.645710+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a52fd427da5d4ef8d649326be357951e6e3c21484bd7b1fea84de1b5f6ed520","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ballvol of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Assume that ~ $0 \leq \mu \ll 1$ and $T_0$ a finite tree of height $r$ with $K$ vertices at level $r$. For any $M \in \mathbb{N}$, it holds that
\begin{equation} 
\nu_n ^\mu \big(\mathcal{B}_{1/r} (T_0) \big) \geq\big(1 + o(1)\big)K \frac{e^{-\mu r}}{4^{|T_0| -K}} \left( \sum\limits_{S=1} ^M  C_{S-1} 4^{-S} \right)^{K-1} \, ,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{B}_{1/r}(T_0)$ is the open ball of radius $1/r$ around the tree $T_0$ in the local topology.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:33:12.352993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a57afcea96c197da349cd90d9f01a0de6da1f3e4efa3f4d8cd575599f32e59b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn 199 (truncation) of arXiv:2608.29416, 'Algorithmic threshold for high-dimensional projection pursuit I: general theory'] For the spherical perceptron, let $\vX\equiv\vX^{\RomI:\RomIII}$ be the three-dimensional process defined by \eqref{e:X.decomp} and \eqref{e:vX} above. Given a (large constant) parameter $\trK$, we define a \textbf{truncated} process $\vX^\trstar$ by initializing
$\vX^\trstar(q_0,a)=\vX(q_0,a) = (0,0,0)\in\R^3$, and setting
    \[
    \vX^\trstar(q_{d+1},a)
    =\begin{cases}
    \vX(q_{d+1},a) &\textup{if $\vX^\trstar(q_d,a)\in[-\trK,\trK)^3$,}\\
    \vX^\trstar(q_d,a) &\textup{otherwise,}
    \end{cases}
    \]
so that the process $\vX^\trstar$ stays frozen upon exiting $[-\trK,\trK)^3$. For the Ising perceptron, we apply the above procedure to obtain $\vX^{\trstar,\RomI:\RomIII}$. We separately define $X^{\trstar,\Ising}$ to be the process $X^\Ising$ from \eqref{e:Delta.X.Ising}, frozen upon exiting $[-\trK,\trK)$. We then denote $\vX^\trstar\equiv(\vX^{\trstar,\RomI:\RomIII},X^{\trstar,\Ising})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.449866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a58924c8e2153ff91a9a6b7e20dca626e6a2ccf5c9877b7a2bf1011077e5ecc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $n,l\in\N, m\in\N_0$, $p_1,...,p_d:\R^l\to\R$ be real-valued (multivariate) trigonometric polynomials, and $x_1,...,x_n\in\R^d$. We say that $x_1,...,x_n$ form an \textit{averaging set} of $(p_1,...,p_d)$ of order $m$ and size $n$ if they form an averaging set of
    \begin{equation}
        (p_1,...,p_d)(\lambda_l|_{[0,2\pi)^l}) = \int_{[0,2\pi)^l} \I\{(p_1(x),...,p_d(x))\in\cdot\}\, dx
    \end{equation}
    of order $m$ and size $n$, i.e.,
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{1}{(2\pi)^l}\int_{[0,2\pi)^l} (p_1(x),...,p_d(x))^{\otimes q}\, dx = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=1}^n x_k^{\otimes q},\quad q\in\{0,...,m\}.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.316408+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a5af5ef3c371e1c47dbb309c657837c07c3d0634865b55b1c5c1c1a9bc9f064","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: hille yosida of arXiv:2506.21466, 'The Markov property for $\varphi^4_3$ on the cylinder'] There exists a self-adjoint linear map $\mathbf H$ acting on a dense domain $D(\mathbf H)\subset \mathcal H$ such that
