{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.579294+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ba3b5efbd0baede8e893d8926400bae2018bda6b0ed9351b622d5f2a03f02f7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 (Finite-type vs.\ continuous-type dichotomy) of arXiv:2608.15987, 'Threshold cascades of interacting diffusions on unimodular random trees: a conditional bra'] Let $A_u$ denote the failure strength of a vertex $u$ (e.g.\ the overshoot $s^{(u)}_{\tau_\theta}-\theta^{\mathrm{fail}}$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item In the saturated regime the conditional law of a child's transmission given the parent's trajectory depends on the parent only through the event $\{u\text{ failed}\}$, not through $A_u$. Hence the failure strengths $A_v$ of the children are conditionally i.i.d.\ and independent of $A_u$, the offspring counts are conditionally Binomial with the deterministic parameter $p_{d,d'}$, and the process is the finite-type GW of Theorem~\ref{thm:reduction}.
\item Outside the saturated regime the coupling $\sigma(s^{(u)}-\theta_{uw})$ lies in its sensitive range, $A_u$ is inherited by the children with positive correlation, and the natural state space is $D\times\R$ (hazard domain $\times$ failure strength), a continuous type. The offspring law is then a Cox (doubly-stochastic) mixture, and criticality is governed by the Perron root of a mean operator on $L^2$ of the strength variable, not by a finite matrix.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.354963+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ba49647f6da899d356411b7aa0bda020206b79ecd0cc710d866703798fe5354","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition MSSAdaption of arXiv:2412.01635, 'On the Weak Convergence of the Function-Indexed Sequential Empirical Process and its Smoot'] (Maximum partial sums of processes)\\

Let $\nu \geq 1$, $\Psi \neq \emptyset$ and $W_1,...,W_n: \Omega \to \ell^\infty(\Psi)$ be arbitrary processes. If, for all $1 \leq i \leq j \leq n,$ it holds 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm E^*\left\{ \norm{ S_{i,j}^W}_{\Psi}^\nu \right\} \leq q^{\alpha}(j-i+1)
\end{equation}
for a pair $(\alpha, q)$ that fulfills condition (equation MSS-ConditionM of the paper) with index $Q \in [1,2^{(\alpha-1)/\alpha})$ , then there exists a constant $A$ that only depends on $\alpha, \nu,$ and $Q$ for which we have
\begin{equation}
\mathrm E^* \left\{ \norm{ M_{i,j}^W }_\Psi^\nu \right\} \leq A q^{\alpha}(j-i+1)
\end{equation}
for any $1 \leq i \leq j \leq n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.291654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ba5ac48621ff3dc01a383135e3c148d6ab2914bb7cf59af476cd1b7da4e9f66","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Girko_reduction of arXiv:2605.21265, 'The non-Hermitian minor process'] Assume the set-up and conditions of Theorem~(theo:main of the paper). There exists a (small) fixed $\delta_c>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Cov}\left(L^{(k_1)}(f_1), L^{(k_2)}(f_2)\right) = \mathrm{Cov}\left(I_{c}^{(k_1)}(f_1), I_{c}^{(k_2)}(f_2)\right) + \mathcal{O}(N^{-c}),

\end{equation}
for any $f_j\in H^{2+\delta}_0(\Omega)$ and $k_j\in[N]$ with $x_j:=k_j/N\in [c_0,1]$, $j=1,2$. The constant $c>0$ does not depend on $N$, and the implicit constant in $\mathcal{O}(\cdot)$ may depend on $\|\Delta f_j\|_{L^2(\Omega)}$ and $|\Omega|$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.351470+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ba792530d14d2c103f7a3b2fc1feb8f630eaa95f2663a1012202a7a359d25fb","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2412.06420, 'Accuracy, Estimates, and Representation Results'] A \emph{Bregman divergence}\index{Bregman divergence} associated with a convex function $\varphi$ is:
