{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.114082+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7bca6ac03ce038e36205cf71825a45b2c20c21f77e1fa2c1bd7de57bbe51cf2e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:unimodularity of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Let $\Gamma$ be a transitive subgroup of automorphism of the graph $G$. If we have the \textbf{mass transport principle (MTP)}
	\begin{equation}

		\sum_{y \in V(G)} f(y,x) =
		\sum_{y \in V(G)} f(x,y)
	\end{equation}
	for any diagonally $\Gamma$-invariant $f  :  V(G) \times V(G) \longrightarrow [0, \infty]$ function and for any $x \in V(G)$, then we say that the action of $\Gamma$ is \textbf{unimodular}.	If the action of $\mathrm{Aut}(G)$ is unimodular on $G$, then we simply say that $G$ is unimodular.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:33:11.964021+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7bccf1690874f870ac825dd2b1046c71647c937be51a3c548aa3d66f0d9d881a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:tree-max of arXiv:2607.24018, 'Jeu de taquin forests \\ and the inverse infinite RSK correspondence'] Given an IYT $T$ and a tree $\tau$ in $\Phi(T)$, the
