{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.680893+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7c512f8386bba152b0f9542ce58ff5174f09e6b804afc2ef167d73ff3c108127","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn d:stabilizing-graph of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] We say that a family of graphs 
    $(\Psi(\mathcal{P}_n))_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$  (where each graph is generated by the same rule \(\mu \mapsto \Psi(\mu)\) represented by the marking neighborhood $\bar N$) forms a {\em stabilizing interaction graph} \(\Psi\) on finite windows with respect to a point process \(\mathcal{P}\) if   for every \(p \in \mathbb{N}\), there exist a fast decreasing function $\varphi_p'$ (see~(equation varphibd of the paper)), such that the  interaction range satisfies:
\begin{equation} 
\sup_{1 \leq n < \infty} \sup_{x_1, \ldots, x_p \in W_n} \mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p}
\bigl(S_n(x_1, \mathcal{P}_n) > s\bigr) \leq \varphi_p'(s), \quad s > 0.
