Then there is a nonzero real number $s$ such that $q_i(t) = \frac{2s}{p_i}t + O(t^2)$, and $\theta(t) = \frac{8s^2}{p_1p_2}t^2 + O(t^3)$. Consequently, $p_1p_2 > 0$, and $\frac{q_1(t)q_2(t)}{\theta(t)} = \frac12 + O(t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.748478+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cc65c64330481e92445e325d794416f755144a68b3a7d1a2e9c442d68b9f932","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 17 of arXiv:2607.28865, 'First-Passage Percolation on Spread-out line graphs: Microscopic Regime'] Fix $j>2\ell-2$ and set
\begin{align*}
    \fL_0:=\{0,1,\ldots,\ell-1\},
    \qquad
    \fR_j:=\{j-\ell+1,\ldots,j\}.
\end{align*}
For $u\in\{\ell-1,\ldots,j-\ell\}$, let
\begin{align*}
    \cC_u^{(j)}
    :=
    \{(x,y)\in\cE[0,j]:x\le u<y\}.
\end{align*}
Then, for every $S\subseteq\cE[0,j]$, the following hold.
\begin{enumeratei}
    \item If $|S|<\lc$, then deleting $S$ does not disconnect $\fL_0$ from $\fR_j$.

    \item If $|S|=\lc$ and deleting $S$ disconnects $\fL_0$ from $\fR_j$, then
    $S=\cC_u^{(j)}$ for a unique $u\in\{\ell-1,\ldots,j-\ell\}$.  Moreover, for every
    $e\in\cC_u^{(j)}$, there is a simple $\fL_0$-$\fR_j$ path whose unique edge in
    $\cC_u^{(j)}$ is $e$.
\end{enumeratei}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.454688+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ccc19fe7b3341f6125b925f953f89ee6990b3461c20fae3a10b4634f2ba5524","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Df eq:1-arm_event of arXiv:2605.14910, 'Sharp Phase Transition for the Formation of Infinite Tubes'] Let $p\in[0,1]$.
  For a width parameter $w>0$ and a scale parameter $n\in\mathbb{N}$, we define the \emph{tubular one-arm probability} $\theta_{w,n}^{\mathrm{tube}}(p)$ by
  \begin{equation}
  \theta_{w,n}^{\mathrm{tube}}(p)
 :=\mathbb{P}_p\left(
  \begin{array}{l}
    \text{$\exists$ an open $k$-tube of width at most $w$ whose initial sphere}\\
    \text{intersects $\Lambda_o$ and terminal sphere intersects $\partial\Lambda_n$}
  \end{array}
  \right).
  \end{equation}
  By convention, when $n\le1/2+w$, we regard the event in~(equation eq:1-arm_event of the paper) as occurring trivially, and hence set $\theta_{w,n}^{\mathrm{tube}}(p):=1$.
  Indeed, when $n\le1/2+w$, there exists a single topological $(k-1)$-sphere intersecting both $\Lambda_o$ and $\partial\Lambda_n$, which may be viewed as a trivial $k$-tube whose initial and terminal spheres coincide.
  We refer to the event in~(equation eq:1-arm_event of the paper) as a \emph{tubular one-arm event} with width at most $w$, and denote it by $\mathcal{T}_{w,n}$~(see Figure~(fig:one-arm of the paper)).","status":"unverified"}

