{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.651804+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7dba675fa54c47e7333b6e0f4f9a4e731eedecb464e877838693d2f89c27ab51","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:holonomy-process of arXiv:2608.15923, 'Universality of two-dimensional Markovian holonomy fields'] A holonomy process on the pointed surface $(\Sigma,o)$ with structure group $G$ is a family $H=(H_\ell)_{\ell\in L_o(\Sigma)}$ of $G$-valued random variables on a common probability space such that, almost surely,
\[ \begin{cases}
 H_{1_o}=1_G \\
 \forall \ell_1,\ell_2\in L_o(\Sigma), H_{\ell_1\ell_2}=H_{\ell_1}H_{\ell_2} \\
 \forall \ell\in L_o(\Sigma), H_{\ell^{-1}}=H_\ell^{-1}
\end{cases}.
\]
Equivalently, it is a random element of $\mathrm{Hom}(L_o(\Sigma),G)$ equipped with the cylinder $\sigma$-field generated by the evaluation maps. If ${\mathbb{G}}$ is a topological map in $\Sigma$ containing $o$ as a vertex, a holonomy process on ${\mathbb{G}}$ is defined in the same way with $L_o({\mathbb{G}})$ in place of $L_o(\Sigma)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.389351+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7dbbf3ea461af3f2ac72fac0d020f4fc4346bfc5e4e7e789935f51b715f4de63","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:stein of arXiv:2507.10531, 'Quantitative central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] Let $X$ be as in (equation eq:tilt of the paper) and $f : \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}$ be some function satisfying $\mathbb{E}[f(X)] = 0$.
Define $b, \delta_0, \delta_1, \delta_1', \delta_2, \delta_3$ as above, and let $Z \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$.
Then we have
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{d}_\mathrm{Was}(f(X),Z) &\leq \frac{C}{|b|} \mleft(\delta_0 + \delta_1 + \delta_2 + \delta_3\mright), \\
    \text{and} \qquad \mathbf{d}_\mathrm{Kol}(f(X),Z) &\leq \frac{C}{|b|} \mleft(\delta_0 + \delta_1' + \delta_2 + \delta_3\mright),
\end{align}
for some absolute constant $C$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:39:11.669208+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7dc10d8d617c06f9ff205110c56c87c8fd42da95ba3761ff2d57a1a57d445844","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 (Exact ordered tuple formula) of arXiv:2608.04799, '2608.04799'] If \(K\le n\) and
\(k_i+r_i-1\le\binom{k_i}{2}\) for every \(i\), then
\begin{equation}
  L_n(\mathbf k,\mathbf r)=\frac{(n)_K}{\prod_{i=1}^q k_i!}\prod_{i=1}^q c(k_i,k_i+r_i-1)\,p_n^{K+R-q}(1-p_n)^{A},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  A
  &=K(n-K)
    +\sum_{1\le i<j\le q}k_ik_j
    +\sum_{i=1}^q
      \left\{\binom{k_i}{2}-(k_i+r_i-1)\right\}
  \\
  &=Kn-\frac{K^2}{2}-\frac{3K}{2}-R+q.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
If the feasibility conditions fail, then \(L_n(\mathbf k,\mathbf r)=0\).","status":"unverified"}

