{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:40:22.388763+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7df9ded94b43d3695d7caf7f1bd281e056e5083db506ba757ad3cb31d4a88348","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem-subspace of arXiv:2506.18827, 'Random walk reflected off of infinity, with applications to uniform spanning forests and s'] Fix a finite, non-empty set $\mathcal A\subset\mathcal V\mathcal G$. 
Let
\begin{equation} 
  \mathring\Theta_{\mathcal A}  := \mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft\{ \theta \in \Theta : \text{$\theta = \nabla f$ for some $f : \mathcal V\mathcal G \to \mathbbm R$ with $f|_{\mathcal A} \equiv 0$} \aftergroup\egroup\originalright\} . 
\end{equation} 
Then $\mathring\Theta_{\mathcal A}$ is a closed linear subspace of $\Theta$. The orthogonal complement $\mathring\Theta_{\mathcal A}^\perp$ is described as follows. We say that $\eta \in \Theta$ is an \textbf{cycle modulo $\mathcal A$} if there exists a path $P : [0,N]\cap\mathbbm Z \to\mathcal V\mathcal G$ such that either $P(0) = P(N)$ or $P(0) , P(N) \in \mathcal A$ with the following property. For each $e = (x,y) \in \overrightarrow{\mathcal E}\mathcal G$, $\eta(e)$ is equal to the number of times $i$ such that $(P(i-1) ,P(i)) = (x,y)$ minus the number of times $i$ such that $(P(i-1), P(i)) = (y,x)$. Then $\mathring\Theta_{\mathcal A}^\perp$ is the closure of the linear span of the set of cycles modulo $\mathcal A$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.401988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7df9fa04b7b9c5272b63cd9e055f9b5632f042199689cbee8902e654a505e26c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: BlockDiagonalDecomposition of arXiv:2603.26932, 'On the satisfaction frequency of spectral characterization conditions'] Consider a matrix $\bM \in \sO^{n\times n}$ that is symmetric, $\bM = \bM^{\T}$. 
    Then, for every $k\leq n$, it holds that $\rank_q(\bM) = n-k$ if and only if there exists invertible $\bG \in \operatorname{GL}_n(\sO)$ such that 
