{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.664488+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7e705e36e530f4706e672aeae9379760a7c496864898c318ed9fb5c37f65bfcc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem complete-flooding of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] Let the complete graph $K_m$ have i.i.d.~edge weights with law $\mathrm{Exp}(1)^{1/\theta}$. For distinct vertices $u,v$, let $T(u,v)$ be their first-passage time, and let
\begin{align*}
 Y^{(m)} := \max_{w\in V(K_m)}T(u,w)
\end{align*}
be the flooding time from $u$. For every fixed $p\geqslant1$, there is $C_p<\infty$ such that, for all $m\geqslant2$,
\begin{align}
 \sup_{u\ne v} \left\VertT(u,v)\right\Vert_p \leqslant C_p\frac{\log m}{m^{1/\theta}},
 \text{ and }
 \norm{Y^{(m)}}_p \leqslant C_p\frac{(\log m)^{\gth}}{m^{1/\theta}},
 \text{ where } \gth=\max\{{1,\theta^{-1}}\}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:23:05.286492+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7e75002e4b001e7d412fb6ccce314c203884f32b609ef6442165e58fa88ec1a0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2608.21607, '\bf \mbox{Subzero matrix completion for sparse data analysis:} large-scale learning of lat'] Let $\{\vec{\alpha}_i\}_{i=1}^m$ and $\{\vec{\beta}_j\}_{j=1}^n$ be sets of vectors in $\mathbb{R}^d$. We define the thresholded similarity matrix for these vectors, with threshold $\tau\!\in\!(-1,1)$, as the $m\times n$ nonnegative matrix~$\mat{S}$ with elements
