{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.481939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7e8b96e1cfb420c8c9849280cf6c62ec7bb7ff037ece8a8bdcd6b373fee528da","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition lr of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Let $X,Y$ be $R^{2}$-valued random vectors with densities $
f_{X},f_{Y}$ with respect to a common dominating measure. We say that $X$ is
smaller than $Y$ in \textit{likelihood ratio order}, and write $X\leq _{
\mathrm{lr}}Y$, if the density ratio $\frac{f_{Y}}{f_{X}}$ is coordinatewise
non-decreasing; that is, 
\begin{equation}
\frac{f_{Y}(x_{1},x_{2})}{f_{X}(x_{1},x_{2})}\quad \text{is non-decreasing
