{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.834414+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f1ee4ebe4d20056c8f12c50bbafc9e991cb59b548c14e8bebe455147338b873","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm eq:diff-SL0 of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] Let  ${\kappa}_1,\ldots, k_{d_2-1} > -1$ and ${\kappa}_{d_2} = -1$. For $(\bm x,\bm y) \in {\mathbb S}^{d_1-1} \!\rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}$, 
let ${\mathfrak D}_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb S} \rtimes \Sigma}$ be the differential operator defined by 
\begin{align}
  {\mathfrak D}_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb S} \rtimes \Sigma}:= &  \sum_{i=1}^{d_2} \left( y_i \partial_{y_i}^2 + (\kappa_i +1) \partial_{y_i} \right) 
      - 2 {\langle} \bm y, \nabla_{\bm y} {\rangle} \partial_{y_{d_2}}  + 2 |{\bm y}|  \partial_{y_{d_2}}^2   \\
       & +(d_1-2) \partial_{y_{d_2}} - (|{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}| - d_1+d_2+1) \partial_{y_d} + \frac{1}{|\bm y|} \Delta_0^{(\bm x)}, \notag
\end{align}
where $\Delta_0^{(\bm x)}$ is the Laplace-Beltrami operator on the unit sphere in $\bm x$ variable. 
Then 
\begin{align}
  {\mathfrak D}_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb S} \rtimes \Sigma} u = - n u, \qquad
       \forall u \in {\mathcal V}_n\big({\mathbb S}^{d_1-1} \!\rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}, {\mathsf W}_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb S} \rtimes \Sigma} \big).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.455455+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f2130b3cbbc6ead075f33efe28d7cc7331a615c1aaf75e1e9ee9dd1e74fe903","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 5 of arXiv:2609.02523, '\bf Strong Ill-Posedness in critical and subcritical regimes for the Hunter--Saxton equati'] For every $s\in (1, \nicefrac{3}{2})$, there exists $u_0\in C^\infty \cap L^\infty(\mathbb R) \cap  \dot H^1(\mathbb R)\cap  \dot H^{s}(\mathbb R)$ such that the corresponding unique global dissipative solution $u$ of \eqref{HS} satisfies 
	\begin{equation*}
		 \underset{{t\in (0,T)}}{ \textnormal{ess sup}} \norm {u(t,\cdot)}_{\dot H^{ s}(\mathbb R)}  = \infty , \quad \text{for all } T>0.
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.485200+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f2957c63cbf081ea7e33bcd24e55b0d00ea816653c442baf7b96959bb05045c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 66 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] For every $w\in\C$, $x>0$ and $y\in\R$ such that $\vert y\vert <x$,
the following holds:
\begin{equation}

\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}
\dfrac{1}{n!}
\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{n}\dfrac{n! (n-1)!}{(n-k)! k! (k-1)! }
\,\vert y\vert^{n-k}\,\vert w\vert^{k}
\Big)
\dfrac{1}{x^{n}}
<+\infty,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{eqnarray}

\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty} (-1)^{n-1}
\dfrac{1}{n!}
\dfrac{w^{n}}{(x-y)^{n}}
&=&
\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty} (-1)^{n-1}
\dfrac{1}{n!}
\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{n}(-1)^{n-k}\dfrac{n! (n-1)!}{(n-k)! k! (k-1)! }
\,y^{n-k}\,w^{n}
\Big)
\dfrac{1}{x^{n}}
\\ 
\nonumber
&=&
\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty} (-1)^{n-1}
\dfrac{1}{n!}
\Lambda_{n}(w,y)
\dfrac{1}{x^{n}}.
\end{eqnarray}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.848679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f299faa227a22c0d1822dc1330385eeafe9aa6237b62b38daf62a38e52dc50f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:LaplaceMethod of arXiv:2410.23915, 'Asymptotic Bounds and Online Algorithms for Average-Case Matrix Discrepancy'] Let $\varphi_n : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a sequence of twice continuously differentiable and concave functions with a unique maximum at $y \in (a,b)$. Assume that $\varphi_n''$ is equicontinuous at $y$ and $\varphi_n''(y) \leq c$ for some $c < 0$ independent of $n$. Then
        \begin{equation}

            \int_a^b \exp{n\varphi_n(x)} dx \approx \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{n\left\lvert\ifblank{\varphi_n''(y)}{\:\cdot\:}{\varphi_n''(y)}\right\rvert}} \exp{n\varphi_n(y)}.
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:25.271915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f2d12ae35f7ad5d971e8c080953b4151a567a31e0c282fca6eb75b2d3199bf2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (Duflo (1997), Corollary 2.1.10) of arXiv:2606.21111, 'On Elephant Random Walk with Delayed Amnesia'] Let $\{M_n,\mathcal{F}_n\}_{n\ge 0}$ be a real, square-integrable vector martingale whose predictable quadratic variation is $\{\langle M\rangle_n\}_{n\ge 0}$. Also, let the following assumptions hold for a deterministic real sequence $\{a_n\}_{n\ge 0}$ which increases to $\infty$:\\
\noindent {(i)} $a_n^{-1}\langle M\rangle_n\xrightarrow{p}\Lambda$, where $\Lambda$ is a deterministic symmetric positive semi-definite matrix, and\\
\noindent {(ii)} Lindeberg's condition is satisfied, that is, for all $\varepsilon>0$,
\begin{equation*}
a_n^{-1}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\mathbb{E}(\parallel M_k-M_{k-1}\parallel^2 \mathbb{I}_{\{\parallel M_k-M_{k-1}\parallel \ge \varepsilon \sqrt{a_n}\}}|\mathcal{F}_{k-1})\xrightarrow{p} 0.
