{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.741056+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f589047422f6c6034af78aaea4fd623f904d27ce4fbc113d737a5b5d3ff8dd0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 110 (Spectral first Picard bounds) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] For every $\eps>0$,
\begin{equation}
  \sup_{\Lambda\ge1}\sup_{t\le T}
  q_{a,\nu,\Lambda}(t,\xi)
  \lesssim_{T,\eps}
  \la\xi\ra^{-4+2\nu+2\betasum+\eps},
  \qquad \nu=0,1.
\end{equation}
For every $0<\theta<\frac12-\betasum$,
\begin{equation}
  \sup_{\Lambda\ge1}
  q^{\Delta}_{a,\nu,\Lambda}(t,t';\xi)
  \lesssim_{T,\eps,\theta}
  |t-t'|^{2\theta}
  \la\xi\ra^{-4+2\nu+2\betasum+2\theta+\eps}.
\end{equation}
For every finite choice of input and output cutoff flags, the difference from
the limiting kernel satisfies the same static and increment estimates with
the weighted phase integral restricted to the union of the corresponding
high-frequency tail sets.  This reduction, and comparison of two non-nested
cutoffs through the same limiting kernel, follow from
\cref{lem:finite-product-cutoff}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.273453+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f59e64a2834e843024a6983690b4c1949127e7d9c701aebd7ca64fe90415ddc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_characterization of arXiv:2411.16452, 'Interface scaling limit for the critical planar Ising model perturbed by a magnetic field'] There exists a unique law ${\mathbb{P}}_{h}^{(\Omega,a,b)}$ such that the following holds.
    The Radon-Nikodym derivative of ${\mathbb{P}}_{h}^{(\Omega,a,b)}$ with respect to ${\mathbb{P}}_{\operatorname{SLE}_3}^{(\Omega,a,b)}$ is given by
    \begin{equation} 
        \frac{{\operatorname{d}} {\mathbb{P}}_{h}^{(\Omega,a,b)}}{{\operatorname{d}} {\mathbb{P}}_{\operatorname{SLE}_3}^{(\Omega,a,b)}}(\gamma)= \frac{1}{\mathcal{Z}_h(\Omega)}\bigg(\sum_{k \geq 0}\frac{h^k}{k!}\int_{\Omega_L^k} f_{L}^{(+,k)}(z_1, \dots, z_k) \prod_{j=1}^k dz_j\bigg)\bigg(\sum_{k \geq 0}\frac{h^k}{k!}\int_{\Omega_{R}^k} f_{R}^{(-,k)}(z_1,\dots,z_k)\prod_{j=1}^{k}dz_j\bigg)
