  Such $\mathfrak{M}$ is said to be \emph{based on} $\mathfrak{F}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.190034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fdd51c10e2946e93760e5ade69fc90916183ce1da70d91f9b3b51a11c37eb3e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: stochastic boundary v2 of arXiv:2506.21466, 'The Markov property for $\varphi^4_3$ on the cylinder'] For every $i\in\{1,2,3\}$, $\alpha \in (\max(0,\frac{i-2}{2}),i/2]$, every $\delta > 0$ sufficiently small, and for every $p\in[1,\infty)$ there exists $C_p>0$ such that  and 
\begin{equation} 
\sup_T \mathbb E\Big[ \Big( \sup_{\tau \in [-1,1]}|\tau|^{\alpha}\| \llbracket (\overline H  W^0_T) ^i \rrbracket (\tau,\cdot)\|_{\mathcal C^{-i/2+\alpha-\delta}_z} \Big)^p \Big] \leq C_p. 
\end{equation}
Furthermore,  there exists a random variable $\llbracket (\overline H W^0_\infty)^i\rrbracket \in L^\infty(|\tau|^{\alpha},\mathcal C^{-i/2+\alpha-\delta}_z)$ -- where this weighted space is defined with the norm appearing inside the expectation (equation eq:stoch-weighted-linfty of the paper) --  such that for every sequence $(T_n) : T_n\rightarrow \infty$, we have
\begin{equation}
\llbracket (\overline H W^0_{T_n})^i\rrbracket\rightarrow \llbracket (\overline H W^0_\infty)^i\rrbracket \, \text{ in }L^\infty(|\tau|^{\alpha},\mathcal C^{-i/2+\alpha-\delta}_z).
\end{equation}
The estimates also hold with $\overline H$ replaced by $H$, and the single harmonic extension replaced by the double harmonic extension. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.283092+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fdf128c84ffc95718b0025f05e2511043b08f322c2c6948cc0d76e640064011","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2606.22656, 'Fluctuations of Point Vortex Ensembles\\ at Small Negative Inverse Temperature'] For every $0<\beta<\beta_0$,
