{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.375751+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8195fc79d6d6fd1eaf81443735e478403c5c6844be9cfefbf763d83c7ba48ead","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2412.01995, 'Exciting games and Monge-Amp\`{e}re equations'] A convex function $u_1: [0,1) \times \Delta \to \mathbb R$ is said to be a viscosity sub-solution to (equation eq:gHJB of the paper) if 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] For any $x_0 \in \Delta$, $\limsup_{(t,x) \to (1,x_0)} u_1(t,x) \leq 0$. 
    \item[(ii)] For any convex test function $\phi \in C^2([0,1) \times \Delta; \mathbb R)$, and any local maximum $(t_0,x_0) $ of $u_1 - \phi$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        -\partial_t \phi(t_0,x_0) \leq H(t_0, u_1(t_0,x_0), \det \nabla^2 \phi(t_0,x_0)).
    \end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
A convex function $u_2: [0,1) \times \Delta \to \mathbb R$ is said to be a viscosity super-solution to (equation eq:gHJB of the paper) if 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] For any $x_0 \in \bar{\Delta}$, $\liminf_{(t,x) \to (1,x_0)} u_2(t,x) \geq 0$. 
    \item[(ii)] For any $(t_0,x_0) \in [0,1) \times \partial \Delta$, $\liminf_{(t,x) \to (t_0,x_0)} u_2(t,x)=+\infty$
    \item[(iii)] For any convex test function $\phi \in C^2([0,1) \times \Delta; \mathbb R)$, and any local minimum $(t_0,x_0)$ of $u_2 - \phi$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        -\partial_t \phi(t_0,x_0) \geq H(t_0, u_2(t_0,x_0), \det \nabla^2 \phi(t_0,x_0)).
    \end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
A continuous function $u: [0,1) \times \Delta \to \mathbb R$ is said to be a viscosity solution to (equation eq:gHJB of the paper) if it is a sub-solution and super-solution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.690818+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_81969777039378d6dd5969660cf7f8b3402c8fba051543aa9bff13a532278505","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 6 of arXiv:2608.30967, 'B-Frames, B-Riesz basis, and Their tensor products'] Let $\{x_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}} \subset \mathcal{B}$ and let $\{e_j\}_{j \in \mathcal{K}}$ be an orthonormal basis  of $\mathcal{H}$.  The following statements are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\{x_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$ is a b-Riesz basis for $\mathcal{Z}$.
  \item There is a b-orthonormal basis $\{E_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$ for  $\mathcal{Z}$ and a bounded bijective operator $ U: \mathcal{Z} \rightarrow \mathcal{Z}$ such that
      \begin{eqnarray}
      U\mathfrak{b}(h,E_k)=\mathfrak{b}(h,x_k), \forall h \in \mathcal{H} \mbox{ and } \forall k \in \mathcal{K}
      \end{eqnarray}
  \item $\{b(e_j,x_k)\}_{j,k \in \mathcal{K}}$ is a Riesz basis for $\mathcal{Z}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.883810+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_81971729e4cf01b4f2d248a0e39ce4143e2d42a64c3c1c138f323cd71e206c93","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 20 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] Let us define the axioms and deduction rules of $\LK_{\rmbeta}$ as follows: We assume the typical rules for first-order logic, and add the following rules for ordinals:
    \begin{center}
        \vspace{1em}
        \begin{mathprooftree}
            \def\fCenter{\vdash}
            \AxiomC{}
            \RightLabel{$(<_\ord)$ ($\ttX \vDash x<y$)}
            \UnaryInfC{$[\ttX]\fCenter \tilde{x}<_\ord\tilde{y}$}
        \end{mathprooftree} 
        \hspace{3em}
        \begin{mathprooftree}
            \def\fCenter{\vdash}
            \AxiomC{}
            \RightLabel{$(\le_\ord)$ ($\ttX \vDash x\le y$)}
            \UnaryInfC{$[\ttX]\fCenter \tilde{x}\le_\ord\tilde{y}$}
        \end{mathprooftree}

        \vspace{1em}
        \begin{mathprooftree}
            \def\fCenter{\vdash}
            \AxiomC{$\cdots\ [\ttX']\fCenter \Gamma, A(\tilde{n}) \quad (n\notin \ttX)\ \cdots $}
            \AxiomC{$\cdots\ [\ttX]\fCenter \Gamma, A(\tilde{n}) \quad (n\in \ttX)\ \cdots$}
            \RightLabel{$(\forall^\ord)$}
            \BinaryInfC{$[\ttX]\fCenter \Gamma,\forall^\ord x A(x)$}
        \end{mathprooftree}

        \vspace{1em}
        \begin{mathprooftree}
            \def\fCenter{\vdash}
            \AxiomC{$[\ttX]\fCenter \Gamma,A(\tilde{n})$}
            \RightLabel{$(\exists^\ord)$}
            \UnaryInfC{$[\ttX]\fCenter \Gamma,\exists^\ord x A(x)$}
        \end{mathprooftree}
    \end{center}
    In $\forall^\ord$, the first form of the hypotheses is enumerated for every possible $\ttX'$ for an extension of $\ttX$ of the form $\ttX\cup \{n\}$, where $n$ is the least number not in $\field \ttX$. (The order of $n$ in $\ttX'$ can be arbitrary).

    We form a $\rmbeta$-preproof from a given set of rules. We say a $\rmbeta$-preproof $\pi$ is a \emph{$\rmbeta$-proof} if for every infinite branch $B$ of $\pi$, the union of all template orders occurring in $B$ is ill-founded.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:51:11.954997+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_819838bf85004c6ffcea7ff5aa8a1c322135a0ce125fa60f0270446f3a82c5ae","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 26 (Small deterministic insertions) of arXiv:2608.07820, 'Linear Spectral Statistics for Entrywise-Transformed Spiked Wigner Matrices under Shifted '] Suppose \(B_N\) is deterministic,
