{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.649968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_819f6809a3aa1f8db021af7f3243255306fe91de47ff150a82caa035f9924173","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Affine Temperley--Lieb algebra of arXiv:2506.01306, 'Asymptotic of Coulomb gas integral, Temperley-Lieb type algebras and pure partition functi'] Fix $n \geq 2$ and $q \in \mathbb{C}^\times$, and set $\delta := -q - q^{-1}$. The affine Temperley--Lieb algebra $\mathrm{aTL}_n := \mathrm{aTL}_n(\delta)$ is the unital associative algebra generated by $\{\Omega_n, \Omega_n^{-1}, \mathrm{id}, e_0, e_1, \dots, e_{n-1}\}$, subject to the relations (with indices modulo $n$):
\[
\begin{aligned}
& e_i^2 = \delta e_i, \quad e_i e_{i \pm 1} e_i = e_i, \quad e_i e_j = e_j e_i \quad \text{if } |i - j| \geq 2, \\
& \Omega_n e_i = e_{i-1} \Omega_n, \quad (\Omega_n^{\pm 1} e_0)^{n-1} = \Omega_n^{\pm n} (\Omega_n^{\pm 1} e_0), \quad \Omega_n \Omega_n^{-1} = \Omega_n^{-1} \Omega_n = \mathrm{id}.
\end{aligned}
\]
These relations imply that $e_i = \Omega_n^{1 - i} e_1 \Omega_n^{i - 1}$ for all $i$, so the algebra is generated by $\{e_1, \Omega_n, \Omega_n^{-1}\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.083967+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_819f8398faf5c1ad9fe2dd2d6dcfeb286043ebd3126fa3a854b89de88a156c32","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:cantelli_nonMarkov of arXiv:2411.12522, 'Canonical insurance models: stochastic equations and comparison theorems'] Let two canonical insurance models $(\alpha, \Lambda, \Phi, B, b)$ and $(\bar \alpha, \bar \Lambda, \Phi, \bar B, \bar b)$  be given. Let   $\Lambda$ and $\bar \Lambda$ have  identical reset points. Suppose that the state-wise prospective reserves $(V^i)$ of the first model satisfy
	\begin{align}
	\begin{split}
			&\mathds{1}_{\{  \bar \Lambda^{i\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\scalebox{1.4}{$\cdot$}}}(t-)<\infty \}} I^i(t-)  \bigg(  B^i(\mathrm{d} t)+ \sum_{j : j\neq i}(b^{ij}(t) + V^j(t)-V^i(t)) \Lambda^{ij}(\mathrm{d} t)\bigg)\\
			& =\mathds{1}_{\{  \bar \Lambda^{i\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\scalebox{1.4}{$\cdot$}}}(t-)<\infty \}} I^i(t-)  \bigg(  \bar B^i(\mathrm{d} t)+ \sum_{j : j\neq i}(\bar b^{ij}(t) + V^j(t)-V^i(t)) \bar \Lambda^{ij}(\mathrm{d} t)\bigg).
			\end{split}
	\end{align}
	Then the two models have  identical state-wise prospective reserves:
	 $$(V^i)_{i \in \mathcal{Z}}=(\bar V^i)_{i \in \mathcal{Z}}.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.843939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_81a5f93567c2b490ae455828ca4edaa3465e279879a581a4abc2e627c628f641","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2609.03840, 'Haglund--Haiman--Loehr formula via Carlsson--Mellit algebra'] \cite[Lemma~4.2,~Example~4.3]{CGM20} 
  We have
  \begin{align} 
