{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:30:08.197215+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_821b0f45c787ebc16979d53172057b78a70ec228764e5764120be3ddd802718b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:conditionalSurvival of arXiv:2608.13452, 'Wasserstein stability of the zero cell of a Poisson hyperplane tessellation under directio'] The conditional survival function of \(X\) given \(Y=y\) is given by 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}\bigl[X \geq x \mid Y = y\bigr] 
        = g(x,y) :=
        \begin{cases}
            \frac{\partial_y F_u(x,y)}{F_u(0,1)} e^{- \gamma(F_u(x,y) - y F_u(0,1))}
            & \text{if $y'\mapsto F_u(x,y')$ is differentiable at $y$},
            \\0 & \text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    In particular, this means that for any $x\geq 0$ and any Borel set $B\subset [0,\infty)$, we have
         \begin{align} 
              \mathbb{P}[X \geq x, Y \in B]
              & = \int_{B} g(x,y) \, \mathbb{P}_Y(\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} y).
         \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.800408+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_821bc2546977a77dba686d7cc55a45db02f338a943b009c7a4f9b39881bf9ecf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:entropy-collapse of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] Under the two-mode approximation, as $k\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
H(k) = x_k\Bigl(1+\log\tfrac{1}{a_3}+2L_\star k\Bigr) + O\bigl(x_k^2\log(1/x_k)\bigr),
\end{equation}
and consequently
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_k = x_k\Bigl(1+\tfrac{1+\log(1/a_3)}{2L_\star k}\Bigr) + O\Bigl(\tfrac{x_k^2}{k}\log\tfrac1{x_k}\Bigr),
\end{equation}
so that $\mathcal{H}_k/x_k\to1$, and
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_k = G_k\,(1+O(1/k)).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.356285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_821efab579ac5daeea97f0c678774647cdc448c0b055752c024210ed6e5f3dc3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:limit-points-drift of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Any limit point $Q\in{\mathcal P}({\mathcal P}({\mathcal C}))$ of the sequence $\big\{\mathcal{L}(\hat{\mu}_n^A): n\in{\mathbb N}\big\}$ assigns probability 1 to the set
	\begin{equation*}
