{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.675471+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82a5cfe570af3249508f1f0309eedf4e81a4e521984342945710f067f6e6bd79","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2608.30073, 'A remark on the fourth order Q curvature on manifolds with dimension at least 5'] Let $\left( M^{n},g\right) $ be a smooth compact Riemannian
manifold with dimension $n\geq 5$. If $R>0$, $Q\geq 0$ and not identically
zero, then $\exists \widetilde{g}\in \left[ g\right] $ such that $\widetilde{
R}>0$ and $\widetilde{Q}>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.964150+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82a7edfc0f0f0dcd8fa69f70824574481c4be9405fa5e621cb1cd306fb297b2a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:uld_regularity of arXiv:2506.23062, 'Shifted Composition IV\@: \\ Toward Ballistic Acceleration for Log-Concave Sampling'] Adopt Assumption~(as:regularity of the paper) and let $\boldsymbol{P}_T$ denote the Markov kernel corresponding to running~(eq:ULD of the paper) for time $T > 0$.
    Then, for all $q\geqslant 1$ and all $x,\bar x,p,\bar p\in\mathbb{R}^d$,
    \begin{align}
        \mathsf{R}_q(\delta_{x,p} \boldsymbol{P}_T \mathbin{\|} \delta_{\bar x,\bar p}\boldsymbol{P}_T)
        &\leqslant qC(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,T)\,\Bigl\{\norm{x-\bar x}^2 + \frac{1}{\gamma_0^2}\,\norm{p-\bar p}^2\Bigr\}\,,
    \end{align}
    where
    \begin{align*}
        C(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,T)
        \lesssim \frac{1}{\gamma} \,\Bigl(\frac{\omega}{\exp(c\omega T)-1}\Bigr)^3 + \gamma\,\frac{\omega}{\exp(c\omega T)-1}\,, \qquad
        \gamma_0
        &\gtrsim \gamma + \frac{\one_{T\leqslant 1/\abs{\omega}}}{T}\,,
    \end{align*}
    where $c > 0$ is a universal constant ($c=1/48$ suffices) and
    \begin{align*}
        \omega
        \coloneqq
        \begin{cases}
            \alpha/(3\gamma) & \text{ if } \gamma \geqslant \sqrt{32\beta} \text{ \textbf{ (high friction setting)}}
            \\
            -\sqrt\beta/3 & \text{ if }\gamma < \sqrt{32\beta} \text{ \textbf{ (low friction setting)}}
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.618254+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82a8b268bdd4990137b0cdbc72b7b20220bf8931ea79ac2d1bb20fb132823c36","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 38 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] Given $k\ge \ell\ge 1$, $\sigma\in(0,1/10)$ and $f\in C_\cc(\R^{\ell})$ let
\begin{equation}

\Xi_k(f):=E\Biggl(f\Bigl(\{X_j+j\slb\}_{j=1}^{\ell}\Bigr)\biggl(\,\prod_{j=1}^{k} \wt P(M_{j}'\le t)|_{t:=X_j+j\slb}\biggr)\,1_{\{X_k\in[k^{\sigma},k^{1-\sigma}]\}}X_k\Biggr),
\end{equation}
where~$X$ is the random walk with step distribution $\NN(0,1/2)$. Let~$\delta\in(0, 1/2)$. Then $\epsilon_{n,k}(u,f)$ defined for each~$u>0$ and $n\ge k$ by
\begin{equation}

E_{Q_{n,u,u}}\Bigl(f\bigl(\{h(x_0)-h(x_j)\}_{j=1}^\ell\bigr)\Bigr)=\frac{4u}n\Xi_k(f)+\frac un\epsilon_{n,k}(u,f)
\end{equation}
obeys
\begin{equation}

\lim_{k\to\infty}\limsup_{n\to\infty}\sup_{n^{\delta}\le u\le n^{1-\delta}}\bigl|\epsilon_{n,k}(u,f)\bigr|=0.
\end{equation}
Moreover, writing $1$ for the constant function equal to~$1$, we have
\begin{equation}

0<\inf_{k\ge1}\Xi_k(1)\le\sup_{k\ge1}\Xi_k(1)<\infty.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:23:58.625670+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82ae5533879d55d76e26b6ecd395d050dd8fb9e132ee8725f50fc0a12924b86e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 197 (Remarkable structure in the $\ingoingcharacteristicsurfacetwoarg{\ubar_2}{[u_1,u_2) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] }$ boundary terms in the elliptic-hyperbolic integral identities]  Let $\SigmatTan = \tander^N \vortrenormalized$ or $\tander^N \GradEnt$. Then, for any $u_1 \le u_2$, the following identity holds:
