{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.560935+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_831ed51ab72bdec25a06ff14c38928236e63bdb66571192df3feb1e247e67302","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: applications in information theory of arXiv:2411.17109, 'On the maximal correlation of some stochastic processes'] Let $X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_n$ be independent real-valued continuous random variables. Let $S\subset T$ be two nested non-empty random subsets of $[n]=\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$, which are independent of $X=(X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_n)$. Let $R$ be the maximal correlation coefficient between $(S,X_S)$ and $(T,X_T)$ given by (equation eq: R Y Z of the paper), where $X_{S}=(X_i)_{i\in S}$ and $X_{T}=(X_j)_{j\in T}$. Denote by $I(Z)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{(f'(z))^2}{f(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z$ the Fisher information of the continuous random variable $Z$ with the density $f(z)$. Then we have that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{t\subset[n]}P(T=t)I(\sum_{j\in t}X_j)\mu_t^2\leq R^2\sum_{s\subset[n]}P(S=s)I(\sum_{i\in s}X_i)\lambda_s^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mu_t=\sum_{s\subset[n]}P(S=s|T=t)\lambda_s$ for $t\subset[n]$ and $(\lambda_s)_{s\subset[n]}$ is an arbitrary real vector. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.553016+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8321896471927ec299c8479b2d1278dcaeed607179b2720678b61ec97383c497","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 10 of arXiv:2608.27143, 'An approximate zero bias transformation for random sums: Applications to sampling with out'] Let $n_1, n_2, n_3\in \mathbb{N}$ be such that $n_1< n_2< n_3$. Let $X_1,X_2,\ldots$ be mean zero random variables with finite variances.
