{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:33:11.957573+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8383d86ea59e38a3908639317040181f082843f3ca94b911e2383a5f7f0d88ec","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:pc-opt of arXiv:2607.14087, 'Stochastic Domination of Gaussian Maxima: A Resolution of the Weak Simplex Conjecture'] \label[corollary]{cor:finite-energy}
In the preceding model, the optimal correct-decoding probability is
\begin{equation}
  P_{\mathrm c}^*(E,M)
  =p_M\!\left(\sqrt{\frac{2EM}{(M-1)N_0}}\right),
  \qquad M\geq2.
\end{equation}
Hence, for $0\leq\epsilon<1$,
\begin{equation}
  M^*(E,\epsilon)
  =\max\!\left(
    \{1\}\cup
    \left\{M\geq2:
      p_M\!\left(\sqrt{\frac{2EM}{(M-1)N_0}}\right)
      \geq1-\epsilon
    \right\}
  \right).
\end{equation}
If $0<\epsilon<1-1/M$, there is a unique $a_{M,\epsilon}>0$ satisfying
$p_M(a_{M,\epsilon})=1-\epsilon$, and the minimum energy for $M$ messages at error \(\epsilon\) is
\begin{equation}
  E^{*}(M,\epsilon)
  =\frac{N_0}{2}\frac{M-1}{M}a_{M,\epsilon}^2.
\end{equation}
For $\epsilon\geq1-1/M$, $E^{*}(M,\epsilon)$ is zero, whereas for
$\epsilon=0$ it is infinite. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.719725+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8384f62f4d79aab200e06fbef46ac5bf721935f4529f1c2302aac8c8336ac690","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2605.13727, 'Stochastic evolution equations\\ driven by arbitrary cylindrical Lévy processes'] A predictable process $\Psi \colon \Omega \times [0,T] \rightarrow L_2(U,H)$ is $L$-integrable if there exists a sequence $(\Psi_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of processes in $\mathcal{S}_{\rm prd}^{\rm HS}$ such that
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
		\item[{\rm (1)}]  $(\Psi_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ converges $P_T$-a.e. to $\Psi$;
		\item[{\rm (2)}] $\displaystyle \lim_{m,n \rightarrow \infty}d_T^{em}(I(\Psi_m),I(\Psi_n))=0.$
	\end{enumerate}
	In this case,  the stochastic integral process $(I(\Psi)(t):\, t\in [0,T])$ of $\Psi$ is defined as the limit 
	of $(I(\Psi_n))_{n\in\N}$ in $\dem_T$. The class of all $L$-integrable $L_2(U,H)$-valued predictable processes will be denoted by $\mathcal{I}_{{\rm prd},L}^{\rm HS}:=\mathcal{I}_{{\rm prd},L}^{\rm HS}(U,H)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:26:47.165207+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8387fcf48ff28984b5bd4f732abb28ca4c5e79a7e1a4e5e5a35f037713adf038","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 39 of arXiv:2608.29416, 'Algorithmic threshold for high-dimensional projection pursuit I: general theory'] For any constants $C,\delta > 0$, there exists a positive constant $c(C,\delta)$ such that the following holds: let $\bA$ be any $N\times N$ diagonal matrix with $\|\bA\|_{\op} \le C$, and suppose $\bg,\acute{\bg}$ are independent standard gaussian vectors in $\R^N$. Then,
    \[
        \frac{|(\bA \bg, \bg) - \Tr(\bA)|}{N}\,,
        \frac{|(\bA \bg, \acute{\bg})|}{N}
        \le \delta
    \]
    with probability at least $1-e^{- c(C,\delta) N}$.

\begin{proof}
We rewrite the random variables of interest as
    \begin{align*}
    (\bA \bg, \bg) - \Tr(\bA)
    &=
    \sum_{i=1}^N a_{i,i}
    \Big((g_i)^2-1\Big)\,,\\
    (\bA\bg,\bg')
    &=
    \sum_{i=1}^N a_{i,i}
        g_i\acute{g}_i\,.
    \end{align*}
The claim then follows by standard Chernoff bounds.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.348187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_838936d0120b3deee6ec72783c6ddad82e82a7e20a8b739979ae70efa6978b6d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2506.15159, 'Conditional central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] Let $N=n(n-1)/2$. 
Then, conditioning on $E_n/N=\widetilde p_n$, we have,
\ben{
d_{\mathcal{W}}\left(\frac{V_n -\mu_{ V_n}}{\sigma_{ V_n}},Z\right)\leq C_\varepsilon n^{-\frac{1}{2}+\varepsilon}
}
for any $\varepsilon>0$,
where $C_\varepsilon$ is a constant depending only on $\varepsilon$ and $B$, $Z\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable,
\begin{align}
    \mu_{V_n}&=N(n-2)\widetilde p_n^2+\frac{2N\t q_n^2\sum_{l=2}^m\beta_{ln}s_l\t p_n^{e_l}}{1-2\t q_n\sum_{l=2}^m\beta_{ln} s_l \t p_n^{e_l-1}},\quad \t q_n=1-\t p_n,  \\ \sigma_{ V_n}^2&=\frac{Nn\t p_n^2\t q_n^2}{\left(1-2\t q_n\sum_{l=2}^m\beta_{ln}s_l\t  p_n^{e_l-1}\right)^2}, 
