{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:32.000546+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_86469b1fd36d069ce66b24533457a5557038a09b74203f3cbf2514feacd8c202","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 10 (Exact law of the total mass) of arXiv:2512.02766, 'Fine-Graining and Continuous Space Scaling Limit of the $H^{2|2}$ Model on the Hierarchica'] The total mass $\mathfrak{m}_{\mathbb{X}}([0,1])$ of the limiting measure of the hierarchical $H^{2|2}$-model is distributed as an inverse Gaussian random variable $\text{IG}(1,\frac{\overline{W}}{2(\rho-1)})$. Especially, $\mathbb{E}[\mathfrak{m}_{\mathbb{X}}([0,1])]=1$.

