{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.340436+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_86c6a23f8630c9a76d2166f46aac3835525ae4b4fb102c8844614ed154550ccd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2512.07202, 'Hitting Probabilities for Hypoelliptic Differential Equations Driven by Fractional Brownia'] Suppose that the vector fields $\mathcal{V}=\{V_i\}_{i=1}^d$ satisfy Hypotheses \ref{h1} and \ref{1.2}.  Let $Y$ be the solution to the RDE (\ref{density1}) on $[0,T]$ and let $p(s,t,x,y)$ denote the joint density of $(Y_s,Y_t)$. For any $\delta>0$, there exist constants $C,\varepsilon>0$ depending on $H,\mathcal{V},T,\delta$, such that
