{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.315324+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_873dd996ac532dea7e485d194e8f90f9aa0d9ea0a78a3dc5ef8e62d6b0e5b324","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem sparse-giant-nearby of arXiv:2606.01627, 'The geometry of the giant component of random geometric graphs'] Fix $\beta,\varepsilon >0, \lambda_0 > \lambda_c$ and $x \in M$.      There are constants $c = c(\lambda_0,d,\varepsilon,\beta,M) > 0$ and $n_0 = n_0(\lambda_0,d,\varepsilon,\beta,M), L' = L'(\lambda_0,d,\varepsilon)$ so that if $r \leq n^{-\beta}$ and $\lambda \geq \lambda_0$ then the following holds. 
