{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.815981+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8885cd4d06324516da1d4b97d24e3a8dcba51ad7e4956bb0055dfe4201afe7df","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:bound_gen_size_ind of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Fix some $R\geq 1$. For any $\alpha,\beta>0$, take $\underline{a}_0=\Bar{a}_0=\alpha$ and $\underline{b}_0=\Bar{b}_0=\beta$, and define $\underline{a}_i,\Bar{a}_i,\underline{b}_i,\Bar{b}_i$ inductively for $i\geq 1$ as in (equation eq:inductive_def_seq_a of the paper) and (equation eq:inductive_def_seq_b of the paper), with $m=m_n$ and $m'=m_n'$ as defined in (equation eq:def_mn_mn' of the paper). Then
    \begin{align*}
        \liminf_{n \to \infty}\CProb{\underline{a}_t\leq \xi_{l(R+t)}\leq \Bar{a}_t,\underline{b}_t\leq \Xi_{R+t}\leq \Bar{b}_t\textrm{ for all }t\geq 1}{\xi_{lR}=\alpha,\Xi_R=\beta}\geq 1-O\left(\alpha^{1-2\gamma}\right),\\ \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation} 
    \end{align*}
    where the constant hidden in the $O(\cdot)$ term above is universal, i.e., independent of $R, \alpha,\beta$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.299131+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_888798156a6adfb9b3f423aeb3350e82d20f52d03a48592c3b4a30d929f41dc6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi admissible measure of arXiv:2604.21663, 'Large deviations for non-irreducible Markov chains on Euclidean spaces'] A measure $\mu \in \mathcal P(\mathbb R^d)$ is said to be \emph{admissible} if 
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $\mu$ is absolutely continuous with respect to $\mathrm {Leb} $;
		\item $\supp\mu\subseteq \overline C$;
		\item for all $j\in \mathcal J$ such that $\mu(C_j)>0$, we have $\beta\leadsto j$;
		\item the relation $\leadsto$ is a total order on $\{j\in \mathcal J\ |\ \mu(C_j)>0\}$;
	\end{enumerate}
	The set of admissible measures is denoted by $\mathcal A\subseteq \mathcal P(\mathbb R^d)$.","status":"unverified"}

