{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.581718+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a1a0080debb2a4ec48a777b17906c14969828c1744624baa93daaac409a5824","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition IM definition of arXiv:2605.16963, 'Stochastic Euler Equations with Pseudo-differential Noise: Continuous and Discontinuous Pe'] A Markov semigroup $\{\mathscr{P}_{t}\}_{t\ge0}$ is called \textbf{Feller} on $E$ if $\mathscr{P}_{t} (C_B(E)) \subset C_B(E)$ for all $t\ge0$. A probability measure $\mu\in\mathbf{P}(E)$ is called an \textbf{invariant probability measure} for $\{\mathscr{P}_{t}\}_{t\ge0}$ if  
		\begin{equation}
			\mathscr{P}^*_{t}\mu=\mu, \quad \text{for all } t > 0.
		\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.297435+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a1aa2fe8049b9fb0c7fcffc4c0995a7fbb93a34247a9294d53e3f13432cebe6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:scale_limits of arXiv:2605.22787, 'Invariant measures for half-space geometric LPP: classification and the one force--one sol'] For each $n\in \mathbb{N}$, consider the half-space geometric line ensemble $(L_i^n)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}$ from Definition (def:le of the paper). We have the following.
\begin{enumerate} [label=(\roman*), font=\normalfont]
\item  If $c\in [0,1]$, then as $n\to \infty$ we have the following weak convergence  
        \begin{align}
            \left(n^{-1/3}L_i^n(tn^{2/3})-\frac{2qn^{2/3}}{1-q}-\frac{qtn^{1/3}}{1-q}\right)_{i\in \mathbb{N}} \stackrel{d}{\to} \frac{\sqrt{q}}{1-q}\left(\mathcal{P}_i^{1-\lfloor c\rfloor}(t)\right)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}
        \end{align}
  under the topology of uniform convergence on compact sets. Here, $\left(\mathcal{P}_i^0(t)\right)_{i\in \mathbb{N}}$ and $\left(\mathcal{P}_i^1(t)\right)_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ are certain line ensembles (defined in [dy25,dsy26,ddy26]) that arises as universal scaling limits
  in the half-space KPZ universality class.

\item  If $c\in (1,q^{-1})$, then as $n\to \infty$ we have
  \begin{align}
      n^{-1}L_1^n(0) \stackrel{p}{\to} \frac{q(c^2-2qc+1)}{(c-q)(1-qc)}, \ \mbox{ and } \ n^{-1}L_{2}^n(\lfloor n^{2/3}\rfloor) \stackrel{p}{\to} \frac{2q}{1-q}.
  \end{align}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.607589+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a1f9979fe861a536824b2466f708ec86d0450f52a803851faec87debc485bdd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 8 of arXiv:2607.08242, 'Convergence of random sums in non-commutative probability'] Suppose $\alpha\neq0$, 

    $\tilde{N}_n$ converges weakly to $N$ with  

    probability measure $\mu_N$, 

    and $\{\tilde{N}_n^m\}$ is uniformly integrable for all $m\in\mathbb{N}$. 
    Then, as $n\rightarrow\infty$, 
    \begin{equation*}
        \tilde{\varphi}(\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{N_n}^m)=\varphi[(\delta_na+\sqrt{1-\delta_n^2}s)^m]+o(1), \ \text{for each}\ \ m\in \mathbb{N}.
    \end{equation*}
    where $a$ is a self-adjoint variable whose all moments agree with those of $\mu_N$, and $s$ is a standard semi-circle variable, independent of $a$. 
 Further, if $\mu_a$ exists, then for every $n$, a probability law for  $\delta_na\cdot1_\mathcal{A}+\sqrt{1-\delta_n^2}s$ also exists.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.436723+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a2355983a9d5cb80bc60bdc9bf28626ce03ae5cf45bb84891c1badf7830855f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th.Liggett of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] For any $d\geq 1$, any $k\geq 1$ and any $p\in (0,1)$, there exists 
$\bar p=\bar p(d,k, p) < 1$ such that the following hold. For any subset $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}$, if $\{X_i\}_{i\in \Lambda}$ is a $k$-dependent field of Bernoulli variables satisfying $\Bb\mathbb{P}{X_i=1}\geq \bar p$, for each $ i \in \Lambda$, then if $\{Y_i\}_{i\in \Lambda}$ is an i.i.d field of Bernoulli variables with parameter $p$, one has 
\begin{align*}
\{X_i\}_{i\in \Lambda}  \text{ Stoch. dominates } \{Y_i\}_{i\in \Lambda}\,.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.728386+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a26df137a5af89f8f1b22712a1bdb01267f42ae372d99cb56d258817ef36dca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:metastable-mixing-rate of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] Suppose $\beta = \frac{\alpha}{\theta}$ with $\alpha >1$ and $\theta > \theta_{0,L}(\alpha)$. Then  
    \begin{align*}
       \lim_{N \to \infty} \frac{1}{N} \log t_{\mathsf{mix}} = \Delta_{\alpha,\theta}\,, 
    \end{align*}
    where 
    \begin{align*}
        \Delta_{\alpha,\theta} := \lim_{\varepsilon\to 0} \lim_{N\to\infty} -\frac{1}{N} \log \pi_{\beta} (|m|\le \varepsilon) \, .
    \end{align*}
    This limit exists $\mathbb P_M$-almost surely, and its value is given explicitly by 
    \begin{align}
        \Delta_{\alpha,\theta} : = \begin{cases}
            \frac{\alpha}{2} - \frac{(\alpha - 1)^2}{4\theta^2} - \frac{1}{2} \log \alpha - \frac{1}{2} & 1 < \alpha \leq \theta \\ 
            \frac{\alpha}{2}(1-\frac{1}{\theta})^2  - \frac{1}{2}\log \theta - \frac{1}{4\theta^2} + \frac{1}{4} & \alpha >\theta
        \end{cases}\,.
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.772139+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a2817fd1cbd1b8f9094366664e23109054d9282a459278d4ecec40abba3c495","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:correlation_inductivestep of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] For all large enough $M$ in the above definitions, there exist constants $\lambda_0 > 1$
