{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.424245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a5bd963758f8bf945bd89d1010cd2b0b773e6ce757e803071a51cc1128b9cad","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem7.4littlewoodchapter2 of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] Fix $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and fix $\alpha,\gamma\in(0,1)$, $B>0,\mu\in (0,2^{-15})$. We can find $c,R>0$ depending only on these constants so that the following holds. For $0\leq k\leq c\alpha n$ and $\epsilon\geq\exp(-c\alpha n)$, fix $v\in \mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ and orthogonal vectors $w_1,
