{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:58:24.503677+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6055e98edfb7518652cb13ac274f0bd9b1fc402008fa7513fa259d70c1baf5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Schwthm4 of arXiv:2601.09537, 'Gene genealogies in haploid populations evolving according to sweepstakes reproduction'] Recall Definition~(def:notation of the paper), and $c_{N}$ from
Definition~(def:cNhapl of the paper).  Suppose a haploid population of size $N$
evolves according to Definition~(hschwpop of the paper) with law on the random
number of potential offspring as given in~(equation eq:4 of the paper).  Then
$\{\xi^{n,N}( \lfloor t/c_{N}\rfloor ); t \ge 0 \}$ convergences in
the sense of convergence of finite-dimensional distributions to
$\{\xi^{n}\} \equiv \set{\xi^{n}(t); t \ge 0}$ as given in each case:
\begin{enumerate} 
\item if  $ \alpha \ge 2$ then

 $\set{\xi^{n}}$ is the Kingman coalescent; 
\item  if  $1 \le \alpha < 2$ then  $\set{\xi^{n}}$ is the  Beta$(\gamma,2-\alpha,\alpha)$-coalescent as in Definition~(def:betacoal of the paper) with $\gamma=1$; 
\item if  $0 < \alpha < 1$ then 
$\set{\xi^{n}}$  is the Poisson-Dirichlet$(\alpha,0)$-coalescent as in
Definition~(def:poisson-dirichlet of the paper).
\end{enumerate}
By Theorem~4d, and Lemmas~13 and 16 in [schweinsberg03],
 as $N\to \infty$,
\begin{equation}

\frac{1}{c_N}  \overset{c}{\sim}  \begin{cases} 1 &  \text{when $0 < \alpha < 1$,} \\
 \log N &  \text{when $ \alpha = 1 $,} \\
 N^{\alpha - 1} &  \text{when $1 < \alpha < 2$,} \\
 N / \log N &  \text{when $\alpha = 2$,} \\
 N  &  \text{when $ \alpha > 2$} \\
\end{cases}

\end{equation}
Let $E = \{\text{given groups of $k_{1}, \ldots, k_{r} \ge 2$ blocks
merge}\}$.  By Lemma~6 in [schweinsberg03], where
$X_1, \ldots, X_N$ denotes the i.i.d.\ random number of potential
offspring produced by the current $N$ individuals according to the law
in~(equation eq:4 of the paper), with $S_N = X_1 + \cdots + X_N$ and
$k_{1},\ldots, k_{r} \ge 2$,
\begin{equation}

 \frac{1}{ c_{N}} \ensuremath{\mathds{P}\left( E \right)} = \frac{\EE{ {(\nu_{1})}_{k_{1}}\cdots (\nu_{r})_{k_{r}} }}{c_{N} N^{k_{1} + \cdots + k_{r} - r} }  \sim     \frac{N^{r}}{c_{N}} \EE{ \frac{ {(X_1)}_{k_{1}}\cdots {(X_{r})}_{k_{r}} }{S_N^{k_{1} + \cdots + k_{r}}}\ensuremath{\mathds{1}_{\left\{ S_N \ge N \right\}}}}
\end{equation}
Moreover,   [schweinsberg03]
\begin{displaymath}
c_N \sim  N\EE{ \frac{ {(X_1)}_2}{S_N^2}\ensuremath{\mathds{1}_{\left\{ S_N \ge N \right\}}}}
\end{displaymath} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.222334+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b61e8207f0430ac41e6f3351f752a5fd67143045d4f6c94286657f62065deb6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:super-initial of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] There exists $C(d) > 0$ such that 
