{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.766116+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6aa9c1364d37db7ec350c2fe7863a50011ab913c16f7844a0bb8da8a555be5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2512.04807, 'Existence and uniqueness of the canonical Brownian motion in non-simple conformal loop ens'] Let $F_1,F_2$ be two sets such that $F_1 \cap F_2$ is a non-empty finite set. For $i=1,2$, let $(\CE_i,\CF_i)$ be a resistance form on $F_i$, and let $(\CE,\CF)$ be the resistance form on $F_1 \cup F_2$ defined by
 \begin{align*}\begin{split}
  \CF &= \{ f : f|_{F_1} \in \CF_1,\, f|_{F_2} \in \CF_2 \} ,\\
  \CE(f,f) &= \CE_1(f|_{F_1},f|_{F_1})+\CE_2(f|_{F_2},f|_{F_2}) .
 \end{split}\end{align*}
 Then the following holds. Let $A \subseteq F_1 \cup F_2$ be a finite set containing $F_1 \cap F_2$, let $A_i = A \cap F_i$ for $i=1,2$, and let $w_A, w_{A,i}$ be the weight functions associated with $\CE|_A, \CE_i|_{A_i}$, respectively.
 Then
 \[
  w_A(x,y) =
  \begin{cases}
   w_{A,1}(x,y)+w_{A,2}(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_1 \cap F_2 ,\\
   w_{A,1}(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_1 ,\, \{x,y\} \nsubseteq F_1 \cap F_2 ,\\
   w_{A,2}(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_2 ,\, \{x,y\} \nsubseteq F_1 \cap F_2 ,\\
   0 ,& \text{otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
 \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:51:12.742023+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6b3b32bf5d1065a9d5020dea05928f323d1979fa9754252253191870ec3465","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 30 of arXiv:2605.02430, '\textbf{Scaling limit of the range of tree-valued branching random walks in random environ'] \textbf{(Brownian snakes)} 
A $q$-dimensional Brownian snake is a r.v. $(H, W)\! :\!  \Omega\!  \rightarrow \! \fSigma_q(\bbR_+)$ that is measurable with respect to $\mathscr F$ and to the Borel $\sigma$-field of $\fSigma_q(\bbR_+)$, such that for all $p\ino \bbN^*$, and all real numbers 
$0\leqo s_1\leko \ldots \leko s_p$, 
\begin{equation}

\big(W_0,W_{\! s_1}, \ldots , W_{\! s_p} \big) \; \overset{\textrm{(law)}}{=} \;  F_p \big( H ; (B^{(j)}) _{0\leq j\leq p}  ;  (s_j)_{1\leq j\leq p} \big)\; , 
\end{equation}
where $B^{(0)}\eqo W_0$, where $(B^{(j)})_{1\leq j\leq p}$ are independent standard $q$-dimensional Brownian motions that are moreover independent from $W_0$ and $H$. \cq","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:12:13.514784+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6cfd852b0ab0d53ed7996313fc1bc80750252be58b042b095c6177b0163b6f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 (Iteration inequality) of arXiv:2608.28528, 'Strong solutions of SDEs with critical discontinuities in diffusion coefficients'] Fix some $0 < \alpha < 1$ and 
$\varsigma$, $\lambda>1$ such that $$\varsigma<\frac{d}{d-2+2\alpha},$$ and $$\frac{1/(1-\alpha)}{\lambda}=\frac{d/(d-2+2\alpha)}{\varsigma}.$$
Then there exist $\theta>0$, $k>1$ and $m_0$ such that for all $m,n \geq m_0$,
for all $r \geq r_0>\frac{2}{2-\sqrt{\delta_a}}$, we have
\begin{multline}

\|u_m-u_n\|_{L^{\frac{r}{1-\alpha}}([s,s+\theta],L^{\frac{rd}{d-2+2\alpha}})}\\ \leq \left(C_0 \|\nabla u_m\|^2_{L^{2\lambda'}([s,s+\theta],L^{2\varsigma'})}\right)^{\frac{1}{r}}(r^{2k})^{\frac{1}{r}}\|u_m-u_n\|_{L^{(r-2)\lambda}([s,s+\theta],L^{(r-2)\varsigma})}^{1-\frac{2}{r}},
\end{multline}
for a constant $C_0$ that does not depend on $m$ or $s$. Here, as usual, $\frac{1}{\varsigma}+\frac{1}{\varsigma'}=1$, $\frac{1}{\lambda}+\frac{1}{\lambda'}=1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.778352+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6cff83f309b8faa5ea37517c736cd06d43b07f02d3476d8c91fc5cf177bae7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 (Uniform local and local-maximum bounds) of arXiv:2609.02257, 'Aperiodic random walks: uniform estimates, local limits and invariance principles'] There are constants \(C,c>0\), independent of \(N,L,z,R\), such that
for all sufficiently large \(N\), \(L,R\in\Z_{\ge0}\), and \(z\in\Z\),
\begin{align}
 \Pp(S_N(L)=z)
 &\le \frac{C}{\sqrt{L+1}}
 \exp\left\{-c\frac{z^2}{L+|z|+1}\right\},       \\
 \Pp\left(S_N(L)=z,
       \max_{0\le j\le L}|S_N(j)|\ge R\right)
 &\le \frac{C}{\sqrt{L+1}}
 \exp\left\{-c\frac{R^2}{L+R+1}\right\}.         
\end{align}
Moreover, for some sufficiently large \(N_0\), the uniform lattice
local central limit theorem holds:
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{L\to\infty}\sup_{N\ge N_0}\sup_{z\in\Z}
 \left|
 \sigma_N\sqrt L\,\Pp(S_N(L)=z)
 -\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}
  \exp\left\{-\frac{z^2}{2\sigma_N^2L}\right\}
 \right|=0.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.088510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6deed903eff2bc9ac96302e7d54bf6c12113910ca61642cd3d508c2ab07e55","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition sub-matrix-block-removal of arXiv:2412.08400, '\vspace{-1.0cm} Characterization of Exponential Families of \\ Lumpable Stochastic Matrice'] Let $F \in \mathcal{F}_\kappa^{+}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ and a non-degenerate surjective lumping function $\kappa \colon \mathcal{Y} \to \mathcal{X}$.
\begin{description}
    \item[Sub-matrix.] 
    For $\mathcal{T} \subset \mathcal{X}$, we write
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
    \mathcal{Y}|_{\mathcal{T}} \triangleq \bigcup_{x \in \mathcal{T}} \mathcal{S}_{x} , \qquad 
    \mathcal{E}|_{\mathcal{T}} \triangleq \mathcal{E} \cap \left( \bigcup_{x,x' \in \mathcal{T}} \mathcal{S}_{x} \times \mathcal{S}_{x'}\right),
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
the resulting sub-graph is  ($\mathcal{Y}|_{\mathcal{T}}, \mathcal{E}|_{\mathcal{T}}$) and the sub-matrix $F|_{\mathcal{T}} \in \mathcal{F}_\kappa^{+}(\mathcal{Y}|_{\mathcal{T}}, \mathcal{E}|_{\mathcal{T}})$ is defined such that for any $(y,y') \in \mathcal{E}|_{\mathcal{T}}$, \begin{equation*}
    F|_{\mathcal{T}}(y,y') = F(y,y').
\end{equation*}
    \item[Block removal.]
    The matrix $F$ from which the block $(x_0, x_0') \in \kappa_2(\mathcal{E})$ has been removed is defined as 
$F^{\setminus (x_0, x_0')}\in \mathcal{F}_\kappa^{+}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E} \setminus (\mathcal{S}_{x_0} \times \mathcal{S}_{x'_0}))$ where for any $(y,y') \in \mathcal{E} \setminus (\mathcal{S}_{x_0} \times \mathcal{S}_{x'_0})$,
\begin{equation*}
        F^{\setminus (x_0, x_0')}(y,y') = F(y,y').
    \end{equation*}
\end{description}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:38:58.717259+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6f76fda7bb768edc5ee49431248a61c0bbbf15f84c1af2bcdd4ab69439d645","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:nuptcctty of arXiv:2511.06321, 'Torus scaling limits and the plateau of the critical weakly coupled $|\varphi|^4$ model in'] Let $\alpha \in [1/2,1]$,  $g$ be sufficiently small,  $\vec{\textbf{a}} \in \operatorname{HB}_{\epsilon_p}$ and $C_{\cD}$ be sufficiently large (independently of $L$).  Then there exists $ \vec\nu_{ptc}  = (\nu^{(\km_1)}_{ptc} )_{\km_1 \in  \kA_0 \cup \kA_1 } $ such that the following hold: with initial condition \begin{align}
	\begin{cases}
		\nu^{(\km_1)}_0  = \nu_{ptc}^{(\km_1)} & (\km_1 \in \kA_0 \cup \kA_1 ) \\
		\nu^{(\km_1)}_0 = g^{(\km_2)}_0  = 0 & (\km_1 \in \kA_2 \cup \kA_3   ,  \; \km_2 \in \kB_1) \\
		g_0^{(\emptyset)} = g  , & {}
	\end{cases}

\end{align}
the bulk perturbative RG flow of infinite length exists,  and $V_j  \in \cD_{j,\varnothing} (\alpha)$ for each $j$.  Moreover,  $\vec{\nu}_{ptc}$ is a continuous function of $\vec{\textbf{a}}$ and differentiable function of $g$ satisfying $\vec{\nu}_{ptc} = O(g)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.908615+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b706847f91903ae86c6ff8f296abd1a65ad873c3a089cfaf31cb79e99a3f203","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma prop:iteration of arXiv:2411.06861, 'Quenched invariance principle for random walks in random environments admitting a cycle de'] Let $f:[0, \infty)\times [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty]$ be a non-negative function. Suppose it holds, that for all $l>k \geq 0$ and $ \sigma>\sigma' \geq 0$
         \begin{align*}
             f(l, \sigma')\leq \frac{C_{\mathrm{DG}}}{(\sigma - \sigma')^\alpha(l-k)^\beta} f(k, \sigma)^\gamma, 
         \end{align*}
         for some constants $C_{\mathrm{DG}}, \alpha, \beta >0$ and $\gamma >1$. Then, there exists a constant $K\equiv K(C, f, \sigma', \alpha, \beta, \gamma)$ such that 
         \begin{align*}
             f(l, \sigma')=0, \quad \forall l\geq K. 
         \end{align*}
         In particular, we may choose
         \begin{align}

             K = f(0, \sigma)^{\frac{\gamma-1}{\beta}}C_{\mathrm{DG}}^{\frac{1}{\beta}}2^{\frac{(\alpha+\beta)}{\gamma-1}+1}(\sigma-\sigma')^{-\frac{\alpha}{\beta}}, 
         \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.723730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b71070be65f3e5a361c4e1caf3e0fac962c6600fae2736c1967f707b50a3e77","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 73 (Bounds for the resonant operators) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] Fix $0<T_0\le1$, a label $(a;b,c)\in\mathfrak L$, exponents
\begin{equation}
 0<s<1-\beta_{b,c},
 \qquad
 \beta_{b,c}<\sigma<1-\beta_{b,c},
\end{equation}
and an adapted localization pair.  Construct all kernels on the full time
triangle $\Delta_{T_0}$.  For every admissible spectral cutoff,
\begin{equation}
 T^{a;b,c}_{\Lambda}=
 \begin{cases}
  \mathcal D^{a;b}_{\Lambda}+\mathcal B^{a;b,b}_{\Lambda},
    &(a;b,c)=(a;b,b)\in\mathfrak L_{\mathrm{diag}},\\
  \mathcal B^{a;b,c}_{\Lambda},
    &(a;b,c)\in\mathfrak L_{\mathrm{off}}.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
For every $0<T\le T_0$, the centered operators
$\mathcal B^{a;b,c}_{\Lambda}$ converge, for every $2\le p<\infty$, in
\begin{equation}
  L^p\!\left(\Omega;
  \mathcal L\!\left(
  E_T^{2,\sigma},
  C_TH^{s-1}\cap L_T^1B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}
  \right)\right).
\end{equation}
If $(a;b,c)=(a;b,b)\in\mathfrak L_{\mathrm{diag}}$, then the deterministic
operators $\mathcal D^{a;b}_{\Lambda}$ also converge in
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal L\!\left(
  E_T^{2,\sigma},
  C_TH^{s-1}\cap L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}
  \right).
\end{equation}
The limits are independent of the admissible cutoff profile.  They are causal,
their shorter-time restrictions are intrinsic in the sense of
Lemma~\ref{lem:time-extension-causality}, and
\eqref{eq:operator-decomposition} is preserved by restriction.  On one
event of probability one,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{I\subset[0,T_0]}
 \|T_I^{a;b,c}\|_{E_{I,0}^{2,\sigma}\to C_IH^{s-1}}<\infty.
\end{equation}
For a diagonal label one has, on the same event,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{I\subset[0,T_0]}
 \|\mathcal D_I^{a;b}\|_{E_{I,0}^{2,\sigma}\to
 L^\infty(I;B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1})}<\infty.
\end{equation}
For a fixed-profile dyadic family, the full sequence converges almost surely
in the same operator topologies.  Moreover, almost surely,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{\substack{I\subset[0,T_0]\text{ interval}\\ |I|\le h}}
 \|\mathcal B_I^{a;b,c}\|_{\mathcal L(
 E_{I,0}^{2,\sigma},L^1(I;B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}))}
 \longrightarrow0
 \qquad(h\downarrow0),
\end{equation}
and, in particular,
\begin{equation}
  \|\mathcal B^{a;b,c}_{[0,T]}\|_{\mathcal L(
  E_T^{2,\sigma},L_T^1B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1})}
  \longrightarrow0
  \qquad(T\downarrow0).
\end{equation}
Along a fixed-profile dyadic family, the same probability-one event gives
both limits uniformly over all dyadic cutoff levels and their pairwise
differences.  Since the block kernels are realized in
$C(\Delta_{T_0};\mathcal K)$ before Volterraization, this event works
simultaneously for every $0<T\le T_0$.  The estimates are uniform on separated speed--mass classes, on compact subsets of the admissible $(\betaW,\betaK)$ region, and for bounded $\mathfrak H_{\mathrm{prof}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:26:02.423470+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b726597c9a71f1e25fb64d2bb0262c399cc76f23fd477b4676e747214810d4c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop maypoletwist of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a $C^0$-foliation on $M$ with a $\mathbb{T}^3$-type planar exceptional minimal set $\Lambda$. For any $\varepsilon > 0$, and after an arbitrarily $C^0$-small isotopy of $\mathcal{F}$ whose size is independent on $\varepsilon$, there exists a maypole green $\mathfrak{G} = (N, \gamma, P)$ for $\Lambda$ of size $\leq \varepsilon$ (with a smooth framing of $P$) such that for every $\delta > 0$, there exist two $C^0$-foliations $\mathcal{F}^\pm_\delta$ on $M \setminus \interior P$ such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathcal{F}^\pm_\delta$ coincide with $\mathcal{F}$ away from $N$ and near $\partial M$,
    \item $T\mathcal{F}^\pm_\delta$ is $\varepsilon$-close to $T\mathcal{F}$ everywhere, and $\delta$-close to it near $\partial P$,
    \item $\mathcal{F}^\pm_\delta$ has \emph{positive} (resp.~\emph{negative}) slope along $\partial P$ in the chosen smooth framing.
\end{itemize}
In particular, for $\varepsilon$ small enough, the minimal sets of $\mathcal{F}^\pm_\delta$ disjoint from $P$ correspond to minimal sets of $\mathcal{F}$, and $\mathcal{F}^\pm_\delta$ coincide with $\mathcal{F}$ near those.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.652660+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b73eff7f043e8ca79ad008082abfe437c2f6ac72353f7135548194eca729813","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2607.24533, 'Colored Markov-Modulated Brownian Motion for Preemptive Workload Stacks'] {Suppose that Assumptions~\ref{assump:intensity} and~\ref{assump:stability} hold, and fix a depleting color $\ell$.} Then $\vect U_\ell$ solves
\eqref{eq:layer-qme}, has a simple zero eigenvalue with right
eigenvector $\e$, and has all remaining eigenvalues in the open left
half-plane.
Let $\vect V_\ell$ denote the complementary solution of
\begin{equation}

\tfrac12(\vect\Delta_\sigma^{(\ell)})^2\vect V_\ell^2
+\vect\Delta_\mu^{(\ell)}\vect V_\ell
+\widetilde{\vect T}^{(\ell)}
=\vect 0,
\end{equation}
whose eigenvalues lie in the open right half-plane. Standard MMBM
Wiener--Hopf theory gives this complementary solution and the
nonsingularity of $\vect V_\ell-\vect U_\ell$
\citep{Asmussen1995,AhnMeini2020}. Define
\begin{equation}

