Consequently, $T_{\mathsf{x}_0} \geq T_{\mathsf{x}_\mathrm{rbw}}+O(1)$, and thus $\mathsf{x}_\mathrm{rbw}$ is the asymptotically fastest initial condition. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.363693+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8be661b00707ef539640c8fbb64b8ce8bf46221f1843ac92406d483e93ddf469","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:path-coupling of arXiv:2601.07045, 'From Continuous to Discrete: \\ a No-U-Turn Sampler for Permutations'] Let $G=(\mathcal{X},E)$ be a finite, connected, undirected graph whose edges
$\{u,v\}\in E$ are assigned lengths $l(u,v)\ge 1$, and let $d$ denote the
resulting shortest-path metric on $\mathcal{X}$. Let $K$ be a Markov kernel on
$\mathcal{X}$ with stationary distribution $\pi$. Suppose that for every edge
$\{u,v\}\in E$ there exists a coupling $(U,V)$ of $K(u,\cdot)$ and $K(v,\cdot)$
such that
\begin{equation}
\EE_{u,v}\!\left[d(U,V)\right]
\;\le\;
d(u,v)\,e^{-\alpha},
\qquad \text{for some }\alpha>0.
\end{equation}
Then, for all $t\ge 0$,
\[
\max_{x\in\mathcal{X}}
\bigl\|K^t(x,\cdot)-\pi\bigr\|_{\TV}
\;\le\;
e^{-\alpha t}\,\diam(\mathcal{X},d),
\]
where $\diam(\mathcal{X},d):=\max_{x,y\in\mathcal{X}} d(x,y)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

