{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.719679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8c3cecc953428cfd9410118f811229af323c665e934be5615d5921bdff169596","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm matrixperp of arXiv:2605.31054, 'On the empirical spectral distribution of matrix perpetuities'] Let $({\mathbf A}_n,{\mathbf B}_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be iid copies of $({\mathbf A},{\mathbf B})$. If 
\begin{align}
{\mathbb E}\left[ \log^+\left(\lambda_{\max}({\mathbf A}{\mathbf A}^\top)\right)\right]<+\infty\quad\mbox{and}\quad {\mathbb E}\left[ \log^+\left(\lambda_{\max}({\mathbf B}{\mathbf B}^\top)\right)\right]<+\infty 
\intertext{and}
\gamma=\inf_{n\geq 1} \frac{1}{n}{\mathbb E}\left[ \log( \lambda_{\max}\left({\mathbf A}_1{\mathbf A}_2\ldots{\mathbf A}_n {\mathbf A}_n^\top\ldots{\mathbf A}_{2}^\top{\mathbf A}_1^\top\right))\right] <0, 
\end{align}
then there exists a unique solution to (equation eq:matperp of the paper). 

Moreover, this solution has a series representation
\begin{align}
{\mathbf X}\stackrel{d}{=} \sum_{n=1}^\infty {\mathbf A}_1\ldots {\mathbf A}_{n-1}{\mathbf B}_n{\mathbf A}_{n-1}^\top\ldots{\mathbf A}_1^\top,
\end{align}
where the series on the right-hand side above converges almost surely, with $({\mathbf A}_n,{\mathbf B}_n)_{n\ge1}$  iid copies of $({\mathbf A},{\mathbf B})$. 

Finally, letting
\[
{\mathbf X}_n={\mathbf A}_n {\mathbf X}_{n-1}{\mathbf A}_n^\top+{\mathbf B}_n,\qquad n\ge1,
\]
where ${\mathbf X}_0$ is independent of $({\mathbf A}_n,{\mathbf B}_n)_{n\ge1}$, we have 
\[
{\mathbf X}_n \stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow} {\mathbf X}.
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.140859+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8c46a9bc88a4bec23eda44753a2f3d2a7353c1628cd74e6f04cf8c9e80eb3315","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary C:non d main of arXiv:2507.01236, 'Disintegration theorem for multifunctions, \\ with applications to empirical Wasserstein d'] Let $\mathfrak{X} = [0, 1]^D$ be the unit cube in dimension~$D$, endowed with the Euclidean metric $d$ and its Borel $\sigma$-field.
Suppose that~$\mu$ is an absolutely continuous probability measure on~$\mathfrak{X}$ with density $f$ satisfying
\begin{equation}

c \leq f(x) \leq C\mbox{\rm \ for every $x \in \mathfrak{X}$},
\end{equation} 
where $0 < c \leq C < \infty$. Given $\alpha \in (0, \infty)$, let
\begin{equation}

r(n)\equiv r(n,D, c, \alpha) := c^{-1/D} D^{1/2} (4 \cdot 3^{D - 1})^{1/D} (2 + \alpha)^{1/(2D)} \left( \frac{\ln n}{n} \right)^{1/(2 D)}.
\end{equation}
Then there exists $n_0 \equiv n_0(D, c, C, \alpha)$ such that, for every $n \geq n_0$, the space $(\mathfrak{X},d,\mu)$ is $(n^{-\alpha},r(n))$-disintegrable. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.554744+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8c48fabf9ce70840fe110ab5f6e0de450b15547de68941f89ee2232f9b09be3d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:not_a_tree_asymp_prob_pairs_1 of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] For all $x,y\in[n]$ with $x\neq y$ and uniformly over $u\in [0,1]$, we have
