		for all Borel measurable $f \colon H^{-\varepsilon} \times M \to [0,\infty]$, we have that
		\begin{equation}
			\E \int f(h, x) d\mu^{\gamma h}(x) = \E \int f\tparen{h + \gamma k(x,\emparg), x} \, d\vol(x) \fstop
		\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.472644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cab22fd38ab91d9ddeb28fbd94515bbd1e0f7d16d7ffdaef8d13b7f417585da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:polytope-learning-examples of arXiv:2606.19763, 'Optimal Sparsification of Gaussian Processes'] Let $r\geq1$, let $0<\eps<1/2$, and let $f:\R^n\to\zo$ be an unknown Boolean function. 
	There is an algorithm which, given access to independent labeled examples
	$(\bx,f(\bx))$ with $\bx\sim N(0,I_n)$, runs in time
	\[
		n^{
			O\pbra{
				\log(\alpha(r,\eps/2))/\eps^4
			}
		}
		=
		n^{\wt{O}(r^4/\eps^6)}
	\]
	and, with probability $9/10$, outputs a hypothesis $h:\R^n\to\zo$ satisfying 
	\[
		\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{G}}(f,h)
		\leq
		\mathrm{OPT}_r(f)+\eps\,.
	\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.311942+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8caecefc91ca1d1f4b810168a83f339dbb4db49b1e623abf4b6527125306b324","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2608.05931, 'Effective Lagrangian regularity and the uniqueness threshold for random H\"older velocity '] Let $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and $b\in\{0,1\}$. Then there exists a mutually independent family of smooth, centered, autonomous, Gaussian fields $\{u^j\}_{j\geq 0}$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $U^{\alpha,b}$ is equal to $\sum_{j= 0}^\infty u^j$ in law,
    \item for each $j\geq 1$, $u^j$ has a finite spatial range of dependence $2^{-j}$,
    \item and there exists $C(d,\alpha)>0$ such that for all $j\geq 0$, $p\geq 1$, and $n\in\{0,1,2\}$,
    \[\|\nabla^n u^j\|_{L^p_\omega C^0_x}\leq  C (p^{1/2}+j^{1/2})2^{(n-\alpha)j}.\]
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if $b=1$ and $d\geq 2$, then
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] $\nabla\cdot U^{\alpha,b}=0$,
    \item[(ii)] and almost surely, letting $Z_\eps$ be defined as in~\eqref{eq:zero sets}, for all $0 \leq \alpha'<\alpha$,
    \[\lim_{\eps\rightarrow 0} \eps^{-\alpha'd}|Z_\eps|=0.\]
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.233418+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cb09a754471c622fe0b586312d96cc6699bd3745bec71b7fa490def44a7bbf3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2609.00567, 'Hodge--Tate splitting and Akizuki--Nakano vanishing in positive characteristic'] For every stack \(\mcalX\), write
  \begin{equation*}
    \iota_{\mcalX}\colon\mcalD_{\qcoh}(\mcalX)\hookrightarrow\mcalD(\mcalX_{\fpqc},\mcalO_{\mcalX})
  \end{equation*}
  for the canonical fully faithful functor. Let \(f\colon\mcalY\to\mcalX\) be a morphism of stacks. Under the description of quasi-coherent complexes as compatible systems on affine objects in \cite{lurie2018Spectral}*{\S 6.2.2}, the site-theoretic derived pullback preserves quasi-coherent complexes. We denote its restriction by
  \begin{equation*}
    Lf^*\colon\mcalD_{\qcoh}(\mcalX)\to\mcalD_{\qcoh}(\mcalY).
  \end{equation*}
  Thus there is a canonical equivalence
  \begin{equation}

    Lf_{\mathrm{sh}}^*\iota_{\mcalX}\xrightarrow{\sim}\iota_{\mcalY}Lf^*.
  \end{equation}
  Both sides of \eqref{SiteQcohPullbackCompatibility} are obtained by restriction along the functor from affine schemes over \(\mcalY\) to affine schemes over \(\mcalX\), followed by derived extension of scalars. Hence this functor is canonically the pullback functor constructed in \cite{lurie2018Spectral}*{\S 6.2.2}.

  Since \(\mcalD_{\qcoh}(\mcalX)\) and \(\mcalD_{\qcoh}(\mcalY)\) are presentable stable \(\infty\)-categories and \(Lf^*\) preserves small colimits, \(Lf^*\) admits a right adjoint. We denote this right adjoint by
  \begin{equation*}
    Rf_*\colon\mcalD_{\qcoh}(\mcalY)\to\mcalD_{\qcoh}(\mcalX)
  \end{equation*}
  and call it the \emph{derived pushforward}. Unless the superscript \(\mathrm{sh}\) is explicitly displayed, \(Rf_*\) always denotes this quasi-coherent right adjoint.

  If \(\mcalX\) is an fpqc algebraic stack, we write
  \begin{equation*}
    \mcalD^+_{\qcoh}(\mcalX)\defeq\bigcup_{c\in\setZ}\mcalD^{\geq c}_{\qcoh}(\mcalX)
  \end{equation*}
  for the full subcategory of cohomologically bounded-below quasi-coherent complexes, where \(\mcalD^{\geq c}\) means that \(H^i=0\) for every integer \(i<c\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.971037+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cb0b1fe2a565c9075ab6a79cc43c252e49d52a40eb29924a0380ef5661879f3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:defQmu of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] Fix $\mu\in(0,1]$. For $t\in\R$, we define
    \begin{equs}
        Q_\mu(t)\eqdef \mu^{-1}e^{-t/\mu}  \1_{\geqslant0}(t)
        \,,
    \end{equs}
the fundamental solution to the differential operator $\mathcal{P}_\mu\eqdef \big(1+\mu\partial_{t}\big) $ on $\R$, with null initial condition at $t=-\infty$. 
We sometimes write $Q_\mu$ for the operator given by convolution with the kernel $Q_\mu(t)$ on $C^\infty_c(\R)$, with the convention that $Q_0=\mathrm{Id}$ the operator with kernel $\delta_\R(t)$.\\
We now introduce the kernels we use to test scale-dependent quantities. For $N\in\N$, we let
\begin{equs}
    K_{N,\mu}\eqdef Q_\mu^{*N}
\end{equs}
stand for the fundamental solution for $\mathcal{P}_{N,\mu}\eqdef\mathcal{P}^N_\mu$. We will also use the short-hand notations 
\begin{equ}
K_\mu\eqdef K_{6,\mu}\,,\;
\text{  and  }\;\mathcal{R}_\mu\eqdef\mathcal{P}_{6,\mu}\,.
\end{equ}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.146793+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cb38287457aeaa891e87d81b5e07874c4920cc5a34823149b060b858d83294b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.16103, 'Non-commutative Stein's Method: Applications to Free Probability and Sums of Non-commutati'] Consider additional ordered symbols $\bar{1},\dots, \bar{n}$ and interlace them with the elements of $[n]$ in the following alternating way:
$$1\bar{1}2\bar{2}\cdots n\bar{n}.$$	
Let $\pi$ be an element of $NC(n)$. Then its Kreweras complement $K[\pi]$ is the maximal element $\sigma\in NC(\{\bar{1},\dots, \bar{n}\})\cong NC(n)$ satisfying 
\begin{align*}
\pi\cup\sigma\in NC(\{1,\bar{1},2,\bar{2},\cdots, n,\bar{n}\}).
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.465198+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cba508afff371ed2f9f2fc1cab0be24dffd2df8386d7814eb5ebe0a34644f9f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 (Stopped mean variation bound) of arXiv:2608.17540, 'Pathwise stability for one-dimensional SDEs driven by Brownian motion and a symmetric stab'] For fixed $\delta\ge2$, $\eps\in(0,1)$ and $n\ge1$, define
\[
  Q_t^{(n)}:=u_{\delta,\eps}(Y_{t\wedge\tau_n}),
  \qquad 0\le t\le T.
\]
Then $Q^{(n)}$ is a nonnegative quasi-martingale, and
\begin{align}
V_T(Q^{(n)})+\E Q_T^{(n)}
\le C\Bigg[&|Y_0|^{\alpha-1}
+\eps^{\alpha-1}
+\frac{\delta}{\log\delta}\frac{B}{\eps^{2-\alpha}}
+\frac{\delta^2}{\log\delta}\frac{A^2}{\eps^{3-\alpha}} \notag\\
&+\frac{\delta}{\log\delta}\frac{S^\alpha}{\eps}
+\frac{\delta^2}{\log\delta}\eps^{\alpha-1}
+\frac{\eps^{\alpha\wt\eta-1}}{\log\delta}
\Bigg], 
\end{align}
where $C$ is independent of $n,\delta,\eps$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.348873+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cbf56085846998475ff2ca050840da34ce18c7c92eb7276b729ef2c53966442","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:zeros:LZ1 of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Let $F:\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ be a measurable function $C \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$. We say $(F, C)$ solves the \emph{Lacker-Zhang fixed point problem} if
    \begin{align}
        &F(x) = C - \log \int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d} \exp\Big( - U(y) - W(x - y)- (\kappa - 1) F(y)\Big) dy, \quad \text{\normalfont a.e. }x \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d,  \\
        &\int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d} \exp \big(- U(x) - \kappa F(x) \big) dx < \infty. 
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.952771+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cc044708ab768586156bed90244ef39d71b416e3fe3a9507d2a8835a50bfa16","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 of arXiv:2604.05234, 'Quantitative propagation of chaos and universality for asymmetric Langevin spin glass dyna'] {\cite[Theorem 4]{StochasticGronwallScheutzow2013}}
    Let $X$ be an adapted, non-negative process with continuous paths and let $h_t\geq0$ be adapted with continuous paths. Assume that $(a_t)_{t\geq 0}$ is a non-negative progressively measurable process and let $M$ be a continuous local martingale with $M_0=0$. If
    \begin{align*}
        X_t\leq \int_0^t a_s X_s\,ds+h_t+M_t
    \end{align*}
    holds for all $t\geq 0$, then
    \begin{align*}
        \bbE\Big[\sup_{s\leq t}\sqrt{X_s}\Big]\leq C\bbE\bigg[\exp\Big(\frac 52 \int_0^t a_s\,ds\Big)\bigg]^{1/5}\bbE\Big[\sup_{s\leq t}|h_s|^{5/8}\Big]^{4/5}
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.206914+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cc7d2c2d2c70d252858fc4b55165ffff6ae048aa2ba0ddbb5f78faf98e9bfc9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi Def:Product-Wt-Complexes of arXiv:2608.29013, 'Weighted Homology and Cohomology of Weighted Polyhedra'] Let $(K,\mu)$ and $(K',\mu')$ be weighted simplicial complexes of the same type. 
  The \emph{Cartesian product} of $(K,\mu)$ and $(K',\mu')$ with respect to some total orderings of the vertices of $K$ and $K'$
   is a weighted simplicial complex $(K\times K',\mu\times \mu')$, where for a simplex
  $\{(v_{i_1},v'_{j_1}),\cdots, (v_{i_s},v'_{j_s})\}$ of
  $K\times K'$,
  \begin{equation} 
   \mu\times \mu'\big( \{(v_{i_1},v'_{j_1}),\cdots, (v_{i_s},v'_{j_s})\} \big):= \mu(\{v_{i_1},\cdots, v_{i_s}\})\cdot \mu'(\{v'_{j_1},\cdots, v'_{j_s}\}).  
   \end{equation}
   It is easy to see that
  $(K\times K',\mu\times \mu')$ has the same type as $(K,\mu)$ and $(K',\mu')$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:48:02.412396+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ccbc2eb3e273629e57875350ba1167fe242fa973fdd0a1b95864e5af37adc99","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mainthm c of arXiv:2608.26564, 'Real ideal points, Conway spheres, and left-orderable Dehn fillings'] Let $K$ be an $(n, m)$ torus-torus, twist-torus, or twist-twist knot in either the base or the flipped-pair class, and let $M$ be its exterior.
\begin{enumerate}
	\item Theorem (mainthm:a of the paper) applies to exactly $nm$, $m$, and $1$ admissible real limiting data respectively, producing that number of distinct real ideal points of $X(M)$ detecting the Conway sphere, each with a two-sided real analytic branch of irreducible $SL_2(\mathbb R)$-characters.
	\item Along every such branch, $\operatorname{tr}\rho_u(\mu) > 2$ while $|\operatorname{tr}\rho_u(\lambda)| \to \infty$; in particular the meridian-normalized lifts define a pair of unbounded augmented holonomy arcs.
	\item Theorem (mainthm:b of the paper) applies to exactly $\min(n, m)$, $1$, and $1$ of these branches respectively; the corresponding arcs lie in $H_{0,0}(M)$ and are unbounded with vertical asymptotes on opposite sides. Consequently, there exist $R^- < R^+$ such that $\pi_1(M(r))$ is left-orderable for every rational $r \notin [R^-, R^+]$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

