{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.581735+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d09bc0441ee9b0e129fa7660259e21c9d30c4e7ecc213076bd291a4716678c4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Quest of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] In Proposition (prop:conv_typical_to_mu of the paper) we have shown that $\mu_\infty$ is determined by particles that satisfy $Y_u(t)\approx e^{-t/2}$. It would be interesting look at the empirical measure of particles that satisfy $Y_u(t)\approx e^{-t/2+\lambda t}$. This should be non--trivial for any $\lambda$ with $\vert \lambda \vert < \sqrt{2\beta}$. In particular, if you look at $\lambda=1/2$ there should {be} a phase transition when $\beta=1/8$ because this is the threshold for local survival. For BBM on $\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$, the  number of particles {at a given speed} is counted by the additive martingale; note that here in the hyperbolic setting
    \begin{equation*}
        \sum_{u\in \mathcal{N}(t)} (Y_u(t))^\lambda e^{-t(\lambda^2+\lambda-2)/2}
    \end{equation*}
    has the same distribution as the regular additive martingale. (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:44.949216+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d0c39a60f723dde98ddc32c76e4cc766f8b0269572d37c2bd9b9258f7f64669","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 (Error estimates) of arXiv:2606.24887, '2606.24887'] Let $\delta \in (0,1)$,  $\Gamma$ be as in \cref{eq:theta} and $\mathfrak c$ as in \cref{lem:roughboundsGT}.  
