{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.993644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e856da18336988dd6ab685cdea1558c8669f4ffc262b416f63e674d835d7b2f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop SG of arXiv:2608.25362, 'Fluctuations of the free energy of the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model with ferromagnetic i'] Let $\beta\geqslant0$ and $0\leqslant\gamma<1$. Then $\ln Z_N(\beta,\gamma) \geqslant \ln Z_N^{SK}(\beta,0)$ almost surely, and for every $t\geqslant0$ and every $N$,
\begin{align*}
    \pr\Big( \ln Z_N(\beta,\gamma) - \ln Z_N^{SK}(\beta,0) \geqslant t \Big) \leqslant \frac{e^{-t}}{\sqrt{1-\gamma}}.
\end{align*}
In particular, if $\nu_N := \var\big(\ln Z_N^{SK}(\beta,0)\big) \to \infty$, then
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\ln Z_N(\beta,\gamma) - \ln Z_N^{SK}(\beta,0)}{\sqrt{\nu_N}} \overset{p}{\longrightarrow} 0.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

