{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.353363+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8f35f3957bbeb30495660a053d712ab2f5a5166293fb03cbd6a4d84d664d5c04","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 24 of arXiv:2606.17472, '2606.17472'] If $\E_0[X_1^2]=\infty$, then we have
\begin{align*}
  \mu_{[a,b]}(dy)&:=\delta_b(dy),\\
  k([a,b])&:=0.
\end{align*}
If $\E_0[X_1^2]<\infty$, then we have on $[a,b]$ 
\begin{align*}

  \mu_{[a,b]}(dy)&=\frac{\sigma^2}{2\E_0[X_1^2]}\delta_a(dy)+\frac{1}{\E_0[X_1^2]}\overline{\overline{\Pi}}(a-y)1_{(a,b)}dy\\
  &\qquad +\left(\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2\E_0[X_1^2]}\int_{(-\infty,a-b)}(a-b-x)^2 \Pi(dx)\right)\delta_b(dy),
   \stepcounter{equation}\tag{\theequation}
\end{align*}and 
