{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.962164+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fa83988c9ff5eb182089c1cec5644dd08ec4ec5993e6499fcfa5091a05f0ac1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2605.11279, 'Flocking with Multiple Types: Competition, Fluid Limits and Traveling Waves'] Suppose that Assumptions \ref{assumption0}, \ref{assumption2}, \ref{assumption3}, and \ref{assumption4} hold, which implies that the sequence $\{\mu_n(\cdot) : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ is $\CCC$-tight on $\mathscr{D}\big([0, \infty), \mathscr{P}_1(\real\times \{+,-\})\big)$, and that there exists a subsequence $\curlbr*{\mu_{n_k}(\cdot)\!: k\in\nat}$ that converges weakly to some measure $\mu(\cdot)$ in $\mathscr{C}\big([0, \infty), \mathscr{P}_1(\real\times \{+,-\})\big)$. Then for each $t\geq 0$ and $f\in \HHH$, 
