{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.087452+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fb75ab9aeec8743d0e237220a79b29f12c2d4ce4333ba4985e24a98b124f330","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2604.25769, 'The conformal dimension of the Brownian tree is one'] Let $(\SCT, d_\SCT, \SM_\SCT; o)$ be an infinite Brownian tree. Let $\mathop{\mathrm{proj}} \colon \BR \to \SCT$ be the canonical projection mapping. Let $T > 0$. Given $\SCT$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{proj}}$, let $\{x_n\}_{n \in \BN}$ be conditionally independent samples from $\SM_\Phi|_{\mathop{\mathrm{proj}}([-T, T])}$ (renormalized to be a probability measure). Then for each $\zeta > 0$, almost surely on the event that $B_1(o; d_\SCT) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{proj}}([-T, T])$, there exists $\varepsilon_\ast \in (0, 1)$ such that 
    \begin{equation}
        B_1(o; d_\SCT) \subset \bigcup \left\{B_\varepsilon(x_n; d_\SCT) : n \in [1, \varepsilon^{-2 - \zeta}]_\BZ, \ x_n \in B_1(o; d_\SCT)\right\}, \quad \forall \varepsilon \in (0, \varepsilon_\ast].
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.446076+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fb7e355789e6ba0ec501e0ec1205429ced1ace3a3f99ba6eb1b2b689b4051e4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn embedded-maps-bound-plaq-ass of arXiv:2411.11676, 'Surface sums for lattice Yang--Mills in the large-$N$ limit'] For a balanced  pair $(s,K)$, we say that  $M=(m,\psi)$ is an \textbf{embedded map with boundary $s$ and plaquette assignment $K$} if $M$ is an embedded map (Definition~(defn:embedded-map of the paper)) which satisfies the following three additional conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item [3.] the number of internal yellow faces sent to $p$ by $\psi$ is equal to $K(p)$, for all $p\in \mathcal{P}_{\Lambda}$.
	\item [4.] the total number of external yellow faces in $m$ is equal to the number of loops in $s$;
	\item [5.] the boundary of each external yellow faces of $m$ is isomorphically sent by $\psi$ to a distinct loop in $s$ preserving the orientation;\footnote{We remark that to determine if the boundary of an external yellow face of $m$ is sent to $\ell$ or $\ell^{-1}$ by $\psi$, one should use the same procedure used to determine if an internal yellow face is sent to $p$ or $p^{-1}$ by $\psi$. See also Figure~(fig-embedded-maps-exemp4 of the paper) for more explanations.}
\end{enumerate}
We denote by $\mathcal{M}(s,K)$ the set of embedded maps with boundary $s$ and plaquette assignment $K$.
For a non-balanced pair $(s,K)$, we define $\mathcal{M}(s,K)=\emptyset$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.850489+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fb87f1cd845bf4749453be1a1ab855b95f59541367e0e475c7ea0a8dcacc545","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop renorm2 of arXiv:2607.10105, 'The four-dimensional Anderson model: a case study for critical SPDEs'] Given $\kappa$, we work as in Definition (def.renormterm of the paper), each time choosing a smallest and leftmost fully paired subinterval and reducing $\kappa$ to $\kappa'$ by removing that subinterval. In the end we get a set of fully paired intervals, whose \emph{left} and \emph{right} endpoints are uniquely determined indices in $[1,m]$, call them $\ell_i$ and $r_i$ for $1\leq i\leq s$. Then we have
\begin{multline}
\mathcal R\mathcal I_{m,\kappa}=\lambda_\varepsilon^m\int_{(\mathbb T^4)^m}\prod_{\substack{j=0\\j\not\in\{r_1,\cdots,r_s\}}}^m G(x_j-x_{j+1})\prod_{i=1}^s \big(G(x_{r_i}-x_{r_i+1})-G(x_{\ell_i}-x_{r_i+1})\big)\\\times\prod_{\{i,j\}\in\kappa}\eta_\varepsilon(x_i-x_j)\Wick{\prod_{i\in S}\xi_\varepsilon(x_i)}\,\mathrm{d}x_1\cdots\mathrm{d}x_m.
\end{multline}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.816730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fc16f0c069b281ec620ee16209f48febd2cd19172262ff1bf79bc7d3405729d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi sol2 of arXiv:2605.10309, 'Exponential Decay of $L^2$-Solutions to Stochastic Nonlinear Schr\"odinger Equations Drive'] \begin{enumerate}
