{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:42:57.238959+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ff94c98c0716893be9724178240b8da1cad75e468df9ec56f034f0cf2b147c3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:good_event_many_scales of arXiv:2609.04140, 'The conformally invariant metric on $\CLE_4$ III:\\ uniqueness'] By possibly taking $\delta \in (0,1)$ to be smaller and $N \in \BN$ to be larger in the definition of the events $E_n(\Psi)$,  we have that the following is true off an event with probability at most $\varepsilon^3$,  for all sufficiently small $\varepsilon$ (in a way depending only on $a$ and $\theta$).  There exists an integer $n$ such that
