{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.831862+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_905c74d006e6d96ddd1141159e0c2b50d80a4e8bba265cd23514a45dd5b78041","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:u-expansion of arXiv:2606.25135, 'Convergence Rates for Semistochastic Processes'] Let $\mathcal{U}^t$ be a strongly continuous Markov semigroup with infinitesimal generator $\mathcal{L}$ and assume that $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_0 +\mathcal{L}_1$, with $\mathcal{L}_0$ generating the sub-Markov semigroup $\mathcal{U}_0^t$. Then the action of $\mathcal{U}^t$ on an observable $f$ can be decomposed into
\begin{equation*}
[\mathcal{U}^{t} f](x) =
[\mathcal{U}_{0}^{t}f](x)
+\int_{0}^{t} \left[\mathcal{U}_{0}^{t-s} \left( \mathcal{L}-\mathcal{L}_0 \right) \mathcal{U}^{s}f\right](x) \, {\mathrm{d}} s \ .
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.094644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_905e510ad9ad4d9705a24b4db75e6a65679954615887b464e8e6002c3abf095c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 39 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] Let $\mathfrak{u}\colon [0,1]\times U\to A$ be a $C^{k,\infty}$-time-dependent local section, $k\geq 1$, and assume that the evolution $\mathfrak{F}_\mathfrak{u}^{t,s}$ of the time-dependent complete lifts $\mathcal{X}_{\mathfrak{u}_t}$ is defined on the relevant domains for all $s,t\in[0,1]$.

Then the following assertions hold for the push-forward of sections. 
\begin{enumerate} 
\item For every local section $w$, the time-dependent section $Y_t:=(\mathfrak{F}_\mathfrak{u}^{t,s})_*w$ is for every $x$ for which the flows are defined the unique solution of 
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial Y_t}{\partial t}(x) = \LB[Y_t,\mathfrak{u}_t]_A(x), \qquad Y_s(x)=w(x).
\end{align}
\item Let $f\colon [0,1]\times U\to A$ be a $C^{k,\infty}$-time-dependent local section. Define pointwise 
$v_t(y) := \int_0^t \mathfrak{F}^{t,r}_{\mathfrak{u},\varphi_{r,t}(y)} \left( f_r(\varphi_{r,t}(y)) \right)\,dr$ whenever all the terms are defined. For fixed $t,y$, the integral is the Bochner integral\footnote{cf. \cite[Sections 23-25]{Sche97} for the definition and basic properties used in the following.} in the Banach fibre $A_y$. Then $v$ is the unique $C^{k,\infty}$-solution of 
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial v_t}{\partial t} - f_t = \LB[v_t,\mathfrak{u}_t]_A, \qquad v_0=0.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.476245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_905f6e9f230fc35ab65fcdcdd75db074a2845ad7da6c50f60a4bfbc66c24b040","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma proj-formula-underived of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\alpha\colon G\to G'$ be a homomorphism of compact Lie groups, and let $X\in\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$, $Y\in\cat{$\bm{G'}$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$. Then the map
    \begin{equation}
        (\alpha_!\textup{pr}_1, \varepsilon\circ \alpha_!\textup{pr}_2)\colon \alpha_!(X\times \alpha^*Y)\to \alpha_!X\times Y
    \end{equation}
    is an isomorphism in $\cat{$\bm{G'}$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.325415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9060fc5e54e9f34b06030eefb72d58ae07dc4c1d40fdb03d100f3ddbb0c7c5cd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def-log-gf of arXiv:2411.06301, 'Inverting the operation of conditioning a branching process on extinction'] For $\lambda = (\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_d ) \in (-\infty,\infty]^d$, define 
\begin{equation*}
g_k(\lambda) := \log f_k(e^{\lambda_1} , \dots, e^{\lambda_d} ) \quad \text{and} \quad 
g(\lambda) := (g_1(\lambda),\dots,g_d(\lambda)) .
\end{equation*}
We also let $g^{(n)}$ be the composition of $g$ with itself $n$ times. 
Then, using~(equation eqn-gen-func-n of the paper), we see that the $k$th component of $g^{(n)}$ is given by
\begin{equation}  
g_k^{(n)}(\lambda) 
= \log f_k^{(n)}(e^{\lambda_1},\dots,e^{\lambda_d})
= \log \mathbbm E_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ \prod_{j=1}^d e^{\lambda_j X_j(n)} \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] .
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.476396+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9061c459c4a0aaca8301ca3482b054525292ca11c49eb60b1a2cb7b399cc3506","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm G-global-as-slice of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\alpha\colon G\to G'$ be a homomorphism of compact Lie groups and let $B\in\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$ cofibrant. Then 
    \begin{equation}
