  \xi(\mathfrak{p}(u_{3}^{s+1}))\le K\Big) \\
  =\,& \frac{(q_n(s))_{2}}{(q_n(s+1))_4}\,
  \e_n\!\Big[(\nu_1^s)_2(\nu_2^s)_2\,
  \mathbf{1}\{\nu_1^s,\,\nu_2^s\le K\}\Big].
  \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279693+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_909143d1e0ac8af78e79eb400d70dfee6ef0de300983d3648137ed19808d987a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 15 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $\{\delta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ and $\{\zeta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ are two independent sequences of random variables, where $\delta_{i}\sim\textnormal{Bernoulli}(p_{i})$ are independent and $\zeta_{i}$ are independent centered $\alpha$-sub-exponential random variables.
	Consider a random vector $\xi=(\xi_{1}, \cdots, \xi_{n})$  with $\xi_{i}=\delta_{i}\cdot\zeta_{i}$. Let $A=(a_{ij})_{n\times n}$ be a symmetric diagonal-free matrix. Then for $0<\alpha\le 2$ and $t\ge 0$, 
	\begin{align}
	\mathbb{P}\{\vert \xi^\top A\xi\vert\ge t  \}
		\le 2\exp\Big(-c(\alpha)\min\Big\{ \frac{t^{2}}{L(\alpha)^{4}\sum_{i, j}a_{ij}^{2}p_{i}p_{j}}, \big(\frac{t}{L(\alpha)^{2}\Vert A\Vert_{l_{2}\to l_{2}}}\big)^{\alpha/2}  \Big\}      \Big),\nonumber
	\end{align}
	where $L(\alpha)=\max_{i}\Vert \zeta_{i}\Vert_{\Psi_{\alpha}}$ and $c(\alpha)$ is a positive constant depending only on $\alpha$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.843653+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_909284a625fa619d2bdf907ab1345dab0d285a4a69542bcb90f3e38bec9eb690","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cancellation of arXiv:2607.07598, 'Universal Central Limit Theorem for non-exchangeable interacting diffusions'] Suppose Assumption (assum.main of the paper) holds with $k \ge 0$. Then, for each $t \in [0,T]$, and each bounded measurable $G : {\mathbb{R}}^d \times {\mathbb{R}}^d\to {\mathbb{R}}^d$,
			\begin{align}
				\sum_{i=1}^n {\mathbb{E}}\Big[\Big|\sum_{j=1}^n \widehat{\xi}_{ij}\,G(X_t^i,X_t^j)\Big|^2\Big]
				&\lesssim \Vert G\Vert_\infty^2\Big( \sum_{i,j=1}^n \widehat{\xi}_{ij}^2
				+ \sum_{i=1}^n\Big(\sum_{j=1}^n|\widehat{\xi}_{ij}|\Big)^2\max_{i,j\in[n]}\xi_{ij}\Big), 
			\end{align}
			and
			\begin{align}
				\begin{split}
					\sum_{i=1}^n {\mathbb{E}}\Big[\Big|\sum_{j=1}^n \widehat{\xi}_{ji}\,G(X_t^j,X_t^i)\Big|^2\Big]
					\lesssim \Vert G\Vert_\infty^2&\Big(  \sum_{i,j=1}^n \widehat{\xi}_{ij}^2 + \sum_{i=1}^n\Big(\sum_{j=1}^n|\widehat{\xi}_{ji}|\Big)^2\max_{i,j\in[n]}\xi_{ij} \\
					&
					+ \sum_{i=1}^n\Big(\sum_{j=1}^n\widehat{\xi}_{ji}\Big)^2\Big).
				\end{split}

			\end{align}
			Also, for each $g_t \in C_b^1({\mathbb{R}}^d\times{\mathbb{R}}^d)$ satisfying $ 		{\mathbb{E}}[g_t(Y_t^1,Y_t^2)] = 0$,
			where $Y^1$ and $Y^2$ are independent copies of the  McKean-Vlasov solution from Proposition (lem:preliminaries of the paper)(equation lem:preliminaries:i of the paper), we have
			\begin{align} 
				{\mathbb{E}} \bigg[\Big( \sum_{i,j=1}^{n} g_t(X_t^i,X_t^j)\Big)^2 \bigg]
				\lesssim
				n^2 \|g_t\|_\infty^2 + n^3 \|\nabla g_t\|_\infty^2.
			\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:43:00.043246+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_90941501cd1e45591f0ed59ade4efb79a609834a0617460866569d12ca61823b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3 (Tenie {\cite[Theorem 1.3) of arXiv:2609.00758, 'Deformations of Compact Calabi--Yau and Fano Varieties with Isolated Singularities'] {Ten}}]
Let $X$ be a compact log-canonical complex $n$-fold, $n\ge3,$ with $\om_X\cong\O_X$ and $H^1(X,\O_X)=0,$ whose singularities are isolated complete intersection singularities and such that
\begin{equation}
\text{if $Y\to X$ is a resolution of singularities then the $\partial\db$ lemma should hold for $Y.$}
\end{equation}
Then there exist an open neighbourhood $\De$ of $0\in\C$ and a proper flat morphism $f:\cX\to S$ with $f^{-1}(0)\cong X$ and such that the singularities of $f^{-1}(t), t\in\De\-\{0\},$ are $1$-Du Bois. The singularities of $X$ which are not $1$-Du Bois become non-singular under this. \qed (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

