{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.972102+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9107365536f1d207a42cb5c9f50695133e19b8cfb6107a641649eb94cc931249","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:asym_cauchy of arXiv:2412.05999, 'Non-Archimedean GUE corners and Hecke modules'] (Skew Cauchy identity) Let $\nu \in \Sig_N, \mu \in \Sig_{N+k}$, and $x_1,\ldots,x_k, y_1,\ldots,y_r$ be indeterminates. Then
\begin{multline}
    \sum_{\kappa \in \Sig_{N+k}} Q_{\kappa/\mu}(y_1,\ldots,y_r;t) P_{\kappa/\nu}(x_1,\ldots,x_k;t) \\
    = \Pi_t(x_1,\ldots,x_k; y_1,\ldots,y_r)\sum_{\lambda\in \Sig_N} Q_{\nu/\lambda}(y_1,\ldots,y_r;t) P_{\mu/\lambda}(x_1,\ldots,x_k;t) 
\end{multline}
where the Cauchy kernel $\Pi_t(x_1,\ldots,x_k; y_1,\ldots,y_r)$ has the form 
\begin{equation}
    \Pi_t(x_1,\ldots,x_k; y_1,\ldots,y_r) = \prod_{\substack{1 \leq i \leq k \\ 1 \leq j \leq r}} \frac{1-tx_iy_j}{1-x_iy_j}=\exp(\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{1-t^n}{n}\sum_{i=1}^kx_i^n\cdot\sum_{j=1}^ry_j^n)
\end{equation}
and (equation eq:asym_cauchy of the paper) is interpreted as an equality of formal power series in the variables.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:11.638688+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_910c1c05fac97a9758fce9338da256174d9a0d8ba37e9c8633aad5ccb85e9c64","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2605.31344, 'Subcritical sharpness for real-valued spin models'] For any $\beta \geq 0$ and $a \geq 2 D \beta$, there exists $B > 0$ such that for any finite $\Lambda \subset V$, $\Lambda' \subset \Lambda$, $\psi \in \mathbb{R}^{\Lambda'}$, and any boundary conditions $\eta \in \mathbb{R}^{V}$,
