{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.952362+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_938c8aa5cb150b12972408e4feb5bc336cfbfb546c4686662206af1e73dbf56f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-nonfreedense of arXiv:2412.03067, 'Geodesic Trees and Exceptional Directions in FPP on Hyperbolic Groups'] Let $G$ be a hyperbolic group such that $G$ is not virtually free, and let $\Gamma$ be its Cayley graph with respect to some finite symmetric generating set. Then the following hold.
	\begin{enumerate}
	\item For $\omega$ in the probability 1 event $\Omega_0$ from Theorem (thm-12proper of the paper), the set of exceptional directions in the forward tree $F(1,\omega)$ is non-empty and dense in ${\partial G}$. 
	\item  For $\xi\in {\partial G}$, and for $\omega$ in the probability 1 event $\Omega_0\cap \Omega_{\xi}$ from Theorem (thm-12proper of the paper), the set of exceptional directions in the backward tree $T(1,\omega)$ is non-empty and dense in ${\partial G}$. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.705649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_938f3397a499bcfd2279a0b5dc36a1c01dba96992b2160ddb4e48ff55b7a6a10","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:uniform_in_range of arXiv:2604.10214, 'Large deviations for maximum local time of simple random walk in dimensions \texorpdfstrin'] Uniformly for $n\in\mathbb N^+$ and $t,r\ge 1$,
\begin{align}
{\mathbb P}(\left\lvert S_n \right\rvert<r)\lesssim \Big(\frac{r}{n^{1/2}}\Big)^d;\qquad
{\mathbb P}(\left\lvert Y_t \right\rvert<r)\lesssim \Big(\frac{r}{t^{1/2}}\Big)^d.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.458704+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_938ff7d27d188ca810e75d0259d86ac7b41c4b4a379aa178c6fa6d8735cec282","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 41 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Let $\gamma > 0$ and $v: \mathbb{N}\in \mathbb{N}_+ \mapsto \mathbb{R}$. Recall the definition of $q_n^r$ in \eqref{defqnr}. Then
\begin{align}
& \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{n}  \sup_{r \in [0,T]} \sum_x \big[ \Theta(n)  \mathbb{L}_n^{\gamma,s} H (\tfrac{x + q_n^r}{n})  - \mathbb{L}^{\gamma} H (\tfrac{x + q_n^r}{n}) \big]^{2}=0,  \\
&  \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{n}  \sup_{r \in [0,T]} \sum_x \big[ \widehat{\mathbb{L}}_{n, \lambda}^{ \gamma} H (\tfrac{x + q_n^r}{n})  - \widehat{\mathbb{L}}_{ \lambda}^{ \gamma} H (\tfrac{x + q_n^r}{n}) \big]^{2} = 0. 
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.685547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93931bd3e4fcf0cd7c99080bb6f78bc021b7aad187543f963b826c6d93e09efd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition mixingdef of arXiv:2604.20215, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices II: Markov Chain Comparison and Cr'] Two  Markov chains $([N], P_N)$ and $([N], \widetilde{P}_N)$  are
\emph{Short-to-Long Comparable}, if their $n$-step transition probabilities  $p_n(x,y)$ and $\widetilde{p}_n(x,y)$ satisfy the following two conditions:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,label=(B\arabic*)]
\item  \textbf{(Average upper bound)}
There exists a sequence $\{b_{n}\}$ of positive  numbers,   where $b_n=b_{n,N}$ may depend on $N$, such that
\begin{equation}\max_{x,y\in [N]}\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_i(x, y) \lor \tilde{p}_i(x, y) \le b_n.
\end{equation}

\item  \textbf{(Average $\ell^1$-$\ell^{\infty}$ distance)}
There exist  two sequences   $\{\epsilon_n\}$ and $\{\delta_n\}$ such that
\begin{equation}
\max_{x\in [N]}\sum_{y\in [N]}\left| p_n(x,y) - \tilde{p}_n(x,y) \right| \le \epsilon_n,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \max_{x,y\in [N]}|p_n(x,y) - \tilde{p}_n(x,y)|\le \delta_n.
\end{equation}
  Let  $\mathcal{E}_n=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_i$ and $\Delta_n=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\delta_i$ the associated cumulative errors.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.775642+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_939475805d6fff9f469144e97a312176c721f6daded96e2f43df4ef9cf60b9ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2 (Basic properties of normal random variables) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] \begin{enumerate}
\item   One has $X\sim\cN(m,\sigma^2)$ if, and only if, $X = m + \sigma Y$ 
with $Y \sim \cN(0,1)$. 

\item   Assume $X\sim\cN(m_1,\sigma_1^2)$ and $Y\sim\cN(m_2,\sigma_2^2)$ 
are defined on a common probability space, and let $Z = X + Y$. Then 
$Z$ is Gaussian, with parameters 
\begin{equation}
 Z \sim \cN \bigpar{m_1+m_2, \sigma_1^2 + \sigma_2^2 + 2\cov(X,Y)}\;.
\end{equation} 

\item   Two Gaussian variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent if, and only if, 
they are uncorrelated, that is, $\cov(X,Y) = \expec{XY} - \expec{X}\expec{Y} = 0$. 
