{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.115592+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93b1b014dd36d119e392df67e910b1aef0d28c6348a4d294ad11f10fc5e2b080","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:random_length_worms_model of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Let us consider a probability mass function $\eta: \mathbb{N}_{>0} \to [0,1] $ and an $\mathbb{N}_{>0}$-valued random variable $\mathcal{L}$ satisfying $\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{L}=\ell)=\eta(\ell), \, \ell \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$. 
	We call the distribution of $\mathcal{L}$ the length distribution of the worms. Let us also consider a nearest neighbour simple random walk $(X(t))_{t \in \mathbb{N}}$ on the vertices of $G$ starting from $X(0)=o$, independent of $\mathcal{L}$.
	Let us define the probability measure $\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}$ on $\mathcal{H}_{o}$ by 
	\begin{equation}

		\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}(H) := P_{o}( \{ X(0), X(1), \ldots, X(\mathcal{L} - 1) \} = H )
	\end{equation}
	for each $H \in \mathcal{H}_{o}$.
	Given any $\lambda >0$, the Poisson zoo $\mathcal{N}_{\eta}^{\lambda} := \mathcal{N}_{\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}}^{\lambda}$ built upon this measure via Definition (def:poisson_zoo of the paper) is called the random length worms model at level $\lambda$, and the corresponding random set $\mathcal{S}_{\eta}^\lambda := \mathcal{S}_{\nu^{\text{worm;}{\eta}}}^\lambda$ is the random length worms set at level $\lambda$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:29:14.973077+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93b65cf30524aca7f05f74bf1efd40e2444f2031529df9e63568466306671b14","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LeA.4 of arXiv:2606.15203, 'The existence of invariant sublinear expectations for $G\text{-}$SDEs'] For each $\theta^1$ and $\theta^2$ in $\mathcal{A}^{\Theta}_{t,T}$, there exists $\theta \in \mathcal{A}^{\Theta}_{t,T}$ such that
\begin{equation}
      E_P[\varphi(X^{t,\xi, \theta}_T)|\mathcal{F}_t] =E_P[\varphi(X^{t,\xi, \theta^1}_T)|\mathcal{F}_t]\vee E_P[\varphi(X^{t,\xi, \theta^2}_T)|\mathcal{F}_t]
    \end{equation}
Consequently, there exists a sequence $\{ \theta^i\}^\infty_{i=1} $ of $\mathcal{A}^{\Theta}_{t,T}$ such that
\begin{equation}
    E_P[\varphi(X^{t,\xi, \theta^i}_T)|\mathcal{F}_t]\nearrow  \Lambda_{t,T}[\xi],\ P\text{-}a.s.
\end{equation}
We also have, for each $r \leq t$,
\begin{equation}
  E_P[\underset{\theta \in \mathcal{A}^{\Theta}_{t,T}}{\rm esssup}E_P[\varphi(X^{t,\xi, \theta}_T)|\mathcal{F}_t]|\mathcal{F}_r]=\underset{\theta \in \mathcal{A}^{\Theta}_{t,T}}{\rm esssup}E_P[\varphi(X^{t,\xi, \theta}_T)|\mathcal{F}_r]  
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:27.994533+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93b6b7720a4f853f33217a8a12a3768c89ff4c69c25102819afe30316a49658f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem mk1k2inftyIthm of arXiv:2608.04730, 'A new look at some aspects of one-dimensional random sequential adsorption and its continu'] Let $m_{k_1;k_2;I}$  be as in Theorem (k1k2Ithm of the paper).
