{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.061320+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_94c238908997acdc344927dc088f88926ef4aefbe209adfd82eccea199c00bb1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:condition-gammaq of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $\gamma : [0, 1] \to [0, 1]^2$ be a continuous path such that $\gamma$ is of class $C^1$ on $]0, 1[$ and $\gamma(]0, 1[) \subset ]0, 1[^2$. We assume that for all $t \in ]0, 1[$ one of the following inequalities holds:
  \begin{equation}
    \gamma_q'(t) \geqslant \max \{0, - u(\gamma(t)) \gamma_p'(t)\},

  \end{equation}
  or
  \begin{equation}
    \gamma_p'(t) \geqslant \max \{0, - v(\gamma(t)) \gamma_q'(t)\}.

  \end{equation}
  Then for all $n \in \mathbb N$ the function $\theta_n \circ \gamma$ is nondecreasing. Moreover, if there exists $t_c \in ]0, 1[$ such that $\theta(\gamma(t)) = 0$ for all $0 \leqslant t < t_c$, and  $\theta(\gamma(t)) > 0$ for all $t_c < t \leqslant 1$, and at $t_c$ one of the aforementioned inequalities holds without equality, then
  \begin{itemize}
    \item for $0 \leqslant t < t_c$, there exists $c_t > 0$ such that for any $n$ large enough, $\theta_n(\gamma(t)) \leqslant \exp(-c_t n)$,
    \item there exists $c > 0$ and $\delta > 0$ such that  $\theta(\gamma(t)) \geqslant c (t - t_c)$ for all $t_c \leqslant t \leqslant t_c + \delta$.
  \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.357695+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_94c3f2d0b0405e96acd7cd5b0ac33f6bbaf71c8f9f220e9e53da7e4d8dfc3f13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:alias-cell of arXiv:2608.23860, 'Revelation Control'] For the binary action menu, the exact refinement value is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
