{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.351255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_971a529e50dd8a1b1802e8be091cd5f1276d74166b0c864b2819c10932d88a50","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary C:-infty-case of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] There is a unique (in law) random continuous function $\mathcal{L}^{-\infty}:\mathbb{H}^2_\uparrow \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For each $\rho \in\mathbb{R}$, $\mathcal{L}^{-\infty}$ is stochastically dominated by $\mathcal{L}^\rho$,  the half-space directed landscape with parameter $\rho$.
    \item For each $u = (x, s; y, s + t) \in \mathbb{Q}^4\cap \mathbb{H}^2_\uparrow$, we have
    $\mathcal{L}^{-\infty}(u) \stackrel{d}{=} \mathfrak{h}^{-\infty}_t\delta_x(y)$,
    where $\mathfrak{h}^{-\infty}_t$ is the half-space KPZ fixed point of parameter $-\infty$, and $\delta_x\in \operatorname{UC}_+$ such that $\delta_x(x)=0$ and $\delta_x(z)=-\infty$ for each $z\neq x$.
\end{enumerate}
We call $\mathcal{L}^{-\infty}$ the \textbf{half-space directed landscape} with parameter $-\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.856644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_971e35ad28836a8e6a1a7a4931cc5409e4b1fb61ac4df1f3e35fb37bdf963442","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:sigmaandv(X) of arXiv:2510.01021, '\Large Random Matrices, Intrinsic Freeness, and Sharp Non-Asymptotic Inequalities\relatedv'] Given a $d\times d$ matrix $X$ with jointly gaussian entries (which we will write as $X = A_0 + \sum_{k=1}^n g_kA_k$) we define the following parameters
\begin{equation}
    \sigma(X)^2 = \big\| \mathbb{E}(X - \mathbb{E} X)(X - \mathbb{E} X)^\top \big\| \vee \big\| \mathbb{E}(X - \mathbb{E} X)^\top(X - \mathbb{E} X) \big\| = \left\| \sum_{k=1}^n A_kA_k^\top \right\| \vee \left\| \sum_{k=1}^n A_k^\top A_k \right\|;
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    v(X)^2 = \big\| \cov(X) \big\| = \sup_{\|B\|_F = 1} \left|\Tr(B^\top A_k)\right|^2,
\end{equation}
where $\cov(X)$ denotes the $d^2\times d^2$ covariance matrix of the entries of $X$;
\begin{equation}
    \sigma_{\ast}(X)^2 = \sup_{u,v\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}}\mathbb{E} \big| u^\top (X - \mathbb{E} X)v\big|^2= \sup_{u,v\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}}\sum_{k=1}^n\big| u^\top A_kv\big|^2.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

