{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.609911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9730c0552644907e904e8752788f4ca6ae990024ffaee9a9ef0181d09f0c2733","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:joint-filtration of arXiv:2608.15515, '\LARGE Weak-Type Bounds for Convolution on the Boolean Hypercube'] For every bounded terminal test $h:G\to\mathbb R$ and every
$\theta\leq t\leqT_{\mathrm o}$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[h(V_{T_{\mathrm o}})\mid\mathcal F_t]
 =P^V_{t,T_{\mathrm o}}h(V_t).
\end{equation}
For the reverse process already defined on $[0,T]$, the increments of the
driving Poisson random measures after $T_{\mathrm o}$ are independent of
$\mathcal F_{T_{\mathrm o}}$.  Consequently, for every bounded $\Phi:G\to\mathbb R$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\Phi(V_T)\mid\mathcal F_t]
 =P^V_{t,T}\Phi(V_t),
 \qquad \theta\leq t\leqT_{\mathrm o}.
\end{equation}
In particular, if
$H_t^\zeta(x)=P^V_{t,T}\1_{\{\zeta\}}(x)$, then
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(V_T=\zeta\mid\mathcal F_t)=H_t^\zeta(V_t).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

