{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.905090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_98d6bdb61a91d6f106c6e70d93b3c09c8d9b18b771c903ef956039b065e43d1b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2606.20948, '\textsc{Slot decomposition of\\ continuous Box-Ball Systems}'] [Mapping  $\cN$ to a carrier process]\rm
Let $\sN\in\cN$ with $\sN=\sN_+\cup \sN_-$. Recall the construction of the excursion $\vep[\sM]$ from $\sM\in\cN_o$ in Definition \ref{17}, and let 
$\tau(\vep)$ be the length of the excursion $\vep$. We have $\tau(\vep)=\se(\vep)=\se(\vep)-\so(\vep)$ as $\so(\vep)=0$ for $\vep \in \cN_0$. Consider the sequence $(\su_k,\sM_k)_{k\ge1}$ obtained in \eqref{250} from $\sN_+\in\cN_+$. Denote $\so_0\vcentcolon =0$, $\se_0\vcentcolon =0$, and for $k\ge1$, 
\begin{align}
 \vep_k&\vcentcolon =\vep[\sM_k], \quad \se_k\vcentcolon =\se(\vep_k),\\
 \so_k&\vcentcolon = \so_{k-1}+ \se_{k-1}+ \su_k, \quad k\ge1.
\end{align}
For $\sN_+\in\cN_+$, we define the carrier process $\zeta_+=\zeta[\sN_+]:\RR_+\to\RR_+$ by concatenating excursions $\vep_k$ separated by intervals of lengths $\su_k$, as follows
\begin{gather}

 \zeta(x) \vcentcolon =
 \begin{cases}
 \vep_k(x-\so_k), 
 &\so_k< x\le \so_k+\se_k, \\
  0, 
 &\so_{k-1}+\se_{k-1}< x \le \so_{k}, 
 \end{cases},\qquad k\ge1.
\end{gather}

The reflection with respect to the vertical axis of $\sN_-\in\cN_-$ is given by
\begin{align}

  \Reflect(\sN_-) &\vcentcolon = \{(-x, \ell):(x, \ell)\in\sN_-\} \in\cN_+.
\end{align}
For $\sN_-\in \cN_-$, we define $\zeta_-=\zeta[\sN_-]:\RR_-\to\RR_+$ by reflecting the previous construction, 
\begin{align}
 \zeta[\sN_-] &\vcentcolon = \Reflect\bigl[\zeta[\Reflect(\sN_-)]\bigr],  
 \\
\text{where}\quad\Reflect\zeta(x)&\vcentcolon = \zeta(-x). \notag
\end{align}
The mapping from $\sN\in \cN$ to the carrier process $\zeta=\zeta[\sN]$ is given by
\begin{align}

  \zeta[\sN](x) =
  \begin{cases}
    \zeta[\sN_+](x),&x\ge 0\\
    \zeta[\sN_-](x),&x<0.
  \end{cases}
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.465900+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_98d7afafa9ec64d5cf5735017df44d60fb8f74a5891ca1b28b3805627756de19","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2606.29131, '2606.29131'] Let $\Lambda_\e^7 f(x,y)$ be defined by \eqref{e2-3-4}.
