{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.863481+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_99ec04999ace2afd4300b5ad00384292a4450e9645b17d87e6f7e48a26045e2b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 10 (Edge branching process $\BP_{\Sr, \ve}$) of arXiv:2605.21459, 'Network evolution with self-reinforcement'] Consider the following branching process: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)]
At time $0$, the population consists of $1$ individual $\tilde{v}_0$. This individual reproduces according to an inhomogeneous Poisson point process with rate function 
\begin{equation}

r_\ve^{\sss(1)}(t) = \frac{1+\delta}{1+\tfrac12\delta}\,(1-\eee^{-t}), \qquad t\geq 0.
\end{equation}
\item[(b)]

For all vertices other than the root, at time $t \ge 0$ after its birth, an existing (non-root) vertex $\ve$ reproduces according to an inhomogeneous Poisson point process with rate function 
    \begin{equation}

        r_\ve(t) = \frac{1}{1+\tfrac12\delta}\,(1-\eee^{-t}),\qquad t\geq 0,
    \end{equation} 
giving birth to a new vertex. We will sometimes refer to the vertices in this branching process as ``edges'' (towards a new individual).
\end{enumerate}
Denote the \emph{entire set of descendants}  of $\tilde{v}_0$ at time $t$ by $(\BP_{\Sr,\ve}(t))_{t \ge 0}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.196851+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_99eed5f51a6819f530f5ed2737e58b9d2d4873e7d0d04c332129ef00fc85cc4c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 33 of arXiv:2510.22783, 'Universality of Cutoff for Riffle Shuffling'] For each index $i\in [N]$ and for each $y \in \al, z \in \ar$, there exists a subset $A_{y,z,i}^{\stable}\subseteq A_{y,z}^{\stable}$ such that
