{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.605321+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a61cacca449adc4a6531250bb9b255dcb9cb7117d388063346852a3c1fc809b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2 of arXiv:2607.08129, '2607.08129'] The sequence $(n^{-1}\log L_n)_{n\ge1}$ satisfies an LDP on $\R$ with speed $n/\log n$ and rate
\begin{equation}
 I_\theta^{\mathrm{end}}(y)
 =\sup_{\lambda\in\R}
 \{\lambda y-\Lambda_\theta(\lambda)\}.
\end{equation}
For every $y\in(0,1)$ there is a unique $\lambda_y\in\R$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 y=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\frac1{j(j+1)}
 \frac{(1-q^j)e^{\lambda_y}}
 {q^j+(1-q^j)e^{\lambda_y}},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 I_\theta^{\mathrm{end}}(y)
 =y\lambda_y-\Lambda_\theta(\lambda_y),
 \qquad 0<y<1.
\end{equation}
More explicitly, define
\begin{equation}
 u_y(x):=
 \frac{(1-q^{m(x)})e^{\lambda_y}}
 {q^{m(x)}+(1-q^{m(x)})e^{\lambda_y}},
 \qquad 0<x\le1.
\end{equation}
Then $\int_0^1u_y(x)\dd x=y$ and
\begin{align}
 I_\theta^{\mathrm{end}}(y)
 =\int_0^1\bigg[
 u_y(x)\log\frac{u_y(x)}{1-q^{m(x)}}
 +(1-u_y(x))
 \log\frac{1-u_y(x)}{q^{m(x)}}
 \bigg]\dd x.

\end{align}
The boundary values are
\begin{equation}
 I_\theta^{\mathrm{end}}(0)=+\infty,
 \qquad
 I_\theta^{\mathrm{end}}(1)
 =-\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\frac1{j(j+1)}\log(1-q^j),
\end{equation}
and $I_\theta^{\mathrm{end}}(y)=+\infty$ for $y\notin[0,1]$.  Its unique zero is $c_\theta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.657716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a61ec62ea3f4e9971fcda0cfbc041d8484cee10be7afb36166b74fdff7aadb0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop of arXiv:2411.09988, 'A Study in Markov Chains, Loop-Erased Random Walk and Loop Soups'] Each of the following methods is a valid way to get the unrooted loop soup.
