{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.521605+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a86e7962f575f183a3c5bdd0baa0183682fd6da47fbf8b4f66aeac868055f20","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn hardy of arXiv:2512.18353, 'A note on the planar Skorokhod embedding problem'] Let $f$ be an analytic function in the unit disc. The $p^{th}$ Hardy
norm of $f$ is defined by 
\begin{equation}
\Vert f\Vert_{\boldsymbol{H}^{p}}:=\sup_{0\leq r<1}\left\{ \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}|f(re^{\theta i})|^{p}d\theta\right\} ^{\frac{1}{p}}.
\end{equation}
The set of analytic functions on $\mathbb{D}$ with finite $\boldsymbol{H}^{p}$
norm is denoted by $\boldsymbol{H}^{p}(\mathbb{D})$ and is called
the Hardy space (of index $p$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.654731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a8a724e5730e6b57d24a7203ce995676609d7cf5ba4131013f2e5c0eb8d4151","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 11 of arXiv:2609.03713, 'A Sharp Mass Threshold for Boundedness in a Critical Quasilinear Chemotaxis Model with Ind'] Let $a>0$, $M=a|\mathbb{B}_R|$ and consider $u\in \Sigma_a$. Then
\begin{equation}
	|\partial_r \phi'(u(r))| \le \frac{\|u\|_\infty}{d}r\,,\quad r\in (0,R]\,,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
	|\phi'(u(r))-\langle\phi'(u)\rangle| \le \frac{M}{\sigma_d(m-1)}\left(\frac{r^{2-d}}{d}+\frac{R^{2-d}}{2}\right)\,,\quad r\in (0,R]\,.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
	0<u(r)\le  \left( a R^d + \frac{a^2 R^d r^2}{d^2 m}\right) r^{-d}\,,\quad r\in (0,R]\,,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{align}
	\partial_r u(r)\le  \frac{a}{dm} u(r) r\,,\quad r\in (0,R]\,.
\end{align}
In fact,
\begin{equation}
	|\partial_r u(r)| \le \min\{u(r),u(r)^{2-m}\}\|u\|_\infty\frac{r}{dm}\,,\quad r\in (0,R]\,,
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
	|\partial_r u(r)| \le \frac{M}{m\sigma_d}\min\{u(r),u(r)^{2-m}\}r^{1-d}\,,\quad r\in (0,R]\,.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.406177+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a8fa058cfe78b4fa8721927641212272d06e2e7f314c342bad4afad5ab781d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem12.234567twovectors of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] In this theorem we take $\nu=2^{-15}$ and $p\in(\frac{1}{2^7B^4},1)$ is a constant depending only on $\zeta$.
    For given $n\in\mathbb{N}$, $0<c_0\leq 2^{-50}B^{-4}$, let $d\leq c_0^2n$ and fix $\alpha_0\in(0,1)$. Let $\mathbf{t}=(t_1,t_2)$ be two positive real numbers. Consider two vectors $X_1,X_2\in\mathbb{R}^d$ that satisfy $\operatorname{LCD}_{\frac{8\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{\nu p}}+\frac{256\sqrt{2}B^2}{\sqrt{c_0}},\alpha_0}^\mathbf{t}(\frac{c_0}{32\sqrt{n}}\mathbf{X})\geq 512B^2$ (where we denote $\frac{c_0}{32\sqrt{n}}\mathbf{X}=(\frac{c_0}{32\sqrt{n}}X_1,\frac{c_0}{32\sqrt{n}}X_2)$). Let $H$ be an $(n-d)\times 2d$ random matrix with i.i.d. rows of distribution $\Phi_\nu(2d;\zeta)$.  Then whenever $k\leq 2^{-21}B^{-4}d$ and $R^2t_1t_2\geq\exp(-2^{-23}B^{-4}d)$, we have the following 
    \begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
        \mathbb{P}_H&(\sigma_{2d-k+1}(H)\leq c_02^{-4}\sqrt{n}\text{ and }\|H_1X_i\|_2,\|H_2X_i\|_2\leq n\text{ for both }i=1,2)\\&\leq e^{-c_0nk/12}(\prod_{i=1}^2\frac{R t_i}{\alpha_0})^{2n-2d},
   \end{aligned}\end{equation}
    where $H_1=H_{[n-d]\times[d]}$, $H_2=H_{[n-d]\times[d+1,2d]}$ and $R=2^{44}B^2c_0^{-3}(\frac{8}{\sqrt{\nu p}}+\frac{256B^2}{\sqrt{c_0}})$, and $\sigma_1(H)\geq\sigma_2(H)\geq\cdots\sigma_{2d}(H)$ are the singular values of $H$ arranged in a decreasing order. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.847799+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a95c2c2100b8ea6a9b5147bd60c873e66d4620b919c1f29c1a09b7bce3cd5fd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: modified transport equation of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] Fix $[a, b] \subset \mathbbm{R}_+$ and a Borel function $\map{\bar \boldsymbol{y}}{[a, b]}{[0, \infty]}$. Suppose that $\boldsymbol{\mu} \in D_{\mathcal{M}_F(\mathbbm{R}_+^2)}[a, b]$ is such that $|\boldsymbol{\mu}|$ satisfies (a) and (b) of Definition (def: fluid problem of the paper) and the integrals
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_a^t\int_{U_g} \left[\mathbbm{1}_{\left\{y \leq \bar \boldsymbol{y}_s\right\}}h_g^x(x, y) + h_g^y(x, y)\right]\boldsymbol{\mu}_s(dx, dy)ds
	\end{equation*}
	are finite for all $t \in [a, b]$. Let $\bar \boldsymbol{r}_s(y) \vcentcolon= \mathbbm{1}_{\left\{y \leq \bar \boldsymbol{y}_s\right\}}$ for all $s \in [a, b]$ and $y \in \mathbbm{R}_+$. We say that $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ solves the transport equation (equation eq: transport equation with given threshold of the paper) with prescribed threshold $\bar \boldsymbol{y}$ in the interval $[a, b]$ if
	\begin{equation}

		\begin{split}
			\angled{\varphi_t}{\boldsymbol{\mu}_t} &= \angled{\varphi_a}{\boldsymbol{\mu}_a} + \lambda_0 \int_a^t \varphi_s(0, 0)ds - \int_a^t \angled{\varphi_s\left[\bar \boldsymbol{r}_s h_g^x + h_g^y\right]}{\boldsymbol{\mu}_s} ds \\
			&+ \int_a^t \angled{\partial_t\varphi_s + \bar \boldsymbol{r}_s\partial_x\varphi_s + \partial_y\varphi_s}{\boldsymbol{\mu}_s}ds
		\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	for all $t \in [a, b]$ and $\varphi \in C_c^1(\mathbbm{R}_+^3)$, and we write $\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathcal{S}_{\bar \boldsymbol{y}}[a, b]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.093697+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a975a1992a8d8d7446a9756d51aa12031981ca9837f567df9d9ecd2fa1279be","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:detailed-balance-convergence of arXiv:2609.02710, 'Asymptotic Mean Value Laplacian\\ on equiregular sub-Riemannian manifolds'] For $f,g\in C_c^\infty(M)$, the identity
\begin{equation}
  \int_M fA_hg\,D_h\,\,d\mu
  =-\frac1{2h^{Q+2}}
  \iint_{\{d(x,q)<h\}}
  (f(q)-f(x))(g(q)-g(x))
  \,\,d\mu(q)\,d\mu(x)
\end{equation}
holds for all  $h>0$ small enough\footnote{Note that our general assumptions do not a priori imply $\mu(B(x,h))<+\infty$ for every $h>0$.}. Moreover, for every
$f\in C_c^\infty(M)$,
\begin{equation}
  D_hA_hf
  \longrightarrow
  \frac12\operatorname{div}_{\mu}\bigl(M^\sharp(df)\bigr)
\end{equation}
in distributions.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.093811+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9aa13771d6bc05d24381c4cd0773ae3ee1251ce7d182db4351697880b59cfb58","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor corollary:exitPx of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] For every  non-negative measurable functions $F,G$ on $\mathbb{D}(\mathbb{R}_+, \mathcal{M}_f(\mathbb{R}_+)\times \mathcal{W}_{\overline{E}}) \times \mathbb{R}$ and $\mathbb{D}(\mathbb{R}_+, \mathcal{M}_f(\mathbb{R}_+)\times \mathcal{W}_{\overline{E}})$ we have 
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}_{0,x,0}\Big[   \sum_{u \in \mathcal{D}} F\big( \,  \emph{\texttt{T}}_u (\rho,\overline{W}), \mathfrak{g}(u) \big) G(\rho^u , \overline{W}^u) \Big]  = 
    \mathbb{E}_{0,x,0}\Big[ \int_0^\infty \mathrm{d} A_s  ~F(\rho,\overline{W}, s ) \mathbf{N}_{x, \widehat{\Lambda}_s}\big(G(\rho,\overline{W})\big)\Big].
