\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:01:25.574292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b0a98b3e4b364932dca2aed0a78a719d9207a8a5d7bb6291a3ded4460ca61a6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2605.15742, 'Turbulent stretching of FENE dumbbell polymer model via special stochastic scaling and sin'] Let $\tau>0, \varepsilon>0$ and	 $N\in   \mathbb{N}$ and  set  $\beta=\zeta \tau, \zeta>0$	   in  (equation Ito-FP_eps-V2 of the paper).  Let $f_{\tau,0}\in H$ and  $f^\varepsilon_{\tau,N}$  be the   quasi-regular weak solution   to  (equation Ito-FP_eps-V2 of the paper)  in the sense of (Def-sol-QR-* of the paper) with the initial data $f_{\tau,0}.$
The following holds.
\begin{itemize}
    \item   Assume there exists $f_0\in H_0$ such that   $f_{\tau, 0} \to f_0$ in $H_0$ as $\tau \downarrow 0.$ Then, we have
    \begin{align*}
    \displaystyle \text{weak}-\lim_{\tau\downarrow 0}\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}\lim_{N\uparrow +\infty}   f^\varepsilon_{\tau,N}= \rho_{0}\otimes\mathbf{M}_0 \text{ in } L^2(0,T;L^2(\Omega;H_0)), \quad \rho_0(x)=\int_D f_0(x,r) dr.
    \end{align*}
 \item     Assume that $f_{\tau,0}$ converges to $ \rho_{0}\otimes\mathbf{M}_0$ in $H_0$ as $\tau \downarrow 0.$ Then,  we have
      \begin{align*}
    \displaystyle \text{weak}-\lim_{\tau\downarrow 0}\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}\lim_{N\uparrow +\infty}   f^\varepsilon_{\tau,N}(t)= \rho_{0}\otimes\mathbf{M}_0 \text{ in } L^2(\Omega;H_0) \text{ for any } t\in [0,T].
    \end{align*}
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.725805+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b0bad3ff8e9c873afc35621e6269fe7a81ff013ee7dadd74cf5457692d84bcc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2608.29761, '2608.29761'] Let $\dis_{\M}$  denote the hyperbolic distance on $\M$.  We say that $\gamma\in \Gamma$ is  $\delta$-collared if the implication
