{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.547576+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b73e8403c4dd0aee2189fcfffd9935c7395c67ca38c5a102bf3c48470beb98e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 15 ($(\beta-\slackmeasure,\sigma)$-local consistency of a retail set) of arXiv:2609.01397, 'Measuring consistency via ensemble margin and local prediction variability: Auditing decis'] Given a Rashomon \smallset{} set $\retailset = \{f_k :  k \in [\Nretail]\} \subseteq \emprashomonset$ of size $\Nretail$, a set of perturbed samples $\localtestmat = \{\localtest_{j}: j \in [\nogaussianpts]\} \subseteq \ltwohsphere(x,\sigma)$, the ensemble prediction $\ensmretset(\retailset,\localtestmat)$ in Definition \ref{def: local ensemble}, and a constant $\slackmeasure >0$, let  $ \localmarginconsistency(\retailset,\localtestmat,x) $ be defined as
\begin{align}
    \localmarginconsistency(\retailset,\localtestmat, x) = \babs{\ensmretset(\retailset,\localtestmat)-\frac{1}{2}} - \frac{1}{\nogaussianpts}\sum_{j=1}^{\nogaussianpts}\frac{1}{\Nretail}\sum_{j=1}^{\Nretail}\babs{f_k(x) - f_k(\localtest_j)}.
\end{align} 
for a fixed test instance $x$. Then, $\retailset$ is said to be $(\beta-\slackmeasure,\sigma)$-locally consistent if,
\begin{align}
    \localmarginconsistency(\retailset,\localtestmat,x) \ge \beta-\slackmeasure
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.094415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b77158b2675ee930cabb0270297113a15fad1ea905b56a593ccf7b410dc33fa","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 33 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] Let $(X_t,\eta_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the unique solution to \eqref{eq:4.28} in the sense of  Definition \ref{Defi 317}. Then, $\mathbb{P}\mathrm{-a.s.}$, for almost every $t > 0$, the law ${\mu_t}$ of ${\bf x} \in {\mathbb S} \mapsto X_t({\bf x})$ (with ${\mathbb S}$ equipped with $\textrm{\rm Leb}_{\mathbb S}$) has a square integrable density $p_t(\cdot)$. {For all $T >0$,} it satisfies
\begin{align}

\mathbb{E}\int_0^T\int_{\mathbb{R}}{|p_{t}(z)|^2}\mathrm{d}z\mathrm{d}t < + \infty. 

\end{align}
Moreover, 
{${\mathbb P}$-almost surely and for almost every $t > 0$},
the density $p_t$ is supported by 
$[X_t(0),X_t(1/2)]$ and is almost everywhere positive on 
$(X_t(0),X_t(1/2))$.
{For $\textrm{\rm Leb}_{\mathbb R}$-almost every $z \in {\mathbb R}$, $X_t$ is differentiable at $\tilde{F}_{\mu_t}(z)$, where $\tilde{F}_{\mu_t}$ is the cumulative distribution function of $\mu_t$, and for almost every 
$z \in (X_t(0),X_t(1/2))$,
\begin{equation}

D_{\mathbf x} X_t \bigl(\tfrac12 \tilde F_{\mu_t}(z) \bigr) = \frac1{2 p_t(z)}.
\end{equation}
In particular, 
for all $T>0$, 
\begin{equation}
{\mathbb E}
\int_0^T \int_{\mathbb R}
{\mathbbm 1}_{(X_t(0),X_t(1/2))}(z)
\frac1{p_t(z)}
\ud z  \ud t =
\frac14 {\mathbb E}
\int_0^T \int_{\mathbb S}
\vert D_{\mathbf{x}}
X_t({\mathbf x}) \vert^2
\ud {\mathbf x}  \ud t
< + \infty.

\end{equation}
Lastly, 
${\mathbb P}$-almost surely and for almost every $t>0$, 
$X_t(\cdot /2)$ is a continuous one-to-one mapping from $(0,1)$ onto $(X_t(0),X_t(1/2))$, with $\tilde F_{\mu_t}$ as inverse. 
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.014060+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b799d0bfd101f8632a984fd52775e210c32c97a388d16d0d2968764daaec3c8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Z-wellposed of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Let Assumptions (ass:LR of the paper), (ass:diff2 of the paper) and~(ass:SC of the paper) hold, let $0 \leq s \leq T$ and $r \in [0,T]$, and let $\mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,s;r)v$ denote the solution of (equation eq:second-var of the paper)--(equation eq:Z-initial-cond of the paper) in the direction $a \in U$. Then for every $v \in H$ and every $a \in U$ this solution exists, is unique in $L^2(\Omega; C([\max(s,r),T]; H))$, and satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\Bigl[\sup_{\max(s,r) \leq \tau \leq t} \norm{\mathcal{Z}^{a}(\tau, s; r)v}_H^2\Bigr] \;\leq\; C\,\lVert v \rVert_H^2\,\lVert a \rVert_U^2 ,
\end{equation}
the constant depending on $T, r, s$ and on the constants of \textup{(SC1)}--\textup{(SC3)}. The directions assemble, $\mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)v = (a \mapsto \mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,s;r)v)$ lying in $L^2(\Omega; C([\max(s,r),T]; L_2(U,H)))$ with
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\Bigl[\sup_{\max(s,r) \leq \tau \leq t} \norm{\mathcal{Z}(\tau,s;r)v}_{L_2(U,H)}^2\Bigr] \;\leq\; C\,\lVert v \rVert_H^2 ,
\end{equation}
independently of the basis of $U$, and $(v,a) \mapsto \mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,s;r)v$ is bilinear. In the $r < s$ branch of (equation eq:Z-initial-cond of the paper) this holds for every $r$; in the $s \leq r$ branch, for every $r$ in Regime~A, where $\mathcal{B}'_u$ extends to $H$, and for almost every $r > s$ in Regime~B, where it acts only on $V$ (Remark~(rem:SC-raised-two-regimes of the paper)).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.711915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b7d83b90570a68d48bd5abccb1c4d271ae06901b2bb2511f8a3d4d6adc04d46","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l.expdecay of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Let $Q_1, Q_2$ be two marking functions on finite marked point processes. Let $\tilde{\mathcal{P}}$ be a marked point process such that the ground process $\mathcal{P}$ satisfies the Palm correlation bound~(equation e:bd_palmcorr of the paper) for $m =1$.
 Suppose for $p\in\mathbb{N}$, $i=1,2$ that there are   fast decreasing functions $\varphi_{i,p}$ as in (equation varphibd of the paper) such that 
\begin{equation}
\sup_{1 \leq n < \infty} \sup_{x_1,\ldots,x_p \in W_n} \mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p}
\bigl(Q_i(\tilde{x}_1, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n)>r\bigr) \le \varphi_{i,p}(r),\quad r > 0.
\end{equation}
 Then 
for all $p \in \mathbb{N}$ there are fast decreasing functions ~$\tilde\varphi_p$ such that
$$
\sup_{1 \leq n < \infty} \sup_{x_1,\ldots,x_p\in W_n}\mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p}\left( \max_{x \in \mathcal{P}_n :  |x - x_1| \leq Q_1(\tilde{x}_1, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n)} Q_2(\tilde{x}, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n) > r \right) \leq   \tilde{\varphi}_p(r), \quad r > 0.
$$
If the assumption (equation e:Q12tails of the paper) holds with the supremum taken up to $n = \infty$, i.e., $\sup_{1 \le n \le \infty}$, then the conclusion  remains valid with  $\sup_{1 \le n \le \infty}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.805831+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b7dffbbd184e9e19c2ad5c8b89755094b5456b4b055e06fb97c1c9284edbdf4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:Int-16 of arXiv:2606.23865, 'Uniform-in-time Gaussian fluctuations for multiscale nonlinear stochastic systems via Mall'] For $0 \le u \le s \le w \le r \le t$, let the term $\mathcal{J}_{16}(t;\varepsilon,\eta)$ be defined as follows:
\begin{multline*}
\mathcal{J}_{16}(t;\varepsilon,\eta)  \triangleq K \int_{u=0}^t \int_{s=u}^t \int_{w=s}^t \int_{r=w}^t  e^{-2 \alpha (t-r)} e^{- \alpha(t-s)} e^{- \alpha(t-w)}   e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (r-u)} e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (s-u)} e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (r-w)} \\
\times e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (w-s)}     dr \thinspace dw \thinspace ds \thinspace du.
\end{multline*}
Then, there exists a time-independent positive constant $K$ such that
$$ \mathcal{J}_{16}(t;\varepsilon,\eta) \le K \eta^3.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:38:58.732393+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b814bf5fc3fe7d94815fa74e266ecc3e4162a86b7af1a30bd1ca951d3c0d7de","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma starconvltnbnd of arXiv:2511.06321, 'Torus scaling limits and the plateau of the critical weakly coupled $|\varphi|^4$ model in'] If $\beta > 0$,  $c\ge 0$ and $a \ge 0$,  then for $t_1 + t_2 = t$,  
