{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.158427+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9c2b2b7b2f8ec3835dc21e2d77f3697abaee6574c5ad92ad75bc745bbc502c0f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2607.02406, 'On the range of competing random walks'] Under \ref{Assumption A1}, \ref{Assumption A2}, \ref{Assumption A3}, we have, for all $k\in\{1,\dots,N\}$,

    \begin{equation*}
    \frac{h(n)^2b(n)^d}{n^2}\left(\R_n^k-\EE\left[\R_n^k\right]\right)\distribution -\left(\gamma^k+\sum_{j\ne k} (\alpha^{j,k}-\EE[\alpha^{j,k}])\right)\left([0,1]_\le^2\right),
\end{equation*}
where $U^1,\dots,U^N$ are $i.i.d.$ copies of $U$ and $\alpha^{j,k}$, $\gamma^k$ denote respectively the intersection local time of $U^j,U^k$ and the renormalized self-intersection local time of $U^k$.

