{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.820898+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9c2cf1797e0f19e2abdfca0eff2c83090ad05b5ca11f909087550eae83ff57cb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:transfer-minors-master of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Let $1\leq s\leq k$, and let
$z^{(1)},\ldots,z^{(s)}\in\mathbb Z^k$ have coordinate sum zero.  Delete
the same $k-s$ coordinates from every row.  If $Q$ is the resulting
$s\times s$ matrix, then
\begin{equation}
 |\det Q|\leq\prod_{i=1}^s\rho(z^{(i)}).
\end{equation}
In particular, if every $z^{(i)}\in\mathcal D_{h,k}$, then
$|\det Q|\leq h^s$.","status":"unverified"}

