\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.234008+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9cb521a33f0f6f99e2497475e21412dbfd65631b37da60605c293055b8d3bb1b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 67 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] Let $\tau$ be a tracial state of $\A$, let $\l$ be its GNS representation, and let $\phi \in \B(\A,\rmM_k)_+$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[a.] Assume that $\phi$ is approximately associated to $\l^{\oplus \infty}$. Then, for every neighbourhood $O$ of $\phi$, there are a positive integer $d_0$ and a neighbourhood $V$ of $\tau$ such that the following holds:
\begin{quote}
If $\pi$ is a $d$-dimensional representation satisfying
\[d \ge d_0 \qquad \hbox{and} \qquad \tr_d\circ \pi \in V\]
then $\X(\pi,O) \ne \emptyset$.
\end{quote}
\item[b.] For every neighbourhood $O$ of $\phi$ there are a positive integer $d_0$ and neighbourhoods $U$ of $\phi_{\rm{sing}}$ and $V$ of $\tau$ such that the following holds:
\begin{quote}
If $\pi$ is a $d$-dimensional representation satisfying
\[d\ge d_0, \qquad \tr_d\circ \pi \in V, \qquad \hbox{and} \qquad \X(\pi,U) \ne \emptyset\]
then $\X(\pi,O) \ne \emptyset$.
\end{quote}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.887578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9cb65750d402d6219338b14fc16bacae386da09dde02b20cebffc0b79fde42b2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 (Properties of Poisson-Voronoi percolation) of arXiv:2504.02435, 'Poisson-Voronoi percolation in higher rank'] Let $G$ be a non-compact connected semisimple real Lie group, $(X,d_X)$ be its symmetric space, $\lambda>0$ and $p\in (0,1)$.
    Then $\omega_p^{(\lambda)}$ defines a random closed subset of $X$ which
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[{\rm (i)}] is $G$-invariant and ergodic, and, moreover, is invariant under all isometries of $(X,d_X)$.
        \item[{\rm (ii)}] satisfies the Harris-FKG-inequality.
        \item[{\rm (iii)}] satisfies $\mathbb P\big(o\in \partial \omega_p^{(\lambda)}\big)=0$.
    \end{itemize}
    Moreover, the Voronoi diagram $\mathrm{Vor}(\mathbf{Y}^{(\lambda)})$ consists of compact subsets of $X$.","status":"unverified"}

