{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.184441+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9d780e5d7b136dcdd248271f614187697784485d7f1e64945f22170790571c56","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2608.26974, 'Why not to use the Gaussian kernel'] Consider a strictly positive-definite stationary kernel $k(x, y) = \Phi(x - y)$. If $\sigma = 0$ and $X_{n+1} = \{x_1, \ldots, x_{n+1}\} \subset \R^d$, then
