{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.857026+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9e9c22ddb6bf1d090e477cce3de26a26a3bc0e16011c4a7c5ace6fbdb3fbfc2b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2511.20519, '\textbf{High-dimensional limits arising from \\ hyperbolic Poisson $k$-plane processes}'] Let \((X_n)_{n\geq 1}\) be a sequence of random variables.
    Assume that their characteristic functions are of the form
    \[ \varphi_{X_n}(t) = \exp(\Psi_n(t)) = \exp\Big(\int_\R (e^{itx} - 1 - itx) \,\nu_n(\dint x)\Big), \quad t \in \R,\]
    with measures \(\nu_n\) on $\R$ that satisfy
    \begin{equation}
        \int_\R x^2 \,\nu_n(\dint x) = 1.
    \end{equation} 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=\emph{(\alph*)}]
    \item If
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{\{|x| > \varepsilon\}} x^2 \,\nu_n(\dint x) \to 0 \quad \text{for all} \quad \varepsilon > 0,
    \end{equation}
    then $X_n \xrightarrow{D}\mathcal{N}(0,1)$.
    \item If
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{\{|x| \leq \varepsilon\}} x^2 \,\nu_n(\dint x) \to 0 \quad \text{for all} \quad \varepsilon > 0,
    \end{equation}
    then $X_n \xrightarrow{D} 0$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.740320+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ea35052f1da8ed224e5328a2067f94486f62345734f9d778bb9edf77be241ac","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:CapVolume of arXiv:2411.19163, '\bfseries Random polytopes in convex bodies:\\ Bridging the gap between extremal container'] For any $\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{S}^{m-1}_+$, ${\boldsymbol{\beta}}\in (-1,\infty)^m$ and $s\in(s_1(\mathbf{v}),\|\mathbf{v}\|_1)$ we have
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}_{m}(C^+(\mathbf{v},s);{\boldsymbol{\beta}}) 
             &\asymp(\|\mathbf{v}\|_1-s)^{\beta+m} \prod_{i=1}^m v_i^{-\beta_i-1}.
    \end{align*}
    Note that $s\in (s_1(\mathbf{v}),\|\mathbf{v}\|_1)$ implies in particular that the latter interval is not empty and $\|\mathbf{v}\|_1\neq s_1(\mathbf{v})$, which is equivalent to $v_i>0$ for all $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.799799+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ea3b0b14189c0efe981756ac07482af123e39c762d7d6da0b328caa89464242","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Nkq<1 of arXiv:2509.24452, 'A family of non-uniform distributions on the set of parking functions generated by random '] Consider $N^{(n)}_k$ as in (equation Nk of the paper)  under the measure $\mathcal{P}_n^{(q)}$ with $q<1$.

\noindent i. For fixed $k$, $\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{N^{(n)}_k}n\stackrel{\text{\rm dist}}{=}(1-q)q^{k-1}$;

\noindent ii. If $\lim_{n\to\infty} nq^{k_n}=\infty$, then $\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{N^{(n)}_{k_n}}{nq^{k_n}}\stackrel{\text{\rm dist}}{=}\frac{1-q}q$.

\noindent iii. If $L:=\lim_{n\to\infty} nq^{k_n}\in(0,\infty)$, then $N^{(n)}_{k_n}$ converges in distribution to the Poisson distribution with parameter $\frac{1-q}qL$;

\noindent iv. If $\lim_{n\to\infty} nq^{k_n}=0$, then $\lim_{n\to\infty}N^{(n)}_{k_n}\stackrel{\text{\rm dist}}{=}0$; (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.773934+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ea4e98d8c8fffcf4c19fd06de6e66650e4bafde1bccaa0004127bfb14283d83","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:double-well-vanish of arXiv:2411.01364, 'Global Dynamics of Granular Media Equations via Stochastic Order'] Consider the following one-dimensional McKean-Vlasov SDE,
\begin{equation}
\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} X_t=-\left[V'(X_t)+\theta\left(X_t-\mathbf E [X_t]\right)\right]\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} t+\sigma(X_t)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} B_t.
\end{equation}
where
\[
V'(x)=
\begin{cases}
    x\big(x-\frac{31}{32}\big)\big(x+\frac{31}{32}\big), & x\in \big(-\frac{31}{32},\frac{31}{32}\big),\\
    0, & x\in \big(-1,-\frac{31}{32}\big]\cup\big[\frac{31}{32},1\big),\\
    x(x-1)(x+1), & x\in (-\8,-1]\cup [1, \8).
\end{cases}
\]
If the parameter $\theta$ and the Lipschitz function $\sigma\colon\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
    \theta\geq\frac{27(9+\sqrt{17})}{128},\quad 0<\inf_{x\in\mathbb R}\sigma^2(x)\leq \sup_{x\in\mathbb R}\sigma^2(x)<\frac{51\sqrt{17}-171}{256},
\end{equation}
and let $r=\frac{9-\sqrt{17}}{8}$, then the exactly same results as in Theorem (thm:double-well of the paper) are valid. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:51:00.237820+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ea7adee80b43f465e4595823f00fac27b5c14c3bd2623f370dba24fbe007076","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:psi_def of arXiv:2604.22528, 'Malliavin calculus for signatures with applications to finance'] Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and words ${\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{w_1}}}, \ldots, {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{w_n}}} \in V$ and ${\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_1}}}, \ldots, {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_n}}} \in V$. We define the switching operator 
    ${\bf\Psi}_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_1}}}, \ldots, {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_n}}}}^{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{w_1}}}, \ldots, {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{w_n}}}}: \eTA \to \eTA$ by
    \begin{equation}
        {\bf\Psi}_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_1}}}, \ldots, {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_n}}}}^{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{w_1}}}, \ldots, {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{w_n}}}} \colon 
        \mathbbm{x} \mapsto \sum_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v_0}}}, \ldots, {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v_n}}}} \mathbbm{x}^{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v_0 w_1 v_1 \ldots w_n v_n}}}} 
        ({\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v_0}}} {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_1}}} {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v_1}}} \ldots {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{u_n}}} {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v_n}}}).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.827411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ead321b55220055735312a6eea92ea9c05d7e2528b61bef3fa769aac155c03a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 of arXiv:2604.10395, 'A remark on the comparison of the sum and the maximum of positive random variables'] Assume that $X_1$ has a strictly positive density $f$ supported on
$(0,\infty)$ and that there exist four positive constants $c_1, c_2, \alpha$, and $\beta$ such that 
\begin{equation} 
\lim_{x \to +0} \frac{f(x)}{x^{\alpha-1}} = c_1.  
\end{equation}  
\begin{equation}
\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{-\log f(x)}{x^{\beta}} = c_2.  
\end{equation}  
Assume that $n \ge 2$ and 
\begin{equation}
\beta < \frac{\log n}{\log n + h(\alpha) - h(n\alpha)}. 
\end{equation}
Then there is no constant $C$ such that $S_n = CM_n$ in distribution. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.655008+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9eb2d0f1d726a01cdd75f37d7b48fd1346cd035b9e8c6f7f96f7f507e09cd343","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition al-converges of arXiv:2603.01823, 'on a fractional stochastic heat equation arising from the disordered pinning model'] For $k_0\in 2\mathbb N$ and $\rho_{k_0,L}$ defined in (equation e:rho-kL of the paper), if $\rho_{k_0,L}\in \mathcal{H}^{\otimes k_0}$, then for all integers $1\le k\le k_0$,
\begin{enumerate}

