{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.329948+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ed2dea85d3606a5f828c21c57343b67996c19157f33fde6d187b4dd09252810","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] An $E$-valued strong Markov process $Z=(Z_t)_{t\geq 0}$ defined on some (filtered) probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},(\mathcal{F}_t)_{t\geq 0},\mathbb{P})$, with probabilities $(\mathbb{P}_x, x\in E)$, is said to be a \textit{self-similar Markov process} if it satisfies the \textit{self-similarity property}: there exists an $\alpha>0$ such that for every $c>0$ and $x\in E$,
	\begin{equation}

		(\{cZ_{c^{-\alpha}t},t\geq0\},\mathbb{P}_x)\stackrel{\text{d}}{=}(\{Z_t,t\geq0\},\mathbb{P}_{cx}).
