{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.718977+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fbeb913f000874d06580ecda9c7f3b0e0cd03e2324e80f01d9f515444fba62c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem BRW.lowdim of arXiv:2411.19145, 'On the intersection of critical percolation clusters and other tree-like random graphs'] One has 
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb P(|\mathcal R_c \cap \widetilde {\mathcal R}_c|>t) \asymp     
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
t^{-4/d} & \text{if }d\in \{1,2,3\} \\
t^{-1}\cdot (\log t)^{-2} & \text{if }d=4 \\
t^{-\frac 4{8-d}} & \text{if }d\in \{5,6,7\}. 
\end{array}
\right. 
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, if $d=8$, there exist positive constants $c_1$ and $c_2$, such that for any $t>1$, 
\begin{equation}
\exp(-c_1\sqrt t) \le \mathbb P(|\mathcal R_c \cap \widetilde {\mathcal R}_c|>t) 
\le \exp\big(-c_2\cdot t^{1/3}\big). \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.817667+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fc43b86aa9b248ecc60df6eef6b69d111ce7665468f565c1435801ba57b9521","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition L:compact-family-split-localization of arXiv:2607.17113, 'Two-time spatial decorrelation for the flat KPZ fixed point'] Fix $0<s<t$ and put $\rho=s/t$ and $m_{s,t}(z,x)=(1-\rho)z+\rho x$.  Fix an
  endpoint $x_0\in\mathbb{R}$, and let $Y^-_{z,x_0}\le Y^+_{z,x_0}$ be the leftmost and
  rightmost maximizers of
  \[
    y\mapsto \mathcal{L}(z,0;y,s)+\mathcal{L}(y,s;x_0,t).
  \]
  For all $R,U\ge1$,
  \begin{align}
    &\mathbb{P}\Bigg(
      \begin{aligned}
      &\{\exists |z-x_0|\le R:
        Y^+_{z,x_0}-m_{s,t}(z,x_0)>U\}\\
      &{}\cup\{\exists |z-x_0|\le R:
        Y^-_{z,x_0}-m_{s,t}(z,x_0)<-U\}
      \end{aligned}
    \Bigg)\notag\\
    &\qquad \le
    C\Big(1+\frac{R}{U}\Big)^2\exp\{-cU^3/t^2\},

  \end{align}
  where $C,c$ depend only on $s,t$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.520509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fc65cac277cabfcc997b0e8765eb8037f0af02c79aee1e1c96a196ea45aeea1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:finite_failure of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{leaving_giant}
    Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \geq 1$ and suppose that $J : V \times V \to \mathbf{R}_+$ is a transitive kernel satisfying $J(x,y) = \Omega(d_G(x,y)^{-d \alpha})$ with $\alpha \in (0,2)$.

    Let $\beta > \beta_c$.

    There exists $c > 0$ such that for all $1\le r< R<\infty$

    \begin{equation}

        \mathbf{P}_{\beta}
        \left(  
            \giantcomp{x}{r}
            \not\subseteq
            K_{R}^{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}
        \right)   
        \leq 
        \exp
        \left(
            -
            c
            \# 
            B(r)^{\min(2-\alpha,1)}
        \right)
    \end{equation}

    for all $x \in V$ with $x \in B(R-r)$.

    In particular, there exists $c > 0$ such that for all $r \in \mathbf{N}$

    \begin{equation}

        \mathbf{P}_{\beta}
        \left(  
            K_{r}^{\scriptscriptstyle (1)}
            \not\subseteq
            K_{\infty}
        \right)   
        \leq 
        \exp
        \left(
            -
            c
            \# 
            B(r)^{\min(2-\alpha,1)}
        \right).
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:27:56.794161+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fc81203f1280976a9bde96b22bb4787ec27bb97a3b64458bf033b9e930a4c76","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2609.00209, 'Exit Times for Brownian Motion and Location Detection'] Let $\mathcal P$ denote our usual parabolic domain and let $u_1^{\mathcal P}(x,y)=\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_{\mathcal P}]$ and $u_2^{\mathcal P}(x,y)=\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_{\mathcal P}^2]$ denote the first two exit time moments of Brownian motion in $\mathcal P$. Then
\begin{align*}
u_1^{\mathcal P}(x,y)&=\frac{1}{2c}(y-cx^2),\\
u_2^{\mathcal P}(x,y)&=\frac{1}{6c}\left(5y-cx^2+\frac{5}{c}\right)u_1^{\mathcal P}(x,y).
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.846771+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fc9e1751e6e1516c0281be7d0c97f128683f2d5bd6abb47a96ff888a0093f29","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2511.10535, 'Eigenvalues of Brownian Motions on $\mathrm{GL}(N,\mathbb{C})$'] For any $k\in\N$ and 
    $0\le t\le T$
    there exists a constant 

    $C_{k,T}$ such that
	$$ \sup_{n,N\geq 1} 
    \|B^N_n(t)\|_{2k} 
    \leq C_{k,T}.$$
    The same holds with $B^N_n(t)$ replaced by $b_n(t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.981237+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fcc3d1f57703a80306354f7463e347fb1dad1b2bfb6339d636c5045ac94ef77","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:truncated_expectation_diff_ineq_at_doubling_scales2 of arXiv:2508.18809, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation III: The upper critical dimension}'] If $\alpha\leq d/3$ then there exist positive constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ such that
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial r} \log \tilde \mathbb E_{r,\lambda} |K| 
&\geq c_1 r^{-\alpha-1}  \tilde \mathbb E_{r,\lambda} \min\{|K|, c_1 r^{(d+\alpha)/2}\} \geq c_2 r^{-1}
\end{align*}
for every $r\geq 1$ and $0\leq \lambda \leq 1$. Consequently, 
there exists a positive constant $c_3$ such that
\[
\tilde \mathbb E_{2r,\lambda}|K| \geq (1+c_3) \tilde \mathbb E_{r,\lambda}|K|
\]
for every $0\leq \lambda \leq 1$ and $r\geq 1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.530153+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fccbcafc4a601d9e5ddd906191eeb90a7b6032dd6ee931dc36ea4050b66064d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Deff def:equiv-lambda-def of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Define the system

\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        du(\cdot) &= [A_uu(\cdot) + \hat f(\cdot,z(\cdot),\theta)u(\cdot)]dt + \hat \sigma(\cdot,z(\cdot),\theta)u(\cdot)H_udW_t \\
        dz(\cdot) &= [A_zz(\cdot) + f_z(\cdot,u(\cdot),z(\cdot),\theta))]dt + \sigma_z(\cdot,u(\cdot),z(\cdot),\theta)H_zdW_t \,,
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
obtained from (equation SPDE of the paper) by linearizing in $u$ in the first $d$ components. By this we mean that $\hat f,\hat \sigma: D \times \mathbb{R}^{m-d} \times \Theta \to \mathbb{R}^d$ are calculated from $f_u,\sigma_u$ by ignoring any term which is nonlinear in $u$. Then we define
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \Lambda &\coloneqq \sup_\nu \mathbb{E}_\nu\Big[-\int_D \sum_{i=1}^d A_iv_i(\cdot) + \hat f_i(\cdot,z(\cdot),\theta)v_i(\cdot) d\mu(\cdot)\\
    &+ \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n \geq 1} a_n^2\Big(\int_D \sum_{i=1}^d\hat \sigma_i(\cdot,z(\cdot),
    \theta)v_i(\cdot)(e_n)_i(\cdot)d\mu(\cdot)\Big)^2\Big] \,,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $v = u/\|u\|_{L^1}$, $(v,z,\theta) \sim \nu$, and $\nu$ ranges over all invariant measures (stationary distributions) on $\{(v,z) \in C(\overline D)_+ \mid \|v\|_{L^1} = 1\} \times \Theta$ for $(u_t/\|u_t\|_{L^1},z_t,\theta)$, where $(u_t,z_t,\theta)$ solves (equation eq:general-linearized-SPDE of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.985091+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fcd6f20333616c49b6b708c98fed85a8cec3b833eab1b3d80257f0e06bdb8ff","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 11 (Realization of an arbitrary finite Gram matrix) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] Let $H$ be an infinite-dimensional real Hilbert space, let
$G=(G_{ab})_{1\le a,b\le d}$ be positive semidefinite, and let $q\ge1$.
There are kernels $u_{N,a}\in H^{\odot q}$ such that
\begin{align}
 q!\ip{u_{N,a}}{u_{N,b}}&=G_{ab},
 \\
 \norm{u_{N,a}\otimes_ru_{N,b}}&=O(N^{-1/2})
 \qquad(1\le r<q).

\end{align}
Consequently $(I_q(u_{N,a}))_{a\le d}\weak N_d(0,G)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.706816+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fcee45e4a167796c6db2ff5ad24bbf13847f00351d982e20b31b268b7d17707","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma distribution of Y ;lemma of arXiv:2605.29095, 'Asymptotics of the number of components of random polynomial lemniscates'] For $r \in (0,1)$, the distribution function of $Y_k(r)$ satisfies  
\begin{align}
    F_r(t) = \mathbb{P}(Y_k(r) \leq t ) = 
    \begin{cases}
         \quad\frac{1}{4(r-t)^2},  \quad &\textit{if}  \quad  \quad  -\infty < t \leq r - \frac{1}{1+r}\\ 
         \quad1- \frac{1}{4(r-t)^2},  \quad \quad &\textit{if}  \quad \quad   r + \frac{1}{1-r} \leq t < \infty,
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
and, for $r< t < r + \frac{1}{1-r}$ 
\begin{align}
     \mathbb{P}(Y_k(r) > t ) \leq 1 \wedge \frac{1}{4(t-r)^2}.
\end{align}
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width = 0.445\textwidth]{Distribution_of_Y_left.png}
    \includegraphics[width = 0.51\textwidth]{Distribution_for_Y_right.png}
    \caption{The unit circle is shown in black, together with curves $\mathcal{C}_t$ for various values of $t$, corresponding to the tail probabilities of $Y_r(k)$: left-sided tail (left) and right-sided tail (right).
    }
    \end{figure}
As a consequence, there exists absolute constants $C_1, C_2, >0$ such that for all $m \geq 2 $, 

\begin{equation}
    \biggl|\int_{-m}^m x dF_r(x)\biggr| \leq \frac{C_1}{m}, 
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
   C_2 \log m \leq   \biggl|\int_{-m}^m x^2 dF_r(x)\biggr| \leq C_1 \log m.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.241450+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fd49308554e4e3ce7db09ef259d0d2557c46fb8ebd87fcbec7b1e67b1da0327","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 (Schur reordering) of arXiv:2605.25715, '2605.25715'] Let $G$ be a real $d\times d$ matrix and $\Sigma\subset \sigma(G)$ be a complex conjugation closed subset of its spectrum (counting multiplicities). Then, there exists an orthogonal matrix $U$ such that $R:=U^T GU$ is in real Schur form (i.e. quasi upper triangular  with blocks of  size $1\times 1$ corresponding to real eigenvalues and blocks of  size $2\times 2$ corresponding to complex conjugate pairs of eigenvalues on the diagonal) and admits the following block partition 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R=\begin{pmatrix}R_{11}&R_{12}\\ 0&R_{22}\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\quad \sigma(R_{11})=\Sigma, \quad \sigma(R_{22})=\sigma(G)\setminus \Sigma,
\end{eqnarray*}
where $R_{11}$ and $R_{22}$ are square matrices.","status":"unverified"}

