{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:29:14.930143+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fec335b2bc0f1ad0350e466288b54fcaae37863f85a985c8ad5bec4f0c9597c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop.relaxation of arXiv:2606.13442, 'Scaling limit of additive functionals for reversible non-gradient exclusion process: criti'] There exists a positive exponent $\delta(d,\mathbf{r}, c_{-},c_+)$, such that for every local function $f$, we have 
		\begin{align}
			\mathbb{V}\!\mathrm{ar}_{\rho}[P_t f] = \frac{\overline{f}' (\rho)^2 \chi(\rho)}{\sqrt{(8\pi t)^{d} \det [\mathbf{D}(\rho)]}} + o(t^{-\frac{d+\delta}{2}}).
		\end{align}
		Here the remainder depends on $f$ and satisfies ${\lim_{t \to \infty}t^{\frac{d+\delta}{2}}o(t^{-\frac{d+\delta}{2}}) = 0}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.392176+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fece3586d64d29c962bdb1a1bf4c1830e352b1b4c72ff4a9077f148a1d5650e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:Dobrushin-k>2 of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] Fix $k\ge 2$.
Let $(\beta_N)_{N\ge1}$ be any sequence such that $\beta_N\to0$ and
$\sup_{N\ge1}\beta_N N^{1/8}<\infty$.
Set $\mathfrak s_N\coloneqq \sinh(\beta_N)$.
Let $f,h:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{R}$ be finite-support functions, with $f$ satisfying
$\sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}}f(x)=0$.
Consider $N^{-1/2}\mathbf x^N\to\mathbf z\in\mathbb{R}^k$.
Then, under $\mathbf P_{\mathbf x^N}^{(\beta_N,k)}$, the processes
\begin{equation}
	\big(
	N^{-1/2}\tttonde{\mathbf R_{Nt}-tN\mathfrak s_N\mathbf 1}
	\big)_{t\ge0}\; \text{,}\;\;
	\qquad
	\big(
	\tttonde{
	N^{-1/2}\textstyle{\int_0^{Nt}}h(R_s^i-R_s^j)\,{\rm d} s
	}_{1\le i<j\le k}
	\big)_{t\ge0}\; \text{,}\;\;
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
	\big(
	\tttonde{
	N^{-1/4}\textstyle{\int_0^{Nt}}f(R_s^i-R_s^j)\,{\rm d} s
	}_{1\le i<j\le k}
	\big)_{t\ge0}\; \text{,}\;\;
\end{equation}
jointly converge in law in $\ensuremath{\mathcal D}([0,\infty);\mathbb{R}^{d_k})$, with $d_k\coloneqq k+k(k-1)$, respectively to
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		\left(
		\mathbf W_t\right)_{t\ge 0}\; \text{,}\;\;\qquad
		\big(\tttonde{\gamma^2(h)L_t^{W^i-W^j}}_{1\le i<j\le k}\big)_{t\ge 0}\; \text{,}\;\;\qquad
		\big(\tttonde{\sigma(f)B^{ij}_{L_t^{W^i-W^j}}}_{1\le i<j\le k}
		\big)_{t\ge0}\; \text{.}
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathbf W=(W^1,\ldots,W^k)$ is a standard $k$-dimensional Brownian motion started from ${\mathbf z}\in\mathbb{R}^k$, the processes $(B^{ij})_{1\le i<j\le k}$ are independent standard Brownian motions independent of $\mathbf W$, and the noise coefficients $\gamma^2(h)$ and $\sigma(f)$ are given, respectively, in (equation eq:W+local-time of the paper) and (equation eq:sigma-squared of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.901496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fef6adb501507b2e2f0d3f6aaac5e6e4282f8ac0d813c0c2ab15e27aeefebcc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem1.3 of arXiv:2411.11408, 'Multidimensional specific relative entropy between continuous martingales'] For a random vector $(V,W)$ we denote by $\mu_{V,W}$ its law and by $\mu_W^{V=v}$ a
regular conditional distribution of $W$ given $V=v$. Let $(X_1,\dots,X_n)$ and $(Y_1,\dots,Y_n)$ be random vectors. Then:
\begin{equation*} \begin{split}
&H(\mu_{X_1, \dots, X_n}|\mu _{Y_1,\dots,Y_n})= H(\mu_{X_1}|\mu_{Y_1})+\\ &\textstyle +\sum _{k=2}^n \int H\big( \mu _{X_k}^{X_1=x_1,\dots,X_{k-1}=x_{k-1}}|\mu _{Y_k}^{Y_1=x_1,\dots,Y_{k-1}=x_{k-1}}\big)d\mu_{X_1, \dots, X_{k-1}}(x_1,\dots,x_{k-1}),
\end{split} \end{equation*}
which   we can also write as: 
\begin{equation*} \begin{split} 
&\textstyle H\big(\mathcal{L}((X_1, \dots,X_n))|\mathcal{L}((Y_1, \dots,Y_n))\big)=\\ &\textstyle H(\mathcal{L}(X_1)|\mathcal{L}(Y_1))
+\sum _{k=2}^n \int H\Big(\mathcal{L}(X_k|X_1=x_1, \dots , X_{k-1}=x_{k-1})\\&\textstyle | \,\mathcal{L}(Y_k|Y_1=x_1, \dots , Y_{k-1}=x_{k-1}) \Big) d\mathcal{L}(X_1, \dots,X_{k-1} )dx_1 \dots dx_{
k-1}.
\end{split}\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.815328+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fef9a9c59935d6b75c5c20ff92ac7c667c24aacaae07e1aecf56f97b8a3e004","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:BP_cT_n of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] The $n$-dependent branching process $\mathcal T_n$ is defined as follows.
  \begin{itemize}
      \item The root $\varphi$ of this process has type $s_0$, and has a random number of offspring of type $s_1$ given by the random variable $D_{n, (s_0, s_1)}$. We also say $\{\varphi\}$ is the zeroth generation $Z_0$ of $\mathcal T_n$.
      \item  For any $r\geq 1$ an integer, let us write
      \begin{align*}
          r_l:=r\bmod l.
      \end{align*}
      Inductively, the set of all vertices at generation $k$ for $k \geq 1$ is denoted by $Z_k$, and has type $k_l$, and each of them has a random number of offspring $D_{n, s_{(k-1)_l},s_{k_l},s_{(k+1)_l}}$. Often we lighten notation, and simply write $s_k$ for $s_{k_l}$ (this should not create confusion; recall the subscripts of $s$ are always $\bmod l$).
  \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.504850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ff296724b3998edf1f367573320d059e31182e2673da459e061b8497c160df1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 33 of arXiv:2608.18075, '2608.18075'] Let the outer endpoint set be
\begin{align}
 C_{\rm out}
 =
 \overline{
 \{\Phi(z,y):z\in\overline{\mathcal U}_{R},\ y\in\mathfrak L\}
 }^{\,H^b\times\PP(H)}.
\end{align}
Then $C_{\rm out}$ is compact and the Lyapunov function $\mathcal W$ from
\Cref{cor:revision-conditional-Lyapunov} is bounded on $C_{\rm out}$.
More precisely, for some $\epsilon>0$,
\begin{align}
 \sup_{z\in\overline{\mathcal U}_{R},\,y\in\mathfrak L}
 \left(
 \|w_\tau(z,y)\|_{c^\beta}
 +
 \left\|
 \frac{U_{w^\circ(z,y)}(\tau,0)\mathsf p}
      {\|U_{w^\circ(z,y)}(\tau,0)\mathsf p\|_2}
 \right\|_{H^{1+\epsilon}}
 \right)<\infty,
\end{align}
where $\mathsf p$ is any unit representative of the projective component
of $z$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:32:58.363072+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ff42df679e45ee3dbdd654888c789e1af156a94dff8732922914fab7d15ddf8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Graphical visualization of certain generalized diagrams of arXiv:2506.06559, 'Bi-infinite incipient cluster in high dimensions'] The diagram of a diagrammatic graph ${\bf G}$ is simply represented by its multigraph component $G$ where the vertices in $S$ are labelled according to $\ell$.

If $\mathcal{G}$ is a generalized diagrammatic graph, then
\begin{itemize}
\item if $G'$ is a fully-labeled diagrammatic graph, the graphical representation of $\mathcal{G}\uplus G'$ is that of $\mathcal{G}$ where we take the union of the two graphs but identify vertices with the same label,
\item if $u\in \mathbb{Z}^d$, for the graphical representation of $\mathcal{G} \circledast u$, we add a label, say $A$, to each edge in the graphical representation of $\mathcal{G}$ and we add a point $u$ connected to an \lq\lq edge\rq\rq~whose other endpoint will be labelled \lq\lq$A?$\rq\rq. That last edge is called a connecting edge and can be labelled in future iterations.
\end{itemize} 

Since a generalized diagrammatic graph is obtained as an iteration of the two previous iterations, we can give a graphical visualization of generalized diagrammatic graphs. We refer to this graphical visualization as a generalized diagram.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:11.411844+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ff487da8965dfde7921c1c28846d999ee852fecdbd82fed1c245652c55245f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 24 (Decorated phase-star subatlas theorem) of arXiv:2606.07931, 'Pointwise Complexity for Gaussian Fields:\\Upper Envelopes, Algorithmic Lower Bounds, and '] Fix \(N,K\ge1\) and \(\kappa>0\) with \(\kappa(K+1)\ge1\).  Let \(G_{N,K,\kappa}\) have root \(v_0\), phase centers \(c_1,\ldots,c_N\), and local chart vertices \(z_{a,1},\ldots,z_{a,K}\).  The root is connected to each \(c_a\) by conductance one, and
\[
C_a:=\{c_a,z_{a,1},\ldots,z_{a,K}\}
\]
is a complete graph with edge conductance \(\kappa\).  For \(I\in\mathcal I\), set \(H_{I,K}:=\bigcup_{a\in I}C_a\), and define
\begin{equation}
\mu_{q,K}:=\frac12\delta_{v_0}
+\frac14\mathrm{Unif}\!\left(\bigcup_{a=1}^N C_a\right)
+\frac14\sum_{I\in\mathcal I}q(I)\,\mathrm{Unif}\!\left(\bigcup_{a\in I}C_a\right).
\end{equation}
Put
\begin{equation}
\mathcal E_{I,K}(\delta):=
\sqrt{\log\frac{4|I|}{q(I)}}+
\sqrt{\frac{1}{\kappa(K+1)}\log\frac{4|I|(K+1)}{q(I)}}+
\sqrt{\log\!\left(\frac{2+\log\log((N+3)(K+3))}{\delta}\right)}.
\end{equation}
Then the full-graph pointwise envelope with prior \(\mu_{q,K}\) satisfies, with probability at least \(1-\delta\), simultaneously for all \(I\in\mathcal I\),
\begin{equation}
\sup_{x\in H_{I,K}}|\eta_x|\le C\mathcal E_{I,K}(\delta).
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every \(\theta\in(0,1/\sqrt2)\),
\begin{equation}
t\ge C_\theta \mathcal E_{I,K}(\delta)^2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\mathbb P\{\tau_{H_{I,K}}\le\tau(t)\}\ge1-\delta.
\end{equation}
Finally,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb P\{\{c_1,\ldots,c_N\}\subseteq X[0,\tau(t)]\}=(1-e^{-t})^N,
\end{equation}
so full cover at confidence \(1-\delta\) must pay inverse-root-local-time \(\log N+O_\delta(1)\).  In particular, if \(\mathcal E_{I,K}(\delta)^2=o(\log N)\), the selected target-cover route is asymptotically smaller than the full-cover route. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.461870+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ff72fba678f9b773b0039039ead0e356137f8eab3388f2492773ba12b778758","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemperturbation of arXiv:2505.11721, 'Variational principles for Hausdorff and packing dimensions of fractal percolation on self'] Let $N\ge \epsilon^{-1}$. If $\boldsymbol{p}=(p^{(n)})_{n\in\mathbb{N}^+}\in \mathscr{Q}^{\ell,\epsilon,\eta,\lfloor\Lambda_a N\rfloor}_{\mathcal{I}}$, set $\boldsymbol{p}_\epsilon=(p_\epsilon^{(n)})_{n\in\mathbb{N}^+}$, where 
$$
p_\epsilon^{(n)}=
\begin{cases}
p_{\max}&\text{if }  1\le n\le   \lfloor N\epsilon\rfloor\\
(1-\lambda\epsilon)p^{(n)}+\lambda\epsilon p_{\max}&\text{if } \lfloor N\epsilon\rfloor+1\le n\le  \lfloor\Lambda_aN\rfloor  \text{ and } H(W_{\boldsymbol{p}}^{(n)})\le H_{\max}/2\\
p^{(n)}&\text{otherwise}.
