{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.698257+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a01b21849eceb8c5ef509ce762379597aa7e1624aefb5985f2c9dcb2cb5f1eb9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 21 (Well defined products) of arXiv:2608.30990, 'Convergence of symmetries: nilpotency, dimension, and perfectness'] For $g_1, \ldots , g_n \in G$, we say the product $g_1 \cdots g_n$ is \emph{well defined} if for each $1 \leq i \leq j \leq n $ there is an element $g_{[i,j]} \in G$ such that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For each $1 \leq i \leq n$, one has  $g_{[i,i]} = g_i$.
        \item If $1 \leq i \leq j < k \leq n$, one has $(g_{[i,j]}, g_{[j+1, k]}) \in \Omega$ and 
        \[    g_{[i,k]} = m  (g_{[i,j]}, g_{[j+1, k]})   .   \]
    \end{itemize}
    In such a case, the element $g_{[1,n]} $ is uniquely defined and is denoted by $g_1\cdots g_n$. If in addition one has  $g_1 = \cdots = g_n = g$, the product $g_1 \cdots g_n $ is denoted by $g^n$. 

    For a subset $U \subset G$, by an abuse of notation we write $g_1 \cdots g_n \in U$ to denote the assertion that the product $g_1 \cdots g_n$ is well defined and it is in $U$.

    For subsets $W , $ $ U \subset G$ and $n \in \mathbb{N}$, we also  write $W ^n \subset U$ to denote the assertion that for any $g_1, \ldots , g_n \in W$ we have $g_1 \cdots g_n \in  U$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:22:39.764915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a01b30fa2f19c27588a1db181b0f8910bc11edb9b35bf815c8fb5fa1d3c06c71","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:8= of arXiv:2508.21658, 'Infinite-dimensional stochastic differential equations for Coulomb random point fields'] For $ \mu $-a.s.\,$ \aaa $, all $ \rR \in \N $, and $ \mua $-a.s.\,$ \yy $, $ \dlog _{\aRy } $ satisfies 
