{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:03:35.813348+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0f98ff8c6d1ef048613823d3770db6be195c388ec9fd03cb62e382d02f0bd9e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:localized-to-global-comparison of arXiv:2608.22910, 'A Commutator Framework for Selective Spectral Alignment in Deep Neural Networks'] At a fixed time, let \(A_\ell(x)\) be symmetric and square-integrable,
possibly independent of \(x\), and set
\begin{align*}
\mathcal E_{A,\ell}^{(k)}
&:=
\int\|\Pi_\ell^{(k)}A_\ell
\Pi_\ell^{(k),\perp}\|_F^2\,\, \mathrm{d}\mu,\qquad \mathcal C_{A,\ell}
:=
\int\|[A_\ell,P_\ell]\|_F^2\,\, \mathrm{d}\mu,
\end{align*}
where the integrals are omitted when \(A_\ell\) is deterministic. If
\(n=m_{\ell+1}\) and
\(\underline\gamma_\ell:=\min_{1\leq k<n}\gamma_\ell^{(k)}>0\), then
\begin{equation}
2\underline\gamma_\ell^2
\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\mathcal E_{A,\ell}^{(k)}
\leq
\mathcal C_{A,\ell}
\leq
2\|P_\ell\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2
\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\mathcal E_{A,\ell}^{(k)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.489826+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0fac2659c55cc1080e0a9f8441faa46230a96db6b12aa15506409ca1a38e607","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 214 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] The fundamental solution of the master problem is the $H^2$-tuple of functions $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}(z_1,z_2) = \big(\mathcal{F}_{h_1,h_2}(z_1,z_2)\big)_{h_1,h_2 = 1}^{H}$ given in terms of the operator \eqref{eq_solution_Master_equation} by
\begin{equation}

\forall h_1,h_2 \in [H] \qquad \mathcal{F}_{h_1,h_2}(z_1,z_2) =
\Op_{h_1}\left[\frac{\sqrt{(* - \alpha_{h_2})(* - \beta_{h_2})}}{2(*- z_2)^2}\boldsymbol{e}^{(h_2)}\,;\,\boldsymbol{\kappa} = \boldsymbol{0}\right](z_1),
\end{equation}
where $*$ is a placeholder for the variable in which this tuple is seen as a function on which the operator $\Upsilon_{h_1}$ is applied, and $\boldsymbol{e}^{(1)},\ldots,\boldsymbol{e}^{(H)}$ is the canonical basis of $\amsmathbb{R}^H$.","status":"unverified"}

