{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:27.994667+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1a4eadd13cb6cadc683723be8d48dd0a45d7d140d5cda9b5041e481832165a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem mk1k2inftyIIthm of arXiv:2608.04730, 'A new look at some aspects of one-dimensional random sequential adsorption and its continu'] Let $m_{k_1;k_2;II}$ and $m_{k_1;k_2;II_1}$ be as in Theorem (k1k2IIthm of the paper).
One has
\begin{equation}
m_{k_1,\infty;II_1}:=\lim_{k_2\to\infty}m_{k_1,k_2;II_1}=D\approx 0.4166,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
m_{k_1,\infty;II}:=\lim_{k_2\to\infty}m_{k_1,k_2;II}=D+(1-D)m_{k_1}\approx 0.4166+0.5834\thinspace m_{k_1},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
D=\frac12\int_0^\infty e^{-\frac12x}e^{-\int_0^x\frac{1-e^{-y}}ydy}dx\approx0.4166.
\end{equation}
Thus also,
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{k_1\to\infty}m_{k_1,\infty;II}=D+(1-D)m_\infty\approx0.8528.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

