{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.773623+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a21c100bf1a1b27eaa9521d6499070d09ced064b945de6708d094bbafe0d13cb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2607.10356, '2607.10356'] Let $\{\CW_n:n\ge 1\}$ be a sequence of independent $\Bb$-valued random variables with mean zero and is \emph{tight} $($\emph{i.e.} satisfies \eqref{m1}, \eqref{m2}$)$.
Suppose $\{\CW_n:n\ge 1\}$ is uniformly integrable, \emph{i.e.}
\begin{equation}
\textstyle\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\Bigl[ \sup_{n\ge 1}\E\bigl[\nrm{\CW_{n}}\Ind_{\{\nrm{\CW_{n}}\ge t\}}\bigr]\Bigr]=0.
 \end{equation}
Then for all $\epsilon>0$ there exists $t\ge 1$, $\{\xa_j: 1\le j\le t\}\subseteq\Bb$, a sequence $\{ \CY_{n}:n\ge 1\}$ of $\Bb$-valued independent random variables, a constant $C<\infty$ and for $1\le j\le t$, a sequence $\{\xi_{n,j}: n\ge 1\}$ of $[-1,1]$-valued independent random variables such that for all $n\ge 1$
\begin{align}
\CW_n=\CY_n+&\textstyle\sum_{j=1}^t \xa_j\xi_{n,j},\\

\E[\xi_{n,j}]&=0,\;\;\;\;\forall\,1\le j\le t,\\
\E[\nrm{\CY_n}]&\le \epsilon\\

\nrm{\CY_n-\CW_n}&\le C.
\end{align}
Further, if $\{\CW_n:n\ge 1\}$ are i.i.d. then $\{\CY_n:n\ge 1\}$ are i.i.d. and for each $1\le j\le t$, $\{\xi_{n,j}: n\ge 1\}$ are also i.i.d.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.306747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a21c6e90c2a86499f4b167c242d9334e2275816de70102d2a7608fe18c0d6ad4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:pushforward of arXiv:2608.07842, 'Resolution of a Conjecture on Shifted R\'enyi Divergence'] For $\beta\in[0,1)$ with $\mu_D(\beta)$ as above, $\mu'_\beta$ is Gaussian with
$$\langle u,\cdot\rangle\text{-marginal }=N\big(m-\mu_D(\beta),\,(1-\beta)^2\sigma^2\big),$$
and with all marginals orthogonal to $u$ equal to those of $\mu$, namely $N(0,\sigma^2)$. Hence, writing $\sigma_q^2(\beta):=q\sigma^2-(q-1)(1-\beta)^2\sigma^2$ and $\mu_1(\beta):=m-\mu_D(\beta)$,
\begin{equation}
J(\beta):=R_q(\mu'_\beta\|\nu)=\frac{q\,\mu_1(\beta)^2}{2\sigma_q^2(\beta)}+\frac{1}{1-q}\log\frac{\sigma_q(\beta)}{\big((1-\beta)\sigma\big)^{1-q}\sigma^{q}}.
