{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.606777+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a37cb72a8d5e1b9b7b1f00101c50dee1c9344be39b7fd45a9b7343cd3bf66301","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 19 of arXiv:2607.08129, '2607.08129'] Let $T_1,\ldots,T_N$ be independent real-valued random variables, and let
$0\le c_i\le\beta$.  Fix an interval $J=(a,b]$ and define
\begin{equation}
 R_J:=\sup_{a\le u\le b}
 \left|\sum_{i=1}^Nc_i
 \left(
 \1_{\{a<T_i\le u\}}-\Prob(a<T_i\le u)
 \right)\right|
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 V_J:=\sum_{i=1}^Nc_i^2\Prob(T_i\in J).
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 \E R_J\le4\sqrt{V_J}.
\end{equation}
There is a universal constant $C_T<\infty$ such that, for every $x>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \Prob(R_J\ge\E R_J+x)
 \le C_T\exp\left\{
 -\frac{x}{C_T\beta}
 \log\left(1+
 \frac{x\beta}{V_J+\beta\E R_J}
 \right)
 \right\}.
\end{equation}
If $\beta=0$, both assertions hold trivially.  In particular, after changing the universal constant if necessary,
\begin{equation}
 \Prob(R_J\ge\E R_J+x)
 \le C_T\exp\bigg\{
 -\frac{x^2}{C_T\{V_J+\beta\E R_J+x\beta\}}
 \bigg\}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.805529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3806a70c6f478c9a507bbf3aad526747fec829db5c3df5dae8f85f7512fb32c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop t:AJRS-Z-scaling of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] {AJRS22}}]
    Recall
    the families of measure-preserving automorphisms of $(\Xi,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ defined in (equation e:automorphism-induced of the paper).
    The following statements are true.
    \begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\roman*)}]
        \item\textup{(Translation).}
        For each $(u,z)\in\mathbb{R}^2$, there exists an event $\mathsf{T}(u,z) \subset \Xi$ with 
        $\mathbb{P}(\mathsf{T}(u,z))=1$ such that on $\mathsf{T}(u,z)$, for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$ and all $\beta\geqslant0$,
        \begin{align*}
            \mathcal{Z}_\beta(s+u,x+z;t+u,y+z) \circ \mathcal{T}_{-u, -z}
            = \mathcal{Z}_\beta(s,x;t,y).
        \end{align*}
        \item\textup{(Shearing).}
        For each $(r,\nu)\in\mathbb{R}^2$, there exists an event $\mathsf{S}(r,\nu) \subset \Xi$ with $\mathbb{P}(\mathsf{S}(r,\nu))=1$ such that on $\mathsf{S}(r,\nu)$, for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$ and all $\beta\geqslant0$,
        \begin{align*}
            e^{\nu(y-x) + \frac{\nu^2}{2}(t-s)}\mathcal{Z}_\beta(s,x+\nu(s-r); t, y+\nu(t-r))
            \circ \mathcal{S}_{r,-\nu}
            = \mathcal{Z}_\beta(s,x;t,y).
        \end{align*}

        \item\textup{(Diffusive scaling).}
            For each $\lambda>0$, there exists an event $\mathsf{D}(\lambda) \subset \Xi$ with $\mathbb{P}(\mathsf{D}(\lambda))=1$ such that on $\mathsf{D}(\lambda)$,
            for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$ and all $\beta\geqslant0$,
            \begin{align*}
                \lambda 
                \mathcal{Z}_{\beta/\sqrt{\lambda}}(\lambda^2s,\lambda x; \lambda^2 t, \lambda y)
                \circ \mathcal{D}_{\lambda^{-2}, \lambda^{-1}} = 
                \mathcal{Z}_\beta(s,x;t,y).
            \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
    In the above three equalities, $\mathcal{Z}_\beta(s,x;t,y)$ is regarded as a real-valued function of the white noise, $\xi \mapsto \mathcal{Z}^\xi_\beta(s,x;t,y)$.
    This is why we use the composition notation $\circ$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

