{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:27:56.793672+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3d1ef736a10fbc9ada1c77b3457d1305220b1283f2cf12790cffe9186a762d4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2609.00209, 'Exit Times for Brownian Motion and Location Detection'] Let $\mathcal E$ be an elliptic domain as above with $a\neq b$ and let $u_1(x,y)=\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_{\mathcal E}]$ and $u_2(x,y)=\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_{\mathcal E}^2]$  be the first two moments of the exit time from $\mathcal E$. Then
\begin{align*}
u_1(x,y)&=\frac{a^2b^2}{2(a^2 + b^2)}\left(1-\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \frac{y^2}{b^2}\right),\\
u_2(x,y)&= u_1(x,y)(Ax^2 + By^2 + C),
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
    A &= -\frac{b^2(5a^2+b^2)}{6(a^4+6a^2b^2 + b^4)}, \\
    B&= -\frac{a^2(a^2 + 5b^2)}{6(a^4+6a^2b^2 + b^4)}, \\
    C&= \frac{a^2b^2(5a^4 + 26a^2b^2 + 5b^4)}{6(a^2+b^2)(a^4+6a^2b^2 + b^4)}.
\end{align*}
Given positive real numbers $c_1$ and $c_2$, if the level curves $u_1(x,y)=c_1$ and $u_2(x,y)=c_2$ intersect, then their intersection is  either four points, two points, or a single point determined by $c_1$ and $c_2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.605022+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3d3d5d452cd35efca533dffc9fa0aa7e7a50ce3b57c3b5fd4eb5c0859c3a681","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem bdlr of arXiv:2608.13084, '\bf Branching random walk in random environment'] There exist constants $C_1,C_2\in(0,\infty)$ such that for any $\delta\in(0,1]$ and any admissible path $(x_j)_{j\le n}\in({\mathbb Z}^d)^n$, one has for any $1\le j\le n$,
\begin{equation}
C_1\widehat{{\mathbb E}}^\omega_{(x_j)_{j\le n}}[ (1-\delta)^{l_j}] \widehat{{\mathbb E}}^\omega_{(x_j)_{j\le n}}[ r_j ] \le \widehat{{\mathbb E}}^\omega_{(x_j)_{j\le n}}[ (1-\delta)^{l_j} r_j ] \le C_2 \widehat{{\mathbb E}}^\omega_{(x_j)_{j\le n}}[ (1-\delta)^{l_j}  ] \widehat{{\mathbb E}}^\omega_{(x_j)_{j\le n}}[ r_j  ] .
\end{equation}
Further, as $\delta\downarrow0+$, uniformly in $j,n$ and $(x_j)_{j\le n}\in({\mathbb Z}^d)^n$,
\begin{equation}
\widehat{{\mathbb E}}^\omega_{(x_j)_{j\le n}} [(1-\delta)^{l_j} r_j ] =  \widehat{{\mathbb E}}_{(x_j)_{j\le n}}^\omega \left[(1-\delta)^{l_j} \right] \widehat{{\mathbb E}}_{(x_j)_{j\le n}}^\omega \left[ r_j \right] + o_\delta(1).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.484108+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3d5bbe3c4fdd584c2fc7ccad14aaa309f26bb09035386f74b58a19939f93f7c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 51 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] Let $j\geq 1$ and $l\geq 0$.
