{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.623785+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4060b3410a7b73d4d4794ca0884a04309edc7f8245af91561364e16352ff5af","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:ergodic theorem for T, continuous of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{H},(U_t)_{t\geq 0},\hat{\mathbb{E}})$ be a regular continuous sublinear expectation system, where $U_t$ is the linear operator induced by a semigroup of measurable transformations $\theta_t:\Omega\to\Omega$. If $(U_t)_{t\geq 0}$ satisfies that $\overline{X}\in\mathcal{H}$, for any $X\in \mathcal{H}$. Then the following two statements are equivalent:
		\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
			\item $\hat{\mathbb{E}}$ is ergodic;
			\item there exists an ergodic probability $P$ on $\sigma(\mathcal{H})$ such that for any  $X\in \mathcal{H}$,  
			\[\lim_{T\to\infty}\frac{1}{T}\int_0^TX\circ \theta_tdt=\int XdP, \text{ }\hat{\mathbb{E}}\text{-q.s.}\]
		\end{enumerate}
		In this case,  $P$ is the unique  ergodic probability with respect to $\theta_t, \ t\geq 0$ in $\mathcal{P}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

