{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:09.924234+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a48666fd21c7980b49d2ed8871f0c2abd0d6ad744b5071e571092bfed45fbb0c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 ({\cite[Theorem 5(a)) of arXiv:2603.27433, 'The $k$-cycle shuffling with repeated cards'] {hough2016random}}]
Let $0<\epsilon<\frac{1}{2}$, let $r=n-\l_1$ and suppose that $r+k+1<(\frac12-\epsilon)n$. Then
\begin{multline}
s_\l(k)=\frac{(n-r-1)^{\underline{k}}}{n^{\underline{k}}}\prod_{i=2}^m\left(1-\frac{k}{n-(1+r+\lambda_i-i)}\right)\prod_{i=1}^m\left(1-\frac{k}{n-(r-\lambda_i'+i)}\right)^{-1}\\
