{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.502952+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4c00b7c21910efcfa3bbb5eaac020f999bfd3f6f24bc052120d65471a99545a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 57 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $\bfx = (X,\bbx) \in \scrc^{\alpha}([0,T],V)$ be a rough path for some index $\alpha \in (\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{2}]$, let $f_0 : [0,T] \times W \to W$ be continuous, and let $f : [0,T] \times W \to L(V,W)$ be of class $C_b^{2\alpha,2}$. Moreover, let $0 \leq q \leq r \leq s \leq T$ and $\xi \in W$ be arbitrary. Let $(Y(q,r,\xi),Y(q,r,\xi)') \in \scrd_X^{2\alpha}([q,r],W)$ be a solution to the equation $Y(q,r,\xi)' = f(Y(q,r,\xi))$ and
\begin{align}
Y(q,r,\xi)_t = \xi + \int_q^t f_0(u,Y(q,r,\xi)_u) \, du + \int_q^t f(u,Y(q,r,\xi)_u) \, d \mathbf{X}_u \quad t \in [q,r].
\end{align}
Furthermore, we set $\eta := Y(q,r,\xi)_r$ and let $(Y(r,s,\eta),Y(r,s,\eta)') \in \scrd_X^{2\alpha}([r,s],W)$ be a solution to the equation $Y(r,s,\eta)' = f(Y(r,s,\eta))$ and
\begin{align}
Y(r,s,\eta)_t = \eta + \int_r^t f_0(u,Y(r,s,\eta)_u) \, du + \int_r^t f(u,Y(r,s,\eta)_u) \, d \mathbf{X}_u, \quad t \in [r,s].
\end{align}
We define the concatenated path $Y(q,s,\xi) : [q,s] \to W$ as
\begin{align}
Y(q,s,\xi)_t :=
\begin{cases}
Y(q,r,\xi)_t, & t \in [q,r],
\\ Y(r,s,\eta)_t, & t \in [r,s],
\end{cases}
\end{align}
and we define the concatenated path $Y(q,s,\xi)' : [q,s] \to L(V,W)$ as
\begin{align}
Y(q,s,\xi)_t' :=
\begin{cases}
Y(q,r,\xi)_t', & t \in [q,r],
\\ Y(r,s,\eta)_t', & t \in [r,s].
\end{cases}
\end{align}
Then we have $(Y(q,s,\xi),Y(q,s,\xi)') \in \scrd_X^{2\alpha}([q,r],W)$, and this path is a solution to $Y(q,s,\xi)' = f(Y(q,s,\xi))$ and
\begin{align}
Y(q,s,\xi)_t &= \xi + \int_q^t f_0(u,Y(q,s,\xi)_u) \, du + \int_q^t f(u,Y(q,s,\xi)_u) \, d\mathbf{X}_u, \quad t \in [q,s].
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.465980+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4c1f6c7c16beea289accb8c0820e01de7a357de114440e3d51e182f22444153","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 13 of arXiv:2604.04267, 'On Sharpest Tail Bounds for Functions \\ of Tail Bounded Random Variables'] Let \(\mathfrak{m}(d) = \{m_{j_1,\dots,j_{d+1}}\}_{j_1,\dots,j_{d+1}=1}^n\) have all non-negative entries. Let \(X_1,\dots,X_d\) be real random \(n \times n\) matrices 
with mutually independent components. Denote the \(i,j\) entry of \(X_k\) by \(X^{(k)}_{ij}\) for all \(i,j,k\). Let \(f_{ijk} \in B\) and 
\(X^{(k)}_{ij} \in \mathcal{T}(f_{ijk})\) for all \(i,j,k\). Denote \(\widetilde{X}_k = \{\widetilde{X}^{(k)}_{ij}\}_{i,j=1}^n\) for \(k=1,\dots,d\). Here 
\(\widetilde{X}^{(k)}_{ij}\) is the ``right-shifted'' version of \(X^{(k)}_{ij}\) (see lemma~\ref{Xtilde} \eqref{Xtildedefn}). Then $\forall t \ge 0$,
