  for an explicit covariance matrix $\Sigma$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.791885+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6bf3b94a840156be1b34b03b77f108cdbceaca6d8d6a364783801138aab7696","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop continuity of arXiv:2605.11785, 'Regularization of a mean-field SDE by an additive common noise: the conditional expectatio'] For any $\epsilon\in (0,\frac 12)$, there exists a constant $C_\epsilon>0$, depending on $\epsilon$, $\sigma^0$, $b$ and $T$ only, such that, for any $M>0$ and any $\mu_0, \widehat \mu_0\in \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d)$, 
\begin{equation}
d_{\rm B_ML}(P^{\mu_0},P^{\widehat\mu_0})\leq C_\epsilon \left(W_1(\mu_0,\widehat \mu_0)^{1/2}+ W_1(\mu_0,\widehat \mu_0)^{1/2-\epsilon}\right) (W_1(\mu_0,\widehat \mu_0)^{1/2} +M).
\end{equation}  
More generally, if we  denote (with a slight abuse of notation) by $P^{\mu_0}(d\bm{x}|\bm{x}^0)$ the conditional distribution of the $X$ component given that $X^0=\bm{x} ^0$ under $P^{\mu_0}$, then, for all $M>0$ and for all $\mu_0, \widehat \mu_0\in \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d)$,
\begin{align}
& d_{{\rm B}_M{\rm L}}\left(Q^{\mu_0}(d\bm{x}^0)\prod_{i=1}^kP^{\mu_0}(d\bm{x}^i|\bm{x}^0),Q^{\widehat\mu_0}(d\bm{x}^0)\prod_{i=1}^kP^{\widehat\mu_0}(d\bm{x}^i|\bm{x}^0)\right) \notag\\
& \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad  \leq C_\epsilon \left(W_1(\mu_0,\widehat \mu_0)^{1/2}+ W_1(\mu_0,\widehat \mu_0)^{1/2-\epsilon}\right)  \left(k W_1(\mu_0,\widehat \mu_0)^{1/2}+ M\right).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.283480+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6c2cb93bfdde1192fa0fa3d1be5b098f5c386f3491a92626770b7f62a43141b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2604.22637, '2604.22637'] For any positive integer $n$ and $k=0,1,2,\dots,n$, the probability generating function of $N_n$ for $-1\leq z\leq 1$ is
\begin{align}
  E[z^{N_n}]
  =\sum_{j=0}^n \binom{n}{j} \binom{z-1}{j} p^j 
  = \sum_{k=0}^n \left( \sum_{j=k}^n \binom{n}{j} p^j c_{j,k} \right) z^k

\end{align}
where $c_{j,k}$ is the coefficient of $z^k$ in the expansion of $\binom{z-1}{j}$, i.e., $\binom{z-1}{j} = \sum_{k=0}^j c_{j,k} z^k$.
Therefore, the probability mass function $P(N_n=k),~k=0,1,2,\dots,n$ is given by
\begin{align}
    P(N_n=k) = \sum_{j=k}^n \binom{n}{j} p^j c_{j,k}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.763485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6c5add227785d19a4f1c5c178907e0e398bbfee89594055cbd50ec595393501","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Finite 2D $\rho$-variation) of arXiv:2512.04646, '\textbf{Canonical Rough Path over Tempered Fractional Brownian Motion: Existence, Construc'] Let $H > 1/4$ and $\rho = 1/(2H)$. Then
\begin{equation}

V_\rho(R_{H,\lambda})
\le C(H,\lambda,T)
:= V_\rho(R_H)
  + K_1(H)\,\lambda^{2H\rho}\,T^{2\rho-2}
  + \frac{K_2(H,\lambda)}{\lambda^{2H\rho}}
< \infty,
\end{equation}
where $K_1(H)$ and $K_2(H,\lambda)$ are explicit constants, and
$C(H,\lambda,T) \to C_{\rm fBm}(H,T)$ as $\lambda \to 0^+$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.727881+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6c6760c2edf99c609c278726b7cc25ce23832a5feefb1d030b7a7e29207eda3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2511.14740, 'Marking and re-marking'] Let $\mathbf X$ be a random variable taking values in $\mathbb N^c$, $\mathsf A \in [0,1]^{c\times d}$ with columns $|\mathbf a_i| \leq 1$, and let $\mathbf Y = \mathsf A \circ X$ be the multinomial re-marking of $\mathbf X$ with parameters $\mathsf A$. Then we have the following.
\begin{enumerate}
\item  $\mathbb E \mathbf Y = \mathsf A \,\mathbb E \mathbf X$.
\item $\DispM( \mathbf Y) = \mathsf A \DispM(\mathbf X)\, \mathsf A^\top$.
\item $\Phi_{\mathbf Y}(\mathbf t) = \Phi_{\mathbf X}(\mathsf A^\top \mathbf t)$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

