{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.385381+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6e8f931d6f9ad04a3da88715fac2065f7a346cc4a2c63649dd6998727f95870","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2607.28004, 'Folding representations of reflected diffusions'] Let Assumption~\ref{ass:coefficients_Z} on the coefficients of the system \eqref{eq:RSDE_YZ} hold. Then:
    \begin{enumerate}[label = (\roman*),noitemsep]
        \item  For every $z \in E$, the function $f(\cdot,z)$ of \eqref{eq:f_Z} is the unique solution to the parameter-dependent nonlinear ODE \eqref{eq:ODE_Z}, where $\widehat \sigma(\cdot,z)$ is given by \eqref{eq:sigma_hat}. Moreover, $f$ is of class $C^1$, $f(\cdot,z)$ belongs to the class $W^{2,1}_{\mathrm{loc}}(I)$ for every $z \in E$, and $f(x,\cdot)$ is of class $C^2$ for every $x \in I$;
        \item  Consider the SDE system
        \begin{equation} 
        \begin{aligned}
            dX_t & = \alpha(X_t,Z_t)dt + dB_t + \sigma_X(X_t,Z_t)^\top d\overline W_t, \\
            dZ_t & = \overline b_Z(X_t,Z_t)dt + \overline \sigma_Z(X_t,Z_t)d\overline W_t,
        \end{aligned}
        \end{equation}
        where $\overline b_Z(x,z) = b_Z(f(|x|,z),z)$,  $\overline \sigma_Z (x,z) = \sigma_Z(f(|x|,z),z)$, $B$ and $\overline W$ are independent univariate and $m$-dimensional Brownian Motions respectively, and $\alpha$, $\sigma_X$ are given by \eqref{eq:alphaZ} and \eqref{eq:sigmaX} respectively. 
        This system has a globally defined weak solution, which is unique in law, for any initial values $(X_0,Z_0) = (x,z) \in \R \times E$;
        \item  The process $(Y,Z) = (f(|X|,Z),Z)$, with $(X,Z)$ as in \eqref{eq:SDE_system_halfline} and $f$ given by \eqref{eq:f_Z}, satisfies the RSDE system \eqref{eq:RSDE_YZ} on $I \times E$ with initial condition $(Y_0,Z_0) = (f(|X_0|,Z_0),Z_0)$, scalar Brownian Motion $W$ as in \eqref{eq:WZ}, and reflection term
        \begin{equation} 
            \Phi = \int_0^\cdot \sigma(0,Z_t)\sqrt{1-\|\rho(0,Z_t)\|^2}\, dL_t^0(X).
        \end{equation}    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.673899+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ea73d7aa8a48da59e3a5509a6951135fff577f7688524150d829fe8a7fb179","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor eq:magique of arXiv:2506.01746, '\bf Optimal Bregman quantization: existence and uniqueness of optimal quantizers revisited'] If $x\!\in U^n$ is a stationary quantizer at level $n\ge 1$  (i.e. has pairwise distinct components with $P$-negligible Bregman--Voronoi boundary and  satisfies the $\phi_{_F}$-master equation~(equation eq:Mastereq of the paper)), then all induced quantizations $\widehat X$ are $\mathbb{P}$-$a.s.$ equal and
\begin{equation}
e_{2}(x, P,\phi_{_F})^2 = \mathbb{E} \, F(X)-\mathbb{E}\, F\big(\widehat X^{x}\big).
\end{equation}
In particular, if $x^{(n)}$ is an optimal $F$-Bregman quantizer then
 \begin{equation}
e_{2,n}(P,\phi_{_F})^2 = \mathbb{E} \, F(X)-\mathbb{E}\, F\big(\widehat X^{x^{(n)}}\big).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.354974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ed5607af312edf7ca4160efab993e19f43d375d887fbbfc5250e6fef2849da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2511.09280, 'Random Walk conditioned to stay above a non-flat floor: curvature effects'] Let \(\epsilon>0\). With the notations above, for any \(n\geq 1\), and non-negative measurable function \(f:\R^{n+1}\to \R_+\),
    \begin{multline}
        E\bigl(f(S) \mathds{1}_{S\geq h_n}\mathds{1}_{S_n\leq h_n(n)+\epsilon}\bigr)
        \\
        =
        e^{-n\int_{0}^1 I(h'(s))ds} E\Bigl(f(Z+h_n)\mathds{1}_{Z\geq 0}\mathds{1}_{Z_n\leq \epsilon}\exp\Bigl(-\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \tfrac{\alpha_k}{n} Z_k\Bigr) \Bigr)e^{O_n(1)},
    \end{multline}
    with \(O_n(1)\) uniform over \(f\), and
    where
    \begin{equation}
        \alpha_k \equiv \alpha_{n,k} = -\frac{h''(k/n)}{H''((H')^{-1}(h'(k/n)))} + b_{n,k},
    \end{equation}
    and the \(b_k\equiv b_{n,k}\)'s are such that \(\sup_{k} \abs{b_{k}} = o_n(1)\). Moreover, the \(b_{n,k}\)'s are independent of \(\epsilon\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:01:15.334084+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ed823a67328f534c77ed2595a84f245aa7fed96e0d31f7838a75b480c542ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.30409, 'Gradient estimates and volume doubling for locally finite weighted graphs with $CD\psi(n,-'] Let $G=(V,E)$ be a locally finite weighted graph satisfying the $CD\psi\left(n,-K\right)$ condition with $K\geq0$, where $\psi\in C^{1}\left(0,+\infty\right)$ is a concave function. Let $b>\frac{1}{2}$ be a constant. Then for any $0<f\in \ell^{\infty}\left(V\right)$,
    \begin{equation}
        \Gamma ^\psi\left(P_t f\right)\leq \psi^\prime \left(1\right)\left(1+\frac{2Kt}{2b+1}\right)  \frac{\Delta P_t f}{P_t f}+\frac{n}{2} \left(\frac{b^2}{\left(2b-1\right)t}+\frac{K^2 t}{2b+1}+K\right).

    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.548956+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ef6968ebfc5bc673b2637ad80f479745bfd46136d8686e9efbbcb95dbe0b25","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 26 (Local variability) of arXiv:2609.01397, 'Measuring consistency via ensemble margin and local prediction variability: Auditing decis'] Given a Rashomon \smallset{} set $\retailset = \{f_{k}: k \in [\Nretail]\}$ of size $\Nretail$, and a set $\localtestmat = \{\localtest_{j} : j \in [\nogaussianpts]\} \subseteq \ltwohsphere(x,\sigma)$ of $\nogaussianpts$ perturbed samples of $x$, we define the local variability at a fixed instance $x$ as, 
\begin{align}
    \localvar(\retailset,x) =  \frac{1}{\nogaussianpts}\sum_{j=1}^{\nogaussianpts}\frac{1}{\Nretail}\sum_{j=1}^{\Nretail}\babs{f_k(x) - f_k(\localtest_j)} 
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.707070+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ef959d0b58ba8a9bfd0b87c4422ab1d27ec98195d3bfa93942e69b772b6467","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem hitting of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] There exist $C,\delta > 0$ such that, given $x \in {\rm D}_n$ and $R > {\rm d}(x,{\rm D}_n^c)$, 
\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{P}_x(\tau_{\partial {\rm B}(x;\log R)} < \tau_\partial) \leq C \left(\frac{{\rm d}(x,{\rm D}_n^c)}{R}\right)^\delta.
\end{equation} In particular, given any $\varepsilon > 0$ and $x,y \in {\rm D}$ with $\| x-y\| \geq \varepsilon$, ${\rm d}(y,{\rm D}^c)>2\varepsilon$ and $\lfloor {\rm e}^n x\rfloor, \lfloor {\rm e}^n y\rfloor \in {\rm D}_n$, 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}_{\lfloor {\rm e}^n x\rfloor}(\tau_{\lfloor {\rm e}^n y\rfloor} < \tau_\partial) \leq 	C_{\varepsilon,{\rm D}} \frac{\left({\rm d}(x,{\rm D}^c)\right)^\delta }{n}
\end{equation} for some constant $C_{\varepsilon,{\rm D}} > 0$ and all $n$ large enough (depending only on $\varepsilon$ and ${\rm D}$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.010967+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6f0fd5e42a5f94a0fc9830e95bc78b005f7eda5df80200cc65aa716e8887eea","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2608.14802, 'Dynamical Gibbs-non-Gibbs transitions for finite-alphabet models on trees'] Suppose that
$\|Df(l)\|_{l}=\lambda$. Then, the operator norm of $DF(l,\dots,l)$, induced by $\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{max}}$ on $T(\Delta^{q-1})^d$ and by $\|\cdot\|_l$ on $T\Delta^{q-1}$, satisfies 
\begin{equation*}
    \|DF(l,\dots,l)\|_{\mathrm{max} \rightarrow l}\leq \lambda.
\end{equation*}
More generally, we write 
\begin{equation*}
\|DF(z^{(1)},\dots,z^{(d)})\|_{\mathrm{max} \rightarrow l}:=\sup_{\substack{(v^{(1)},\dots,v^{(d)})\in T(\Delta^{q-1})^d\\ (v^{(1)},\dots,v^{(d)})\neq (0,\dots,0)}}\frac{\|DF(z^{(1)},\dots,z^{(d)})(v^{(1)},\dots,v^{(d)})\|_l}{\|(v^{(1)},\dots,v^{(d)})\|_{\mathrm{max}}} 
\end{equation*} 
for all $z^{(1)},\dots,z^{(d)}\in\mathrm{ri}\left(\Delta^{q-1}\right)$.
Here, the maximum norm $\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{max}}$ on $T(\Delta^{q-1})^d$ is defined by
\begin{equation}
    \|(v^{(1)},\dots,v^{(d)})\|_{\max}
    :=
    \max_{1\leq i\leq d}\|v^{(i)}\|_l 
\end{equation}
for all $v^{(1)},\dots,v^{(d)}\in T\Delta^{q-1}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.547606+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6f9a58ab63ddbabb496f35c62cb0b03accacc2b9b30c99b343ee11c448472c5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Profile cut-off phenomenon for Galerkin projection,
		$\gamma\in (0,1/3\rbrack$) of arXiv:2603.29473, '$\chi^2$--cut-off phenomenon for Galerkin projections of Fokker--Planck equations with mon'] \hfill

	\noindent
	Assume that $\gamma \in (0,1/3]$, $\delta(\varepsilon) = \varepsilon^{\frac{1-\gamma}{1+\gamma}}$, and $n\in \mathbb{N}$.

	\noindent
	For any $x_0\neq 0$  profile cut-off holds true in the sense of~\cref{def:pro},
	where $d_{n,\varepsilon}$ is defined in \cref{eq:Ga}, and for sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$ the cut-off time $t_\varepsilon$
	is the unique solution to
	\begin{equation}
		e^{-2\lambda_{n,\varepsilon} t_\varepsilon}
		\left ( \int \varrho_{\varepsilon,0}(x) \psi_{n,\varepsilon}(x) \dx  \right )^2 = 1,
	\end{equation}
	equivalently,
	\begin{equation*}
		t_\varepsilon = \frac{1}{\lambda_{n,\varepsilon}} \log \left  ( \left \lvert \int \varrho_{\varepsilon,0}(x) \psi_{n,\varepsilon}(x) \dx  \right \rvert \right ).
	\end{equation*}
	The time window is given by $w_\varepsilon:= \varepsilon^{\frac{1-\gamma}{1+\gamma}}$ and the profile function $\mathfrak{p}(r):=e^{-\lambda_n r}$, $r\in \mathbb{R}$.

	\noindent
	For $x_0 = 0$, let
	\begin{equation*}
		E_n:=\{1\leq j\leq n:\ \psi_j(0)\neq 0\}.
	\end{equation*}
	If $E_n=\emptyset$, then $d_{n,\varepsilon}(t,0)=0$ for all $t\geq 0$. Otherwise, letting $m:=\max E_n$, the centered initial datum exhibits profile cut-off with cut-off time determined by
	\begin{equation*}
		e^{-2\lambda_{m,\varepsilon} t_\varepsilon}\left(\int \varrho_{\varepsilon,0}(x)\psi_{m,\varepsilon}(x)\,\dx\right)^2=1,
	\end{equation*}
	the same time window $w_\varepsilon:=\varepsilon^{\frac{1-\gamma}{1+\gamma}}$, and profile function $\mathfrak p(r)=e^{-\lambda_m r}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:50:36.763116+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ff0214cbb0d55900284994f3ab241d9fb6388c9461d07e142691f3b54e3847","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 (Properties of Bessel functions) of arXiv:2609.04155, 'The subconvexity problem for symmetric square\\ $L$-functions in Level aspect'] For $x>0$, and $r\in\R\cup i\R$
