{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.712716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a84a4a8a19485f37154439985849825e9fab5252fc5f07da182fff0612d0b6e2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 35 (Covariance branch decomposition) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] Let $a,b\in\mathfrak C$ with $a\ne b$.  For $\ell\ne0$ in the wave case,
\begin{align}
  \sigma_b(\ell;s,t)
  =\mathfrak q_b(\ell)\Bigg[{}&\frac{s}{2\omega_b(\ell)^2}
      \cos((t-s)\omega_b(\ell))\notag\\
  &+\frac{\sin((t-s)\omega_b(\ell))}{4\omega_b(\ell)^3}
   -\frac{\sin((t+s)\omega_b(\ell))}{4\omega_b(\ell)^3}\Bigg].
\end{align}
On $|\ell|\sim N$, the last two branches are bounded by
$C N^{-3+2\beta_b}$.  In the first branch, after multiplication by the outer
Duhamel kernel and expansion into exponentials, each sign component has the
form
\begin{equation}
  e^{\ii(t-s)\Phi_{a|b}^{\varepsilon_0,\varepsilon_1}(q,\ell)}
  \mathfrak a_{a|b}^{\varepsilon_0,\varepsilon_1}(q,\ell,s)\wh w(q,s),
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
  \abs{\mathfrak a_{a|b}^{\varepsilon_0,\varepsilon_1}(q,\ell,s)}
  +\abs{\partial_s\mathfrak a_{a|b}^{\varepsilon_0,\varepsilon_1}(q,\ell,s)}
  \lesssim_T N^{-3+2\beta_b}.
\end{equation}
The constants depend on the forcing profiles only through $\mathfrak H_{\mathrm{prof}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.933745+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a84b85520b353483d425e996a5d952f2dde358b97951388c8f0583b98febba6a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:surface_tension of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] The \textit{surface tension} $\sigma: \mathcal{N}(\Lambda)\to \mathbb{R}_+$ is given by
    \begin{equation}
    \sigma(s) := -\lim_{n\to \infty} \lim_{N\to \infty} \frac{1}{N n^d} \log \frac{Z_{n,N}^s}{(N!)^{n^d}}.
\end{equation}
It is straightforward to check that $\log (Z_{n,N}^s/(N!)^{n^d})$ forms as subadditive sequence in $n,N$, so $\sigma(s)$ exists for any fixed $s\in \mathcal{N}(\Lambda)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.430523+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a84c74513a83fac3e13583d132e2f6b59a7087ba6d28df713a600c73f1c0eafe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi app_def_SOP of arXiv:2411.16464, 'Generating social networks with static and dynamic utility-maximization approaches'] For a given individuals graph $\mathcal{G}_N(\mathscr{I} , \alpha, C)$, we define the Social Optimization Problem (SOP) as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\argmax_{\alpha} \mathcal{O} (\alpha, C) =  \sum^N_{i=1} U_i(\alpha^i, C^i)\\
\text{s.t} \quad \begin{cases} 
\alpha \in [0 ,1)^{n_N} \\
\nexists i \in \Iintv*{1, N }, \forall j \in \Iintv*{1, N-1 }, \alpha^i_j = 0 
\end{cases}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
{\footnotesize With $U_i$ the utility function of agent $i$, that is its second component in the individuals space.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:41.028392+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a84dbce4a6022ca86635e360ef0e11d81f474ba99dbfc6c9f9db4a4674f03753","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2-l14 of arXiv:2412.04015, 'Linear fluctuation of interfaces in Glauber-Kawasaki dynamics'] Fix a bounded function $F\colon {\mathbb R}_+ \times {\mathbb T}^d_N \to {\mathbb R}$,
