{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.728726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a921f80f34cab801210e75d2fe95f9bb65a5e216abb642a4a0738237ec5197e8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem SouvlakiA of arXiv:2412.09290, 'Asymptotics of Harish-Chandra transform and infinitesimal freeness'] Let $A=A(N)$ be a random Hermitian matrix of size $N$ and assume
  that for every finite $r$ one has
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{N} \log \text{} \mathbb{E} [H \text{}
    C (x_1, \ldots, x_r, 0^{N - r} ; \lambda_1 (A), \ldots, \lambda_N (A))] =
    \sum_{i = 1}^r \Psi (x_i), 
  \end{equation}
  where $\Psi$ is a smooth function in a complex neighborhood of $0$ and the above
  convergence is uniform in a complex neighborhood of $0^r$. Then the random measure
  $N^{- 1} \sum_{i = 1}^N \delta \left( N^{- 1} \lambda_i (A) \right)$ converges, as $N
  \rightarrow \infty$, in probability, in the sense of moments to a
  deterministic measure $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$ on $\mathbb{R}$ whose moments are
  given by
  \begin{equation}
    \int_{\mathbb{R}} t^k \ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}} (d \text{} t) = \sum_{m = 0}^k
    \frac{k!}{m! (m + 1) ! (k - m) !}  \left. \frac{\mathrm{d}^m}{\mathrm{d} x^m}
    ((\Psi' (x))^{k - m}) \right|_{x = 0} . 
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.970957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a922e61920c889079056ddf94bad09522d07b25091c2512284bdbc6cda0a1c81","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:G_mu of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] Fix a smooth, increasing function $\chi:\R\rightarrow[0,1]$ 
such that $\chi|_{(-\infty,1]}=0$ and $\chi|_{[2,\infty)}=1$.
For any $\mu\in[0,1]$ and $t\geqslant0$, 
