{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.434104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9a025e0cdc37a1661966c81dfdd31c97e690b46fbd5b5e970a9ba11ac4835a1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def model of arXiv:2601.10568, 'Hydrodynamic Limit with a Weierstrass-type result'] Let ${\textcolor{blue}{\beta}}$ be a fixed continuous function in $\mathbb{T}$ and such that $0< \beta(u)\leq 1$ for each $u\in\mathbb{T}$. Fix $0<{\textcolor{blue}{\ell_N}}=o(N)$ such that $\ell_N\to+\infty$ as $N\to+\infty$. We will study the time-scaled Markov generator $\ndef{\mathfrak{L}_N}$, given by
\begin{align}
    \mathfrak{L}_N
    :=N^2\mathcal{L}^{\beta,\ell_N}, 
\end{align}
The generator $\ndef{\mathcal{L}^{\beta,\ell_N}}$ is defined, for each $f:\Omega_N\to\mathbb{R}$, through 
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{L}^{\beta,\ell_N}[f]
    &:=\sum_{x\in\mathbb{T}_N}
    \tau^x\mathbf{c}_{\beta,\ell_N}
    \Big\{
        \tau^x\mathbf{e}_{0,1}
        +
        \tau^x\mathbf{e}_{1,0}
    \Big\}
    \nabla_{x,x+1}[f],
\end{align}
where the \textit{exclusion constraint} for a hopping to the right is given by $\mathbf{e}_{0,1}$, while to the left, $\mathbf{e}_{1,0}$; and $\tau^x\mathbf{c}_{\beta,\ell_N}$ the main dynamical constraint acting in the node $\{x,x+1\}$. They are precisely given, for each $\eta\in\Omega_N$, by $\textcolor{blue}{\mathbf{e}_{0,1}}(\eta)=\eta(0)(1-\eta(1))+(1-\eta(0))\eta(1)$ , $\textcolor{blue}{\mathbf{e}_{1,0}(\eta)}=(1-\eta(0))\eta(1)$; and $\textcolor{blue}{\mathbf{c}_{\beta,\ell_N}}>0$ is defined in terms of \textit{windows of interaction}: for each $0\leq j\leq \ell_N$, let ${\textcolor{blue}{W_j}}:=\llbracket -j,-j+\ell_N+1\rrbracket\backslash\{0,1\}$ and shorten $\ndef{\langle \eta\rangle_{j}}\equiv\tfrac{1}{\ell_N}\sum_{x\in W_j}\eta(x)$ for the density in the box $W_j$. Then,
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{c}_{\beta,\ell_N}(\eta)
    :=
    \frac{1}{\ell_N+1}
    \sum_{j=0}^{\ell_N}
    \beta(\langle \eta\rangle_{j})
    .
\end{align}
The dynamics is illustrated in Figure (fig:dynamics of the paper). It is convenient to shorten the rates
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{r}_{\beta,\ell_N}^j(\eta):=(\mathbf{e}_{0,1}(\eta)+\mathbf{e}_{1,0}(\eta))\beta(\langle \eta\rangle_{j})
    \quad\text{and}\quad
    \mathbf{r}_{\beta,\ell_N}
    :=
    \frac{1}{\ell_N+1}
    \sum_{j=0}^{\ell_N}
    \mathbf{r}_{\beta,\ell_N}^j
    .
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:09.926058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9ab8503af3ecd69712e32454f47480b7850506d71222858bf7a9589db69e032","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 27 of arXiv:2603.27433, 'The $k$-cycle shuffling with repeated cards'] Let $n=ml$, such that $2\le l\le n^{o(1)}$. Moreover, suppose that $x=n^{o(1)}$. Sample a uniformly random subset $T$ of size $x$ in $[n]$, then sample a uniformly random element $\sigma\in\S_{[n]\backslash T}$ and view it as an element of $\S_n$ by fixing all of the elements in $T$. Then, we have
