The width, hence the premium, carries no $\kappa$: the factor $\kappa^{2}$ in Corollary~(cor:protocol of the paper) is a finite-sample certificate, not an asymptotic necessity, and (ii)--(iii) meet Corollary~(cor:sizelb of the paper) within an absolute factor; a $\sqrt{2/\pi}$ width makes (i) exactly $\rho$. (Proof: Appendix~(app:upper of the paper); empirical check: Appendix~(app:exp of the paper).) (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.117824+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_abd9abde2004622293e80b686096e70ee4985a5c468c881a22ea8fdd38d2c56d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2608.17022, 'Sharp hypocoercive convergence estimates for underdamped Langevin dynamics with specular r'] Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ be a bounded convex domain with $C^{2,1}$ boundary, $U\in C^2(\overline\Omega)$, and let $\mu$ be the Gibbs measure (equation eq:gibbs of the paper) on $\Omega\times\mathbb{R}^d$. Assume:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the position marginal $\mu_x$ satisfies the Poincar\'e inequality
\begin{equation}
\nrm[\mu_x]{g}^2\le\frac1m\,\nrm[\mu_x]{\nabla_xg}^2,
\qquad\text{for all }g\in H^1(\mu_x)\text{ with }\textstyle\int_{\Omega} g\,\mathrm{d}\mu_x=0,
\end{equation}
with constant $m>0$;
\item[(ii)] the Hessian lower bound $\nabla^2U(x)\succeq-K\,\mathrm{Id}$ holds on $\Omega$ for some $K\ge0$;
\item[(iii)] the well-posedness Assumption~(ass:WP of the paper) holds.
\end{enumerate}
Let $f(t)=e^{t\mathscr{L}}f_0$ solve (equation eq:bke of the paper) with the specular boundary condition and $f_0\in\Lmuz$. With the friction coefficient
\[
\gamma=\sqrt{16m+2K},
\]
the following decay estimate holds for all $t\ge0$:
\begin{equation}
\nrm{f(t)}\le\sqrt3\,e^{-\Lambda t}\nrm{f_0},\qquad
\Lambda=\frac16\,\frac{\sqrt m}{\sqrt{2+\frac{K}{2m}}+\sqrt{4+\frac{K}{2m}}}.
\end{equation}
In particular, when $U$ is convex on $\Omega$ (so $K=0$), the choice $\gamma=4\sqrt m$ yields
\[
\Lambda=\frac{2-\sqrt2}{12}\,\sqrt m.
\]","status":"unverified"}

