{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.635577+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ac6e045a24bb4ffe4171c358bddfe11b1ec9635131e4df386fda415366dd83ec","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:reversal of arXiv:2411.13707, 'High-degree cubature on Wiener space \\ through unshuffle expansions'] The reversal operator $*$ (equation eq:reversal of the paper) is a coalgebra morphism w.r.t.\ $\Delta_\shuffle$.
\begin{proof}
Let $v_1,\ldots,v_n \in V$, $x = v_1\ldots v_n$, $r(i) \coloneqq n - i + 1$. Then, recalling the notation (equation eq:coproduct of the paper) 
\begin{align*}
    (* \otimes *) \circ \Delta_\shuffle x 
    ={}& \sum_{I \sqcup J = [n]} (v_I)^* \otimes (v_J)^* \\
    ={}& \sum_{I \sqcup J = [n]} (v^*)_{r(I)} \otimes (v^*)_{r(J)} \\
    ={}& \sum_{I' \sqcup J' = [n]} (v^*)_{I'} \otimes (v^*)_{J'} \\
    ={}&\Delta_\shuffle(v^*) . \qedhere
\end{align*}
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:31:20.594146+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ac6e582103ab8ad169769b903a6a78983c083129098887a593e9e19d91984e25","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Predictive regret under posterior approximation) of arXiv:2608.25706, 'Fast rates in Bayesian online learning with approximate posteriors'] Under \cref{ass:predictive-stability},
    \begin{equation}
