hence \(\Phi_{0.40}(f)> \Phi_{0.40}(\mathrm{Maj}_5)\). In particular, majority is not optimal at \(p=0.4\) already for \(n=5\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.971835+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ac89b258448a508a55d2a240a62f381c1a3f9ed95a525251ce210af64c0fd36f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:shifted_chain_rule of arXiv:2506.23062, 'Shifted Composition IV\@: \\ Toward Ballistic Acceleration for Log-Concave Sampling'] Let $\mathsf{X}$, $\mathsf{X}'$, $\mathsf{Y}$ be three jointly defined random variables on a standard probability space $\Omega$. Let $\mathbf{P}$, $\mathbf{Q}$ be two probability measures over $\Omega$, with superscripts denoting the laws of random variables under these measures. Then

	\begin{align*}
		\KL(\mathbf{P}^{\mathsf{Y}} \mathbin{\|} \mathbf{Q}^{\mathsf{Y}})
