{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.534888+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ad264326bff27a40c903d19c88b5a1200b8c12b0f92916057f480f706d25f701","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2603.28093, 'Random Stability of Random Variables'] Pick a distribution $Q$ on $[0, \infty)$, and assume $G(Q) \ne \{1\}$ is nontrivial. Then let $N$ be any nontrivial random variable with distribution from $G(Q)$, and let $Y$ be the standard $N$-stable random variable. Then:
