{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.973352+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ad576ed7c60df0ae0ce03fca5290df7bf567f3b3b2f69d5ba9c3d70b8ac5358a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm sum over surfaces representation in 't hooft limit of arXiv:2508.13827, 'Surface sums in two-dimensional large-$N$ lattice Yang--Mills: Cancellations and explicit '] {borga2024surfacesumslatticeyangmills}}]
There exists a number $\upbeta_0>0$ such that the following is true. Let $\Lambda_1\subseteq\Lambda_2\subseteq\hdots$ be any sequence of finite subsets of the lattice $\mathbb{Z}^2$ such that $\mathbb{Z}^2 = \cup_{N=1}^{\infty}\Lambda_N$. If $|\upbeta|\leq \upbeta_0$, then for any string $s=\{\ell_1,\dots,\ell_n\}$,
	\begin{align}
		\lim_{N\to\infty}\phi_{\Lambda_N,N,\upbeta}(s) =
		\prod_{i=1}^n\phi(\ell_i),
	\end{align}
	where
	\begin{equation}
		\phi(\ell)=\sum_{K:\mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^2}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}} \phi^K(\ell), \quad\text{with}\quad \phi^K(\ell)=\sum_{M\in\mathcal{N}\mathcal{P}\mathcal{M}(\ell,K)}\upbeta^{\area(K)}w_{\infty}(M).
	\end{equation}
	Here, $\area(K)= \sum_{p\in \mathcal{P}}K(p)$, the set $\mathcal{N}\mathcal{P}\mathcal{M}(\ell,K)$ is a certain collection of connected planar maps with the disk topology and $w_{\infty}(M)$ is a specific (positive or negative) weight associated with each map $M$.\footnote{We do not need the precise (and somewhat lengthy) definitions of the set $\mathcal{N}\mathcal{P}\mathcal{M}(\ell, K)$ and the weights $w_{\infty}(M)$ in this paper; interested readers can refer to the original paper for details. See also the discussion around (equation eq:mle-2 of the paper) for further explanation of why the precise definitions of the set $\mathcal{N}\mathcal{P}\mathcal{M}(\ell, K)$ and the weights $w_{\infty}(M)$ are not needed here.}

The infinite sum $\phi(\ell)$ is only over finite plaquette assignments $K:\mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^2}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$, i.e.\ plaquette assignments such that $\sum_{p\in\mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^2}}K(p)<\infty$. Moreover, the infinite sum $\phi(\ell)$ is absolutely convergent.\footnote{The sum $\phi^K(\ell)$ is a finite sum for all $K$ and $\ell$.} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.210315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ad5c39f7ebc34ea0721fa63e44316d4e4e26b593c21ec07787850fc220579c64","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 21 of arXiv:2604.27242, 'Statistical Inference for Homogenization Limits Driven by Wiener or Hermite Processes'] The following asymptotics hold as \(N\) grows
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If \(\alpha\in(-1,0)\): \begin{equation}
