{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.075662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae24a8aabfa7cdecc2bd7952b49528c0742ef5f161ae98f6d7f9ba64cb2b4cf7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thma 1 of arXiv:2609.03791, 'Canonical Bochner--K\"{a}hler potentials via the Sasaki--K\"{a}hler correspondence'] A real-valued function $F$ on a complex manifold $M$ is a K\"{a}hler potential of a Bochner--K\"{a}hler structure in a neighbourhood of a point $p\in M$ if and only if
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{bmatrix} 0& z^* & \frac{\I}{2} \end{bmatrix} \e^{2\I AF}\begin{bmatrix} 1\\ z\\ 0 \end{bmatrix}=0
    \end{equation}
    in some coordinates $z=(z_1,\ldots,z_n)$ centered at $p$ for some matrix $A\in \mathfrak{gl}_{n+2}(\mathbb{C})$ of the form 
    \begin{equation}   
    A=\begin{bmatrix}
        0& -2\I a^* & r\\0&\I X& a\\1&0&0
    \end{bmatrix},
    \end{equation}
    where 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $X\in \mathfrak{gl}_{n}(\mathbb{R})$ is diagonal with entries ordered such that $X_{j,j}\geq X_{j+1,j+1}$,
        \item $a$ is a vector with non-negative real entries satisfying $a_{j+1}=0$ whenever  $X_{j,j}=X_{j+1,j+1}$, and
        \item $r$ is a real number.
    \end{itemize}

    Moreover, such a matrix $A$ is uniquely determined by the germ at $p$ of the Bochner--K\"{a}hler structure being represented. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

