{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.562309+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae3ffdc4d87aa19409b9886b72be9a289e25af977a5f1f6773e8ef5a88e2c659","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm general-I of arXiv:2605.21366, 'The Martin boundary of the Directed Landscape'] There exists an event of full probability on which the following hold, for any eternal solution $\mathfrak h$ and any $s\in\mathbb{R}$.
     \begin{enumerate} [label={\rm(\alph*)}, ref={\rm\alph*}]   \itemsep=3pt 
    \item For any $\vartheta\in\mathbb{R}\times\{-,+\}$, $S\in\{L,R\}$,  ${\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}}\in\{-,+\}$, and $r\in\mathbb{R}$, $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_\vartheta(s)={\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}}\infty$ is equivalent to $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_\vartheta(r)={\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}}\infty$.
    \item For each $S\in\{L,R\}$ and any $\vartheta_1\neq\vartheta_2$ in $\mathbb{R}\times\{-,+\}$, $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_1}(s)=I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_2}(s)$ implies $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_1}(r)=I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_2}(r)$ for all $r\le s$. We then say that  $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_1}$ and  $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_2}$ coalesced. 
    \item For each $S \in \{L,R\}$ and any distinct $\vartheta_1,\vartheta_2 \in \mathbb{R}\times\{-,+\}$,  if both $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_1}$ and $I^{S,\mathfrak h}_{\vartheta_2}$ are finite, then they eventually coalesce. Consequently, for any $\vartheta_1\precneq\vartheta_2$ in $\mathcal V^{S,\mathfrak h}_s$, there exists $r < s$ such that  $\vartheta_2 \notin \mathcal V^{L,\mathfrak h}_r$ and $\vartheta_1 \notin \mathcal V^{R,\mathfrak h}_r$. 
    \item The representation (equation h-rep of the paper) can be restricted to $(\theta,{\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}})\in \mathcal V^{L,\mathfrak h}_s$:
    \begin{align}
    h(x,s)=\sup_{(\theta,{\raisebox{0.5pt}{\scaleobj{0.7}{\boxempty}}})\in\mathcal V^{S,\mathfrak h}_s}\bigl\{W^{\theta{\raisebox{1pt}{\scaleobj{0.6}{\boxempty}}}}(x,s;x_0,s_0)+a^{\theta{\raisebox{1pt}{\scaleobj{0.6}{\boxempty}}}}_{(x_0,s_0)}(\mathfrak h)\bigr\}
    \end{align}
More precisely, let $x_1<x_2$ be consecutive jump points of $x\mapsto \vartheta^{L,\mathfrak h}_{(x,s)}$ {\rm(}equivalently, consecutive values of $I^{L,\mathfrak h}_{\hspace{0.6pt}{\raisebox{1pt}{\scaleobj{0.6}{\bullet}}}\hspace{0.8pt}}(s)${\rm)}, allowing $x_1=-\infty$ or $x_2=\infty$ if there is no preceding or succeeding jump. Set $(\theta,{\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}})=\vartheta^{L,\mathfrak h}_{(x_2,s)}$, with the convention that if $x_2=\infty$, then $(\theta,{\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}})$ is the value attained after the last jump at $x_1$. Then
\begin{align}
\mathfrak h(x,s)=W^{\theta{\raisebox{1pt}{\scaleobj{0.6}{\boxempty}}}}(x,s;x_0,s_0)+a^{\theta{\raisebox{1pt}{\scaleobj{0.6}{\boxempty}}}}_{(x_0,s_0)}(\mathfrak h),
\quad \text{for all } x \in (x_1,x_2],
\end{align}
where the endpoint $x_2$ is excluded when infinite. An analogous statement holds with $L$ replaced by $R$, $(x_1,x_2]$ replaced by $[x_1,x_2)$, and $(\theta,{\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}})=\vartheta^{R,\mathfrak h}_{(x_1,s)}$, with the convention that if $x_1=-\infty$, the interval is $(-\infty,x_2)$ and $(\theta,{\raisebox{-0.2pt}{\scaleobj{1}{\boxempty}}})$ is the value before the first jump at $x_2$.
     \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.316848+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae425c699c516e5a1987dcd57793631512b2ba7f2dfea3c9e28a18e270974203","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:shadow3 of arXiv:2608.30973, 'Pseudo-Anosov flow and dynamics on guts'] Let \(l\) be a component of the frontier of \(\Theta(\widetilde{S})\) in \(P_{\varphi}\). Then,
