{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:05:57.818773+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae4f1c1a29c79254edc403b0f7a89f205bd20b2ca499c9abf33f64bbba103427","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 30 of arXiv:2608.30570, '2608.30570'] If a smooth curve $\sigma_i: [t_i^-, t_i^+]\to \mathcal{A}_k$ satisfies
 \begin{align*}
 \frac{d f\circ\sigma_i}{dt}(t)<0, \forall t\in (t_i^{-} ,t_i^+),
 \end{align*}
 we call $\sigma_i$ a {\bf negative gradient curve of $f$} in $\mathcal{A}_k$.

 If $\{\sigma_i\}_{i=1}^{l}$ are finite many  negative gradient curves of $f$ in $\mathcal{A}_k$ such that for each $1\le i<l$,  there exists a curve
 $\eta_i:[\tau_i^{-},\tau_i^+]\to \mathcal{A}_k$ satisfying
 \begin{align}
f(\eta_i(\tau))&\ge f(\sigma_{i}(t_i^+)), \forall \tau\in [\tau_i^-,\tau_i^+],\\
 \eta_i(\tau_i^-)=\sigma_i(t_i^+) \text{ for } 1\le i\le l, \quad &\text{ and } \quad \eta_i(\tau_i^+)=\sigma_{i+1}(t_{i+1}^-), \text{ for } 1\le i<l.
 \end{align}
 then we call $\sigma=\cup_{i=1}^l (\sigma_i\cup \eta_i)$ a {\bf pseudo negative gradient curve of $f$} in $\mathcal{A}_k$.    Note that we can choose new parametrization of the curves such that $t_{i+1}^{-}=\tau_{i}^+$ and $t_i^+=\tau_i^-$ such that $\sigma$ is a continuous curve defined on an interval. In the following, we always assume this.

 We say that a pseudo negative gradient curve $\sigma=\cup_{i=1}^{l} \sigma_i\cup \eta_i$ {\bf joins $x$ and $y$} if $\sigma_1(t_1^-)=x$ and $\eta_l(\tau_l^+)=y$.

Moreover, we introduce the notation $c(\sigma)=\cup_{i=1}^l\{\sigma_i(t_i^-),\sigma_i(t_i^+)\}$ and call it the {\bf joint points} of $\sigma$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:01:01.732091+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae4f3052dc8230b0f5ba2d8faef8ed557351f49a297d45ce94cc166f0775fe93","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:equivalent_descriptions of arXiv:2609.00163, 'Decompositions and diagrams of symplectic surfaces in Weinstein domains'] Let $K$ be a quasipositive transverse knot in the boundary of a Weinstein domain $(W,\omega)$, with  associated pointed mapping class $(\phi, F)$. The following are 
    descriptions of a positive ascending surface $S$ in $W$ with boundary $K$:
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item[(BUD)] a banded unlink diagram for $S$ in a Kirby--Weinstein diagram for $(W,\omega)$; 
        \item[(QPF)] a quasipositive factorization of $\phi$;
         \item[(BD)] a bisection with divides, i.e.\ a decomposition of $S$ into two trivial symplectic disk systems whose boundaries are max-$sl$  unlinks in a connect sum of standard $S^1 \times S^2$'s;
         \item[(SDD)] a shadow diagram with divides, i.e., an arc-and-curve system on a closed orientable surface satisfying the criteria in Definition~(shadowdiagram.def of the paper);
        \item[(PMC)] a factorization of $\phi$ into two quasipositive pointed mapping classes, i.e., $(\phi_1, F_1)$ and $(\phi_2, F_2)$ such that they each correspond to max-$sl$  unlinks in a connect sum of standard $S^1 \times S^2$'s as in Definition~(def:PMC of the paper).
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.775043+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae51652a067d5f4b14e4989e775bc0eb5bf417d908f7a8f4ee2b4a5f0016e71d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:linear of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] For $\mathbf{W}_1, \mathbf{W}_2 \in \mathcal{W}_N$, we have
