{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.328724+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae55314ac7e5436c6c45c04c00950d6cbd0e7d56277fdd3c0364b614a03f676a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma omega-fewer-quantifiers of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $X\in\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Sp}$ be levelwise closed, i.e. for all inner product spaces $V\in \textbf{\textup{O}}$, $X(V)$ is a closed orthogonal $G$-space. Then $X$ is a $G$-global $\Omega$-spectrum if and only if $\tilde\sigma({\mathcal{U}}_H)^\varphi\colon X(V)({\mathcal{U}}_H)^\varphi\to(\Omega^WX(V\oplus W)(\mathcal U_H))^\varphi$ is a weak homotopy equivalence for every homomorphism of compact Lie groups $\varphi\colon H\to G$, some (hence any) complete $H$-universe ${\mathcal{U}}_H$, and all $H$-representations $V,W$.
    \begin{proof}
        The assumptions guarantee that both $X(V)$ and $\Omega^WX(V\oplus W)$ are closed orthogonal $(G\times K)$-spaces for all $K$-representations $V,W$. Thus, Lemma~(lemma:we-between-closed of the paper) shows that $X$ is a $G$-global $\Omega$-spectrum if and only if \[\tilde\sigma({\mathcal{U}}_H)\colon X(V)({\mathcal{U}}_H)\to\Omega^WX(V\oplus W)({\mathcal{U}}_H)\]
        is a $\mathcal G_{H,K\times G}$-equivariant weak equivalence for all compact Lie groups $H,K$ and all $K$-representations $V,W$. As ${\mathcal{U}}_H$ is also a complete universe for every closed subgroup of $H$ (Remark~(rk:universe-subgroup of the paper)), this is equivalent via replacing $H$ by its subgroups to $\tilde\sigma({\mathcal{U}}_H)$ inducing a weak homotopy equivalence on $(\psi,\varphi)$-fixed points for all compact Lie groups $H$ and $K$, $K$-representations $V$ and $W$, and homomorphisms $\psi\colon H\to K$ and $\varphi\colon H\to G$. Restricting $V$ and $W$ to $H$-representations along $\psi$, this simplifies to the condition from the statement of the lemma, finishing the proof.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.818085+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae564d2ebfa0ed554f6e8e3891136fa7c9ccac1826f83d54f90f911448657c0d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:hereditary-endpoint of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Suppose the seed chart is prime-energy stable with exponents $(a,\mu)$.
Then, for every fixed $t\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$,
\begin{equation}
 |\mathcal R(h,d+1+t)|\geq h^{2a-\mu+t-o(1)}.
\end{equation}
Thus the deficit $d-(2a-\mu)$ from the ambient exponent is invariant throughout
this prime-energy-stable suspension tower.  In particular, the endpoint
identity $2a-\mu=d$ implies
\begin{equation}
 |\mathcal R(h,d+1+t)|=h^{d+t+o(1)}
\end{equation}
for every fixed $t\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.883090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae56e5be1290e5e666b8b05da2deb05ce0f46244f64abbf7f686e7feeae391de","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] A \emph{$\rmbeta$-language} is a many-sorted first-order language $\calL$ with a distinguished type $\ord$ together with a distinguished binary predicate $\le_\ord$, and the only terms in $\calL$ of type $\ord$ are variables. In particular, we do not allow constant symbols of type $\ord$ or function symbols of value $\ord$. A \emph{$\rmbeta$-theory} is a theory in some $\rmbeta$-language.

    A structure $M$ in a $\rmbeta$-language $\calL$ is a \emph{pre-$\rmbeta$-model} if the \emph{ordinal part} $(\ord^M,\le_\ord^M)$ of $M$ is a linear order. If the ordinal part of $M$ is a well-order, we call $M$ a \emph{$\rmbeta$-model}.
    We also define the following semantic implication notions: For a $\rmbeta$-language $\calL$ and a $\rmbeta$-theory $T$ in $\calL$, and a formula $\phi$ in $\calL$,
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $T\vDash_{\pre\rmbeta} \phi$ if every pre-$\rmbeta$-model of $T$ satisfies the universal closure of $\phi$. We call $\phi$ is \emph{pre-$\rmbeta$-valid in $T$}.
        \item $T\vDash_{\rmbeta} \phi$ if every $\rmbeta$-model of $T$ satisfies the universal closure of $\phi$. We call $\phi$ is \emph{$\rmbeta$-valid in $T$}.
    \end{enumerate}
    We omit $T$ in the previous notations when $T=\varnothing$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:57:07.201751+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae5a903317d0253830d1fb5d53990c0749ffa10fc02e10b10b16fa49f499c4b6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:iovw-rw-hitting of arXiv:2509.04333, 'The limiting law of the Discrete Gaussian level lines'] {IOVW20}}]
Consider the 2\emph{D} effective random walk ${\ensuremath{\mathsf S}}(\cdot)$. Let $\tau_G$ be the hitting time of a set $G\subset \mathbb Z^2$. There exists a constant $C>0$ such that, for every sequence $\delta_\ell$ that goes to~$0$ arbitrarily slowly as $\ell\to\infty$, and for all $u,v\in \mathbb Z$ such that $u/\sqrt{\ell},v/\sqrt{\ell}\in (0,\delta_\ell)$,    
\begin{equation*}
\hat{\ensuremath{\mathbf P}}_{(0,u)}(\tau_{(\ell,v)}<\tau_{\mathbb H_-}<\infty\mid {\mathsf{Bdd}}) \sim C \frac{h^+(u)h^-(v)}{\ell^{3/2}}\,, 
\end{equation*}
where $h^\pm$ are positive harmonic (in particular, asymptotically linear) functions. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.575880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae5c5c713f9d107237d2b26eca7612354964f2de97bdfa6d58b1a33edf8d4f47","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma tauBM of arXiv:2412.08932, 'Gaussian heat kernel asymptotics for conditioned random walks'] For any $x \geqslant 0$, $n\geqslant 1$ and $0 \leqslant a < b \leqslant \infty$, it holds  
\begin{align*}
\bb P \left( x +  \sigma B_{n} \in \left[a, b\right],  \tau_{x}^{\sigma B}>n \right) 
=  \frac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{ n} } \int_{a}^{b}  \psi \left( \frac{x}{\sigma \sqrt{n}},\frac{y}{\sigma \sqrt{n}} \right) dy. 
\end{align*}
In particular, by taking $a = 0$ and $b = \infty$, for any $x \geqslant 0$ and $n\geqslant 1$, 
\begin{align*}
\bb P \left( \tau_{x}^{\sigma B} > n \right) 
= 2 \Phi \left( \frac{x}{\sigma\sqrt{n}} \right)-1 
= \frac{2}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi n} } \int_{0}^x  e^{ - \frac{s^{2}}{2 \sigma^2 n} } ds.   
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

