{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.847743+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aea1ebf274c397cba92eb04f3d290a4dd65d76de0234fb8390d8fe6beaa1f898","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2608.29486, 'Cabled braids and their crossing matrices'] Let $b \in \mathcal{B}_n$. 
Let $b^{(k)}=f(b_1, b_2, \dots , b_n;b) \in \mathcal{B}_{n+1}$ with $b_1= \dots =b_{k-1}=b_{k+1}= \dots = b_n=id_1$ and $b_k=id_2$ ($k \in \{ 1, 2, \dots , n \}$). 
Then $C(b^{(k)})$ is obtained from $C(b)$ as follows. 
Divide $C(b)$ into $3 \times 3$ blocks by separating $k-1, 1, n-k$ rows and columns as follows: 
\begin{align*}
C(b)= \begin{bmatrix}
A_{11} & A_{12} & A_{13} \\
A_{21} & 0 & A_{23} \\
A_{31} & A_{32} & A_{33} 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{align*}
The crossing matrix of $b^{(k)}$ is given by 
\begin{align*}
C(b^{(k)})= \begin{bmatrix}
A_{11} & A_{12} & A_{12} & A_{13} \\
