{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:33:06.570057+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aefe268e7cce9cd8846733fa4cb4768417ab51d1079157fbebb3218bbd8467f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 61 ({\cite[Propositions 5.4-5.6) of arXiv:2608.26068, 'All once-extended 3D TQFTs are Reshetikhin--Turaev theories'] {BDSV4}}] 
    Let $p_+$ be as in Definition \ref{defn:cat-consts}. $Z_\mathcal{C}(\eta^\dag),Z_\mathcal{C}(\epsilon^\dag),Z_\mathcal{C}(\nu^\dag),Z_\mathcal{C}(\mu^\dag)$ act as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For $\nu^\dag$, contract the source ribbon graph along its one edge. Then, $$Z_\mathcal{C}(\nu^\dag):\Hom(\mathbbm{1},\mathbbm{1}) \rightarrow k$$ is the linear map sending $\id_{\mathbbm{1}}$ to $\frac1{p_+}$.
        \item For $\mu^\dag$, the only nonzero entry is $$Z_\mathcal{C}(\mu^\dag)_1^1: k\rightarrow \Hom(\mathbbm{1},\mathbbm{1})\otimes \Hom(\mathbbm{1},\mathbbm{1})$$ which sends $1$ to $p_+\id_{\mathbbm{1}}\otimes\id_{\mathbbm{1}}$.
        \item For $\epsilon^\dag$, $Z_\mathcal{C}(\epsilon^\dag)_i^j$ is only nonzero when $i=j$. Contract the target ribbon graph along the edge going through the left tube of the torus, and then by applying Lemma \ref{lem:cyc-shift}, we are left with defining linear maps $$Z_\mathcal{C}(\epsilon^\dag)_i^i: k \rightarrow \bigoplus_k \Hom(X_i\otimes X_k^*, X_i\otimes X_k^*).$$ These send $1$ to $\frac1{p_+}\sum_k\dim k\id_{X_i\otimes X_k^*}$.
        \item For $\eta^\dag$, contract the source ribbon graph along its internal edge. The only nonzero entries are the linear maps $$Z_\mathcal{C}(\eta^\dag)_{ij}^{ij}: \Hom(X_{i}\otimes X_{j},X_{i}\otimes X_{j})\rightarrow k$$ sending each $f$ to $p_+\frac{\tr(f)}{\dim i \dim j}$.
    \end{itemize}
    The remaining entries are necessarily the zero linear map as either the source or target space of labels is zero. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.771659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aeff0a9a9bc4bed3df7bf6a3bdfb39882d5aa25fe383537f1bb0393a5b5c7ec8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 (Count-chain transition) of arXiv:2605.14511, 'Clumsy and Careless:\\ Stationary-Entry Flux in Non-monotone Coupon Collectors'] The process $K_t$ is a Markov chain on $\{0,1,\ldots,n\}$. Its transition rule is
\[
K_{t+1}\mid K_t=k\sim
\begin{cases}
\Bin(k,q),&\text{with probability }k/n,\\[1mm]
\Bin(k+1,q),&\text{with probability }(n-k)/n.
\end{cases}
\]
In particular, the chain is irreducible and aperiodic and therefore has a unique stationary law $\nu_n$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:31.958165+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af02471cc1c35679f55589eb27cfc43789f097a1c7da6fc9a0b907beb31f2807","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 10 of arXiv:2512.02286, 'Pfaffian Transition Probability and Correlation Kernel of TASEP in Half-space'] For fixed integers $N\geq M\geq 0$, let $x=(x_1,\dots,x_N)$ be the ordered coordinates of TASEP particles at time $t$ and let $y=(y_1,\dots,y_M)$ denote the ordered initial coordinates of the particles. In the case $y_M > N-M+1$, the half-line open TASEP has transition probability from an empty state to $x$ given by the following: when $N+M$ is even
	\begin{equation}

		\mathbb{P}_t(y\to x) = (-1)^{\binom{N}{2}}\mathrm{e}^{-\alpha t} \pf\left[\begin{array}{cc}
			\mathsf{Q} & \mathsf{U} \\[5pt]
			-\mathsf{U}^T & 0
		\end{array}\right],
	\end{equation}
	when $N+M$ is odd
	\begin{equation}

		\mathbb{P}_t(y\to x) = (-1)^{\binom{N}{2}}\mathrm{e}^{-\alpha t} 
		\pf\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
			\mathsf{Q} & \mathsf{p} & \mathsf{U}\\[5pt]
			-\mathsf{p}^T & 0 & 0 \\[5pt]
		 	-\mathsf{U}^T & 0 & 0
		\end{array}\right].
	\end{equation}
    Here, $\mathsf{Q}$ is a skew-symmetric $N\times N$-dimensional matrix and $\mathsf{p}$ is a column vector of length $N$ which are the same as those in Theorem \ref{thm: transition prob pfaffian kernel}, while $\mathsf{U}$ is a $N\times M$-dimensional matrix whose entries are given by
    \begin{align*}
        [\mathsf{Q}]_{i,j} := & Q_{i,j}(x_{N-i+1},x_{N-j+1}), \\ 
        [\mathsf{p}]_i := & p_{i}(x_{N-i+1}), \\
        [\mathsf{U}]_{i,k} := & U_{i-k}(x_{N-i+1}-y_{M-k+1}),
    \end{align*}
    for all $1\leq i,j\leq N$ and $1\leq k\leq M$. The functions $Q_{i,j}$ and $p_i$ are defined by \eqref{eq:Q-kernel} and \eqref{eq:p-kernel} respectively while we define the integral kernel
	\begin{align}

		U_{k}(z) := \oint_\gamma \frac{\dd w}{2\pi\ii} \frac{(w-1)^{N-M-k}\mathrm{e}^{t(w-1)}}{w^{z-k+N-M+1}},
	\end{align}
    where the contour $\gamma$ encloses the (possible) pole at the origin. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.799557+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af03b1c3c15bcb159317d883eb52ce5efe4e0328a5810b81d22a176b6eed0fe0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 69 of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] Let $\delta > 0$ and assume $|\eta_i - \eta_{i+1}| \geq N^{-1 - \delta}$ for all $i = 1, \dots, N-1$. Then
