{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.724532+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af79ae73937d8b3204a1a5fda768c44246e3a3dbf04dd3e566e11c24d798a55b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:ulip-uip of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] A logic $L$ with a language $\mathscr{L}$ is said to enjoy \emph{Uniform interpolation property (UIP)} if
  for any $\varphi \in \mathscr{L}$ and any finite $P \subseteq \mathord{\mathrm{PropVar}}$,
  there is $\chi \in \mathscr{L}$ such that:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathrm{V}(\chi) \subseteq \mathrm{V}(\varphi) \setminus P$;
    \item $\varphi \to \chi \in L$;
    \item $\chi \to \psi \in L$ for any $\psi \in \mathscr{L}$ such that $\varphi \to \psi \in L$ and $\mathrm{V}(\psi) \cap P = \emptyset$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

