{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.324583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b33105eae937e064313d816b663eee30f7f8f82906b844943266dba518782e81","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop-conjugate of arXiv:2411.06301, 'Inverting the operation of conditioning a branching process on extinction'] Let $X = \{X(n) \}_{n\in\mathbbm N_0}$ be a positive regular, non-singular $d$-type branching process (Definition~(def-bp-basic of the paper)) with generating function $f$. Assume that the extinction probability started from any state is one. 
Let $A \in [1,\infty)^d$ solve $f(A) = A$. Define a new branching process $\widehat X = \{\widehat X(n)  \}_{n\in\mathbbm N_0}$ with offspring distribution determined by
\begin{equation} 
\widehat{\mathbbm E}_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ F(X_1(1) , \dots, X_d(1))   \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] 
 = \frac{1}{A_k} \mathbbm E_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ F(X_1(1),\dots,X_d(1)) \prod_{j=1}^d A_j^{X_j(1)} \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] ,\quad\forall k\in \{1,\dots,d\}    
\end{equation}  
for every bounded measurable function $F : \mathbbm N_0^d \to \mathbbm R$. Then the extinction probability for $\widehat X$ started from an individual of type $k$ is $1/A_k$, and the $\widehat{\mathbbm P}_k$-conditional law of $\widehat X$ given the event that it eventually becomes extinct is the same as the $\mathbbm P_k$-law of $X$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:46.468071+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b33780a0499ba5f5526ffdad6990bcb22ff217ce9424f47c36683464184e7688","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 60 (Change of variables maps) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] We define the change of variables map from geometric to Cartesian coordinates as follows:
	\begin{align} 
		\Upsilon(t,u,x^2,x^3) 
		& 
		\eqdef (t,x^1,x^2,x^3).
	\end{align}

	We define the change of variables map from geometric coordinates 
	to rough adapted coordinates as follows:
	\begin{align} 
		\CHOVgeotorough{0}(t,u,x^2,x^3) 
		& 
		\eqdef (\timefunctionarg{0},u,x^2,x^3).
	\end{align}

	We define the map $\CHOVgeotomumuxmu$ 
	from geometric coordinates to ``$(\upmu,\muX \upmu,x^2,x^3)$-space''
	and its Jacobian 
	$\CHOVJacobiangeotomumuxmu$ as follows:
	\begin{subequations}
		\begin{align} 
			\CHOVgeotomumuxmu(t,u,x^2,x^3)
			& \eqdef (\upmu,\muX \upmu,x^2,x^3),
			\\
			\CHOVJacobiangeotomumuxmu(t,u,x^2,x^3)
			& \eqdef 
			\frac{\partial \CHOVgeotomumuxmu(t,u,x^2,x^3)}{\partial(t,u,x^2,x^3)}
			=
			\frac{\partial (\upmu,\muX \upmu,x^2,x^3)}{\partial(t,u,x^2,x^3)}.

		\end{align}
	\end{subequations}

	We define the map $\CHOVroughtomumuxmu{0}$ 
	from rough adapted coordinates to ``$(\upmu,\muX \upmu,x^2,x^3)$-space''
	and its Jacobian 
	$\CHOVJacobianroughtomumuxmu{0}$ as follows:
	\begin{subequations}
		\begin{align} 
			\CHOVroughtomumuxmu{0}(\timefunctionarg{0},u,x^2,x^3)
			& \eqdef (\upmu,\muX \upmu,x^2,x^3),
			\\
			\CHOVJacobianroughtomumuxmu{0}(\timefunctionarg{0},u,x^2,x^3)
			& \eqdef 
			\frac{\partial \CHOVroughtomumuxmu{0}(\timefunction,u,x^2,x^3)}{\partial(\timefunctionarg{0},u,x^2,x^3)}
			=
			\frac{\partial (\upmu,\muX \upmu,x^2,x^3)}{\partial(\timefunctionarg{0},u,x^2,x^3)}.

		\end{align}
	\end{subequations}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:17:33.984660+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b33815ef659727c2aebeb18a9464b67ec862c6f4aa5354c46732bb74e0c4ba5e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2609.03339, 'Exterior Serrin Rigidity for the Homogeneous \texorpdfstring{$k$}{k}-Hessian Equation\\'] Define 
$$J^j[w_{\varepsilon}]:=T^{ij}(D^2w_{\varepsilon})(w_{\varepsilon}Z^{w_{\varepsilon}}_{i}-Z^{w_{\varepsilon}}(w_{\varepsilon
    })_{i}),$$
    $$Q^j[w_{\varepsilon}]:=T^{ij}(D^2w_{\varepsilon})(w_{\varepsilon})_{i},$$
    $$ K^j[w_{\varepsilon}]:=T^{ij}(D^2w_{\varepsilon})Z^{w_{\varepsilon}}_i,$$
    then 
    \begin{equation}
        \diver  Q[w_{\varepsilon}]=kF_{\varepsilon},
    \end{equation}\
\begin{equation}
        \diver K[w_{\varepsilon}]=x\cdot DF_{\varepsilon}+k(1+q)F_{\varepsilon},
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}
