{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.446027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3acd084c93ff67433213edce0b5122f621e6d0e54538076caf61eefead16be2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 7 of arXiv:2606.15842, 'A small noise approximation for Muller's Ratchet'] Let $(S_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the semigroup generated by $G^{(1)}$. Started in $x \in \mathbb S$, the solution of \eqref{eq:dynsys1} satisfies, for $\xi \in \mathbb R_+$, 
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
  S_t g_{\xi}(x) & = g_{\xi}(x(t)) = \frac{g_{\alpha t + \xi}(x)}{g_{\alpha t}(x)}
  \exp\Big(-\theta(1-e^{- \xi})(1-e^{-\alpha t})\Big)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
as well as, for $\xi, \eta \in \mathbb R_+$,
\begin{align}

    G^{(1)}S_t g_{\xi} & = \exp\Big(-\theta(1-e^{- \xi})(1-e^{-\alpha t})\Big)\Big(\lambda e^{-\alpha t}(1 - e^{-\xi})\frac{g_{\alpha  t + \xi}}{g_{\alpha t}} + \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \frac{g_{\alpha  t + \xi}}{g_{\alpha t}}\Big), \\ 
    G^{(1)}S_t g_{\xi} g_{\eta} & = (S_t g_{\xi})\, G^{(1)}S_t g_{\eta} + (S_t g_{ \eta})\, G^{(1)}S_t g_{ \xi}, \\ 

    \nu \cdot G^{(2)}S_t g_{ \xi} & = 
    \frac{g_{\alpha  t + \xi}(x)g_{2\alpha t} - g_{\alpha t} g_{2\alpha t+\xi}}{g_{\alpha t}^3}\exp\Big(-\theta(1-e^{-\xi})(1-e^{-\alpha t})\Big), \\
    \nu \cdot G^{(2)}S_t g_{\xi} g_{\eta}  & = \exp\Big(-\theta(2-e^{-\xi} - e^{- \eta})(1-e^{-\alpha t})\Big) \notag \\ 
     &  \cdot 
    \frac{
    3g_{\alpha t + \xi} g_{\alpha  t + \eta}g_{2\alpha t} - 2 g_{\alpha  t + \xi} g_{\alpha t}g_{2\alpha t + \eta} - 2 g_{\alpha t + \eta} g_{\alpha t} g_{2\alpha t+\xi} + g_{\alpha t}^2 g_{2\alpha t+\xi+\eta}}{g_{\alpha t}^4}. 
\end{align}
Moreover, for $f_{t,\xi,\eta} := \nu \cdot G^{(2)}S_t g_{\xi} g_{\eta}$, there are bounded functions $g_{s, t, \xi, \eta}, g^1_{s, t, \xi, \eta}, g^2_{s, t, \xi, \eta} \in \text{span} \{g_{\xi_1} \cdots g_{\xi_m}: m \in \mathbb N, \xi_1,...,\xi_m \in \mathbb R_+ \}$ and $a, a_1, a_2$ (all not depending on $\nu$) such that 
\begin{align}

    S_s f_{t,\xi, \eta} = \frac{g_{s,t,\xi, \eta}}{g_{\alpha (s+t)}^{a}}, \qquad G^{(1)} S_s f_{t,\xi, \eta} = \frac{g^1_{s,t,\xi, \eta}}{g_{\alpha (s+t)}^{a_1}}, \qquad \nu \cdot G^{(2)} S_s f_{t,\xi, \eta} = \frac{g^2_{s,t,\xi, \eta}}{g_{\alpha (s+t)}^{a_2}}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.273470+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3b13a55d4aefcddc416e2ea040f6e7bbdb14a2ad6fa0219ffd667dab4b33e41","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Filtered process) of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] A filtered process is a five-tuple 
