{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.497191+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3eed9140cd216219e73796bab45fb3655d714574deb7d0e67f5c8ec78dc9c5f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary An-strict of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Assume the hypotheses of \textit{Claim~(An-monotone of the paper)}.
Fix $n\geq 0$ and define, on $[0,\tfrac{1}{2}]$, 
\begin{equation}
p_{n}(x)=(1-2x)(L_{n}(1-x)-L_{n}(x)),  
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
q_{n}(x)=|D_{n}(x)|=|L_{n}(x)+L_{n}(1-x)-1|.  
\end{equation}
If

\begin{enumerate}
\item $A_{n}>0$,

\item $p_{n}$ is strictly decreasing on $[0,\tfrac{1}{2}]$,

\item $q_{n}$ is strictly increasing on $[0,\tfrac{1}{2}]$,
\end{enumerate}

then 
\begin{equation}
\left( E_{n}-\frac{1}{2}\right) \left( E_{n}+E_{n+1}-1\right) >0,

\end{equation}
and consequently 
\begin{equation}
A_{n+1}<A_{n}.  
\end{equation}
In particular, if the above three conditions hold for every index $n$ with $
A_{n}>0$, then $\{A_{n}\}_{n\geq 0}$ is strictly decreasing until it reaches 
$0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.788121+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3f0d95febfb8b255704ca0ae369e682720ac0bb68d6bc7864d076796a986db5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem Lem6.1-replacement of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] For $R$ and $\Delta$ as in Proposition~(prop:horizontal-splitting-beta-uniform of the paper), there exists $c_3,c_4$ and $s_0$ (independent of $\beta)$ such that if $\beta>\beta_0, s\ge s_0$ and $\ell \ge \beta e^{2\beta}$, and $\bar \alpha \ge \sqrt{C \log \ell}$ for $C$ large independent of $\beta$, then 
        \begin{align*}
            \pi_R^\eta (\partial \lambda_1 =\{x,y\}\,,\,\partial \lambda_2 = \{u,v\}) \le \ell^{c_3} e^{ - c_4 \bar \alpha^2}\,.
        \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

