\]
where, for $\tilde x \in \R^{d_1}$, $(\mathbf x_t(\tilde x))_{t \ge 0}$ denotes the solution of the averaged ODE \eqref{eq:averaged0}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.284115+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5428fd3a89fedd25f594c3eb75c5d3bbd15e86ada0dbca8ac87c3c093e218f4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 25 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Consider the Young-It\^o SDE as below
\begin{equation}
\dd x_t =g(x_t) \dd t+ f(t, x_t) \dd B^H_t + \sigma(x_t) \dd W_t,\ x_0 = x.
\end{equation}
Here, the integral with respect to fractional Brownian motion \(B^H\) is in the pathwise Young integrals by Wiener integrals that introduced in Section 2. The integral with respect to BM \(W\) is in the It\^o sense.
Assume \(f\) is of \(BC^2\), \(\sigma\) and \(g\) are both Lipschitz continuous and linear growth with respect to \(x\). Then, for any function \(\Psi\in C_b^2\), 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Psi(x_t)&=\Psi(x_0)+\int_{0}^{t}D_x\Psi(x_r)g(x_r)\dd r+\int_{0}^{t}D_x\Psi(x_r)f(r, x_r)\dd B_r^H\\
&+\int_{0}^{t}D_x\Psi(x_r)\sigma(x_r)\dd W_r+\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{t}D^2_{xx}\Psi(x_r)a(x_r)\dd r
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
holds. Here, let \(a:=\sigma\sigma^*\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.512468+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5432647586e5c8e7e73363055bc57a6286a9230c15477d43ede5ad70c2391b1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary general-lobes of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Assume that $Q$ is continuous and strictly increasing
