{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.080682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b88212646f85c1e10618b3a7b3fb4293ff797cf0890838f3603c91f1ebd1db3e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem maintheorem of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] Let $\pi$ be a  reproduction law and $\omega$ a distribution on $\mathbb{R}$ such that:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item both $\pi$ and $\omega$ have finite expectation,
        \item the law $\omega$ is atomless and admits a density on some open interval,
        \item denoting by $\phi$ the generating function of $\pi$ and setting $\hat{\phi}= \frac{\phi'}{\phi'(1)}$, the function
