\item If $(r_k)$ is log-convex, then $\prod_ir_{\alpha_i}\geq r_1^{q-2}r_2$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.061883+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b94c6e9cc558c69c879685315794c8084d5cdcafed7579d3c37912b08fff6c31","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem bootstrap of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Let $I$ be an open interval in ${\mathbb R}$ and let $y_0\in I$. 
For each $n\in {\mathbb N}$, let $A_n$ be an event, and let $\{Y_n(y)\}_{y\in I}$ be a stochastic process. 
Let $r\in{\mathbb R}$. 
Suppose that the following two conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item There is a continuous function $f$ on $I$ such that
\begin{equation} 

    \lim_{n\to\infty} \mathbb{P}(\{Y_n(y)\le r \} \cap A_n) =f(y)
    \qquad \text{for every} \quad y \in I\setminus \{y_0\}.
\end{equation}
\item There is a continuous function $g$ on $I\times I$ satisfying $g(y,y)=0$ for $y\in I$,  such that
\begin{equation*}

    \lim_{n\to\infty} \mathbb{P}(Y_n(y)\le r, \, Y_n(y')>r  ) =g(y,y') 
    \qquad \text{for every} \quad  y, y'\in I \quad \text{with} \quad y\neq y'. 
\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate}
Then, (equation eq:convergenced2 of the paper) also holds for $y=y_0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.128087+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b94e72acdd919a2e3b3d9ed1fd9601d88a0d2e75d5bd2a29a496b1573b3f098d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop B-prop of arXiv:2411.15954, 'A gradient model for the Bernstein polynomial basis'] For each $n,L\in\mathbb{N}$ with $L\geq n$, the B($n$,$L$) enjoys the following properties:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]

    \item \textit{Partition of the unity}: for any integer $ 0<L<N/2 $ it holds that
    \begin{align}
    \mathcal{L}_N^{\text{SSEP}}
    =\sum_{n=0}^L\mathcal{L}_N^{n,L};
    \end{align}

    \item \textit{Symmetry}: for any $\eta\in \Omega_N$ it holds that $\mathbf{b}_{n,L}(\eta)=\mathbf{b}_{L-n,L}(\overline{\eta})$, with $\eta\mapsto\overline{\eta}$ the map that exchanges the particles by vacant sites and vice-versa;

    \item \textit{Interpolation}: for each $n\in\mathbb{N}_+$ it holds that \textnormal{B($n$,$n$)$=$PMM($n$)};

    \item \textit{Range}: For each $\eta\in\Omega_N$ it holds that $\mathbf{b}_{n,L}(\eta)\in [0,1]$. 

    \item \textit{Blocked configurations}: there is $\eta\in\Omega_N$ such that $\mathbf{b}_{n,\ell}(\tau^x\eta)=0$ for any $x\in\mathbb{T}$;

    \item \textit{Mobile Clusters}: any box of length $L+2$ with exactly $n+1$ particles constitutes a mobile cluster. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:26:02.365347+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b94fe594e3f41c59a9db48d9f15fdcc1a0d99f4ab2a432973502e4ddc89087fe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn $\partial$-ribbons of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] A \textbf{system of $\bm \partial$-ribbons} adapted to $Z$ is a finite collection of pairwise disjoint smoothly embedded strips $R_m = \sigma_m \times [-1, 1] \subset M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ such that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The arcs $\sigma_m \times \{\pm1\}$ are tangent to the foliation $\mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$, and each ribbon is transverse to $\mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ and tangent to $Z$,

        \item For the initial point $p_m$ of $\sigma_m$, the interval $p_j \times [-1,1]$ lies on $\partial_* M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ away from $N_\partial^\mathrm{win}$, and for the end point $q_m$, the interval $q_m \times [-1,1]$ lies either in the interior of a vertical side of an annular fence, or in the interior of a window. Moreover, $R_m$ intersects $\partial M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ along these intervals only, and transversally. 

        We further require that the ribbons are tangent to $\partial_y$ inside of $N_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$.
    \end{itemize}

    This system is \textbf{$\bm \partial$-full} if for each point $p \in \partial_* M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$, the leaf of $\partial \mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ passing through $p$ intersects interiors of initial intervals of $\partial$-ribbons, or interiors of windows, on both sides of $p$.","status":"unverified"}

