{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.251015+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9c64eeb460055af8817a59d70800dd8d4815d32d8a8fba8c48a8674b66a805a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 58 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Fix integers $j_0 \in [0, \mathfrak{j}]$ and $i_0 \in [0, a_{j_0}]$. Assume that, for any integers $j \in [j_0, \mathfrak{j}]$ and $i \in [0, a_j]$ such that either $j > j_0$ or $i < i_0$, the following holds w.o.p.: 
\begin{flalign}

	\begin{aligned}
		& H_{\mathsf T_{i,j},j} (w) \le \mathcal{H}_{q_{i}} (w) + N^{\delta-1}, \qquad \text{for each $w \in \mathfrak{X}$}; \\
		& H_{\mathsf T_{i,j},j} (w) \le \mathcal{H}_{q_{i}} (w), \qquad \qquad  \quad  \text{for each $w \notin \mathfrak{L}_{q_i}^+ (\delta)$}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{flalign}

\noindent Then w.o.p., we have for any $t \in [\mathsf{T}_{i_0, j_0}, N^5]$ that 
\begin{flalign}

	\begin{aligned}
		& H_{t,j_0} (z_0) \le  \mathcal{H}_{q_{i_0}} (z_0) + N^{\delta-1}, \qquad \text{for each $z_0 \in \mathfrak{X}$}; \\ 
		& H_{t,j_0} (z_0) \le \mathcal{H}_{q_{i_0}} (z_0), \qquad \qquad \quad    \text{for each $z_0 \notin \mathfrak{L}_{q_{i_0}}^+ (\delta)$}.
	\end{aligned} 
\end{flalign} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.580662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9c9aa641c4fd49611be125945f434ca516388dbdd795bfd060e0bc2925b0a8d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm.dl-convergence of arXiv:2606.30517, '\textsc{Convergence towards Ideal Poisson--Voronoi tessellations with a focus on Diestel--'] The Diestel--Leader graph ${\rm DL}(p,q)$ satisfies the conditions of (thm.ctfg of the paper). If $p\geq q$, then $\restrict{F}{\mathbb N}$ is log-regularly-varying with index $\ln p$. In addition, the measure $\nu_0$ on $\partial {\rm DL}(p,q)$ has the following description:
		\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
			\item If $p>q$, then $\nu_0$ is concentrated on $\partial T\subseteq \partial {\rm DL}(p,q)$ and is the harmonic measure on $\partial T$. In this case, {${\rm IPVT}_{\xi}({\rm DL}(p,q)) = \pi^{-1}({\rm IPVT}_{\xi'}(T))$ for some $\xi'$}, where $\pi:{\rm DL}(p,q)\to T$ denotes the projection on the first coordinate.
			\item If $p=q$, then $\nu_0$ is the average of the harmonic measures on $\partial T$ and $\partial T'$. 
		\end{enumerate}
		In addition, the edge-measured version of ${\rm DL}(p,q)$ satisfies (thm.ctfemg of the paper) with the same growth index $\ln p$, and the measures $\nu_t$ in (thm.ctfemg of the paper) are identical to the measure $\nu_0$ of the vertex-measured version. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.596078+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9ceb7ffe1083728a25f261c3296c6adbab774c2e9cc2e9307dd00c78cb670c9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:JumpHKE of arXiv:2412.05030, 'Characterization of Subordinate Symmetric Markov Processes'] Let $\phi_j$ be a scale function.
  For a  regular Dirichlet form $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$ of pure-jump type, 
  we say that HK($\phi_j$) holds if there exists a heat kernel $p(t,x,y)$ of $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$
  and 
  there exist a properly exceptional set $\mathcal{N}$ and 
  constants $c_1 ,c_2 > 0$ such that
  for all $t > 0$ and $x,y \in M \backslash \mathcal{N}$,
  \begin{align}
    c_1 p^{(j)}(t,x,y) \le p(t,x,y) \le  c_2p^{(j)}(t,x,y).

  \end{align}
  Here, $p^{(j)}(t,x,y)$ is defined by
  \begin{equation}
    p^{(j)}(t,x,y) := \frac{1}{V(x,\phi_j^{-1}(t))} \wedge \frac{t}{V(x,d(x,y))\phi_j(d(x,y))}, \quad t > 0,x,y \in M.

  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.086712+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9cf6ec4663ada069dc97d875137eb21a758f2d30fabab47a9cb299fc949d2e7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary co-atomic decomposition of arXiv:2411.12623, 'Random signed measures'] Let $\xi$ be a random signed measure on $S$, then
