{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.621647+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba0d2034bab5aa9f408f7ae99dda70d631275cd04e370633099ce0f8087bb190","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 (Boundary of  weighted $\ell_p$-balls) of arXiv:2605.11995, 'Intrinsic volumes of \(\ell_p\)-balls and a continuum of Maxwell--Poincar\'e--Borel laws f'] With the above notation, the following hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] For every $p>1$, the boundary  $\partial \mathbb B_p^n(a)$ is a $C^1$-hypersurface.  The outer unit normal at $x\in \partial \mathbb B_p^n(a)$ (the Gauss map of $\mathbb B_p^n(a)$ at $x$) is given by
\begin{equation}
\nu(x)=\frac{\nabla F(x)}{\|\nabla F(x)\|}
=
\frac{\left(a_1^p |x_1|^{p-2}x_1,\ldots, a_n^p |x_n|^{p-2}x_n\right)}
{\left(\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^{2p}|x_i|^{2p-2}\right)^{1/2}}.
\end{equation}
Let $q=\frac{p}{p-1}$ be the H\"older conjugate of $p$.  The support function of $\mathbb B_p^n(a)$ is
\begin{equation}
h_{\mathbb B_p^n(a)}(u) := \sup_{x\in \mathbb B_p^n(a)} \langle u, x\rangle
=
\left(\sum_{i=1}^n |u_i/a_i|^q\right)^{1/q}, \qquad u\in \R^n.
\end{equation}
\item[(b)] For every $p>1$, the boundary is $C^2$ near every point $x\in \partial \mathbb B_p^n(a)$ such that $x_i \neq 0$ for every $i=1,\ldots, n$.  If $p\geq 2$, then the boundary $\partial \mathbb B_p^n(a)$ is $C^2$ at every point.
\item[(c)] For all $p>1$, the curvature measures of $\mathbb B_p^n(a)$ do not charge the set $\partial \mathbb B_p^n(a) \cap \{x_1\ldots x_n=0\}$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.846670+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba19a81b13edabd2ae24c3a55051f2d1dc2243585295bf83ac08a430f1ed6625","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:GaussianEigenvalues1 of arXiv:2410.23915, 'Asymptotic Bounds and Online Algorithms for Average-Case Matrix Discrepancy'] Let $X \sim \mathrm{GOE}(m)$. The joint probability density function of the ordered eigenvalues $\lambda_1(X) \leq \ldots \leq \lambda_m(X)$ is given by
        \begin{equation*}
            \lambda \mapsto C_m \exp{-\frac{1}{4}\left\lVert\ifblank{\lambda}{\:\cdot\:}{\lambda}\right\rVert_2^2} \Delta(\lambda)
        \end{equation*}
        on the Weyl chamber $\mathbb{R}^m_\geq \colonequals \{\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^m : \lambda_1 \leq \ldots \leq \lambda_m\}$, where the constant $C_m$ is defined as
        \begin{equation}

            C_m \colonequals 2^{-m(m+3)/4} \prod_{i=1}^m \frac{1}{\Gamma(i/2)}.
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:45:27.887581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba1a908c0143a35a0e1db0ff1301b7254fcd6f57491fc656ca9e42032f35dd5b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:flatly_parallel of arXiv:2607.02410, 'Polynomial mixing for polygonal side matchings'] Let $e_{1} = \{A,B\},e_{2} = \{C,D\} \in C_{\mathrm{cross}}$ be two chords which do not cross each other. The chords partition the sides of $P \setminus \{A,B,C,D\}$ into three disjoint sets: 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item The set $I^{e_1}$ of sides which are separated from $e_2$ by $e_1$.
		\item The set $I^{e_2}$ of sides which are separated from $e_1$ by $e_2$.
		\item The set $I^{e_1,e_2}$ of sides which are between $e_1$ and $e_2$.
	\end{itemize}
	The chords $e_{1}$ and $e_{2}$ are said to be \textit{flatly parallel} if every chord with an endpoint in $I^{e_1,e_2}$ has both endpoints in $I^{e_1,e_2}$, and none of those chords intersect each other.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.191671+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba1b1aa103139084b1cad928040c0ddcb40763e66964aa0106af0e9a17c49519","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 (Trace incompatibility at the wall) of arXiv:2609.03916, 'Halfspace Theorems for Anisotropic Minimal Surfaces and Perimeter Minimizers'] Let $L\subset H$, let $G:=H\setminus L$, and suppose that on a relatively
open set $\Gamma\subset P$,
\begin{equation}
 \Tr_PL=1\qquad\HH^n\text{-a.e. on }\Gamma.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 |D\chi_L|\llcorner\Gamma=\HH^n\llcorner\Gamma,
 \qquad
 |D\chi_G|\llcorner\Gamma=0.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the identity
\begin{equation}
 |D\chi_G|=\HH^n\llcorner P+|D\chi_L|
\end{equation}
cannot hold when $\HH^n(\Gamma)>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.796069+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba1b36469aa741243b262935e93c05e38c51cc0f025d02346c178e4ef27a3e9c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:integral_edge_crossing of arXiv:2509.23496, 'Crossing probabilities in geometric inhomogeneous random graphs'] Let $\gamma$ and $\delta$ be as in Proposition~(prop:asymp_escape_crossing_probabilities of the paper). Then, we have
