{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.700479+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba4891d06bed6eb646a7a696c6a0b0a1643fe122840ac49d538a5a1df9b6d491","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:production of arXiv:2609.00668, 'Rigidity of Euclidean Minimal Hypersurfaces under Nonuniform Diagonal Dilations'] Assume that the weights are pair-sum nonresonant and put $N=\binom n2$. Suppose there exist $N$ distinct output levels $c_1,\ldots,c_N>0$ for which the isoquants $M_{c_r}$ are Euclidean minimal. Then every isoquant is affine. More precisely, for any fixed reference level $c_0>0$ there are constants $A_i>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
M_c=\left\{x\in\mathbb R_{++}^n:\sum_{i=1}^n A_i\left(\frac{c}{c_0}\right)^{-g_i/q}x_i=1\right\},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
f(x)=c_0\rho(x)^q,
\end{equation}
where $\rho(x)>0$ is the unique solution of
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^nA_ix_i\rho(x)^{-g_i}=1.
