for some probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb R$, then $\mu_n$ converges weakly to $\mu$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.321148+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bad18c5437e5ae518fe993ed1315cfbfe18c2bb7e20874b363ea92712edabbc4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2607.27150, 'Modelling Anomalous Diffusion: The Role of CTRWs and Non-Local Dynamics'] Let $c>0$ be a scale parameter and denote by $Y_c(t)$, $t \geq 0$, and $Y_c^+(t)$, $t \geq 0$, respectively, the CTRW and the OCTRW defined as above, depending on $c>0$. Assume that $(S^c_{[cu]},T^c_{[cu]})$, $u\ge0$, converges to $(A(u), S(u))$, $u\ge0$, weakly in $D([0, +\infty), \mathbb{R}^{d+1})$ {as $c\to +\infty$}\footnote{Here, the notation $[cu]$ denotes the integer part of the real number $cu$.}, with $S$ being strictly increasing and unbounded. Then, as $c \to +\infty$,
		\begin{align}
			&( Y_c(t), T_c(t) ) \to (A(L(t)-), S(L(t)-))^+\\
			& ( Y_c^+(t), T_c^+(t) ) \to (A(L(t)), S(L(t)))
		\end{align}
		weakly in $D([0, +\infty), \mathbb{R}^{d+1})$ (endowed with the Skorokhod $J$ topology).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.963721+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bad39c0de757782b3539d6a211a49c67effc7ffe6440c0345f5fc6874bd4d7c0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 (Replacement Lemma) of arXiv:2604.00742, 'Delayed Logistic equation as a limit\\ of long memory Markov chains'] Define the integrated error
