{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.568981+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bbce0bf5c5fea6bb9b850fb9db63febb8db3ccddfed4a2adfdc91e9be7680c9c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 11 of arXiv:2605.30779, '2605.30779'] Let $X$ be an $N$-dimensional real symmetric (resp.~complex Hermitian) matrix with distinct eigenvalues $\mu_1 > \dotsb > \mu_N$, $\w$ be a unit $N$-dimensional column vector in $\realR^N$ (resp.~$\compC^N$), $d$ be a real number, and $Y = X + d \w \w^{\top}$ (resp.~$Y = X + d \w \w^*$) has distinct eigenvalues $\lambda_1 > \dotsb > \lambda_N$. We assume $\{ \mu_j \}^N_{j = 1} \cup \{ \lambda_j \}^N_{j = 1}$ consists of $2N$ distinct real numbers, and for any $k = 1, \dotsc, N$, $\x_k$ is a unit eigenvector of $Y$ associated with $\lambda_k$. Then
  \begin{equation}
    \w^\top(\lambda_k I - X)^{-1} \w =\frac{1}{d},
  \end{equation}
  and
  \begin{equation} 
    \lvert \langle \x_k, \w \rangle \rvert^2 = \frac{\lvert \langle \w, (\lambda_k I - X)^{-1} \w \rangle \rvert^2}{\langle \w, (\lambda_k I - X)^{-2} \w \rangle} = \frac{1}{d^2} \frac{1}{\langle \w, (\lambda_k I - X)^{-2} \w \rangle}.
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:06:55.256915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bbd03a94d6160e1d19be39f869050f1f9208729363900fae9abdae3e13d4248d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:sequential-consistency of arXiv:2512.21026, 'Minimax Duality in Game-Theoretic Probability\thanks{Work in progress: mistakes are likely'] For gamble space $(\Omega,\mathcal{Z})$ and measurable variable $X:\Omega\to\overline{\mathbb{R}}$, we define
  \begin{align}
    \overline{\mathbb{E}}^0 X &:= \sup_{P\in\Delta_0(\mathcal{Z})} \mathbb{E}_P X~,
  \end{align}
  and $\underline{\mathbb{E}}^0 X := -\overline{\mathbb{E}}^0(-X)$.
  Similarly, for a sequential gamble space $(\mathcal{Y},\hat\mathcal{Z},T)$, we define
  \begin{align}
    \overline{\mathbb{E}}^* X &:= \sup_{P\in\Delta_0^T(\hat\mathcal{Z})} \mathbb{E}_P X~,
  \end{align}
  and $\underline{\mathbb{E}}^* X := - \overline{\mathbb{E}}^*(-X)$.
  In both cases, if $\mathbb{E}_P X$ is undefined, we define it to be $\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.693831+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bbd177cb9db6395bdda931d4ddaff7414d4398320fd014deaa9bea46ff2eb649","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop p:300.1 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] If $\gamma$ is chosen small enough, then, given any $0 < \eta' < \eta < \frac{1}{2}$ small enough (depending only on $\gamma$), for each $u,k \geq 0$ there exists a probability measure ${\rm O}^{k}_u$ on $\mathbb{R}^{{\rm Q}(k)}$ such~that 
