{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.147354+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bbd886c2caecd0a65ef903490ac8fc3444b3d8d0ef9d5c1f608a07e9c04662a2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:fringe of arXiv:2606.24465, 'History estimation in random recursive trees:\\Pointwise approach via iterated Jordan cent'] For a vertex $v\in [n]$, we call the subtree of $v$ in the rooted tree $(T_n, 1)$ the fringe tree attached to $v$, or the fringe tree of $v$. For its size, we write 
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{Fr}_n(v):=|(T_n, 1)_{v\downarrow}|=|(T_n, \mathrm{pa}(v))_{v\downarrow}|,
\end{equation}
    where the last equality holds for all $v \geq 2$.
    Furthermore, given $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $v \in [n]$, we define the fringe tree of $v$ witnessed after time $m$ as the set of vertices in $(T_n, 1)_{v\downarrow}$ whose unique path to $v$ in $T_n$ has no edges  that arrived before time $m+1$.  We write $\mathrm{Fr}_n^{>m}(v)$ for its size.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.359501+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bbe15aa3fa7981e5ef6a5c8025cbdaf886769f80a1f3aec4e7fcea1bf627ae6f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 (Coalescent process) of arXiv:2511.09517, 'From Cannings model to Brownian motion conditioned on local time profile'] Fix a height $0 \le h^* \le h(q_n)$ and an integer $1\le k\le q_n(h^*)$. For $0 \le j \le h^*$, set
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  \cX^{(k)}_j = \cX^{(k)}_j(n,h^*)&=\big\{\text{Anc}(u^{h^*}_i,j):1\le i\le k\big\} \\
  &=\big\{v \in V(\mathscr{T}^n) : |v| = j,\ \operatorname{Desc}(v) \cap \{u^{h^*}_i:1\le i\le k\} \neq \emptyset \big\}.  
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The coalescent process (based at height $h^*$) is then defined as
\[
X^{(k)}_j = X^{(k)}_j(n,h^*) := \#\cX^{(k)}_j,\qquad 0 \le j \le h^*.
\]
In the special case $k=q_n(h^*)$, we write $\cX_j$ and $X_j$ in place of $\cX^{(k)}_j$ and $X^{(k)}_j$, respectively.

In other words, $X^{(k)}_j$ represents the number of distinct ancestral lineages at height $j$ traced from $k$ uniformly sampled vertices at height $h^*$.  
At $j=h^*$, we have $X^{(k)}_0 = k$, since all sampled vertices are distinct.  
As $j$ decreases, the lineages are traced backward through the tree and may coalesce whenever two or more sampled vertices share a common ancestor.  
Thus, the process $\big(X^{(k)}_j : 0 \le j \le h^*\big)$ is non-increasing as $j$ decreases, and each downward jump corresponds to a coalescence event between ancestral lineages.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.358961+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bbe263b0a5a440781ff547d2887376e5a6918bd1609fd4d211e26788e43d5c9d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop pr.PreKin of arXiv:2411.13099, 'Long time behavior of killed Feynman-Kac semigroups with singular Schr{ö}dinger potentials'] Then, for all $\mathsf y\in \mathscr R^{2d}$,   there exists a unique strong solution $(Y_t(\mathsf y),t\ge 0)$ to (equation eq.Lcin of the paper) over $\mathscr R^{2d}$ whose sample paths are a.s. continuous.  
