{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.821906+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bcc04ab8a4ffd01cf1621c8c51a56a42292fc90e96d01ddc68650825143665f1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ordered-group-shadow of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Let $\Gamma$ be any linearly ordered abelian group, let
$A=(a_0<\cdots<a_{k-1})\subset\Gamma$, where $k\geq2$, and let
$h\in\mathbb Z_{\geq1}$.  Write
$r=r_h^\Gamma(A)$ and $d=k-1-r$.  There are integers
\[
 0=b_0<b_1<\cdots<b_{k-1}
\]
such that, for every $1\leq j\leq h$ and $u,v\in\mathcal U_{j,k}$,
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{sgn}_{\Gamma}\!\left(\sum_i(u_i-v_i)a_i\right)
 =\operatorname{sgn}\!\left(\sum_i(u_i-v_i)b_i\right),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 b_{k-1}
