{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.087421+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd66a1badc0601bd955d3022a40c851ab9cf9b6c63c22057fdcabe1cbfd1bd70","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition lazy-cycle-criterion of arXiv:2412.08400, '\vspace{-1.0cm} Characterization of Exponential Families of \\ Lumpable Stochastic Matrice'] If any of the following two equivalent conditions are satisfied, then $\mathcal{W}_{\kappa}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ forms an e-family.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(i)\xspace] For any $P \in \mathcal{W}_{\kappa}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$, there exist a pair of non-negative matrices $D$ and $\Pi$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
        P = D + \Pi,
    \end{equation*}
    where $\kappa_\star \Pi$ is a permutation matrix over $\mathcal{X}$ and $D$ is diagonal.
    \item[(ii)\xspace] For any $(y,y') \in \mathcal{E}, \kappa(y) = \kappa(y')$ implies that $ y = y'$ and the graph $$\left( \mathcal{X},   \left\{  (\kappa(y),\kappa(y')) \colon (y,y') \in \mathcal{E}, \kappa(y) \neq \kappa(y') \right\}  \right)$$ is a cycle.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.415694+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd66ea261c06ccfc00cb8484475928d0c3c9f1927915830fa4749dc5c799f7fa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem3.1 of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] Let $\zeta\in \Gamma_B$ for some $B>0$. Let $A_n$ be any $n\times n$ symmetric matrix with $A_n\in\mathcal{E}$ (see Lemma (mainquasirandomness1 of the paper) for the event $\mathcal{E}$, and let $c_*>0$, $\mu>0$ be the two constants given there) and let $X\sim \operatorname{Col}_n(\zeta)$ be a random vector independent of $A_n$. Consider two fixed locations $\lambda_1,\lambda_2\in[-4\sqrt{n},4\sqrt{n}]\subset\mathbb{R}$.


  For each $i=1,2$ denote by $\mu_1(\lambda_i):=\sigma_{\operatorname{max}}\left((A_n-\lambda_i I_n)^{-1}\right)$. Then, for any $t_1,t_2\in\mathbb{R}$ and any $u\in\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$, we have the estimate for any $\delta_1,\delta_2\in[e^{-c_*n/2},1]:$
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
    &\mathbb{P}_X\left(\left| \left\langle (A_n-\lambda_iI_n)^{-1}X,X\rangle-t_i\right|\leq \delta_i\mu_1(\lambda_i),\quad i=1,2;\quad \langle X,u\right\rangle\geq s\right)\\&\lesssim \delta_1\delta_2\cdot e^{-s}\cdot \int_{\{\theta\in\mathbb{R}^2:\sum_{i=1}^2 |\theta_i\delta_i|^2\leq 2\}} I(\theta)^\frac{1}{2}d\theta+e^{-\Omega(n)},\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $I(\theta)$ is defined as follows: 
\begin{equation}
I(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{J,X_J,X_J'}\exp\left(\langle (X+X')_J,u\rangle-c\left\|\sum_{i=1}^2 \frac{\theta_i}{\mu_1(\lambda_i)}(A_n-\lambda_iI_n)^{-1}(X-X')_J\right\|_2^2
    \right),
