{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:31:20.587269+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bf824975a0b430566bc84e46c25043f2efdaff598fe63e3d1b92605dec9ec576","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2608.25164, 'A time-fractional Kalman filter'] Let the process $N(t)$ be defined as in \eqref{4.21}--\eqref{4.3}. Then the following properties hold
\begin{myenumerate}
    \item
    $N$ is a Gaussian process;
    \item
    $\E[N(t)]=0$ for all $t$;
    \item
    $\E[N^2(t)]= \int_0^t D^2(s) ds$;
    \item 
    $\mathcal{L}(N)=\mathcal{L}(Y)$;
    \item
    $N$ has orthogonal, and hence independent, increments;
    \item
    Define
    \begin{align}
        dM(t)&=\frac{1}{D(t)} dN(t)= \frac{1}{D(t)}dY(t) -\frac{G(t)}{D(t)} \widehat{X}(t)dt \nonumber\\
        &=\frac{G(t)}{D(t)} \big[X(t)-\widehat{X}(t)\big]dt + dB_2(t).
    \end{align}
Then, for all $s,s'\geq0$,
\begin{align*}
\E[M(s)M(s')]=\min(s,s').
\end{align*}
 \item 
 $M$ is a Brownian motion.
    \end{myenumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.457300+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bf825385cd75e29799b3fa0d10d70f14ae2c5c2a2a3fbf35e7a813f478a5b810","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 26 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Let $\gamma >0$, $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$, $n \in \mathbb{N}_+$ and $ \widehat{b}_n \in \mathbb{R}$. Moreover, let $(F_x)_{x \in \mathbb{Z}}: \Omega \mapsto \mathbb{R}$ be a sequence of mappings such that 
