{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.794113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfa4b4498b0bc73af49a49bb563794b132dbd3602591d1600e67ea1e9f91f433","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 77 (Antipode) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] The \emph{antipode} $\cA:\spanF\to\spancF$ is defined inductively by $\cA(\unit)=\unit$ and 
\begin{align}
 \cA(\Gamma) &= -\Gamma - 
 \sum_{\substack{\unit\neq\overline\Gamma\subsetneq\Gamma, \overline\Gamma \in \Fminus}} 
\cA(\overline\Gamma)\cdot(\Gamma/\overline\Gamma)\\
&= - \Gamma - \cM(\cA\otimes\id) \DeltaCKred(\Gamma)\;.

\end{align} 
Here $\DeltaCKred = \DeltaCK - \Gamma\otimes\unit - \unit\otimes\Gamma$ 
denotes the \emph{reduced coproduct}, and the map $\cM:\spancF\otimes \spancF\to\spancF$ denotes multiplication, defined 
by $\cM(\Gamma_1\otimes\Gamma_2) = \Gamma_1 \cdot \Gamma_2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.597670+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfa62b9b6d0663cc0ee9eaaf37f804d684a0ffbbc4a65f2bbe3a923f376d4156","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 of arXiv:2608.18688, 'Regularity Preservation for Jump-Type Stochastic Transport Equations with Singular Drift'] A local $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-process is called a local $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-martingale if, for any $x\in\mR^d$,  the stopped process $\partial^kX_t^{\tau_n(x)}(x)$, $0\leq |k|\leq m$, $n=1,2,\cdots$ are  martingales. It is called a local $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-processes of bounded variation if, for any $x\in\mR^d$,  $\partial^kX_t^{\tau_n(x)}(x)$, $0\leq |k|\leq m$, $n=1,2,\cdots$ are bounded variation. Furthermore, it is classified as a  local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-semimartingale if it can be expressed as the sum of a  local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-martingale and a  local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-process of bounded variation. 	Additionally, it is designated as a continuous local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-semimartingale 
	if both components in the sum are continuous in $(t,x)$.

	If $\tau(x)=\infty$ for any $x$ a.s., these are called global $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-martingale etc.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.033279+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfa79862e666fcec8ddd616ff596016a99db1517ba8b96434b3c9aa0d0b2b4eb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 (Fixed-time regularity of functionals of the decoupled flow) of arXiv:2605.03057, 'Uniform-in-time quantitative fluctuations of large scale interacting particle systems'] Let Assumption \ref{ass:flow-smothness} hold. Let $ \varphi \in C_b^7\left( \R^d \right) $.
For $ t \ge 0 $, $ x \in \R^d $, and $ \mu \in \Ptwo $, let $ X_t^{x,\mu} $ be the decoupled flow
associated with the McKean--Vlasov equation, and define
\begin{equation*}
u_t\left( x,\mu \right) = \E\left( \varphi\left( X_t^{x,\mu} \right) \right).
\end{equation*}
Then, for every fixed $ t \ge 0 $, all multi-indices $ \gamma $ and $ \beta $, and
  every integer $ n \ge 0 $ with $1 \le \abs{\gamma} + \abs{\beta} + n
  \le 7$, the mixed derivative
\begin{equation*}
\partial_x^\gamma \partial_v^\beta \partial_\mu^n
u_t\left( x,\mu \right)\left( v_1,\dots,v_n \right)
\end{equation*}
exists and is continuous in $\left( x,\mu,v_1,\dots,v_n \right)$. More precisely, every such derivative is a finite sum of terms of the form
\begin{equation}

\E\left(
\nabla^r \varphi\left( X_t^{x,\mu} \right)
\left[
\partial_x^{\gamma_1} \partial_v^{\beta_1}\partial_\mu^{n_1}
X_t^{x,\mu}\left( \mathbf{v}_1 \right),\dots,
\partial_x^{\gamma_r} \partial_v^{\beta_r}\partial_\mu^{n_r}
X_t^{x,\mu}\left( \mathbf{v}_r \right)
\right]
\right),
\end{equation}
where $ 1 \le r \le 7 $, for each $1 \leq j \leq r$, $\mathbf{v}_j$
denotes an $n_j$-tuple of Lions directions, and each
$\partial_x^{\gamma_j} \partial_v^{\beta_j}\partial_\mu^{n_j}
X_t^{x,\mu}\left( \mathbf{v}_j \right)$
is a mixed derivative of the flow of positive total order $1 \le \abs{\gamma_j} + \abs{\beta_j} + n_j
  \le 7$.","status":"unverified"}

