{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.677662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bff7ff054ac5990d3642ca5a4894f1ecd878dd672294685cc6c563fd1a92eab6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2609.03750, '\bf Pullback Measure Attractors, Zero-Noise Limits, and Moderate Deviations for 2D Stochas'] Suppose Hypotheses \ref{PEeqAssum-2.2}, \ref{PEeqAssum-2.3}, \ref{PEeqAssum-2.4} and \eqref{PETNs-3.01} hold. For every $r\leq t$, set
	\begin{align}
		\Theta(t,r,\zeta)=e^{-\alpha_2\int_{r}^{t}(|{v}(s, r, \zeta)|^2\|{v}(s, r, \zeta)\|^2+|\partial_{z}{v}(s, r, \zeta)|^2\|\partial_{z}{v}(s, r, \zeta)\|^2)ds},
	\end{align}
	where $\alpha_2>0$ is chosen below so as to absorb the nonlinear convection terms.
	 Then, for every $\tau \in \mathbb{R}$ and $D \in \mathfrak{D}$, there exist ${T} := {T}(\tau, D) > 1$ and  $\epsilon_{0}\in (0,1)$ such that, for all $\epsilon\in (0,\epsilon_{0})$ and $t \geq {T}$, the solution $v$ of \eqref{PEeq-2.15} satisfies
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{E}\left[\Theta(\tau,\tau-t,\zeta)\|{v}(\tau, \tau - t, \zeta)\|^2\right] \leq \mathcal{R}_4 + \mathcal{R}_4\int_{-\infty}^\tau e^{-\kappa(\tau - s)}|g(s)|^{4} {d}s,
	\end{align}
	where $\zeta\in L^4(\Omega,\mathscr F_{\tau-t};\mathcal H)$ with $\mathscr{L}(\zeta) \in D(\tau - t)$, and $\mathcal{R}_4$ is a positive constant independent of $\tau$, $\epsilon$ and $D$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.366244+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bffa66b1d41cdaa2b8a07d2d75f35ebc0255c595ec5eeae7da926b8ac794acdf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 16 of arXiv:2607.27535, 'Universal topological statistics on \\ triangulated singular spaces'] Let $\theta, \epsilon, \ell, \reach$ be fixed real numbers satisfying $\theta \in (0, \pi]$, $\epsilon \in [0, \sin(\theta/4)]$, and $\ell \in [0, \epsilon \reach]$. Define the following function $g(\lambda)$:
    \begin{align*}
        g(\lambda) = \sqrt{\ell^2 + \lambda^2 - 2\ell \lambda \cos \theta} - \epsilon (\ell + \lambda)
    \end{align*}
    Then $g(\lambda)$ has the following lower bound:
    \begin{align*}
        \min_{0 \le \lambda \le \epsilon \reach} g(\lambda) \ge \ell (\sin \theta_+ - 3\epsilon) \text{, where } \theta_+ = \min\bigg( \theta, \frac {3\pi}4 \bigg)
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.444094+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfffa015a67b344b099b1f73b339e84a8b99b10f5a324d2cd9b6e07f8e5a640d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor exponents of arXiv:2606.19583, 'Power-law hypothesis and (un)fairness of PageRank on undirected multi-type PAMs'] For $k \in S$,
\begin{align}
&\liminf_{n \rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{P}(R_{\phi_n}(G_n)>r \mid X_{\phi_n}=k) \geq (1+o(1))\frac{\Gamma(m_k+\frac{2}{\varphi_k^{\boldsymbol{m}}})}{\Gamma(m_k)}\gamma^{-(\lambda_k^{\boldsymbol{m}}-1)}r^{-(\lambda_k^{\boldsymbol{m}}-1)}\cr
&\limsup_{n \rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{P}(R_{\phi_n}(G_n)>r \mid X_{\phi_n}=k) \leq (1+o(1))\frac{\Gamma(m_k+\frac{2}{\varphi_k^{\boldsymbol{m}}})}{\Gamma(m_k)}r^{-(\lambda_k^{\boldsymbol{m}}-1)}.  
\end{align}
Thus, the exponent for the limiting power-law tails of the PageRank of a uniformly chosen vertex of color $k$ is $\lambda_k^{\boldsymbol{m}}-1=\frac{2}{\varphi_k^{\boldsymbol{m}}}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.492682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c0002285fd0f8f356dd8bc351e2368fc6cf0dc46f2165b6fe4700a094df17b23","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 2 of arXiv:2512.02538, 'On the spectral geometry of Liouville quantum gravity'] The Liouville Brownian motion $(Z_t)_{t\ge 0}$ is the process obtained as a time-change of $X$, namely $Z_t = X_{F^{-1}(t)}$, where $F^{-1} (t)$ denotes the inverse of the additive functional of $X$ (the so-called quantum clock) given by 
\begin{equation}
F(t) = \int_0^t e^{\gamma \ph(X_s)} \dd s := \lim_{\eps \to 0} \int_0^t \eps^{\gamma^2/2} e^{\gamma \ph_\eps(X_s)  } \dd s.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.680031+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c00029ac044fd3676a165657e1d5f6606d386788c7f128783db6c2f42969c323","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2607.25986, 'Asymptotics of a two-species particle system associated to the doubly parabolic Keller-Seg'] For any $\sigma \in (0,\frac 12]$, any $\alpha>0$, any $\gamma \in (\frac 32,2)$, any $T>0$, any $\eta>0$,
there exists a constant $A_{\sigma,\eta,T}$ (also depending on $\lambda,\theta,\gamma,\alpha,c_0$) such that 
if $(N,M,\e)\in \Aa_\sigma$, for all $u\in [0,T]$,
\begin{align*}
\E\Big[\int_0^u |D^1_t|^{2\gamma-2} \dd t \Big] \leq &
\Big((1+\eta)C_4(\theta,\alpha,\gamma)\Big)^{2\gamma-2}
\E\Big[\int_0^u \int_0^t \int_{\R^2} \frac{\phi(y) \dd y\dd s\dd t}{(t-s+\alpha|R^{1,2}_{t,s}-\sqrt \e y|^2)^{\gamma}}\Big]
+ A_{\sigma,\eta,T},
\end{align*}
where $C_4(\theta,\alpha,\gamma)=C_3(\theta,\alpha)\kappa(\frac{1}{2}, \gamma-1\big)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:16.151099+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c002600b4fbf0692f6ab087269f5ce74a4cbde3b2e3f42ae5573e57cd0bd2c2b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2604.13454, 'Invariant and periodic measures in classical spin systems on infinite lattices with highly'] Given $x \in \ell_\rho^{2\theta}(\mathbb{Z}^+)$, let $\{ \Lambda_n \}$ be a sequence of finite sets of $\mathbb{Z}^+$ with $\{ X^{(n),x} \}$ being the solutions to the associated finite-dimensional SDEs (\ref{CUTSDE}).
		For $k\leq n$, we denote by $X^{(n), x}_{(k)}$ the part of $X^{(n), x}$ that lives in the finite set $\Lambda_k$, that is, $X^{(n), x}_{(k)} = (X^{(n),x}_{1}, \ldots, X^{(n),x}_{k})$. Then for any $k>0$, $\epsilon > 0$ and $T > 0$ there exists a constant $V(\epsilon,T,k) > 0$ such that for any $n, m \geq V(\epsilon,T,k)$
		\begin{align} 
			\mathbb{E}\sup_{t \in [0, T]} \| X^{(n), x}_{(k)}(t) - X^{(m),x}_{(k)}(t) \|^2_{\mathbb{R}^{\Lambda_k}} \leq \epsilon.
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.130775+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c0061bcebe70067e062c48b7714e3746edf639907d41ce402f7cd925a2365b0b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:dg of arXiv:2510.09818, 'On the Fourier dimension of fractional Brownian graphs'] Let $H\in (0,1/2)$. Let $0<s_1<\cdots<s_I$ and $a = (a_1,\dots,a_I) \in \mathbb R^I$ with $\sum_{i=1}^I a_i = 0$. 
	Recall that the function $g_a$ is defined by (equation g_a of the paper).
	Then:
	\begin{enumerate}
	\item 
	For any $i \in \{1,\dots, I\}$,
	\begin{align}
		|\partial_{s_i}g_a(s)| \le \|a\|_\infty^2\, I \left[ (s_i-s_{i-1})^{2H-1}{\bf 1}_{\{i \ge 2\}} + (s_{i+1}-s_i)^{2H-1} {\bf 1}_{\{i \le I-1\}} \right].
\end{align}
	\item 
	For any $i<j$ in $\{1,\dots, I\}$,
	\begin{align}
		|\partial_{s_i} \partial_{s_j} g_a(s)| \le \|a\|_\infty^2 (s_j-s_i)^{2H-2}.
\end{align}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c00695c27c2d61520e7ba960c42efb3c5b4dd0025fac0dbd519b1b5a16020dd7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:integrable of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] A critical point $u_* \in \mathcal{B}$ is said to be \emph{integrable} if, for every $\phi \in K$, there exists a one-parameter family $(u_t)_{t\in(-\delta,\delta)} \subset \mathcal{B}$ of critical points of $I_\gamma$ such that
\begin{align*}
    u_0=u_*, \qquad \left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}u_t=\phi.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.163910+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c00743096aab0ca113c9f28f889af4c894310347c7602039d8ed62f76ce013a9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:freclt of arXiv:2411.16103, 'Non-commutative Stein's Method: Applications to Free Probability and Sums of Non-commutati'] Let $\{a_{k}\}_{k\geq 1}$ be a collection of self-adjoint free random variables defined on  $(\mathcal{A},\tau)$, with $a_{k}$ having analytic distribution $\mu_{k}$. If the $a_{k}$ are identically distributed and standardized, with finite moments of order $3$, then the analytic distribution of 
