{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.201833+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c011882b360e49be509c58c27bad558b1bb50900f844e5249954cbe67c89d078","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 21 of arXiv:2510.23130, 'Hidden regular variation for stochastic recursions with diagonal matrices\ \thanks{E.D. wa'] Suppose that the closed subgroup generated by $\supp(\wt{Q})$ is equal to $G=\R^2 \times K$ and that $\bb \rho = \int \bb x \mu(d\bb x) \neq \bb 0$. Assume further that $\int \norm{ \bb x}^2 \, Q(d \bb x) < \infty$.




	Then there is $c>0$ such that for every continuous function $f $ with  $\| f \| _{\dri}<\8 $ and every $k_1 \in K$ we have
	\begin{equation}
		\lim _{r\to \8}r^{1\slash 2}\, f *\mathbb{\wt U}(r\bb \rho ,k_1)=c\sum _{k\in K}\int _{\R ^2}f (\mathbf{s},k) \ d\mathbf{s}.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.714418+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c01393c3a7c3e8dcc877c6a3caa4a9b102db893c811cdc8e4bd8811b817ae2cd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 49 (Two-parameter Banach-valued time lift) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] Let $B$ be a separable Banach space and
$\Delta_T=\{(t,s):0\le s\le t\le T\}$, equipped with the Euclidean metric.
Let $X:\Delta_T\to L^{p_0}(\Omega;B)$ satisfy, for some $p_0\ge2$ and
$\theta>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{u\in\Delta_T}\|X(u)\|_{L^{p_0}(\Omega;B)}\le A,
 \qquad
 \|X(u)-X(v)\|_{L^{p_0}(\Omega;B)}\le B_0|u-v|^\theta.
\end{equation}
If $p_0\theta>2$, then $X$ has a $B$-valued continuous modification and,
for every $2\le p\le p_0$,
\begin{equation}
 \|X\|_{L^p(\Omega;C(\Delta_T;B))}
 \lesssim_{T,p_0,\theta}A+B_0.
\end{equation}
The same assertion holds for a sequence of kernels, and for their differences,
with constants uniform whenever the two bounds in
\eqref{eq:time-lift-hypotheses} are uniform.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.365704+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c015c69c3c5900abc3b61e2621cef12d43bd000e08dbeabf72fa6caec1790d36","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn eq:-19 of arXiv:2411.13403, 'Path weighting method for spot sensitivities and its variance reduction'] We denote by $\hat{Z}$ the vector obtained by reflecting the first
element of $Z$, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\hat{Z}=(-Z_{1},Z_{2},\dots,Z_{N}),
\end{equation}
and by $\bar{Z}$ the vector obtained by replacing the first element
of $Z$ by zero, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\bar{Z}=(0,Z_{2},\dots,Z_{N}).
\end{equation}
Moreover, the process $\hat{S}_{t}$ and $\bar{S}_{t}$ are defined
to be generated by replacing $Z$ with $\hat{Z}$ and $\bar{Z}$ in
((eq:-1 of the paper)) respectively, i.e. 
\begin{align}
