{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.357178+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c106f0cb98e447c971f91a3c3719366b3c0237987bf71b2884b99a672d62d397","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:adaptive-bellman of arXiv:2608.23860, 'Revelation Control'] Let $h_0<\cdots<h_J$ be admissible revelation depths and let $c_j$ be the priced marginal cost of moving from $h_j$ to $h_{j+1}$. If deeper revelation can be purchased sequentially, then
\begin{align}
V_J^{\rm dyn}&=C_{h_J},\\
V_j^{\rm dyn}&=\max\left\{C_{h_j},\;\mathbb E[V_{j+1}^{\rm dyn}\mid\mathscr F_{h_j}]-c_j\right\}.

\end{align}
Thus static information refinement becomes an optimal-stopping control problem over the revelation filtration. In a general fork--probe--promote system the state must also retain promoted-state information needed to make the transition Markov. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

