{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.332917+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c300df17ce7dceb0033d8335325eb88e5aba248d9194575621f1bdb17f80701e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.03959, 'The combinatorial Hopf algebra of signed graphs'] Given a connected signed graph $\Sigma = (V,E, \sigma)$ and $S\subseteq V$, define $I_\Sigma(S)$ to be the set of edges $e\in E$ satisfying one of the following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sigma(e)=+$ and the endpoints of $e$ are in $S$
\item $\sigma(e) = +$ and the endpoints of $e$ are in $V\backslash S$ 
\item $\sigma(e) = -$ and $e$ has one endpoint in $S$ and the other one in $V\backslash S$.
\end{itemize}
Then define $$\zeta_x(\Sigma) := \sum_{A \subseteq V} x^{rk(I_\Sigma(A))}. $$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.685814+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3076fb03429ef8645e284863ccb84cce8aef5f4949bf5cfccfbaf0d4cfac448","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:edge_universality of arXiv:2604.20215, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices II: Markov Chain Comparison and Cr'] For  $\alpha > 1$, set 
\begin{equation}
     \tilde{p}_n(x, y)=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{k\in \mathbb{Z}}f\Big(\frac{x-y+kN}{W}\Big), \quad M:=\sum_{k\in \mathbb{Z}} f\Big(\frac{k}{W}\Big),
\end{equation}
where the density profile $f$ satisfies the comparison conditions in Definition~(ass:ft of the paper) with respect to the $\alpha$-stable density $f_{\alpha}$. 
Under the same assumptions on the Markov chains $([N], P_N)$ and $([N], \widetilde{P}_N)$ as in Theorem~(prop:super_4 of the paper), the scaling limits of the correlation measures established in Theorem~(thm:convergence_proof of the paper) ($\alpha$-stable case) also hold for the matrix $X$. In particular, a new tricritical point process emerges at the critical bandwidth and critical external source, as described in Theorem~(thm:triple_critical_convergence of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.185147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c30f9336214cfe3c232d2db213c51dbb821ece47c3ca977f77e3d15edc2fa343","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2608.26974, 'Why not to use the Gaussian kernel'] Consider a stationary strictly positive-definite kernel $k(x, y) = \Phi(x - y)$ for a continuous and integrable $\Phi \colon \R^d \to \R$. Suppose that there are $c > 0$ and $s > 0$ such that $(\mathcal{F}\Phi)(\omega) \geq c (1 + \lVert \omega \rVert_2^2)^{-s}$ for all $\omega \in \R^d$.
  Let $\sigma \geq 0$ and $X_n = \{x_1, \ldots, x_n \} \subset \R^d$.
  If $\delta(x, X_n) = \min_{x_i \in X_n} \lVert x - x_i \rVert_2 \leq 1$, then
  \begin{equation} 
    \V_\sigma(x \mid \mathcal{D}_n) \geq C \delta(x, X_n)^{2s - d},
  \end{equation}
  where $C > 0$ is a constant that does not depend on $\sigma$, $x$, or $X_n$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.764652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3120cea891cae44da11c7a925b1dc30402a55a328748e872fdf70047ed77689","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 56 of arXiv:2607.21374, 'Local structure at the maximum and sharp persistence asymptotics of rough fractional Brown'] For every \(\eta>0\),
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{T\geq2}
  \widehat{\PP}_T\left(
    \int_R^{T-\sigma_T}
      e^{-(M_T-B_{\sigma_T+s})}\,\mathrm ds>\eta
  \right)=0,

\end{equation}
where the integral is zero when its lower endpoint exceeds its upper
endpoint.  Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{a\geq2}
  \widehat{\PP}_a\left(
    \int_R^{a-U_a}e^{-R_a(s)}\,\mathrm ds>\eta
  \right)=0.

\end{equation}
Under \(\mu_H\),
\begin{equation}
  \int_0^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds<\infty
  \quad\text{almost surely},
  \qquad
  \int_R^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds\longrightarrow0

\end{equation}
almost surely and in probability.
The equivalence of the two finite-horizon formulations is the
self-similar identification \eqref{eq:long-horizon-scaling}.","status":"unverified"}

