{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:08.996916+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c37a597b7bcc9f8a3ce748c1992e1dca9ed07324da5cbffedb04393603d317cd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:var-sol of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] An $H$-valued continuous $\mathcal{F}_t$-adapted process $X = \{X(t)\}_{t \in [0,T]}$ is a
\emph{variational solution} (or \emph{analytically strong solution}) of~(equation eq:SPDE of the paper) if:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
  \item $X \in L^p(0,T; V) \cap C([0,T]; H)$ $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.;
  \item $\mathcal{A}(\cdot, X(\cdot)) \in L^{p'}(0,T; V^*)$ $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., where $p' = p/(p-1)$;
  \item $\mathcal{B}(\cdot, X(\cdot)) \in L^2(0,T; L_2(U, H))$ $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.;
  \item for all $t \in [0,T]$ and all $\varphi \in V$, the following identity holds $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.:
  \begin{multline}
    \ip{X(t)}{\varphi}_H + \int_0^t \dual{\mathcal{A}(s, X(s))}{\varphi}\,\mathrm{d}s \\
    = \ip{x}{\varphi}_H + \int_0^t \ip{\mathcal{B}(s, X(s))\,\mathrm{d}W(s)}{\varphi}_H,
  \end{multline}
  where the last integral is the It\^o stochastic integral taking values in $\mathbb{R}$ via the pairing $\ip{\mathcal{B}(s,X(s))\,\mathrm{d}W(s)}{\varphi}_H = \sum_{k=1}^\infty \ip{\mathcal{B}(s,X(s))f_k}{\varphi}_H\,\mathrm{d}B^k_s$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.694090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c37b5f756aac14784e4bfb9bf3d030176b6965b94a039842b33760dbac024f02","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemDerivativeIdentities of arXiv:2411.18563, 'Lower tails for triangles inside the critical window'] The following identities hold for any $\lambda \ge0$, $\zeta \in [0,1]$, and any graph $H$.
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{E}_{\lambda,\zeta}|G| &= \lambda  \frac{\partial}{\partial \lambda}\log Z(\lambda,\zeta)  \\
        \mathbb{E}_{H,\lambda,\zeta} |S| &= \lambda  \frac{\partial}{\partial \lambda}\log \Xi_H(\lambda,\zeta) \\
        \mathbb{E}_{H,\lambda,\zeta} |E(S)| &=   - (1-\zeta) \frac{\partial}{\partial \zeta} \log \Xi_H(\lambda,\zeta) \,. 
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.090167+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c37bda1a4e2c23100396adfae961d2a371e8dd763142365780bf3ccb9112824d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 27 (Control of the remainder) of arXiv:2604.25877, 'Asymptotic height of Plancherel random trees'] Fix $C>0$ and let $h\le C\log n$. For any $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and any vertex $u$ with $|u|=h$, on the event
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal G_{n,\alpha}(u):=\left\{\min\{K(u_\ell):0\le \ell\le h-1\}\ge
n^\alpha\right\},
\end{equation*}
we have the deterministic bound
\begin{equation}
R_h(u)\le \frac{2h}{n^\alpha}\le \frac{2C\log n}{n^\alpha}=o(\log n).
\end{equation}
Consequently, on $\mathcal G_{n,\alpha}(u)$, $
\log K(u)=\log n - S_h(u) + o(\log n)$.
Moreover, the estimate \eqref{eq:remainder-control} holds uniformly over any collection of vertices
$\mathcal U_h\subseteq\{u:|u|=h\}$ on the intersection event $\bigcap_{u\in\mathcal U_h}\mathcal G_{n,\alpha}(u)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:08.999553+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c37c454bf290f573d18ee28b9eed2ccb3826dc58fdedefadd3f58f240f43b61f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:first-var of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Under Assumptions~(ass:LR of the paper), (ass:diff1 of the paper), and~(ass:SC of the paper), the \emph{first variation process} $Y(t,r)$ for $0 \leq r \leq t \leq T$ is the linear map $H \to L^0(\Omega; H)$ defined fibrewise: for each $v \in H$, $Y(t,r)v$ is the unique variational solution in the \emph{tangent solution class}
\[
  \mathcal{Y}_r \;:=\;
  \begin{cases}
    \bigl\{\,w \in C([r,T]; H)\;:\; \mathcal{E}_r^T(w) < \infty \ \text{a.s.}\,\bigr\}, & \text{under (SC1)(a)},\\[2pt]
    C([r,T];H) \cap L^2(r,T;V), & \text{under (SC1)(b)},
  \end{cases}
\]
tested against $V$ (a.s.), of the linear stochastic evolution equation
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  \mathrm{d}[Y(t,r)v] + \mathcal{A}'_u(t, X(t))\,[Y(t,r)v]\,\,\mathrm{d}t &= \mathcal{B}'_u(t, X(t))\bigl([Y(t,r)v]\bigr)\,\,\mathrm{d}W(t), \quad t \in (r, T],\\
  Y(r,r)v &= v,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
with $\mathcal{A}'_u(t, X(t)) \in L(V, V^*)$ the Fr\'echet derivative of $\mathcal{A}$ at $X(t)$ (in the strong-operator-continuous sense of Assumption~(ass:diff1 of the paper)) and $\mathcal{B}'_u(t, X(t)) \in L(V;\,L_2(U, H))$ the Fr\'echet derivative of $\mathcal{B}$ at $X(t)$. Well-posedness of the fibrewise equation is Theorem~(thm:Y-wellposed of the paper) under~(SC1).

The operator-valued notation $Y(t,r): H \to H$ is used throughout as a convenient shorthand for the fibrewise action $v \mapsto Y(t,r)v$; $Y(t,r)$ is not in general an element of $L(H)$ almost surely. Viewed as a map $H \to L^0(\Omega; H)$, it is $L^2(\Omega; H)$-continuous under~(SC1)+(SC2) by Theorem~(thm:Y-wellposed of the paper)(II). Almost-sure $L(H)$-membership would require a strictly stronger structural input (Hilbert--Schmidt regularity, analytic-semigroup smoothing in operator norm, etc.); the operator-norm moment $\mathbb{E}[\lVert Y(t,r) \rVert_{L(H)}^q]$ is not provided by the abstract framework. The $V$-integrability $Y(t,r)v \in V$ a.e.\ in $t$ (a.s.) makes $\mathcal{B}'_u(t, X(t))(Y(t,r)v) \in L_2(U, H)$ well-defined for a.e.\ $t$ without requiring an $H$-extension of $\mathcal{B}'_u$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:33:06.540631+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c37c619bbac7b7a90cc90e58de28e12bdaf118b565ba4bbee6b6d49ddf0b8dce","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2608.25925, '\textbf{Jump Closure and Limit Uniformization\\in the Ideal Completion of the Turing Degre'] A \emph{Turing ideal} is a nonempty set $I\subseteq\DT$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item if $\mathbf b\leT\mathbf a\in I$, then $\mathbf b\in I$;
