{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.115248+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3b8489d3c97b612c00a90043f056a3686a89f331ce35bd8f5fcb96345d6c028","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:poisson_zoo of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Given the $\Gamma$-rotation invariant measure $\nu := \nu_{o}$ and some intensity parameter $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$, let us consider independent Poisson random variables $N_{x,H}^{\lambda} \sim \mathsf{Poisson}( \lambda \cdot \nu_x(H) )$ indexed by $x \in V(G)$ and $H \in \mathcal{H}_x$.
	We say that $N_{x,H}^{\lambda}$ is the number of copies of the animal $H$ (rooted at $x$).
	We call the collection of random variables
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{N}_{\nu}^\lambda := 
		\left\{ 
		N^\lambda_{x,H} \, : \, x \in V(G), H \in \mathcal{H}_x 
		\right\}
	\end{equation}
	the \textbf{Poisson zoo} at level $\lambda$ (with measure $\nu$) and the random set
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{S}_{\nu}^\lambda := 
		\bigcup_{x \in V} \bigcup_{H \in \mathcal{H}_x} \bigcup_{i = 1}^{ N_{x,H}^{\lambda} } \, H
	\end{equation}
	the \textbf{trace} of the Poisson zoo at level $\lambda$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.717809+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3bbf81e43b076c994d9261f0e84f6e8b319b9427c99d34f2c777a6fc6003a19","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] For an f-model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, we define a \emph{hybrid satisfaction relation} $\Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M}$ on $W \times \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$ by:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $x \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \to \psi_2 \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, (x' \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \mathrel{\Rightarrow} x' \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \psi_2)$
    \item $x \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \Box \varphi     \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, \forall y' \sqsupset x'\, (y' \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item $x \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \exists y \sqsupset x\, (y \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item The other cases are identical to the classical $\Vdash_\mathfrak{M}$.
  \end{enumerate}
  We write $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^{\text{h}} \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(h-)valid in} $\mathfrak{M}$'') to mean $\forall x \in W\,(x \Vdash^{\text{h}}_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$,
  and $\mathfrak{F} \vDash^{\text{h}} \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(h-)valid on} $\mathfrak{F}$'') to mean that $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^{\text{h}} \varphi$ for every f-model $\mathfrak{M}$ based on $\mathfrak{F}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.712587+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3bca0ace2b56c563dc5fb0d38bd8a33238cde4c7e51a2261e2bf30be02cf0d8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 34 (Finite-cutoff Wick decomposition) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] For every $(a;b,c)\in\mathfrak L$, every finite cutoff $\Lambda$, and every
localized dyadic tuple $(N,R,L,Q,M)$ with $L\sim_{\mathrm{ap}}R$,
\begin{equation}
 T^{a;b,c}_{\Lambda,N,R,L,Q,M}=
 \begin{cases}
  \cD^{a;b}_{\Lambda,N,R,L,Q,M}
   +\cB^{a;b,b}_{\Lambda,N,R,L,Q,M},
   &(a;b,c)=(a;b,b)\in\mathfrak L_{\mathrm{diag}},\\
  \cB^{a;b,c}_{\Lambda,N,R,L,Q,M},
   &(a;b,c)\in\mathfrak L_{\mathrm{off}}.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
The centered term is a second homogeneous Gaussian chaos.  Its
two-localizer frequency kernel is displayed in
\eqref{eq:two-localizer-kernel}.  If $b=c$, the deterministic
contraction satisfies $r=-\ell$; hence $L\sim N$, and the resonant relation
$L\sim_{\mathrm{ap}}R$ also forces $R\sim N$.  The strict relation $n=q$ of
the translation-invariant reference core is replaced after localization by a
Schwartz kernel rapidly decaying away from $n=q$ in $n-q$.  If $b\ne c$, the deterministic
branch is absent.","status":"unverified"}

