{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:34:45.738611+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3d828d3e0ac02662eae3d7facede4c4e2f7a0bee8b04246368fd7c457ec2e0a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Cayleyformula of arXiv:2609.01990, 'Jacquet-Zagier treatment of the beyond endoscopy trace formula for \(\GL_2\)'] For $\text{GL}_2$, the representation $\text{Sym}^m(\sigma_k)$ decomposes into irreducible as 
    \begin{equation}
        \text{Sym}^{m}(\sigma_k) = \bigoplus_{r=0}^{km/2} \mathfrak{c}(k,m,r) \left(\text{Sym}^{km-2r}(W) \otimes (\det)^r \right) 
    \end{equation}
    where the Plethysm coefficients $\mathfrak{c}(k,m,r)$ denote the multiplicities in the decomposition. These are given by the Cayley-Sylvester formula: 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathfrak{c}(k,m,r) = p_{m.k}(r) - p_{m,k}(r-1), \qquad p_{m,k}(-1) = 0, \qquad 0 \leq r \leq \lfloor \frac{km}{2} \rfloor 
    \end{equation}
    with $p_{m,k}(r)$ defined by 
    \begin{equation}
        \sum_{r=0}^{km} p_{m,k}(r) q^{r} = \prod_{j=1}^{k} \frac{1-q^{m+j}}{1-q^j} = \qbinom{m+k}{k}_q 
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

