{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.651603+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3f3cdc93b92506ff325d2b4815b3ef1066f5cb40226e51da4f7976efeab63cf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 7 of arXiv:2605.07050, 'Universality of the fluctuations of the free energy in generalized Sherrington--Kirkpatric'] Recall the definition of $F_N(\beta)$ in Definition \ref{defn:sub_Gaussian}. Suppose that $W$ is a Wigner matrix, $\sqrt{N} W_{ij}$ $(i<j)$ is symmetric, and one of the following is true:
\begin{itemize}
\item the (rescaled) entry $\sqrt{N} W_{ij}$ is strictly sub-Gaussian, or
\item the (rescaled) prior $\sqrt{N} x_i$ is bounded, and its distribution is absolutely continuous in a neighborhood of $0$.
\end{itemize}
Suppose that Assumption \ref{assump:second_moment} holds with $\SNR_c$. Then, the statement in Theorem \ref{thm:free_energy_1} holds without any change for any $\beta \in (0, \sqrt{\SNR_c})$. In particular, it holds for any $\beta \in (0, 1/\sigma)$ if the prior is sub-Gaussian with variance proxy $\sigma^2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:15:16.658431+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3f4d4b3c3a080b5460cd5073ddd5f026aab9bf6d3c48e117b6065cd4223313f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 3 of arXiv:2608.30796, 'Tumor boundary instability induced by pressure feedback'] Let \(\lambda>0\) and \(\gamma>0\). The zero mode is neutral,
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{E}_0(\lambda,\gamma)=0.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{subequations}

\begin{align}
\mathcal{E}_l(\lambda,\gamma)
&=\frac{l^2}
{2(\sqrt{\lambda}+1)(\sqrt{\lambda}+\sqrt{\gamma})}
+O(l^4)
\qquad\text{as }l\to0^+,
\\
\mathcal{E}_l(\lambda,\gamma)
&=-\frac{l}
{(\sqrt{\lambda}+1)(\sqrt{\lambda}+\sqrt{\gamma})}
+\frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda}+1}
+O(l^{-1})
\qquad\text{as }l\to+\infty.

\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Consequently, sufficiently high-frequency modes are stable, and the
continuous modal growth-rate curve has a unique positive zero
\(l_*(\lambda,\gamma)>0\). More precisely,
\begin{equation}

\mathcal E_l(\lambda,\gamma)>0
\quad\text{for }0<l<l_*(\lambda,\gamma),
\qquad
\mathcal E_l(\lambda,\gamma)<0
\quad\text{for }l>l_*(\lambda,\gamma).
\end{equation}

For a fixed integer \(l\geq1\), let \(\lambda_0^l>l^2\) denote the
threshold for the model without pressure feedback, characterized by
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{E}_l(\lambda_0^l,0)=0,
\end{equation}
as established in \cite{feng2025nonsymmetric}. If
\(0<\lambda<\lambda_0^l\), then there exists a unique
\(\gamma_l^*(\lambda)>0\) such that
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{E}_l(\lambda,\gamma_l^*(\lambda))=0.
\end{equation}
Furthermore,
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{E}_l(\lambda,\gamma)<0
\quad\text{for }0<\gamma<\gamma_l^*(\lambda),
\qquad
\mathcal{E}_l(\lambda,\gamma)>0
\quad\text{for }\gamma>\gamma_l^*(\lambda),
\end{equation}
and the crossing is transversal:
\begin{equation}

\partial_\gamma\mathcal{E}_l
(\lambda,\gamma_l^*(\lambda))>0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.882767+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3fac9b5fad123f68bf38d05d3a4d62e0617921451ba99efd3b9bdc1c04732bf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:three of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] {cgv2025StecconiTodino}}{}]
We define three deterministic positive real numbers associated to $M,g,\phi$: the \emph{average variance $\sigma$}, the \emph{average frequency $\lambda$} as follows.
\begin{equation} 
\sigma^2=\sigma(\phi)^2:=\fint_M 
\mathbb{E}\left\{ \phi(x)^2\right\} dx,\quad \lambda^2=\lambda(\phi)^2:=\frac{1}{\sigma^2}\fint_M \mathbb{E}\left\{ \|d_x\phi\|^2\right\} dx,
\end{equation} 
and the \emph{maximal eccentricity} of $\phi$:
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned} 
\varepsilon=\varepsilon(\phi):=\max_{x\in M,\ u\in S(T_xM)}\left|\frac{\sqrt{\mathbb{E}\left\{ |\langle d_x\phi,u\rangle|^2\right\}}}{\sigma\lambda}\sqrt{n}-1\right| 
\\
+\max_{x\in M}\left|\frac{\sqrt{\mathbb{E}\left\{ \phi(x)^2\right\}}}{\sigma}-1\right|
+
\max_{x\in M,\ u\in S(T_xM)}\left|\langle d_x\frac{\sqrt{\mathbb{E}\left\{ \phi(\cdot)^2\right\}}}{\sigma}, u\rangle \right|\frac{\sqrt{n}}{\lambda}.
\end{aligned}\end{equation}
When $\varepsilon(\phi)=0$, we say that $\phi$ is a \emph{homothetic field}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.196345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3fc46fa63c54d371bdd7fda01323e79258b4cd65c3722d59b7fd75d79564ea1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 28 of arXiv:2510.22783, 'Universality of Cutoff for Riffle Shuffling'] For any $y\in \al, z\in \ar$ and $x\in A_{y,z}$, there exists $\mathcal S'$ with $\mathbb P(\mathcal S')\geq 1-\exp(-N^{\delta})$ such that the following holds.
    \begin{equation}
         \max_{j\in [N]}\mathbb P(\iota(x)=j,S_K\in\cS'|U_x) =O(N^{-c_F(x)+2\delta}),
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}
         \max_{j\in [N]}\mathbb P(\tau(x)=j,S_K\in\cS'|U_x) =O(N^{-c_F(x)+2\delta}).
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.535768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3fe17a1c5fb642504f5871395e871853a52deef974356f78685cca17e1d2b90","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary e:induction_eq2 of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] The quantity $V_m(F_1, j \vert F_2)$ satisfies the recurrence equation
			\begin{equation}
				\nabla \cdot ({\mathbf{a}}^{F_1} \nabla V_m(F_1,j \vert F_2)) = -\nabla \cdot \mathbf{W}_m(F_1,j \vert F_2) \quad \text{in } \mathrm{int}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m),
			\end{equation}
			where 
			\begin{align}

				&\mathbf{W}_m(F_1,j \vert F_2) \\
				\nonumber &:= \sum_{e\in (E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) \setminus F_2) \setminus F_1}({\mathbf{a}}^{e}-\mathbf{a})\Big(\nabla V_m(F_1,j-1 \vert F_2) - \nabla V_m(F_1\cup \{e\}, j-2 \vert F_2) \\
				\nonumber & \qquad  + \nabla V_m(F_1\cup \{e\}, j-1\vert F_2)\Big) \\
				\nonumber &\qquad +\sum_{e\in F_1}({\mathbf{a}}^{e}-\mathbf{a})\Big(\nabla V_m(F_1\setminus\{e\},j \vert F_2)-\nabla V_m(F_1,j-1\vert F_2)\Big).
			\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.658071+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c400661129c30a8cd533a496d99f572e7023e0f1015ec31a49ffce7a99cec3b5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 of arXiv:2605.08345, '\vspace{-18mm}\textbf{Quantitative ergodicity for gene regulatory networks with transcript'] If the inequality 
