{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.185401+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4f5136ebd82bd7feac47d63e378295b26ef5929366cac30116078e8bff6320c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2608.26974, 'Why not to use the Gaussian kernel'] Consider a Gevrey kernel with $\eta \in (0, 1)$. 
  Let $\sigma \geq 0$ and $X_n = \{x_1, \ldots, x_n \} \subset \R^d$.
  If $\delta(x, X_n) = \min_{x_i \in X_n} \lVert x - x_i \rVert_2$ is sufficiently small, then
  \begin{equation*}
    \V_\sigma(x \mid \mathcal{D}_n) \geq C \exp(- c \,\delta(x, X_n)^{-\kappa}) ,
