{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.966459+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c508925c28f3dd768dab02ac28a88139b7c9af5e44c4d1b8840dfc10a6b10785","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 28 of arXiv:2607.28614, 'Tampered Memory Elephant Random Walk on one-dimensional integer lattice'] Let $\theta^*$ be an equilibrium point for the mean field function $h$, that is, $\theta^* \in \{\theta: \, h(\theta)=0\}$, where $h$ is as in \eqref{Eq: recursion_general_SA}. Let $\theta_n$, $\Delta M_n$, and $r_n$ be as in \eqref{Eq: recursion_general_SA}, and $\theta_n \longrightarrow \theta^*, \text{ a.s.}$ as $n \to \infty$. Let $\delta>0$ be such that,
\begin{equation}
    \sup_{n \ge 1}\E \left[\lvert \lvert \Delta M_{n+1}\rvert \rvert ^{2+\delta} | \ \FF_{n}\right]< \infty, \text{ a.s.}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, as $n \to \infty$
\begin{equation}
\E \left[ \Delta M_n \left( \Delta M_n \right)^\top | \ \FF_{n}\right] \longrightarrow \Gamma, \text{ a.s.}
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma$ is a deterministic symmetric positive semi-definite matrix and for any $\epsilon>0$, 
\begin{equation}

(n+1)\E\left[\lvert \lvert r_{n+1} \rvert \rvert ^2 \mathbf{1}_{\{\lvert \lvert \theta_n - \theta^*\rvert \rvert \le \epsilon\}} \right] \longrightarrow 0.
\end{equation}
Let us assume that the eigenvalues of $D(-h(\theta^*))$ are all real and positive. Let $\rho^{*}$ be the smallest eigenvalue. If $\rho^{*} >1/2$, then as $n \to \infty$ 
\begin{equation}

\sqrt{n}\left(\theta_n - \theta^*\right)\stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow} N(0, \Sigma),
\end{equation}
where the variance-covariance matrix $\Sigma$ is given by 
\begin{equation}

\Sigma = \int_{0}^{\infty}{\left(e^{-(D(-h(\theta^*)-\frac{1}{2}I_d) u}\right)^\top \Gamma e^{-(D(-h(\theta^*)-\frac{1}{2}I_d) u} \mathrm{d}u},
\end{equation}
where $I_d$ denotes the $d$-dimensional identity matrix. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.895538+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c50fc9621c9da25bea01c23a6fb46af1bd736928eb2ace1b9c1eb55006f59981","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:chk of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] [\texorpdfstring{[Keeler,hoerm1968,canzani_MRWsurvey,CH20]}{}]
We have the following pointwise Weyl's laws on $(M,g)$, a compact Riemannian manifold without boundary. The next asymptotic relations hold, as $\ell\to +\infty$, for all $k\in \mathbb{N}$ but not necessarily uniformly in $k$.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Without additional assumptions (result by H\"ormander [hoerm1968], equivalent to (thm:hoermplaw of the paper))
    \begin{equation} 
\delta_{[0,\ell]}^M=O\left( \frac{1}{\ell}\right),
    \end{equation}
  with $\rho_\ell=\rho(M)$ fixed small enough. 
    \item On a manifold without self-focal points (result by Canzani and Hanin [canzani_hanin_2018_cinfscasymp,CH20] reported in [Keeler])
   \begin{equation} 
\delta_{[0,\ell]}^M=o\left( \frac{1}{\ell}\right)
    \end{equation} 
    with $\rho_\ell=o(1)$. Such $M$ is a particular case of what Canzani and Hanin [CH20] define as a \emph{manifold of isotropic scaling}, that is a manifold $M$ such that: with $\alpha=1$ one has $\delta_{[\ell-1,\ell]}=o(1)$ in infinitesimal range $\rho_\ell=o(1)$.
    \item On a manifold without conjugate points (result by Keeler  [Keeler]):
   \begin{equation} 
\delta_{[0,\ell]}^M=O\left( \frac{1}{\ell\log(\ell)}\right)
    \end{equation} 
in constant range $\rho_\ell=\frac12 \mathrm{inj}(M,g)$, where $\mathrm{inj}(M,g)>0$ is the injectivity radius.
\item If $M=\mathbb{R}^n$, then (obviously, and by (lem:euclidWeyl of the paper))
\begin{equation} 
\delta_{[0,\ell]}^{\mathbb{R}^n}=0,
\end{equation} 
in any range $\rho$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

