{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.644194+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5166c4801fcb9b459ae510ace05b84c933c0717fb039e727cf61036f23380af","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2605.13518, 'The inertial It\^{o} drift and its applications to particle collision'] Assume Hypothesis \ref{hyp}, and for every $\a \in\,[0,+\infty]$, denote by  $x^\alpha$  the unique solution of the problem
\begin{equation}

\begin{array}{l}
\ds{dx^\alpha(t)=(\gamma^{-1}b)(x^\alpha(t))+f_\alpha(x^\alpha(t))\,dt+(\gamma^{-1}\sigma)(x^\alpha(t))\, B\,dw(t),}	\end{array}
	\end{equation}
where $f_\alpha:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}^d$ is the vector field \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\ds{	[f_\alpha(x)]_{i}=\partial_l\,\gamma^{-1}_{i,j}(x)	[\alpha N_\alpha(x)]_{l,j}+\partial_l\,(\gamma^{-1}_{i,j}\sigma_{j,k})(x)A^{-1}_{k,h}[L_\alpha(x)]_{l,h},}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
and  $N_\alpha \in\,\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$ and $L_\alpha\in\,\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}$ are the matrices 
\begin{equation}

L_{\alpha}(x):=\frac 1\alpha \int_0^\infty e^{-\frac 1\alpha \gamma(x)t}\sigma(x)Me^{-A^\star t}\,dt,	
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

N_\alpha(x):=\int _0^\infty e^{-\gamma(x) t}\,[L_{\alpha}(x)\, \sigma^\star(x)+\sigma(x) L^\star_{\alpha}(x)]\,e^{-\gamma^\star(x)t}\,dt,	
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}

M:=\int_0^{+\infty} e^{-A s}BB^\star e^{-As}\,ds.	
\end{equation}
Then, if
\[\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\frac{\mu(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}=\alpha,\]
for every $\eta>0$ and $T>0$ we have
\begin{equation}

\lim_{\e\to 0}\mathbb{P}\left(	\sup_{t \in\,[0,T]}|x_{\epsilon}(t)-x^\alpha(t)|>\eta\right)=0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.196106+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c516b30ad72dc2a75a3dafe2489ed519716c3546294aeef8df90822257b8daac","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 25 of arXiv:2510.22783, 'Universality of Cutoff for Riffle Shuffling'] Fix $c\in (0,1)$ and $\eps>0$. If $K\geq (\overline C_{\mu}+\varepsilon)\log N$, then there exists $\delta=\delta(\eps,c)$ and $\rho=\rho(\delta,c),\varphi=\varphi(\delta,c)$ such that the following holds. For all weak-$c$-$(\chi,\rho,\varphi)$-almost $\mu$-like shuffle process $\bn$, if the first shuffle is $\chi$-good, we have
