{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.814415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5a879d9d2ff340f08ab84b4838b59f39b57edd4833f8ad6c0f1725b75bb268e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm l:girsanov of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] Let $H \sim N(0,\sigma^2)$ be a centered Gaussian random variable of variance $\sigma^2$.
    For any Borel set $S\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R})$,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}\left[\exp\left(H - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\right)\1_{H \in S}\right]
        = \mathbb{P}(H + \sigma^2 \in S).
    \end{align*}
    In other words, the law of $H$ under the tilted measure $\exp(H-\frac{\sigma^2}{2})\,d\mathbb{P}(H)$ is $N(\sigma^2,\sigma^2)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.694008+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5abc9bd8fefc51c577e729e5136fd936cece24898a2d3dd3d309b654c581423","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2609.02193, 'State-law dynamics of McKean-Vlasov stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on $\mathbb R^'] Assume \textnormal{(A1)-(A4)}. There exists a constant $C_q>0$, independent of $\epsilon\in[0,\epsilon_0]$, $t$ and $\mu$, such that, with
	\begin{align}
		R_{P,q}^q=\frac{4C_q}{q\lambda_0},
	\end{align}
	one has, for every $t\geq0$ and $\mu\in\mathcal P_q(H)$,
	\begin{align}
		M_q\big(\varphi_P^\epsilon(t)\mu\big)
		\leq
		e^{-q\lambda_0t/4}M_q(\mu)+R_{P,q}^q.
	\end{align}
	In particular,
	$\varphi_P^\epsilon(t)\mathcal P_q(H)\subset\mathcal P_q(H)$
	for all $t\geq0$.

	Define
	\begin{align}
		K_P
		&=
		\Big\{
		\nu\in\mathcal P_q(H):
		M_q(\nu)\leq R_{P,q}^q+1
		\Big\},\\
		K(\omega)
		&=K_H(\omega)\times K_P,
		\qquad \omega\in\Omega_0,
	\end{align}
	where $K_H$ is given by Lemma~\ref{Le5}. Then $K_P$ is a nonempty closed subset of $(P,\mathcal W_2)$ and belongs to $\mathfrak D_P$. The family $K=\{K(\omega)\}_{\omega\in\Omega_0}$ is a closed measurable random set in $H\times P$ and belongs to $\mathfrak D$.

	Moreover, for every $D\in\mathfrak D$ and $\omega\in\Omega_0$, there exists $T=T(D,\omega)>0$ such that, for all $t\geq T$ and $\epsilon\in[0,\epsilon_0]$,
	\begin{align}
		\Phi^\epsilon(t,\theta_{-t}\omega)
		D(\theta_{-t}\omega)
		\subset K(\omega).
	\end{align}
	Consequently, $K$ is a closed $\mathfrak D$-pullback absorbing random set for $\Phi^\epsilon$ in $H\times P$, uniformly in $\epsilon\in[0,\epsilon_0]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.779635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5adbb9e51f2b085296c6a793111629278747965f4f164fc4aec10ae81284a80","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Prop:recipe_bounds of arXiv:2609.02325, 'A Payne-Weinberger inequality\\for quantum dot Dirac operators'] Let $\Omega\subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded domain with $C^2$ boundary. Given $m\geqslant 0$ and~$\mathcal B>0$, let~$a>0$ and~$\theta\in(-\frac \pi 2, \frac \pi 2)$ be such that
        $a = \vartheta(\theta) ( m + \mathcal B )$.
    Then, 
    \begin{equation}
    	\lambda_\Omega(\theta,m) \leqslant \mathcal B\quad\text{if and only if}\quad \mu_\Omega(a) \leqslant \mathcal B^2-m^2.
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, $\lambda_\Omega(\theta,m) = \mathcal B $ if and only if $ \mu_\Omega(a) = \mathcal B^2-m^2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.066503+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5b3258e4e646f76680b54106b1885fcfa5c3f960e2d786c03c5c782ba2b539c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2606.27520, 'Critical curve of loop percolation on the $d$-regular tree'] Let $\kappa\geq \kappa_c$. For a finite subset of vertices $F$,
\begin{equation}
    \mu_\kappa(\ell : \ell \cap F \neq \emptyset)
    =
    \log \det\big( G^\kappa|_{F} \big),
\end{equation}
where $G^\kappa$ is the Green function viewed as a matrix $(G^\kappa(x,y))_{x,y}$, and $G^\kappa|_{F}$ is its submatrix indexed by $F \times F$.
In particular, for $n$ distinct vertices $v_1, \ldots, v_n$,
\begin{equation}
    \mu_\kappa\big( \ell : v_i \in \ell \text{ for } i \in \{1,\ldots,n\} \big)
    =
    \sum_{\substack{A \subset \{v_1,\ldots,v_n\} \\ A \neq \emptyset}}
    (-1)^{\# A + 1}
    \log \det\big( G^\kappa|_{A} \big).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.845391+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5b9da70ea1a68531bd11299c86edc425cb75f43dd904f6f413909d625a680d8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 (Expansion of the collision operator) of arXiv:2609.03847, 'A counterexample to McKean's conjecture for the Landau-Coulomb equation'] The collision operator has the expansion
	\begin{equation}
		Q(f_R,f_R)(v)	=
		\bigl(Q_{R,1}+Q_{R,2}+Q_{R,3}\bigr)(v/R)	+
		\mathcal E_R^Q(v) \qquad\text{for all }v \in \Omega_R^{\rm out},
	\end{equation}
	where
	\begin{equation}
		Q_{R,1}	=R^{-K-6}\frac{\Pi^\perp_v}{|v|}:\nabla^2G,
		\qquad	Q_{R,2}	=\frac{R^{-K-8}}{|v|^3}
		\lp3\frac{v\otimes v}{|v|^2}-\Id	\rp:\nabla^2G,
		\qquad	Q_{R,3} 	=R^{-2K-6}Q(G,G).
	\end{equation}
	For every $N\geq0$, there exist constants $c_N,C_N>0$ such that, for all
	sufficiently large $R$,
	\begin{equation}
		\int_{\Omega_R^{\rm out}}\left(
		\frac{|\mathcal E_R^Q(v)|^2}{g_R(v)}
		+	|\mathcal E_R^Q(v)|\langle v\rangle^N	\right)\d v
		\leq
		C_N \exp \lp -c_NR^{6/5} \rp.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

