{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.130586+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c662e50bf6cc82f318b3abcb7d39ed6eb187b4f33daae8be85bd12bb3b770465","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pr:I[sigma,G] of arXiv:2510.09818, 'On the Fourier dimension of fractional Brownian graphs'] For any $\varepsilon \in \{-1,1\}^{2q}$ and $\sigma \in \Sigma(q)$ as defined in (equation b4 of the paper), let $G_\varepsilon$ be the function defined in (equation lambda,T of the paper) and $\mathcal{I}[\sigma; \varepsilon, G_\varepsilon]$ be as defined in (equation I[sigma,G] of the paper).
Recall the indices $k_1,\dots,k_m$ and $k_1',\dots,k_\ell'$ defined in (equation J1:J2:J3 of the paper).
Also recall the variables $s_1,\dots,s_I$ and indices $p_1,\dots,p_\ell$ introduced in Lemma (lem:variables of the paper).
Then we have
\begin{align*}
	|\mathcal{I}[\sigma; \varepsilon, G_\varepsilon]|
	\le T^{2q-m-I} \sum_{P \in \mathscr{P}_2\{p_1,\dots, p_\ell\}} \pi^{\# P} \int_{0<s_1<\cdots<s_I<T} \exp(\pi g_a(s)) \prod_{B \in P}|g_a^{(B)}(s)| ds
\end{align*}
where $g_a$ is defined in (equation g_a of the paper) and $a=(a_1,\dots,a_I)$ is defined in (equation j_1-j_I of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.415962+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c663c55745a36979542b730a20fef648390d5c31c285e96a5e4258f34b52da2f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Delta theta denominator stays positive of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] Let $(\lambda,C^2)\in S^0$ with $\Delta z(\lambda,C^2)=0$. Then 
