{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.863395+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c6c098652764bbb1b950266aaa4b419ea86f4a94be05e48bc48fd8f8b4fd590c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 49 of arXiv:2609.03875, 'Measure theory without infinities'] Let $\mathbb K$ be an algebraic ring and $\mf A$ be both an HCLM$_{\mathbb{K}}$ and a left $\mathbb K$-module.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item[(i)]  Let $\mu$ be an $\mf A$-valued measure. If $k\in\mathbb K$ is invertible, then $k\boxdot \mu = k\cdot\mu$.
  \item[(ii)] If $k,l\in\mathbb K$ and $\mu$ is an $\mf A$-valued premeasure, then $(kl)\boxdot\mu=k\boxdot(l\boxdot\mu)$ and $(k+l)\boxdot\mu = (k\boxdot\mu)\boxplus(l\boxdot\mu)$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.642782+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c6c4e508813fcd747fa1a140f9129949bae663592dae2f24539dd73bf41e2d5e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The sequence of free cumulants $\kappa_1,\kappa_2,\kappa_3,\kappa_4,\ldots$ is as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For $\mu=\delta_c$ the free cumulants are $c,0,0,0,\ldots$

\item For $\mu=\gamma_t$ the free cumulants are $0,t,0,0,\ldots$

\item For $\mu=\pi_t$ the free cumulants are $t,t,t,t,\ldots$

\item For $\mu=\beta_t^2$ the free cumulants are $0,t,0,t,\ldots$
\end{enumerate}
Also, for the compound free Poisson laws the free cumulants are $\kappa_n(\pi_\nu)=M_n(\nu)$.","status":"unverified"}

