{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.015617+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c71ad3693689eaf5d0a22ec7159049c2e05b072dff610f9ef356210a0be15c63","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 40 (Gaussian concentration implies quantitative blowing-up, \cite{CMRU}) of arXiv:2602.14618, 'Finitary coding and Gaussian concentration\\ for random fields'] \leavevmode\\
Let $Y$ be an ergodic random field with law $\nu$, and assume $Y$ satisfies the Gaussian concentration bound \eqref{def-GCB} with constant $C$. Then $Y$ has the blowing-up property. More precisely, for any 
$\Lambda\Subset\Z^d$ and any $E\subseteq B^\Lambda$ with $\nu(E)>0$,
