{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.640420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c77a842e4b567f49c1f46809bc64cb583b9052ee45d1f2c84cda27c525ef5220","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Let $A$ be a $C^*$-algebra, given with a positive trace $tr:A\to\mathbb C$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The elements $a\in A$ are called random variables.

\item The moments of such a variable are the numbers $M_k(a)=tr(a^k)$.

\item The law of such a variable is the functional $\mu_a:P\to tr(P(a))$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.793835+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c77d5de45e90becab56fc26484a660c356ab1638dd9b1414bb72d8741778cff0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 76 (Connes--Kreimer extraction-contraction coproduct) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] The \emph{Connes--Kreimer extraction-contraction 
co\-product} $\DeltaCK: \spanF \to \spancFminus \otimes \spanF$ is defined by 
\begin{equation}

 \DeltaCK(\Gamma) = \Gamma\otimes\unit + \unit\otimes\Gamma 
+ \sum_{\substack{\unit\neq\overline\Gamma\subsetneq\Gamma, \overline\Gamma \in \Fminus}} 
\overline\Gamma\otimes(\Gamma/\overline\Gamma)\;, 
\end{equation} 
where the sum ranges over all \emph{divergent} subgraphs $\overline\Gamma$, and 
$\Gamma/\overline\Gamma$ denotes the graph obtained by replacing $\overline\Gamma$
by a single vertex. The subgraphs have to be \emph{full}, in the sense that if an edge
$e$ belongs to $\overline\Gamma$, all edges connecting the same vertices also belong 
to $\overline\Gamma$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:19.042607+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c781ec51218483998af86f298fdace9eae5e7e09ca919587be7d17f7c9bfc513","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm-P2 of arXiv:2609.02552, 'Resolvent Estimates for the Stokes Operator \\ in a Three-Dimensional Lipschitz Domain'] Let $d=3$ and $\lambda\in \Sigma_\theta$.
Let $(u, \phi )$ be the same as in Theorem (thm-P1 of the paper).
Suppose $x_0 \in \partial\Omega$ and $0< r<  r_0$.
Then, for $2\le q< 3+\varepsilon$ and $p=\frac{2q}{3}$, 
\begin{equation}
\left(
\fint_{B(x_0, r)\cap \Omega} |\phi -\beta|^q \right)^{1/q}
+\left(\fint_{B(x_0, r)\cap \partial\Omega}
|\phi -\beta|^p \right)^{1/p} 
\le C M_p(|\nabla u|) (r),
\end{equation}
where 
 $\beta=\fint_{B(x_0, r) \cap \partial\Omega} \phi $ and  $C$ depends only on $q$ and $\Omega$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.523784+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c782b8e2b412ad9840cca2d98d303b3a8a6c21c88262fa3a4bc56f8892659b6c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm main_result of arXiv:2411.03482, 'Ergodicity of 2D singular stochastic Navier--Stokes equations'] Let $P_t$ be the Markov semigroup of (equation e:SNS of the paper). Let $V_{\alpha}: \mathcal{C}^{-\kappa} \rightarrow \mathbf{R}$ defined above be the Lyapunov function for the Markov process. There exist some positive constants $0 < \alpha_0\leq 1$ and $C > 0$ such that, if the noise $\xi$ satisfies Assumption~(as:noise of the paper) and $\limsup_{k\rightarrow \infty} |\phi_k| \leq \frac{1}{2}\alpha_0$\,, then there exists some $\gamma > 0$ such that for any $t \geq 1$ and $x\in \mathcal{C}^{-\kappa}$, we have
\begin{equ}

    \big(P_{t} V_{2\alpha_0}\big)(x) \leq C e^{-\gamma t} V_{2 \alpha_0}(x) + C\;.
\end{equ}
As a result, the solution to (equation e:SNS of the paper) admits at least one invariant measure $\mu_\star$. 
Moreover, for any $N \in \mathbf{N}^*$ there exists some $\gamma_N > 0$ such that for any $x \in L^2 \cap \mathcal{C}^{-\kappa}$ and $t \geq 0$ we have 
\begin{equ}

    \big(P_{t} V^N_{2\alpha_0}\big)(x) \leq C^N e^{-\gamma_N t} \|x\|^N + (CN)^{2N}\;.
\end{equ} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279390+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c782d691696bdd13f496ff4ff7b532ad11f07f1c2e3a4e30636f76ed1bfd7289","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 (Theorem 3.1.1 in \cite{de_la_Pena_Book}) of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $F: \mathbb{R}^{+}\to \mathbb{R}^{+}$ be a convex function and $A=(a_{ij})_{n\times n}$ be a diagonal-free matrix. If $\{\xi_{i}, i\le n\}$ is a sequence of independent random variables and $\tilde{\xi}_{i}$ is an independent copy of $\xi_{i}$, then there exists a universal constant $C$ such that
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{E}F\Big(\big\vert\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\xi_{i}\xi_{j}\big\vert   \Big)\le \mathbb{E}F\Big(C\big\vert\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\xi_{i}\tilde{\xi}_{j}   \big\vert\Big).
