{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.403057+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c8b88866a8e6f5cc2f453587737c341afd123cde3c0be021b24902449ab36d7b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 13 of arXiv:2607.28004, 'Folding representations of reflected diffusions'] Let Assumptions~\ref{ass:domain} and \ref{ass:coefficients_convex} hold. Then,
    \begin{enumerate}[label = (\roman*),noitemsep]
        \item  for every $y \in \R^d\setminus\{0\}$ the flow equation \eqref{eq:flow_oblique} has a unique solution $t \mapsto \eta_t(y)$ on a maximal time interval $[0,T(y))$. We have $T(y) < \infty$ and  $\eta_t(y) \to 0$ as $t \uparrow T(y)$ for all $y \in \R^d\setminus\{0\}$; 
        \item  every $y \in D^* := D\setminus \{0\}$ can be uniquely written as $y = \eta_t(z)$ for some $z \in \partial D$ and $t \in [0,T(z))$. That is, the map
        \[\eta:\mathcal{U} \to D^* \quad \text{given by} \quad (z,t) \mapsto \eta_t(z), \quad \text{where} \quad \mathcal{U} = \{(z,t): z \in \partial D,\,  0 \le t < T(z)\}\]
        is a bijection. As such, we can write $\eta^{-1}(y) = (h(y),\theta(y))$ for maps $h:D^* \to \partial D$ and $\theta:D^* \to [0,\infty)$;
        \item  the flow map $(y,t) \mapsto \eta_t(y)$ and the inverse maps $h$ and $\theta$ are all of class $C^2$. 
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.369775+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c8bd0472bc41eb52dcc2a71764d3114a7d18f49ce80c974f22481db1c4120979","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition guessing strategy symmetry of arXiv:2507.10294, 'Guessing Strategies for Shelf-Shuffling Machines'] We have $I_j^*=I_{n-j+1}^*$ for all $1\leq j\leq n$, that is, the best guesses in position $j$ and in position $n-j+1$ are the same.
    \begin{proof}
