{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.414452+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c9297d2961298f7eb487789f4583c999987015b99b323763dcf8e40bb8ce2c65","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition cellular-fundamental-class of arXiv:2608.27904, 'Instantons, indefinite 4-manifolds, and Dehn surgery'] Let $(P,\Delta)$ be a stratified-smooth manifold of dimension $n$ and $X$ be a CW-complex. For each nonnegative integer $i$, let $X^{(i)}$ denote the $i$-skeleton of $X$.
A continuous map $f: P \to X$ is said to be \textit{cellular} if $f(P^{(i)}) \subset X^{(i)}$ for any $i$. With coefficients in $\Bbb F_2$, the cellular fundamental class $f_*[P]$ is the element of $C_n^{\rm cell}(X)$ defined as 
\begin{equation}
	\langle f_*[P], e_\alpha\rangle = \deg(f: (P, P^{(n-1)}) \to (e_\alpha/ \partial e_\alpha,*)) \mod 2,
\end{equation}
where $e_\alpha$ denotes an $n$-cell in $X$.","status":"unverified"}

