{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.482231+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cb43686b670d1c5c0249d6b0350a1ceae376508bd7ed9ea785f6d51ec779044a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Given a metric space $X$, let us call $K\subset X$ compact when any open cover $K\subset\cup_iU_i$ has a finite subcover. Then, the following happen:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Compact implies closed and bounded.

\item The converse of this fact is not true.

\item Closed inside compact is compact.

\item If $K_1\supset K_2\supset K_3\supset\ldots$ are non-empty compacts, then $\cap_iK_i\neq\emptyset$.

\item If $K$ is compact, any sequence $\{x_n\}\subset K$ has a limit point in $K$.
\end{enumerate}
Also, coming as good news, in the case $X=\mathbb R^N$, compact means closed and bounded.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:33:06.541093+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cb46405b19f8e26e2e0cf82dca9c1c85381381dd8f200614a2f14f0cf002d37e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 (Uniformization gate) of arXiv:2608.25925, '\textbf{Jump Closure and Limit Uniformization\\in the Ideal Completion of the Turing Degre'] Let
\[
\mathbf a_0\ltT\mathbf a_1\ltT\mathbf a_2\ltT\cdots
\]
be a strictly increasing sequence of Turing degrees. Put
\begin{equation}
