{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.823441+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbb596f704fe2d2df809500debebcee4a98f460580bd40fd38f2ebbefd4756a8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:markov-maximum of arXiv:2607.24640, 'The Yang-Mills measure on surfaces via Morse theory'] There exists $r_0>0$ such that, for every $0<r<r_0$, for every bounded and measurable function $\Psi:G\rightarrow  \mathbb{R} $, and for every bounded function $\Phi:\Omega\times G^{2g}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ that is $\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}_{\gamma_r}^G(1)$-measurable, one has
