{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:37:28.982815+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbb875f8bd70b78aa9cf1b76fcb9daa0fa2bb0e50e662fa8e593e445cdd33167","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 1.1 of arXiv:2412.00708, 'Stochastic PDE approach to fluctuating interfaces'] We have
\begin{align}  
b_K^N(\eta,\varphi) & = \frac{N^2}{2N^d} \sum_{p,q\in{\mathbb{T}}_N^d: |p-q|=1} 
c_{p,q}(\eta)(\eta_p-\eta_q) 
\left(\varphi\left(\tfrac{q}N\right)  - \varphi\left(\tfrac{p}N\right) \right), \\

\Gamma_K^N(\eta,\varphi) & = \frac{N^2}{2N^{2d}} \sum_{p,q\in{\mathbb{T}}_N^d: |p-q|=1} 
c_{p,q}(\eta) (\eta_p-\eta_q)^2
 \left(\varphi\left(\tfrac{q}N\right)  - \varphi\left(\tfrac{p}N\right) \right)^2.
\end{align}
In particular, when $c_{p,q}\equiv 1$ called simple Kawasaki dynamics
or simple exclusion process,
one can rewrite (equation eq:b of the paper) as
\begin{align}  
b_K^N(\eta,\varphi) & = \frac1{N^d} \sum_{p\in{\mathbb{T}}_N^d} \eta_p \, \Delta^N
\varphi\left(\tfrac{p}N\right)   \equiv \langle\rho^N,\Delta^N\varphi\rangle,
\intertext{where}  \notag
\Delta^N\varphi\left(\tfrac{p}N\right) &= N^2 \sum_{q\in {\mathbb{T}}_N^d:|q-p|=1}
\left(\varphi\left(\tfrac{q}N\right)  - \varphi\left(\tfrac{p}N\right) \right).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.487183+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbbcbf2a1186f62da0ab61f7927d5bff3bafeb12b7ba600f28e0bd2719a710c2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conj 89 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] Let $n\geq 1$.
For $\varepsilon>0$, consider the family of random variables
\begin{displaymath}
(A, (Z_{j,\varepsilon},[\Gamma_{Z_{j,\varepsilon}}])_{1\leq j\leq n}).
\end{displaymath}
As $\varepsilon\to 0$, this family converges in law towards
\begin{displaymath}
(A,(Z_{j},[\Gamma_{j}])_{1\leq j\leq n}),
\end{displaymath}
with the following properties.
\begin{itemize}
\item $A$ is the same FPS as previously.
\item $(Z_{j})_{1\leq j\leq n}$ are random points on $A$,
sampled, conditionally on $A$, in an i.i.d. way according to the probability measure
\begin{displaymath}
\dfrac{\nu_{A}}{\nu_{A}(A)},
\end{displaymath}
where $\nu_{A}$ is the FPS measure \eqref{Eq nu a Mink non norm}.
\item The family of equivalence classes of loops
$([\Gamma_{j}])_{1\leq j\leq n}$
is \textbf{independent} from
$(A,(Z_{j})_{1\leq j\leq n})$,
with an i.i.d. distribution according to the measure
$\hat{\mu}^{\rm loop}_{\text{SLE}_{4}}$.
\end{itemize} (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.247129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbbd9a683ddbd6350eca6cd51f27f77b6b9719d8a7935f032418affb188490d5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:I_n asymp of arXiv:2607.01946, 'Second-order fluctuations for a phase transition in random partitions'] Assume $\alpha$, $\nu$, and $(j_n)_{n\in{\mathbb N}}$ satisfy the assumptions in Theorem (thm:1 of the paper). Then for every $t\in(1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)$, 
 we have
\begin{equation}
I_{n,j_n}(t)\sim \alpha \Gamma(j_n)\nu\pp{\frac n{j_n}}t^\alpha.
\end{equation}
In particular, 
\begin{equation}
{\mathbb E} \wt C_{j_n}(nt) =\frac{I_{n,j_n}(t)}{\Gamma(j_n+1)} \sim \alpha t^\alpha \frac1{j_n}\nu\pp{\frac n{j_n}} = t^\alpha\sigma_{n,j_n}^2.
\end{equation}
Moreover, we have local uniform convergence in $t$ in the following sense:
\begin{align}
\lim_{n\to\infty}\sup_{t\in[1-\epsilon',1+\epsilon']}\abs{\frac{I_{n,j_n}(t)}{\alpha\Gamma(j_n)\nu(n/j_n) t^\alpha} - 1} = 0, \nonumber\\
\lim_{n\to\infty}\sup_{t\in[1-\epsilon',1+\epsilon']}\abs{\frac{{\mathbb E}\wt C_{j_n}(nt)}{\sigma_{n,j_n}^2 t^\alpha} - 1} = 0, 
\end{align}
for all $\epsilon'\in(0,\epsilon)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.330306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbbdc8c9f710838d55144c8dfef81e71475ccc83da6fcfe6549d4380037917ce","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm gl-sp-pres of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Both ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\textup{gl}$ and ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}$ are globally presentable.
    \begin{proof}
        In light of Corollary~(cor:LSp-vs-Sp-param of the paper), it will suffice to prove the first statement. 

        By Proposition~(prop:L-gl-Bousfield of the paper), each individual category ${\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-gl}$ is presentable, so it remains to show global cocompleteness. For this, let us write $R\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\textup{gl}\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}$ for the right adjoint from the previous lemma. If $X\in\PSh(\textup{Glo})$ is any presheaf, then $R(X)\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\textup{gl}(X)\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}(X)$ is a fully faithful right adjoint (as a limit of such functors), so its left adjoint is a Bousfield localization. Moreover, if $f\colon X\to Y$ is any map in $\PSh(\textup{Glo})$, then $f_!\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}(X)\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}(Y)$ descends through the localization functor as $f^*$ preserves the essential image of the fully faithful right adjoint $R$ (the latter being part of a global functor); in particular, $f^*\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}(Y)\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}(Y)$ has a left adjoint, and it only remains to verify the Beck--Chevalley condition for pullbacks in $\PSh(\textup{Glo})$. As localizations are in particular essentially surjective, this may be done after precomposing with ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\textup{gl}$. Using once more that Beck--Chevalley maps compose, this then follows at once from ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}$ being globally cocomplete (Proposition~(prop:Splev-cc of the paper)) and the localization being a left adjoint.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.410016+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbbee4045db153ee3dfed6d1e363ce9a2b6175ecd918e300197cace56639ca15","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2604.07207, 'Mixing times of step-reinforced random walks'] For any $\alpha \in (0,1)$, any $n\geq k\geq 1$ and $\varepsilon>0$, one has
