{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.334804+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbcc797246f8a7a8508b4d0e8deeb1729f63ee512c6ab94fe3b2490303389252","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2512.05466, 'Stochastic Flows and Marked Stable Processes'] The following statements hold almost surely, for all $(a,r),(a',r')\in (0,\infty)\times \bR$ and $(b,x)\in \bR_+\times (r,\infty)$: 
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item  We have $(b,x)\in \cT_{(a,r)}$ if and only if $(a,r)$ is an ancestor of $(b,x)$ for both $\cB$ and $\cR$. In particular, there is the identity
\begin{equation}
    \cT_{(a,r)}=\{(b,x)\in \bR_+\times (r,\infty)\,:\, a=\cB^*_{-x,-r}(b)=\cR^*_{-x,-r}(b)\}.
\end{equation} 

\item   If $(b,x)\in \cT_{(a,r)}$, then the points  $(\cR^*_{-x,-y}(b),y)$ and $(\cB^*_{-x,-y}(b),y)$ for $y\in (r,x)$ also belong to $\cT_{(a,r)}$.
\item  $\cT_{(a,r)}$ is bounded.
    \item Either $\cT_{(a,r)}$ and $\cT_{(a',r')}$ are disjoint or one is included in the other.

\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.158730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbceaae40cd5f2e6a9185f2ce059ab8787dca21148c280e088a5dcf25ff1cef8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2607.02406, 'On the range of competing random walks'] We have the following decomposition formula for $\R_n^k$
