{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.873340+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbe231acf9df4781a05aee7d0a2a84e44af9139a0da8e4433137c8f203f9d683","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 37 (Absolute block reconstruction) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] For $1\le r<\infty$, if
\begin{equation}

 \sum_{L,Q\in\Dya}b_r(T;L,Q)<\infty,
\end{equation}
then $T\in\Sch_r(H)$ and
\begin{equation}

 \norm T_{\Sch_r}\le\sum_{L,Q}b_r(T;L,Q).
\end{equation}
More generally, let $1\le p<\infty$ and let
$T:\Omega\to\cL(H)$ be strongly operator measurable, with every
$\Delta_LT\Delta_Q$ strongly measurable as an $\Sch_r$-valued map.  If
\[
 \sum_{L,Q\in\Dya}
 \norm{\Delta_LT\Delta_Q}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}<\infty,
\]
then $T$ agrees almost surely with an element of
$L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)$ and
\[
 \norm T_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 \le\sum_{L,Q\in\Dya}
 \norm{\Delta_LT\Delta_Q}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}.
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.501614+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbe4c415fbe28a4efffb3ff7fb3b5d0265c37df6f1387ab8377baa27f0c44591","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:diagonal-free-spike of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[corollary]{cor:diagonal-free-spike}
Consider the diagonal-free ordered model
\[
 h_{N,\mathrm{df}}^J(x)=N^{-1/2}
 \sum_{\substack{I\in[N]^p:\ i_1,\ldots,i_p\ \mathrm{distinct}}}J_Ix_I,
 \qquad x\in\mathbb S^{N-1}(1),
\]
with i.i.d. centered \(K\)-sub-Gaussian entries having common law
\(\mu\).  There is \(M=M(p,K)\) such that, for all sufficiently large
\(a\) and every \(\eta>0\),
\begin{align}
 &\liminf_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log
 \mathbb E\operatorname{Crt}_{N,\max}^{J,\mathrm{df}}
 \left([ap^{-p/2}-M,(a+\eta)p^{-p/2}+M]\right)\notag\\
 &\hspace{45mm}\ge-\overline I_\mu(a,\eta).
\end{align}
Here \(\operatorname{Crt}_{N,\max}^{J,\mathrm{df}}\) denotes the local-maximum count for \(h_{N,\mathrm{df}}^J\).  Thus the localized one-spike lower-bound mechanism does not rely on repeated tensor indices; this corollary does not compare its exponent with Gaussian diagonal-free complexity.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.433442+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbe94e0d59bd243053b817418b039b095ca47e7dbef1410be1335d7245c0c8a1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma4.7equation of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] ([campos2025singularity], Lemma 4.5)
