{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.873008+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbec9526cc055537c5205e1835b5a54d0a4ad33e6f8b32d0f4bba61a2b9b1495","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:quasicont-conseq of arXiv:2510.13659, 'On the energy image density conjecture of Bouleau and Hirsch'] Let  $(X,d,\mu, \mathcal{E}_p, \mathcal{F}_p, \Gamma_p)$ be a regular $p$-Dirichlet space.
		If $f\in \mathcal{F}_p$ is quasicontinuous and let $A\subset X$ be Borel measurable, then the following hold: 
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item If $\cCap(A)=0$, then $\Gamma_p\langle f\rangle(A)=0$.
			\item If $g \in \Lip(\mathbb{R})$ with $g(0)=0$, then $g \circ f \in \mathcal{F}_p$ and 
			\[
			\Gamma_p\langle g\circ f\rangle  \le( \Lip_a[g] \circ f)^p \cdot \Gamma_p\langle f\rangle.
			\]
			\item If $g\in \Lip(\mathbb{R}^n)$ with $g(0)=0$, and $f=(f_1,\dots, f_n)$ with $f_i\in \mathcal{F}_p$ and $f_i$ are quasicontinuous, then
			\begin{equation} 
				\Gamma_p\langle g\circ f\rangle  \le \left(1 \vee n^{(p-2)/2}\right) (\Lip_a[g] \circ f)^p\cdot \sum_{i=1}^n \Gamma_p\langle f_i\rangle.
			\end{equation}
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:51:11.967575+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbedd347b001c953bb1ac5a55229263b329e318352937a1630548989b12a9b1d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Singular lifting, counterterms, and gauge) of arXiv:2608.08756, 'Infinite-Dimensional L\'evy Area and Spectral Ancestry'] The defects in \eqref{cc:eq-lifting-defect} obey the nonabelian identity
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde\alpha_{t,v}(\Omega_{s,u,v})\star\Omega_{s,v,t}
 =\operatorname{Ad}_{\widetilde\alpha_{t,u}(Q_{s,u})}
      (\Omega_{u,v,t})\star\Omega_{s,u,t}

\end{equation}
for \(s\le u\le v\le t\).  If
\(Q'_{s,t}=r_{s,t}\star Q_{s,t}\), \(r_{s,t}\in K\), then
\begin{equation}
 \Omega'_{s,u,t}
 =\widetilde\alpha_{t,u}(r_{s,u})\star
  \operatorname{Ad}_{\widetilde\alpha_{t,u}(Q_{s,u})}(r_{u,t})
  \star\Omega_{s,u,t}\star r_{s,t}^{-1}.

\end{equation}
Consequently, corrected lifts
\(\widetilde\Phi_{s,t}=k_{s,t}\star Q_{s,t}\) are causal exactly when
\begin{equation}
 k_{s,t}=\widetilde\alpha_{t,u}(k_{s,u})\star
 \operatorname{Ad}_{\widetilde\alpha_{t,u}(Q_{s,u})}(k_{u,t})
 \star\Omega_{s,u,t}.

\end{equation}

Nevertheless, the unrestricted interval problem has no algebraic existence
obstruction.  If \(H_t\in p^{-1}(A_t)\), \(H_0={\mathbf 1}\), then
\begin{equation}
 L^H_{s,t}:=
 \bigl(\widetilde\alpha_{t,s}(H_s)\bigr)^{-1}\star H_t

\end{equation}
is a causal lift, and every causal lift has this form with
\(H_t=L_{0,t}\).  The based vertex gauges \(b_t\in K\), \(b_0={\mathbf 1}\), act by
\begin{equation}
 (L^b)_{s,t}
 :=\widetilde\alpha_{t,s}(b_s)^{-1}\star L_{s,t}\star b_t,

\end{equation}
freely and transitively on all unrestricted lifts.  Thus causality
shows that \(\Omega\) is the defect of the chosen pointwise section rather
than an obstruction to an unrestricted rooted lift; it selects no
renormalization gauge.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.834927+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbee05ed354ff91fc807f9e9a88b600567a10022ef03d4d86fbbd0b0c77dfeb7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2604.10820, 'A Strict Gap Between Relaxed and Partition-Constrained\\ Spectral Compression in a Six-Sta'] Let $K$ be a symmetric stochastic $3\times 3$ matrix with eigenvalues $1,\kappa_2,\kappa_3$.
