{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.813629+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccd5444a6b84015aa7a8d9f2caba19c4778861e5ba6a8926dbb7fc66a46aa515","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm t:pathtogeo of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] {DZ24}}]
    Fix $x,y\in\mathbb{R}$.
    For $n > 0$, consider the annealed polymer $\overline{X} \sim \overline{\mathbb{P}}_{1,(0,x n^{2/3}), (n,  y n^{2/3})}$, and define the scaled version 
    \begin{align*}
        \overline{X}^{(n)}(r) \coloneqq  n^{-2/3}\, \overline{X}(r n),
        \qquad r\in[0,1].
    \end{align*}
    Restricting $\overline{X}^{(n)}$ to $(0,1)$ yields a random element of $C((0,1))$, where $C((0,1))$ has the topology of uniform convergence on compact sets. 
    As $n\to\infty$, we have the weak convergence
    \begin{align}
        \overline{X}^{(n)}\Big|_{(0,1)}
        \xrightarrow{\,d\,}
        2^{1/3} \Pi_{(0,x2^{-1/3}),(1,y2^{-1/3})}
        \Big|_{(0,1)},
    \end{align}
    where $\Pi_{(0,x2^{-1/3}),(1,y2^{-1/3})}$ is the directed landscape geodesic from $(0,x2^{-1/3})$ to $(1,y2^{-1/3})$
    (see (def:geodesics of the paper)).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.063627+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccd56412672e2b3609b381259ab3de6c6a1989ae4842e245d5a070f19a2cddfb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] [Tower Property I] 
Let  $S$ be an arbitrary element of $\mathcal{S}$ and  $\rho \le \tau$ be two stopping times; also let $f \in Q$. Then, 
\begin{equation}  
\overline{\sigma}_{\rho}( \overline{\sigma}_{\tau}~f)(S) = \overline{\sigma}_{\rho}[{\bf 1}_{\{\tau = \infty\}}~f+{\bf 1}_{\{\tau < \infty\}}~\overline{\sigma}_{\tau} f](S)\le \overline{\sigma}_{\rho} f(S).
\end{equation}
and, consequently,
\begin{equation} \nonumber  
 \underline{\sigma}_{\rho} f(S) \leq \underline{\sigma}_{\rho}[{\bf 1}_{\{\tau = \infty\}}~f+{\bf 1}_{\{\tau < \infty\}}~\underline{\sigma}_{\tau} f](S) = \underline{\sigma}_{\rho}( \underline{\sigma}_{\tau}~f)(S).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.303207+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccd673323ef8b0ff3c96143656bf9f11766f1427fb655d039e29e97b45d49cf7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2607.18070, 'The Brownian loop-catcher'] For every \(0<\lambda\le 1\) (i.e.~\(-2\le c<0\)), there is a
unique probability law on compact connected sets
\(K\subset\overline D\) with
\(K\cap\partial D=\{a,b\}\) such that
\begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}\left[\mathbf 1_{\{K\cap A=\varnothing\}}
        \exp\left\{-\lambda m_D(K,A)\right\}\right]
        =\mathbb P\left[\gamma_{\mathrm{BE}}^{D;a,b}\cap A=\varnothing\right],
\end{equation}
for every compact \(A\subset\overline D\) such that $A\cap\{a,b\}=\varnothing$ (with no requirement that \(A\) attach to \(\partial D\)).

For \(\lambda>1\) (i.e.~\(c<-2\)), no probability law on compact
connected sets satisfies~(equation eq:intro-main-id of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.423722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccd8b9a061391d2ef6127169ef8801f0d68892af74c34326df6c797fc73d1e0a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:growing-L-pressure of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[corollary]{cor:growing-cutoff-regimes}
In the setting of (thm:growing-point-cutoff of the paper), suppose
\[
 r_N=L_N\sqrt{\frac{\log N}{N}},
 \qquad L_N\longrightarrow\infty,
 \qquad r_N\le1.
\]
Then the cutoff is Haar-full.  Pressure universality holds if
\begin{equation}
 L_N=o\left(
 \frac{N^{(p-2)/(2(p-1))}}{\sqrt{\log N}}
 \right),
\end{equation}
and the expectation-ratio conclusion holds if
\begin{equation}
 L_N=o\left(
 \frac{
 N^{\frac{p-2}{2(p-1)}
 -\frac1{(m-1)(p-1)}}}
 {\sqrt{\log N}}
 \right).
\end{equation}
The latter permits a growing \(L_N\) precisely when
\((m-1)(p-2)>2\).

Equivalently, for \(r_N=N^{-a}\), the cutoff is Haar-full and pressure
universal whenever
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{2(p-1)}<a<\frac12.
\end{equation}
It is Haar-full and satisfies the ratio conclusion whenever
\begin{equation}
 \frac{m+1}{2(m-1)(p-1)}<a<\frac12.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.553363+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccda9f8398afc84685e912b031b1412472a23926be378d846ac14fa8205f77f8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 10 (Solutions for $0\neq\theta \in \mathbb C \backslash \mathbb{R}$) of arXiv:2604.02129, 'Realisation of constraints in underdamped Langevin dynamics'] Let $c^2 < 4ab$, i.e.\ $\theta$ defined in~\eqref{def:theta} satisfies $\theta= i|\theta| = i \sqrt{4ab-c^2}$. Then~\eqref{eq:lemsollangevin} admits the solution
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    \begin{split} 
       q_t &= \Bigl[ \cos\Bigl(t\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr)+ \frac{c}{|\theta|}\sin\Bigl(t\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr)  \Bigr] e^{-\frac{c}{2}t}q_0 + \frac{2a}{|\theta|} \sin\Bigl(t\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr)e^{-\frac{c}{2}t}p_0 \\ 
       & \qquad - \mfrac{2a}{|\theta|} \int_0^t \sin\Bigl((t-s)\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr)e^{-\frac{c}{2}(t-s)}  \nabla V(q_s,\tilde q_s) ds   +  \frac{2a\tilde c}{|\theta|} \int_0^t \sin\Bigl((t-s)\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr)e^{-\frac{c}{2}(t-s)}  dB_s,
    \end{split}\\
    \begin{split}
    p_t &=  - \frac{2b}{|\theta|}  \sin\Bigl(t\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr)e^{-\frac{c}{2}t}q_0 + \Bigl[\cos\Bigl(t\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr) - \frac{c}{|\theta|}\sin\Bigl(t \mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr)\Bigr]e^{-\frac{c}{2}t}p_0 \\
    & \qquad - \int_0^t \Bigl[ \cos\Bigl((t-s)\frac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr) - \frac{c}{|\theta|} \sin\Bigl((t-s)\frac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr) \Bigr] \nabla V(q_s,\tilde q_s) e^{-\frac{c}{2}(t-s)} ds \\
    &\qquad+ \  \tilde c \int_0^t \left( \cos\Bigl((t-s)\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr) - \frac{c}{|\theta|} \sin\Bigl((t-s)\mfrac{|\theta|}{2}\Bigr) \right) e^{-\frac{c}{2}(t-s)} dB_s.
    \end{split}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}","status":"unverified"}

