{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:58:24.499213+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd09f10356937bf46049b494f53c6718aca894e285c7043812a0010e1dd2e8b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:InfiniteVol-PhaseTrans of arXiv:2601.05798, '{I}mry-{M}a phenomenon for the hard-core model on $\mathbb{Z}^{2}$'] Consider the hard-core model
	on ${\mathbb{Z}}^2$ with activity field $\boldsymbol{\lambda}$,
	$0\le \lambda_v<\infty$ for all $v$.
	The even and odd infinite-volume measures
	are well-defined as the (unique) local weak limits of finite-volume measures along any exhausting sequence $\Lambda \uparrow {\mathbb{Z}}^2$:
	\begin{equation}
		\mu_{{\mathbb{Z}}^2,\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{e} = \lim_{\Lambda \uparrow {\mathbb{Z}}^2} \mu_{\Lambda, \boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{e} \,, \quad \quad
		\mu_{{\mathbb{Z}}^2,\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{o} = \lim_{\Lambda \uparrow {\mathbb{Z}}^2} \mu_{\Lambda, \boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{o}. \vspace{-.2cm}
	\end{equation}
	Furthermore,
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)]
		      If $\mu_{{\mathbb{Z}}^2, \boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{o}(v\in I) = \mu_{{\mathbb{Z}}^2, \boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{e}(v\in I)$ for every $v\in {\mathbb{Z}}^2$, then $\mathcal{G}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\mathbb{Z}^2)$ is a singleton set.
		\item[(ii)] For any site $v \in {\mathbb{Z}}^2$, $
			      \mu_{{\mathbb{Z}}^2, \boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{e}(v \in I) - \mu_{{\mathbb{Z}}^2, \boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{o}(v \in I)\ge 0$ if $v$ is even and $\le 0$ if $v$ is odd.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.956378+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd0d299b8b820ed66620d76cdbaed5d9b7deb9bb0bd99ddb6363c4248e6a2bc5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2605.11264, 'Uniform sampling of multitype continuous-time Bienaym\'e-Galton-Watson trees'] Fix $i\in [d]$. For any $\bm{\ell}\in \z^d_+\setminus\{\bo 0\}$ with $\ell_i>0$ and $0< t< T$, we have 
