{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:16:46.128246+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce2feea81293bfbe891d3a0799ef562bad75ea2b6cd68f521b7759e24b49007d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:2nd-pNC of arXiv:2605.01444, 'From second moments to pairwise negative correlation: \\applications to minimal and unifor'] Suppose $G=(V,E)$ is a finite, connected graph. Let ${\ensuremath{\mathscr T}} (G)$ be the set of spanning trees of $G$ and let ${\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} $ be an $\mathrm{Aut}(G)$-invariant probability measure supported on ${\ensuremath{\mathscr T}} (G)$ (${\ensuremath{\mathbf E}} $ denotes the corresponding expectation); examples include the UST measure ${\ensuremath{\mathbb P}} _\mathrm{UST}$ and the MST measure ${\ensuremath{\mathbb P}} _\mathrm{MST}$. For $e,f\in E$, denote by $e\sim f$ if $e$ and $f$ are adjacent edges in $G$ (i.e., they share a common endpoint), and by $e\not\sim f$ if they are non-adjacent. Write $n=|V|$. For $x\in V$ and $\omega\in\{0,1\}^E$, let $\deg(x;\omega)$ denote the degree of $x$ in the subgraph $\big(V,\eta(\omega)\big)$. Then we have the equalities
	\begin{equation}
	\sum_{e\sim f}{\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} \big[\omega(e)=\omega(f)=1\big]=\sum_{x\in V}{\ensuremath{\mathbf E}} \big[ \deg(x;\omega)^2 \big]-2(n-1)\,,
	\end{equation}
	where the sum on the left-hand side runs over all ordered pairs of distinct adjacent edges $e,f$; and
	\begin{equation}
	\sum_{e\not\sim f}{\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} \big[\omega(e)=\omega(f)=1\big]=n(n-1)-\sum_{x\in V}{\ensuremath{\mathbf E}} \big[ \deg(x;\omega)^2 \big]\,,
	\end{equation}
	where the sum on the left-hand side runs over all ordered non-adjacent pairs of edges.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.355344+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce3226f28d9a948153e485695450ca483a7add4a61f93a2701881f49751be218","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma optimal-control-structure:finite-p of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Let $p\in[2,\infty)$ and $\delta>0$. Let $\alpha\in\mathbb{A}$ be the unique optimizer for $V_{p,\delta}^{\infty}$. Then, there exists $\hat{\alpha} : [0,1]\times{\mathbb R} \to {\mathbb R}$ such that
