{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.719355+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce534b31d1b26abbe644f147108d313d0731fc1b69cb32e4bb08f9d83dd61367","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:tail_Holder of arXiv:2605.30999, 'Functional central limit theorems for $U$-statistics of $\beta$-mixing data'] Let $h\colon S^m\to \mathbb R$ be a measurable function and let $\left(X_i\right)_{i\in \mathbb Z}$ be a strictly stationary sequence. Suppose that 
\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{t\to +\infty}\max_{1\leqslant k\leqslant m}
    \sup_{\bm{i}\in\operatorname{Inc}^k} t^p\mathbb P\pr{\abs{h\pr{X_{i_1},\dots,X_{i_k},X_1^*,\dots,X_{m-k}^*}}>t  }=0.
\end{equation*}
Then for each $k\in\llbracket 1,m\rrbracket$, 
\begin{equation*}
   \lim_{t\to +\infty} t^p \sup_{\bm{i}\in I^k_\infty}  \mathbb P\pr{\abs{\pi_{k,m}\left(h\right)\pr{X_{\bm{i}}}}>t}=0. 
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.425270+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce54206867f661719d3e1012ccde79c38ebd91f9e33d94a31cc48a0dd086badd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 164 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Consider a uniformly random tilings of $A\times B\times C$ hexagon with $D\times D$ hole (the bottom point of the hole is at fixed distance $\mathfrak{t}$ from the left side of the hexagon and at fixed distance $\mathfrak{h}$ from the bottom side).
 Using the notations \eqref{eq_N_hexagon_hole} and \eqref{eq_L_hexagon}, the $N$ horizontal lozenges on the vertical section at distance $\mathfrak{t}$ from the left border with coordinates $1\leq \ell_1<\ell_2<\dots<\ell_N\leq L$ have probability distribution
\begin{equation}

 \amsmathbb P_N(\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_N)=\frac{1}{\mathscr{Z}_N}\cdot \prod_{1 \leq i<j \leq N} (\ell_j-\ell_i)^2 \cdot \prod_{i=1}^N w(\ell_i),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}

w(\ell)=
\left\{\begin{array}{lll}
  (\ell)_{C-\mathfrak{t}}\cdot(A+\mathfrak{t}+1-\ell)_{B-\mathfrak{t}}\cdot(\mathfrak{h}+1-\ell)_D^2&& \textnormal{if}\,\,0<\mathfrak{t}\leq \min(B,C),\\
  (\ell)_{C-\mathfrak{t}}\cdot(A+B+1-\ell)_{\mathfrak{t}-B}\cdot(\mathfrak{h}+1-\ell)_D^2 && \textnormal{if}\,\,B\leq \mathfrak{t}\leq C,\\
  (\ell)_{\mathfrak{t}-C}\cdot(A+C+1-\ell)_{B-\mathfrak{t}}\cdot(\mathfrak{h}+1-\ell)_D^2&& \textnormal{if}\,\,C\leq \mathfrak{t}\leq B,\\
  (\ell)_{\mathfrak{t}-C}\cdot(A+B+C+1-\mathfrak{t}-\ell)_{\mathfrak{t}-B}\cdot(\mathfrak{h}+1-\ell)_D^2 && \textnormal{if}\,\,\max(B,C)\leq \mathfrak{t}<B+C,
 \end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
where $(a)_n$ is the Pochhammer symbol and $\mathscr{Z}_N$ is a normalizing
constant.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.815615+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce54695130de7f9fb1cf1cfcd31ea899dadbc3ba4765172399a6d2f9987d6ef5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Yn of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] The process $\mathcal Y_n$ is a subprocess of $\mathcal T_n$, and satisfies the following. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item The root of $\mathcal Y_n$ is $\varphi$, the root of $\mathcal T_n$. The set of children of $\varphi$ in $\mathcal Y_n$ is the set of all vertices in generation $l$, i.e., $Z_{l}$ of $\mathcal T_n$. Thus, letting $Z'_1$ denote the first generation of $\mathcal Y_n$, we have $Z'_1=Z_l$. 
    \item In general, inductively, for any $p$, the $p$-th generation $Z'_p$ of $\mathcal Y_n$ is the set $Z_{pl}$, with the parent in $\mathcal Y_n$ of each vertex $v \in Z'_p$, is the ancestor $u$ of $v$ in $Z_{(p-1)l}$ in $\mathcal T_n$. Note that $u \in Z'_{p-1}$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.472746+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce570c7f868268c17c9ed262c0a88eac306fee0959e81804cd957ed50e00c620","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:polytope-learning-queries of arXiv:2606.19763, 'Optimal Sparsification of Gaussian Processes'] Let $r\geq1$, let $0<\eps<1/2$, and let $f:\R^n\to\zo$ be an unknown
	Boolean function. 
	There is an algorithm which, given black-box query access to $f$, runs in
	time
	\[
		\poly(n)\cdot
		2^{
			\poly\pbra{
				\log(\alpha(r,\eps/2))/\eps
			}
		}
		= \poly(n)\cdot 2^{\poly(r, 1/\eps)}
	\]
	and, with probability $9/10$, outputs a hypothesis $h:\R^n\to\zo$ satisfying
	\[
		\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{G}}(f,h)
		\leq
		\mathrm{OPT}_r(f)+\eps\,.
	\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.906959+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce5b7b434f316a018dcc5b8ecb3da2d1568898ec6249b752f0bbbc28b61b7402","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:endgame of arXiv:2608.26707, 'An Exponential Lower Bound for the Permanent\\of Random Bernoulli Matrix'] Let $k_*$ and $r_*$ be as above.
There exist absolute constants $c,C>0$ with the following property.
For all sufficiently large $n$, condition on $\mathcal{F}_{k_*}$ and suppose that some $A_*\in\binom{[n]}{k_*}$ is $\lambda$-heavy.
Then
