{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:03:35.732407+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce644b2bb282edcb53aeba0e8d21b376960f4bd06cca408476faf20cdfd87a26","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop UstatTail of arXiv:2609.02743, 'Sharp mean-field estimates for diffusive log/Riesz gases in the Hilbert--Schmidt regime'] There exists a universal constant $L>0$ with the following property.  Let $\{h_{ij}\}_{1\le i,j\le n}$ be bounded, completely degenerate kernels, let $\{\xi_{i}^{(1)},\xi_{j}^{(2)}\}_{1\le i,j\le n}$ be independent random variables in a measurable space $(S,\mathscr{S})$, and define
\begin{align}
A &\coloneqq \max_{1\le i,j\le n} \|h_{ij}\|_{L^\infty},
\qquad\qquad
C^2 \coloneqq  \sum_{1\le i,j\le n} {\mathbb{E}}\brak[\big]{h_{ij}^2(\xi_i^{(1)},\xi_j^{(2)})},\\
B^2 &\coloneqq \max_{1\le i,j\le n}\ \max\brac[\bigg]{\Big\|\sum_{i'=1}^n {\mathbb{E}}\brak[\big]{h_{i'j}^2(\xi_{i'}^{(1)},\cdot)}\Big\|_{L^\infty},\ \Big\|\sum_{j'=1}^n {\mathbb{E}}\brak[\big]{h_{ij'}^2(\cdot,\xi_{j'}^{(2)})}\Big\|_{L^\infty}},\\
D &\coloneqq \sup\ {\mathbb{E}}\brak[\Bigg]{\sum_{1\le i,j\le n}h_{ij}(\xi_i^{(1)},\xi_j^{(2)})\, f_i(\xi_i^{(1)})\, g_j(\xi_j^{(2)})},
\end{align}
where the supremum in $D$ runs over all $f_i,g_j:S\to{\mathbb{R}}$ with ${\mathbb{E}}[\sum_i f_i^2(\xi_i^{(1)})]\le 1$ and ${\mathbb{E}}[\sum_j g_j^2(\xi_j^{(2)})]\le 1$.  Then, for every $t>0$,
\begin{align}
\mathbb{P}\Bigg(\Big|\sum_{1\le i,j\le n}h_{ij}(\xi_i^{(1)},\xi_j^{(2)})\Big| \ge t\Bigg)
\le L\exp\pa*{-\frac{1}{L}\min\brac*{\pa*{\frac{t}{C}}^{2},\ \frac{t}{D},\ \pa*{\frac{t}{B}}^{2/3},\ \pa*{\frac{t}{A}}^{1/2}}}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.354861+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce65635c6152b48d9d426753b5604c511d32062b247c4ba7e66bbb12ae6f92d6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 17 of arXiv:2607.16017, 'On large networks of integrate-and-fire neurons with short-term synaptic plasticity'] Grant Assumptions~\ref{ass:fmin}-- \ref{ass:minf}. Then the process $ (V^\alpha, X^\alpha ) $ is positive Harris recurrent and possesses a unique invariant probability measure $ \mu_\infty (\d v, \d x) .$ Moreover, there exist $ \lambda , C  > 0 $ such that 
