{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.679353+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cfdd1cf996eec26923b73512548188a90dca701431036277857c20ec7ac0171c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2607.25986, 'Asymptotics of a two-species particle system associated to the doubly parabolic Keller-Seg'] Suppose Assumption~\ref{mol} and that $\chi<\chi^*_\theta$. Fix $\sigma\in (0,\frac 12]$.

\vip

(i) Set
\begin{equation}
\Aa_\sigma=\{(N,M,\e) : N\geq 2, M>0, \e\in (0,1], N M\e^{3+\sigma}\geq 1, N M^{1+\sigma} \e^{2+\sigma} \geq 1\}.
\end{equation}
The family 
$((X^{1,N,M,\e}_t)_{t\geq 0} : (N,M,\e)\in \Aa_\sigma)$ is tight in $C(\R_+,\R^2)$.

\vip

(ii) Fix $N\geq 2$ and consider $(M_n)_{n\geq 1}$ valued in $(0,\infty)$ and $(\e_n)_{n\geq1}$ valued in 
$(0,1]$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to \infty} M_n= \infty, \quad \lim_{n\to \infty} \e_n=0 \quad \text{and} \quad \lim_{n\to\infty}
M_n\e_n^{3+\sigma}=\infty.
\end{equation}
Then the family $((X^{k,N,M_n,\e_n}_t)_{t\geq 0, k \in I_N} : n\geq 1)$ is tight in $C(\R_+,(\R^2)^N)$
and any limit point $((X^{k,N}_t)_{t\geq 0, k \in I_N}$ as $M\to \infty$ is a $(\rho_0,c_0,N)$-1SKS system.

\vip

(iii) Consider  $(M_N)_{N\geq 2}$ valued in $(0,\infty)$ and $(\e_N)_{N\geq2}$ valued in $(0,1]$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to \infty} \e_{N}=0, \quad \lim_{N\to \infty}  N M_N\e_N^{3+\sigma}=\infty\quad \text{and} 
\quad \lim_{N\to \infty} N M_N^{1+\sigma}\e_N^{2+\sigma}=\infty.
\end{equation}
Then the family $(\mu^{N,{M_N}} : N \geq 2)$ is tight in $\Pp(C(\R_+,\R^2))$ and any (possibly random) 
limit $\mu$ a.s. solves $\mathcal{(MP)}(\rho_0,c_0)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.760722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cfdecaae30dcac77d5eb46d345d9ff51b0862d78ac44b0192b3da750ced678c5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:measurable-finite-rank-invariants of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] From the preceding definitions we obtain the following three properties.
