{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.741547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d0234446ad9749ac6d3fb032c5955f848c10b7ef915b4b8dfc0198985ebb9962","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary exchangeability_degrees of arXiv:2606.09386, 'High-degree vertices in uniform recursive directed acyclic graphs with freezing'] For $m,n \in \mathbb{N}$, let $G^{(n)}$ be a URD and let $G_1$ be the resulting graph in the Kingman $(m,n)$-coalescent. Recall that $\mathbb A_n$ and $\mathbb F_n$ are the sets of active and frozen vertices of $G^{(n)}$, respectively. With $\sigma_a$ and $\sigma_f$ uniform permutations of the element of $\mathbb A_n$ and $\mathbb F_n$, respectively,
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned} 
\big({}&(\deg_{G_1}(a_v), \ell_{G_1}(a_v), u_{G_1}(a_v))_{v\in [A_n]},(\deg_{G_1}(f_w), \ell_{G_1}(f_w), u_{G_1}(f_w))_{w\in [F_n]}\big)\\
&\overset \mathrm{d} = \big((\deg_n(\sigma_a(o)),\sigma_a(o),u_n(\sigma_a(o)))_{o\in \mathbb A_n},((\deg_n(\sigma_f(u)),\sigma_f(u),u_n(\sigma_f(u)))_{u\in \mathbb F_n})\big).
\end{aligned} \end{equation} 
And, jointly for $i,j\in\mathbb{N}$, $A\subseteq [A_n]$, and $F\subseteq [F_n]$, 
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
({}&|\{v\in A: \deg_{G_1}(a_v)=i\}|,|\{w\in F: \deg_{G_1}(f_w)=j\}|)\\
&\overset \mathrm{d}= (|\{o\in \mathbb A_n: \deg_n(\sigma_a(o))=i\}|,|\{u\in \mathbb F_n: \deg_n(\sigma_f(u))=j\}|).
\end{aligned}\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.586400+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d025481b0bbe3d288d7da9263828d4c612c626b53fdccb9e8ffff2f270ec1d9a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thrm:GreenKernel1 of arXiv:2411.09976, 'Occupation times on the legs of a diffusion spider'] The Green kernel of the diffusion spider $\mathbf{X}$ has a density~$\mathbf{g}_\lambda$ with respect to the speed measure~$\mathbf{m}$, which is 
given for $ x\geq 0$ and $y\geq 0$ by
\begin{equation}

\mathbf{g}_\lambda((x,i),(y,j))=
\begin{cases}
\varphi(y)\tilde{\psi}(x,i), &\quad x\leq y,\ i=j, \\[2pt]
\varphi(x)\tilde{\psi}(y,i), &\quad y\leq x,\ i=j, \\[2pt]
c_\lambda^{-1}\,\varphi(y)\varphi(x), &\quad i\neq j,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}

\tilde{\psi}(x,i):=\frac1{\beta_i}\psi^{\partial}(x)+\frac1{c_\lambda}\varphi(x)
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}

c_\lambda:=-\frac{d}{dS}\varphi(0+)>0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.005092+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d02c229a20cf7f89761f418ca2f2e003737023c2ce18e69a456e71c40eafff28","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_reset_2 of arXiv:2606.27549, 'On Analytically Tractable Multidimensional Diffusions via Doob $h$-Transforms, with Resett'] Let $\widehat{\bf X}_t^{\rm R}$ and ${\bf X}_t^{\rm R}$ be the $n$-dimensional diffusion processes satisfying the assumptions of Proposition (prop_reset of the paper), so their transition p.d.f.'s satisfy the relation in (equation rel_f of the paper). If the intensity of the resetting processes $N_t$ and $N_t'$ is a positive constant $\lambda$, then the stationary distribution of ${\bf X}_t^{\rm R}$ is expressed in terms
    of the stationary distribution of $\widehat{{\bf X}}_t^{\rm R}$, given in (equation dens_stazionaria of the paper), via the following product-form relation:

    \begin{equation} 
        f^{\rm R}({\bf x}\,|\,{\bf y}^{\rm R})=\frac{h({\bf x})}{h({\bf y}^{\rm R})} \widehat{f}^{\rm R}({\bf x}\,|\,{\bf y}^{\rm R}).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:24:34.399766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d02cff6d571b65c0ce9788edc8bc0f317dde77e84a3316f84fda3fe53b82350f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 9 of arXiv:2608.31054, 'Revisiting Picard's proof of the Picard--Weber--Weck selection theorem'] Let $\Omega\subseteq \R^d$ be a global Lipschitz domain and $\Phi\colon Q\to \Omega$ be an admissible transformation. Writing $J_\Phi := \Grad(\Phi)$ and $\tilde{u} := u \circ \Phi$, the following assertions hold.

    (a)
    Let $  u \in H_0(\Rot;\Omega)$. Then 
    $J_\Phi^\top \widetilde{  u} \in H_0(\Rot;Q)$ and
    \begin{equation*}
        \Rotcirc_Q{(J_\Phi^\top \widetilde{  u})} 
        =  J_\Phi^\top\,\widetilde{\Rotcirc_\Omega   u}\,J_\Phi =: ( J_\Phi^\top \otimes J_\Phi^\top )\widetilde{\Rotcirc_\Omega   u}.
    \end{equation*}

    (b)  Let $  u \in H(\div;\Omega)$. Then $(\adjugate J_\Phi) \widetilde{  u} \in H(\div;Q)$ and
    \begin{align*}
        \div_Q{( (\adjugate J_\Phi) \widetilde{  u})} 
        = (\det{J_\Phi})\widetilde{\div_\Omega   u}.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.187888+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d039fd3e6a334d6b91e37195a4b169f5ee4873e0ec51ba87e6c0e52fb4aba1da","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 of arXiv:2604.18871, 'Quantitative approximation of the Vlasov(--Fokker--Planck)--Navier--Stokes system by stoch'] Let Assumption~\ref{assump-particles} hold with $p>d$ and $\gamma\in (\frac{d}{p}, 1)$. If $d=3$, assume further that $\frac{\gamma}{2} + \frac{3}{2}(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{p}) <1$.
