{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:20:55.703416+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d03d2a9b2c97e8e71e871ac2dd32589f7f4b5f30861b7062b18fc9f92d701376","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LEM:Error1 of arXiv:2411.07840, 'Central limit theorem for the focusing $Φ^4$-measure in the infinite volume limit'] Under the orthogonal decomposition $h(x)=h^\perp(x)+\langle Q, \Re h\rangle \gamma(x)$ in (equation eqn: decomp of the paper), we have that on the set $S_{Q,x_0}\cap \mathcal{K}^L_\delta$ in Proposition (PROP:REDUC of the paper)
\begin{align}
\int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2}} (Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0})^2|\Re h|^2 \, \mathrm{d}x &\le (1+\varepsilon ) \int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2}} (Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0})^2 | \Re h^\perp|^2 \, \mathrm{d}x+\delta\int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2}}  | h^\perp|^2 \, \mathrm{d}x \\
L^3\mathcal{E}(Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}, L^{-\frac 32}h) &\le \frac 1{L^\frac 32} \int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2} }  Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}  |h^\perp|^3 \, \mathrm{d}x  +\frac {1}{L^3} \int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2}} |h^\perp |^4 \, \mathrm{d}x +\delta \int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2}} |h^\perp|^2 \, \mathrm{d}x

\end{align}

\noindent
for arbitrary small constants $0<\delta \ll \varepsilon$, where 
$L^3\mathcal{E}(Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}, L^{-\frac 32}h)$ is defined in (equation Eerror of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.264345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d03efbb969d67adf953460a6d6773f9138c44cc8b339c2b618af2a076c4b7b9a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2604.19924, 'Fluctuation theory for spectrally negative Lévy processes killed by additive functionals'] For any $T>y$
				, a locally bounded function $h:\R\to\R$, and two Radon measures $\nu$ and $\lambda$ such that 
				\begin{align}
					\inf_{x\in[y,T]}(1-W^{(q)}(0)(\nu-\lambda)\{x\})>0,
				\end{align}
				the equation
				\begin{align}
					H(x,y)= h(x-y)+\int_{[y,x]}W^{(q)}(x-z)H(z,y)(\nu-\lambda)(dz),\qquad x\in\R.
				\end{align}
				admits a unique locally bounded solution {$H(x, y)=H_{\nu-\lambda}^{(q)}(x, y)$} on $[y,T]$. Furthermore, if $h(x)\geq0$ for all $x\in[y,T]$ and $\nu-\lambda$ is a non-negative measure, then $H(x,y)\geq0$ for all $x\in[y,T]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.602627+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d042ceada4c3a67b0c97d84698ec03216998462a4193bc0a401e51d1097d79b7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:smoothedlower of arXiv:2606.11248, 'Persistent Homology of the Planar Wiener Sausage: Brownian Scaling and a Logarithmic Expec'] Let $\psi\ge0$ be bounded and supported in $[r_{0},r_{1}]\subset(0,\infty)$. Then
