{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.616209+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d0cdd3febfe353b84c3bb963cf46c0dbe449dbe1795dbadeb450b1cdd561f14e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 (``Shape'' of local extrema of GFF) of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] There exists a probability law~$\nu$ on~$\R^\N$ such that the following holds:
Given~$f\in\Cloc^+$, $k\ge1$ and~$u\in\R$, let $g_{k,u}\colon[0,1]\times\R\to[0,\infty)$ be defined by
\begin{equation}

\texte^{-g_{k,u}(v,s)}:=\wt E\Bigl(\texte^{-f(v,\,s, \, h(\zero)-h(\zero\cdot))}\,\Big|\, h(\zero) = \max_{y\in\BbbL_k}h(y)=m_k+u\Bigr)
