{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:51:11.975699+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d10d1161e8f0f566204a0115f50bb0b835a919c86e91c5a6862ecdeeed517013","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 45 (Fermionic trace-class calculus) of arXiv:2608.08756, 'Infinite-Dimensional L\'evy Area and Spectral Ancestry'] The operators in \eqref{cc:eq-fermionic-superoperators} do not depend on
the chosen nuclear representation of \(Q\), and
\begin{equation}
 \|\mathrm d\Gamma(Q)\|\leq\|Q\|_1,
 \qquad
 \|\mathcal B_Q(T)\|_1\leq\|Q\|_1\|T\|_1.

\end{equation}
For \(P,Q\in\mathcal S_1(H)\),
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal B_P\mathcal B_Q=\mathcal B_Q\mathcal B_P.

\end{equation}
If \(Q,T\succeq0\), then \(\mathcal B_Q(T)\succeq0\), and
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Tr}\mathcal B_Q(T)
 =(\operatorname{Tr}Q)(\operatorname{Tr}T)
  -\operatorname{Tr}(\mathrm d\Gamma(Q)T)
 \leq(\operatorname{Tr}Q)(\operatorname{Tr}T).

\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{j!}\mathcal B_Q^j(P_0)=\Lambda^jQ,
 \qquad
 \|\Lambda^jQ\|_1\leq\frac{\|Q\|_1^j}{j!}.

\end{equation}
Consequently, for \(Q\succeq0\) and \(x\geq0\),
\begin{equation}
 e^{x\mathcal B_Q}(P_0)=\Gamma_{\wedge}(xQ),
 \qquad
 \operatorname{Tr}_{\mathcal F_{\wedge}(H)}\Gamma_{\wedge}(xQ)
 =\det(I+xQ).

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

