{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.975769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5a9dc115f9ff84300ecf507070ccc643fb11902597743b428bcf774702512ea","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2606.11271, '\LARGE The $K$-th nearest neighbor random walk on a Poisson point process gets trapped'] For any $\varepsilon >0$, $A>0$ and $n\in \N$, $X_n$ is called an $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer point if there exist some $\rho \in [0,A]$ and some $c\in \R^d$ such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(i)$] $B(c,\varepsilon) \subset B(X_n, \rho) \cap (\R^d \setminus E_n)$;
\item[$(ii)$] $| (\chi \smallsetminus \{X_n\})\cap \left(   B(X_n, \rho) \cap E_n  \right) | < M$.
\end{itemize}
The set of $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer points is denoted by
$$
\V_p (\varepsilon, A) \coloneqq \{ x\in \chi \,:\, \exists n \in \N \,,\, X_n =x \textrm{ and $X_n$ is $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer}\} \,.
