{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.446849+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d60ed2a3e7d3330e5a58d0f8a9b7fdc185244a0394a30114cfbf57e00d45a29c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $n\in\N$, $\mu\neq0$ be a finite measure on $\R^d$, $J$ be an index set, \linebreak $\{f_j:j\in J\}\subseteq L^1(\mu)$, and $x_1,...,x_n\in\R^d$. We say that $x_1,...,x_n$ form an \textit{averaging set} of $\mu$ and $\{f_j:j\in J\}$ of size $n$ if
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{1}{\mu(\R^d)}\int f_j(x)\, \mu(dx) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=1}^n f_j(x_k),\quad j\in J.
    \end{equation}
    If we do not say otherwise, we require that $x_1,...,x_n$ are pairwise different. Then we also say that $\{x_1,...,x_n\}$ is an averaging set of $\mu$ and $\{f_j:j\in J\}$ of size $n$.","status":"unverified"}

