{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:52:51.769885+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d77ea35031ba5573839c91bb27901fef007d20c8e813fd43f43737e0f12d25fe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop upper bound for the giant of arXiv:2605.03151, 'Giants through Higher-Order paths in Random Simplicial Complexes'] Let $(X_n)_{n \geq 1}$ be a sequence of $d$-dimensional simplicial complexes with $s_{d-1}(X_n) \to \infty$ as $n \to \infty$. Assume that $X_n$ converges locally weakly in probability to $(X, o) \sim \mu$. Let $\zeta := \mathbb{P} \left( s_{d-1} \left( \mathcal{C}(o) \right) = \infty \right)$, the survival probability of the $d$-dimensional connected component of the root $o$ in $X$. Then, for every $\epsilon > 0$, it holds that
