{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:20:30.554569+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8e2d338defa12be1de7fd2a4d2c9d37671feae90873e200fd5bd65ef5b07cb0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2609.01826, 'An extension theory for fully fractional Schr\"odinger equations with memory'] The solution $U$ of the extension problem \eqref{ep} admits the following representation in phase space
\begin{equation}
\widehat U(\xi,z,\tau)  = \frac{2^{1-s}}{ \G(s)} \left(z \theta(\xi,\tau)  |p(\xi,\tau)|^{1/2}\right)^s K_{s}\left(z \theta(\xi,\tau)  |p(\xi,\tau)|^{1/2}\right) \hat u(\xi,\tau)\ \ \ (\xi,\tau)\in L^+\cup L^-, 
\end{equation}
in the sense that one has
\begin{equation}
\underset{z\to 0^+}{\lim}\ \widehat U(\xi,z,\tau) = \widehat u(\xi,\tau).
\end{equation}
Moreover, $U$ satisfies the Dirichlet-to-Neumann identity:
\begin{equation}
- \frac{2^{2s-1} \G(s)e^{i\frac{\pi s}2} }{\G(1-s)}\ \underset{z\to 0^+}{\lim} z^{1-2s} \widehat U_z(\xi,z,\tau) = (i\,p(\xi,\tau))^s\,\widehat u(\xi,\tau) = \widehat{\Le^s u}(\xi,\tau). 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.087558+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8e71184fb78c426627600ac55d15bd231c4361ca72388f0e9a2fec2728353dd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2604.25769, 'The conformal dimension of the Brownian tree is one'] For each $\eta > 0$, there exists a metric $D_\sigma$ on $X$ and a constant $C > 0$ such that the identity mapping $\mathrm{id} \colon (X, D) \to (X, D_\sigma)$ is quasisymmetric and
