{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.327362+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9da909c8759257b1cef5af9ba0ffbabb4faca5f1e06e3dcf96f69c7cdc91f80","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma G-global-spectra-tensoring of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The smash product $\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}_*\times\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}\to\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}$ is a left Quillen bifunctor for the $\mathchoice{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}$-model structure on $\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}_*$ and the $G$-global model structure on $\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}$. Moreover, if $X\in\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}_*$ is $\mathchoice{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}$-cofibrant, then $X\smashp{-}$ is fully homotopical, and if $Y\in\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}$ is flat, so is ${-}\smashp Y\colon\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}_*\to\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}$.
    \begin{proof}
        The pushout product axiom for cofibrations follows from Lemmas~(lemma:restr-gl-sp-right-Quillen of the paper) and~(lemma:restr-inj-gl-sp-left-Quillen of the paper) as in the proof of Lemma~(lemma:G-Top*-tensored of the paper). Moreover, if $X\in\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}_*$ is $\mathchoice{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}$-cofibrant, then $X\smashp{-}\cong\Sigma^\infty X\smashp{-}$ is homotopical by Proposition~(prop:gl-flatness of the paper), as is ${-}\smashp Y$ for any flat $Y$. With these established, the pushout-product axiom for acyclic cofibrations then follows as in the proof of Proposition~(prop:smash-gl-left-Quillen of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:24:34.411287+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9daaaebe1890a78271f1969b20eafd625c11e0cef4200e11658c44d7e24ba5d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 24 of arXiv:2608.31070, 'Sub-Laplacians on Compact Lie Groups: Heat Kernels, Distance, and Zeta Determinants'] For every compact connected $G$ there exist integers $A,M,N\ge0$ and constants $c,C>0$ such that, for $0<t\le1$ and $Y\in\gLie$,
		\begin{align}
			&j_G^{\nc}(Y)^{1/2}e^{-|Y|^2/(4t)}q_t^{\C}(e^{iY})
			=\vol(G)\frac{e^{t|\rho_G|^2}}{(4\pi t)^{\dim G/2}}
			+R_t(Y),

		\end{align}
		where
		\begin{equation}
			|R_t(Y)|\le Ct^{-N}e^{-c/t}(1+|Y|)^M.
		\end{equation}
		More precisely, each nonzero Weyl orbit $\mathcal O$ in the semisimple lattice satisfies
		\begin{equation}
			\left|
			\frac{\sum_{\gamma\in\mathcal O}\varepsilon_G(\gamma)
				\Pi_G(Y-i\gamma)e^{-i\langle\gamma,Y\rangle/(2t)}}
			{\Pi_G(Y)}
			\right|
			\le Ct^{-N}(1+|Y|+\ell_{\mathcal O})^A.
		\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.078619+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9dc6d551583016150e22f3a8ae75fc8691fcb7139013425c3fe5da9c5bd9f7f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2601.15223, 'Large time behaviour for a class of 2D and 3D stochastic non-Newtonian fluids of different'] Assume that condition (equation third-grade-paremeters-res of the paper) holds. Then:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)] on bounded domains, system (equation equationV1 of the paper) possesses pullback random attractors whenever the forcing satisfies  $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{K}}\in\mathrm{L}^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb{R};\mathbb{H}^{-1}(\mathbb{Q}))$ and there exists a number $\delta\in[0,\frac{\sigma^2}{2})$ such that for every $c>0$,
		\begin{align*}
			\lim_{s\to-\infty}e^{cs}\int_{-\infty}^{0} e^{\delta\zeta}\|\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{K}}(\cdot,\zeta+s)\|^2_{\mathbb{H}^{-1}}\/\mathrm{d}\/ \zeta=0.
		\end{align*}

		\item[(ii)] on unbounded domains, system (equation equationV1 of the paper) possesses a unique random attractor provided the forcing satisfies $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{K}}\in\mathrm{L}^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb{R};\mathbb{L}^{2}(\mathbb{Q}))$ and there exists a number $\delta\in[0,\frac{\sigma^2}{2})$ such that for every $c>0$,
		\begin{align*}
			\lim_{s\to-\infty}e^{cs}\int_{-\infty}^{0} e^{\delta\zeta}\|\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{K}}(\cdot,\zeta+s)\|^2_{2}\/\mathrm{d}\/ \zeta=0.
		\end{align*}

		\item[(iii)] on both bounded and unbounded domains, system (equation equationV1 of the paper) admits an invariant measure, corresponding to $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{K}}\in\mathbb{H}^{-1}(\mathbb{Q})$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{K}}\in\mathbb{L}^{2}(\mathbb{Q})$, respectively.
		\item[(iv)] on both bounded and unbounded domains, system (equation equationV1 of the paper) admits at most one invariant measure for $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{K}}=\boldsymbol{0}$.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.962364+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9df005657d216489d4ad79cd31df4dc79ceadc6d3386af896b128f12e87da19","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3 of arXiv:2607.22414, 'Asymptotic fluctuations in supercritical multi-type Crump--Mode--Jagers processes'] Assume~\ref{assumpt:Malthusian alpha} through~\ref{assumpt:hreprMoments} hold,
and let $\varphi$ be characteristic with c\`adl\`ag paths satisfying~\ref{assumpt:dRimean} through~\ref{assumpt:local ui}
for which~\eqref{eq:meanAs1} holds. Let $\mathcal{N}$ be a standard normal variable independent of Nerman's martingale limit $W$.
	\begin{enumerate}[\normalfont(i), wide]
		\item If $n=-1$, then
		\begin{equation*}
			e^{-\frac\alpha2t} \big(\cZ_t^\varphi - H_\Lambda(t) - H_\dLambda(t) \big) \tod \sigma \Big(\frac{W}\beta\Big)^{\!1/2} \mathcal{N}
			\quad	\text{as } t \to \infty
		\end{equation*}
		where
		\begin{equation*}
			\sigma^2 = \int_\G e^{-\alpha t} \bu^\trans \Var[\bxi*f^\varphi + \varphi](t) \, \ell(\dd t)
		\end{equation*}
		for the function
		\begin{equation}
			\begin{split}
				f^\varphi(t) 
				&= r(t)\1_{[0,\infty)}(t) - \!\!\!\sum_{\lambda\in\intLambda} e^{\lambda t} \sum_{k=0}^{k_\lambda-1} A_{\lambda,k+1} \int_\G \E[\varphi](s)\frac{(t-s)^k}{k!} e^{-\lambda s} \, \ell(\dd s) \1_{(-\infty,0)}(t).
			\end{split}
		\end{equation}
		Moreover, $\sigma=0$
		if and only if $\cZ_t^{\varphi} = H_\Lambda(t) + H_\dLambda(t) + \be_{\tau(\varnothing)}^\trans r(t)$ a.\,s.\ for any $t \geq 0$.
		\item If $n\geq0$, then
		\begin{equation*}
			\frac{e^{-\frac\alpha2 t}}{\sqrt{t^{2n+1}}} \big(\cZ_t^\varphi - H_\Lambda(t) - H_\dLambda(t) \big) \tod \frac{\rho_n}{\sqrt{2n+1}} \Big(\frac{W}\beta\Big)^{\!1/2} \mathcal{N}\quad	\text{as } t \to \infty.
		\end{equation*}
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:37:14.890973+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9e1c83eb0be49901ac73969b008925b0dc6a63b7bbb1c1a96748b36e0da20e3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 12 of arXiv:2607.11608, '2607.11608'] Let  $r\ge 1$. Then, 
