{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.903303+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc65bfb76801f256b71ae5585ca69dabffbb8480c1e86eacbe34ccd2fa964d8a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:KPZ of arXiv:2511.08479, '\Large From disordered systems to the Critical 2D Stochastic Heat Flow'] \ 
Let $h^\varepsilon$ be the solution of the mollified 2D KPZ equation (equation eq:2DKPZ of the paper) with disorder strength $\beta_\varepsilon=(\hat\beta+o(1)) \sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\log \frac{1}{\varepsilon}}}$ for some $\hat\beta\in (0,1)$. Then for any test function $\phi\in C_c(\mathbb{R}^2)$, we have
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\beta_\varepsilon} \int_{\mathbb{R}^2} \phi(x) \Big(h^\varepsilon(t,x)-\ensuremath{\mathbf  E}[h^\varepsilon(t,x)] \Big) {\rm d}x
\xrightarrow[\,\varepsilon\to 0\,]{d} \int_{\mathbb{R}^2} \phi(x) v(t, x) {\rm d}x,
\end{align}
where $v(t, x)$ is the solution of the Edwards-Wilkinson equation (equation linearSHE of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:09.088750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc66bd0a6516382107ac6e44e89e1649eb89d207cc1c61adeae166194cc22126","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Upper bound of cumulants GUE of arXiv:2411.11341, 'Ultra high order cumulants and quantitative CLT for polynomials in Random Matrices'] Let $\mathcal{X}\sim $ GUE or GOE. Consider the polynomial $P(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{D})$ in ((def of P of the paper)) and the random variable $\mathbf{X}$ defined in ((object of the paper)). Suppose that $\mathbf{C}_1$ is satisfied. 
Then there exists a constant $\theta_1\equiv \theta_1 (K, M, t)\in (0, 1)$ such that 
\begin{align}|K_r(\mathbf{X})|\leq \frac{r!^{\frac{M}{2}}}{(\theta_1 N)^{r-2}}, 
\end{align}
for any $N$ and $r\geq 3$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.496214+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc66fa1e92e9c2a316c5c9a9c6b4bb3fe6064ecbaaa7d113b55bb5e4025f46ad","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2608.18075, '2608.18075'] Let $u:\Omega_\tau\to\mathcal H$ be Borel and suppose that
\begin{align*}
 u\in\mathbb D^{1,p}(\mathcal H)
 \qquad\text{for every finite }p,
\end{align*}
where $\mathbb D^{1,p}(\mathcal H)$ is the usual Malliavin Sobolev space \cite{Bogachev10}.
Define
\begin{align*}
 \mathbf u(\omega)(t)=\int_0^t u(\omega)_r\,\dd r,
 \qquad
 T_u(\omega)=\omega+\mathbf u(\omega).
\end{align*}
Suppose moreover that, for some $d\ge0$,
\begin{align}
 \|u\|_{\mathcal H}\le Cd,\qquad
 \|D_{\mathcal H}u\|_{\HS}\le Cd,\qquad
 \|D_{\mathcal H}u\|_{\rm op}\le\frac12
\end{align}
$\mathbf P$-almost surely. Then $(T_u)_\#\mathbf P\sim\mathbf P$
and
\begin{align*}
 \operatorname{Ent}\bigl((T_u)_\#\mathbf P\mid\mathbf P\bigr)
 \le C_{\rm Ram}d^2,
\end{align*}
where $\operatorname{Ent}(\cdot\mid\cdot)$ denotes relative entropy and
$C_{\rm Ram}$ depends only on the fixed constant $C$ in
\eqref{eq:Ramer-small}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.084291+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc697364f130a0d4f03f08b643599b12aa13068200278304c58ddd6cd455d582","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary InverseGammaU of arXiv:2504.18739, 'discrete analogues of second-order Riesz transforms'] Let $S$ be a random variable with an inverse Gamma distribution of parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$ given by $\alpha=\frac{d+2}{2}$, $\beta=\frac{|m|^2}{4}$. Let $B_t$ be the standard $d$-dimensional Brownian motion. Then, for $m\in\mathbb{Z}^d$, with $m\ne 0$,
\begin{align}
    U(m)=-\mathbb{E}\left(\frac{1}{H_{S}\left(\frac{B_S}{\sqrt{2}}+\frac{m}{2}\right)}\right) \quad \text{and}\quad U(m)=U(-m). 
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.230968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6a048c1c0b868d1386490d5a3983767a52e90cb78a7dc7f968805f5faf4390","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] For a down-right path $\lambda=(z_i)_{i\in \Z} \in \Lambda$ and a function $h_0 \colon \lambda \rightarrow \R \cup \{-\infty\}$ we define the \textbf{last passage percolation height function} $\hL$ with initial condition $h_0$ on $\lambda$ by
\begin{equation}
    \hL_{\lambda}(h_0;x,y) := \sup_{u \in \lambda} \big( h_{0}(u) + T_{u,(x,y)}\big) 
\end{equation} for all $x,y \in \Z$ with $(x,y) \succeq u $ for some $u\in \lambda$. . The \textbf{periodic height function} $\hLp_{\lambda}$ is defined as in \eqref{eq:LPPRecursion}, but with respect to the periodic last passage times $\Tper$ from Definition~\ref{def:LastPassageTimes} and a down-right path $\lambda=\lambda^{\per}_N \in \Lambda_{N,k}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.843077+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6a1aeab3247a3ff79e42527181d60b1e10074609ea7942642a67a8aec55118","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2608.13631, 'The classical discrete laws near the mode:\\ complete local expansions for the negative bi'] Let $t_c$ be the vertex of the quadratic part of $A_1$ of \eqref{eq:hyp-Am}.  Then
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{gathered}
		t_c=-\frac{(N-2n)(N-2K)}{2N^{2}},\\
		\mu+t_c+\tfrac12-\frac{(n+1)(K+1)}{N+2}
		=-\frac{(2K-N)(2n-N)}{N^{2}\,(N+2)} .
	\end{gathered}
	\end{equation}
	and the discrepancy satisfies
	\[
		\Bigl|\mu+t_c+\tfrac12-\frac{(n+1)(K+1)}{N+2}\Bigr|<\frac1{N+2} .
	\]
	Consequently, whenever $(n+1)(K+1)/(N+2)\notin\Z$, the unit window
	$\bigl(\mu+t_c-\tfrac12,\,\mu+t_c+\tfrac12\bigr]$ contains exactly one integer and it is
	the mode \eqref{eq:hyp-mode-classical}; when $(n+1)(K+1)/(N+2)\in\Z$ the window boundary
	falls within $1/(N+2)$ of the tied pair, and the integer it selects is one of the two
	modes.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.509600+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6db1ede2a0aa93bfeedd079bd8ffe0f20fd6abca153fc37f85b8a507888e3e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:fourier_identities of arXiv:2607.14266, 'The Swapping Mechanism for Interacting Diffusions: Framework, Comparison with Switching, a'] For $B\in\mathbb{R}$ and $s,t>0$, the following distributional identities
			hold:
			\begin{align}
				\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{i\psi B}
				\cosh\!\bigl(st\sqrt{\psi^{2}+1}\bigr)\mathrm{d}\psi
				&= \frac{st\pi}{2}\int_{0}^{1}
				\frac{I_1(st\sqrt{1-z})}{\sqrt{z(1-z)}}
				\bigl[\delta(B-ist\sqrt{z})
				+\delta(B+ist\sqrt{z})\bigr]\mathrm{d} z
				\notag\\
				&\quad+\pi\bigl[\delta(B-ist)+\delta(B+ist)\bigr],
				\\[4pt]
				\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{i\psi B}
				\frac{\psi\sinh(st\sqrt{\psi^{2}+1})}
				{\sqrt{\psi^{2}+1}}\mathrm{d}\psi
				&= \frac{st\pi}{2}\int_{0}^{1}
				\frac{I_1(st\sqrt{1-z})}{\sqrt{1-z}}
				\bigl[\delta(B-ist\sqrt{z})
				-\delta(B+ist\sqrt{z})\bigr]\mathrm{d} z
				\notag\\
				&\quad+\pi\bigl[\delta(B-ist)-\delta(B+ist)\bigr],
				\\[4pt]
				\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{i\psi B}
				\frac{\sinh(st\sqrt{\psi^{2}+1})}
				{\sqrt{\psi^{2}+1}}\mathrm{d}\psi
				&= \frac{st\pi}{2}\int_{0}^{1}
				\frac{I_0(st\sqrt{1-z})}{\sqrt{z}}
				\bigl[\delta(B-ist\sqrt{z})
				+\delta(B+ist\sqrt{z})\bigr]\mathrm{d} z.

			\end{align}
			The Bessel summation identities used below are
			\begin{align}
				\sum_{k=\ell}^{\infty}\binom{k}{\ell}\frac{A^{2k}}{(2k)!}
				&= \frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,(A/2)^{\ell+1/2}\,I_{\ell-1/2}(A)}{\ell!},
				\\
				\sum_{k=\ell}^{\infty}\binom{k}{\ell}\frac{(st)^{2k+1}}{(2k+1)!}
				&= \frac{(2st)^{\ell+1/2}\sqrt{\pi}\,I_{\ell+1/2}(st)}
				{2^{2\ell+1}\,\ell!},

			\end{align}
			both following from $I_\nu(A)=\sum_{m=0}^\infty(A/2)^{2m+\nu}/
			[m!\,\Gamma(m+\nu+1)]$ by reindexing.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.344989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6db74f7b935a655e643c85ff8be10c81c1ac312f17f2496ff993420338dc63","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] Let $x \geq 0$. Assume that, for any $\eta > 0$ and $\pi_0 \in \Sym_n$, there exist $\gamma > 0$, a random subset $\Neg \subseteq \Sym_n$, and a function $F: B(\pi_0, x - \eta) \setminus \Neg \to \mc{P}(\Sym_n)$ verifying the following conditions.

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item if $\pi \in \Dom F$, $|F(\pi)| \geq e^{\gamma n \log n}$;
        \item if $\pi \in \Dom F$ and $\pi' \in F(\pi)$, $\Proba(\pi'| G, H, (p_e)) \geq e^{-o_{\Proba}(n \log n)} \Proba(\pi| G, H, (p_e))$;
        \item if $\pi' \in \Sym_n$, $|F^{-1}(\pi')| \leq e^{o_\Proba(n \log n)}$ (uniformly over $\pi'$);
        \item $\Proba(\pi^* \in \Neg) = o(1)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, if $\hat{\pi}$ is an estimator of $\pi^*$, there exist at least $x(n - o_\Proba(n))$ vertices $v \in [n]$ such that $\hat{\pi}(v) \neq \pi^*(v)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.680336+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6e3a7d6acaac9df849bad858ec359b6dd0756484c29a8d51c2457dee7f14e9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l.moments-cumulants of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] For a fixed  collection of integers $k_1,\ldots,k_p$, $p\ge2$,
 assume that  for all $J\subset[p]$,
 \begin{equation}

 \sup_{(x_j)_{j \in J} \in\mathbb{R}^{d|J|}} 
 m^{(k_j:j \in J) }( x_j : j \in J)<\infty.
 \end{equation}

 The following statements are equivalent:
 \begin{enumerate}[wide,label=(\roman*),  labelindent=0pt]
\item There exist fast decreasing functions $\tilde\phi=\tilde\phi_{k_1,\ldots,k_p}$
and constants
	$\tilde C=C(k_1,\ldots,k_p)$, such that
  for any subset  of integers  $J\subset [p]$, $|J|\ge2$, a nonempty, proper subset
  $I\subsetneq J$, and all configurations $\{x_j:  j \in J \}, x_j \in\mathbb{R}^d$, of distinct points
	we have

\begin{equation}
	\Bigl|m^{(k_j : j\in J)}(x_j : j\in J)-
	m^{(k_j: j\in I)}(x_j:j\in I)\, m^{(k_j: j\in J\setminus I)}(x_j:j\in
	 J\setminus I)\Bigr|\le
	\tilde C\tilde\phi(s)\,,
	\end{equation}

	where  $s:=d\bigl(\{x_j:j\in I\}, \{x_j:j\in  J\setminus I\}\bigr)$.
      \item  	 There exist fast decreasing functions $\tilde\phi_\top=\tilde\phi_\top^{k_1,\ldots,k_p}$ and constants
	$\tilde C^\top=\tilde C^\top(k_1,\ldots,k_p)$, 
        such that for any subset  of integers $ J\subset [p]$, $|J|\ge2$ and all configurations  $x_j\in\mathbb{R}^d$, $j\in J$ of distinct points
	we have

	\begin{equation}
	|m_{\top}^{(k_j:j\in J)}(x_j : j\in J)|\le
	\tilde
	C^\top\tilde\phi_\top\bigl(\mathrm{diam}(x_j : j\in J)\bigr)
	\,,
	\end{equation}

where $\mathrm{diam}(x_j : j\in J):=\max_{i,j\in J}(|x_i-x_j|)$.
 \end{enumerate}
 Moreover, the bound~(equation e.Usell-clustering-bound of the paper)  for $J=[p]$ implies
 	\begin{equation}
\sup_{x_1\in \mathbb{R}^d}\int_{
(\mathbb{R}^d)^{(p-1)}}|m_\top^{\bk k1p}(\bk x1p)|\,\mathrm{d} x_2\cdots \mathrm{d} x_p< \infty.
	\end{equation}

Further, $\tilde\phi_\top$ and $\tilde C^\top$ in (ii) depend only on $\tilde \phi$, $\tilde C$, the dimension $d$ and the bound in (equation e.moments-uniformly-bounded of the paper) and similarly the $\tilde\phi$ and $\tilde C$ in (i) depend only on $\tilde \phi_\top$, $\tilde C^\top$ and the bound in (equation e.moments-uniformly-bounded of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.279095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc717c26529da11c9b91c5dade6a8d64deadca72b7366ad89b69431fbacb9211","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pro: Lp norm of q_infty of arXiv:2603.14163, '\LARGE \bf Tail Bounds for Queues with Abandonment: Constant, Moderate, Large Deviations, '] \textnormal{($L^p$ norm of queue length)} For SSQ, under assumption (ass:heavy_overload of the paper), the $L^p$ norm of the centered queue length is bounded in the below interval
    \begin{align}
        \| \hat{q} \|_{L^p} &\leq \min\{(C_{1,\lambda,\mu}\sqrt{\frac{1}{\gamma}} \sqrt{p} + C_{2,\lambda,\mu} p),  (C_{3,\lambda,\mu} \frac{p}{\log(1+\gamma p/\lambda)})\} \\
        \| \hat{q} \|_{L^p}&\geq C_{4,\lambda,\mu} \exp\left( -C_{5,\lambda,\mu} \frac{1}{ \gamma p} \right) \frac{p}{\log(1+\gamma p/\lambda)} 
    \end{align}
    All the above constants are only dependent on $\lambda, \mu, C$ and $\alpha$. Their closed forms are given in (equation eq: constants for L-p norm of the paper) (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.035351+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc72dae5b29423d183514d787c353083e67c4e3b050b5dda0434f74bf3e90631","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition dhe of arXiv:2609.03926, 'Overcolored Partition $k$-tuples Restricted by Parity of the Parts'] {wom}}}] 
	A modular form $f(z)\in M_k(\Gamma_0(N), \chi)$ is called a Hecke eigenform if for every $m\ge 2$ there is a complex number $\lambda(m)$ for which
	\begin{align}
		f(z)\mid T_m=\lambda(m)f(z).
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:01.871561+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc74cf995b562a3cc931d5b1451b9d61aa6e36a113271a4c866464961a90f643","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop.finite of arXiv:2512.19897, 'On the convoy of the ASEP speed process'] Let $\pi_k$ be the initial distribution of $Q_1$ in the case of (equation initial of the paper) after taking the limit $\lambda \uparrow x, \mu \downarrow x$. We have
    \begin{equation}
        \pi_k = \frac{\left(q;q\right)_{\infty}}{\left(q;q\right)_{k}} q^k.
    \end{equation}
    In addition, $Q_1$ has finite mean for any fixed $q \in [0,1)$ 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}\left[Q_1\right] < \infty.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.481007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc76c06648661f8b7a16973c4d4e002b25adcf85d5d047c4990368e33cc821d9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:PCM of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] \medskip \noindent  A random vector $X=(X_{1},\ldots ,X_{d})$
is \textit{paired countermonotone}, or PCM, with respect to the pairing (\ref
{2050}), if:

\begin{enumerate}
\item for each $r=1,\ldots ,m$, the pair $(X_{2r-1},X_{2r})$ is
countermonotone in the bivariate sense;

\item the blocks 
\begin{equation*}
(X_{1},X_{2}),\ (X_{3},X_{4}),\ldots ,(X_{2m-1},X_{2m})
\end{equation*}
are independent.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.848213+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc782527bea603b69b5a21f91f601d126f2952648ca2d5eb430621d0877aa9eb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: threshold for null initial condition of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] Recall from (equation eq: equilibrium threshold of the paper) that
	\begin{equation*}
		y_* \vcentcolon= \inf\asetc{y \geq 0}{\lambda_0\int_0^y S_g(u, u)du \geq 1},
	\end{equation*}
	where $y_* \vcentcolon= \infty$ if the integral is strictly less than one for all $y \geq 0$. Suppose that $\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathcal{S}^+$ has initial condition the null measure. Then
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar y_*(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t) = \begin{cases}
			\infty 	& \text{if} \quad 0 \leq t < y_*, \\
			y_* 	& \text{if} \quad y_* < \infty \quad \text{and} \quad t \geq y_*.	
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.770568+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc79ac59a4ca35da8a71fd7e32d80392ee9bbcf587a75cea0f12d04b70a0f06d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Satz thm:sufficiency-log of arXiv:2411.18155, 'Besov regularity of random wavelet series'] Let $d,k\in\mathds{N}$, $s,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\theta\in\mathds{R}$, and $p,q\in (0,\infty]$.
