{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.307308+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dce4ae4b747e896d372e870a6ae8f66d68f7a927f82e31f7e1c2500f696703d7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:rep_W of arXiv:2411.14818, 'Scaling limits of solitons in the Box-Ball system'] For any $\mathbf{q} \in \mathcal{Q}$, $k \in {\mathbb{N}}$, $0 \le \ell \le k - 1$, $i \in {\mathbb{Z}}$ and $n \in {\mathbb{Z}}_{\ge 0}$, we have
                \begin{align}
                    &Y^{{i}}_{k-\ell}\left(\Psi_{\ell}\left(\eta\right),n\right) \\
                    &= \frac{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{k-\ell}\left(\theta^{\ell}\mathbf{q}\right)}{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{1}\left(\theta^{k-1}\mathbf{q}\right)} \left(n -  M^{{i}}_{k}\left(\eta,n\right) \right) \\
                    & \ + 2\sum_{h = \ell + 1}^{k - 1} \frac{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{h-\ell}\left(\theta^{\ell}\mathbf{q}\right)}{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{1}\left(\theta^{h-1}\mathbf{q}\right)} \sum_{j = X^{{i}}_{k - h}\left(\Psi_{h}\left(\tilde{\eta}\right), 0 \right)  + 1}^{X^{{i}}_{k - h}\left(\Psi_{h}\left(\tilde{\eta}\right), n \right) } \left( \zeta_{h}\left({\eta,}j\right) - \alpha_{h}\left(\mathbf{q}\right) \right) ,
                \end{align}
            with convention $\sum_{\ell = 1}^{0} = 0$. In particular, we have 
                \begin{align}
                    Y^{{i}}_{k}\left(\eta,n\right) 
                    &= \frac{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{k}\left(\mathbf{q}\right)}{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{1}\left(\theta^{k-1}\mathbf{q}\right)}\left(n -  M^{{i}}_{k}\left(\eta,n\right) \right) \\
                    & \ + 2\sum_{h = 1}^{k - 1} \frac{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{h}\left(\mathbf{q}\right)}{v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{1}\left(\theta^{h-1}\mathbf{q}\right)} \sum_{j = X^{{i}}_{k - h}\left(\Psi_{h}\left(\tilde{\eta}\right), 0 \right)  + 1}^{X^{{i}}_{k - h}\left(\Psi_{h}\left(\tilde{\eta}\right), n \right) } \left( \zeta_{h}\left({\eta,}j\right) - \alpha_{h}\left(\mathbf{q}\right) \right) .
                \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:18:54.264720+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dce6894bcc2590da9b963bd0430def4fd2d713a76a52687282e6fa84c4782e01","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] We say that a variable $f:X\to\mathbb R$ is uniform if its law has a density, taking only 2 values. That is, the law must be of the following type,
$$\mu=\frac{1}{\lambda(E)}\,\chi_E(x)dx$$
for a certain subset $E\subset\mathbb R$, with $\lambda$ being the Lebesgue measure. In the case of an interval $E=[a,b]$, the corresponding law must be given by the following formula,
$$\mu=\frac{1}{b-a}\,\chi_{[a,b]}(x)dx$$
with the $\chi$ symbols standing in all this, as usual, for characteristic functions.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.771434+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dce97adc74ffcff576b6818535de3e10bcbb3cab21d0d30a92853641150d2567","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor: boolean_vs_grg of arXiv:2411.02917, '\hspace*{2mm}\\[-16mm] Stein's Method for Spatial Random Graphs'] Consider a determinantal point process on $\R^d$ with kernel $K$ fulfilling the above assumptions and intensity $\mu = K(x,x)$. Let $\Xi$ be the restriction of the point process to $\mathcal{X}_{1/q}$. For a strictly increasing function $\psi$ with $\psi(0)=0$, consider the edge process where $r_*=\hat{r}_*$ satisfies $\Prob(R \geq r_{*}) > 0$ and $\hat{R}_i = r_{*} + \psi(R_i - r_*)$. Denote by $P_{r_*,q} \otimes Q_{r_*,q}$ the distribution of the percolation graph $(\Xi_{r_*,q}, \Sigma_{r_*,q})$ of the Boolean model based on $\hat{R}_i$ after thinning and rescaling. \\
Let furthermore $\lambda = \lambda_{r_*,q} = \mu \tfrac{p(r_*)}{q}$, $t = q^{1/d}r_*$. Denote by $P^{\lambda}$ the homogeneous Poisson process distribution with intensity $\lambda$ and set $\kappa_t(x,y) = \1_{\{\norm{x-y} \leq 2t\}}$.\\
Then
\begin{align*}
W_{\mathbb{G},i} \bigl(&P_{r_*,q} \otimes Q_{r_*,q} \hspace*{1.5pt} , \, P^{\lambda} \otimes Q^{\kappa_{t}}\bigr) \\[1mm]
&\leq 2 c_V(\lambda_{r_*,q}) \,\lambda_{r_*,q} \abs{\mathcal{X}} \frac{q}{1-q} \\[-0.5mm]
&\hspace*{6mm} {} + c_E(\lambda_{r_*,q}) \, \lambda_{r_*,q}\, c_d\, \mu \abs{\mathcal{X}}
    d \, 2^{d} \E \bigl[ \psi(R-r_*) \bigl(r_* + \psi(R-r_*) \bigr)^{d-1} \, \1\{R \geq r_*\} \bigr] \\[1mm]
&\leq \mathrm{const} \Bigl(\min \bigl\{ \lambda_{r_*,q} \abs{\mathcal{X}}, 1 + \log^{+}(\lambda_{r_*,q} \abs{\mathcal{X}}) \bigr\} \frac{q}{1-q}  \\[-0.5mm]
&\hspace*{6mm} {} + \min \bigl\{ \lambda_{r_*,q} \abs{\mathcal{X}}, 1 \bigr\} \mu \E \bigl[ \psi(R-r_*) \bigl(r_* + \psi(R-r_*) \bigr)^{d-1} \, \1\{R \geq r_*\} \bigr] \Bigr),
\end{align*}
where $c_d = \frac{\pi^{d/2}}{\Gamma(d/2 +1)}$ is the volume of the unit ball in $\R^d$ and $c_V(\lambda)$, $c_E(\lambda)$ are defined in Equations~(equation eq: SteinPoissonV of the paper) and~(equation eq: SteinPoissonE of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.067020+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dceb4d627ca0a525be1543d16ddf48bb4cfbca026fc1ff2426afab4a5ba8559a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary semigroup of arXiv:2606.28575, 'Global smooth solutions by high mode Lie-Transport noise for Logarithmically Hyperdissipat'] $A:X_1\subseteq X_0\rightarrow X_0$ generates an analytic semigroup of negative type on $X_0$ which we denote by $e^{At}$. Moreover, $-A$ admits a bounded $H^{\infty}$ calculus with angle zero. Finally, for $\theta\in (0,1),$ and  $p\in [1,+\infty]$, setting 
\begin{align*}
    D_A(\theta,p)=\Big\{x\in X_0:t\rightarrow v_t=\norm{t^{1-\theta-\frac{1}{p}}A e^{At}x}_{X_0}\in L^p(0,1)\Big\}
\end{align*}
endowed with the norm $\left\|x\right\|_{D_A(\alpha,p)}=\left\|x\right\|_{X_0}+\left\|v\right\|_{L^p(0,1)}$,
it holds
\begin{align}
    [X_0,X_1]_{\theta}&=D((-A)^{\theta})= H^{2(\theta-1)}_g,\\

    \left(X_0,X_1\right)_{\theta,p}&=D_A(\theta,p)=B^{2(\theta-1)}_{g,2,p},\\

    D_A(\theta,1)&\hookrightarrow D((-A)^{\theta})\hookrightarrow D_A(\theta,\infty).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["S is a separable metric space","\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu weakly"],"created_at":"2026-10-07T19:28:52+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcee3d2ef103c9bd3264b089aa143def","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let S be a separable metric space, and let \mu_n, \mu be Borel probability measures on S such that \mu_n \Rightarrow \mu weakly. Then there exists a single probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) and S-valued random variables X_n, X defined on \Omega such that the law of X_n is \mu_n for all n, the law of X is \mu, and X_n(\omega) \to X(\omega) as n \to \infty for \mathbb{P}-almost every \omega \in \Omega.

Preserved duplicate statement and commentary:
Let (mu_n) be a sequence of probability measures on a separable metric space (S, d) converging weakly to a probability measure mu. Then there exist random variables X_n and X, all defined on a common probability space, with X_n distributed as mu_n and X distributed as mu, such that X_n converges to X almost surely. [Billingsley, Convergence of Probability Measures]","status":"unverified"}

