F_N^*$$
where $F_N=(w^{ij})_{ij}$ with $w=e^{2\pi i/N}$ is the Fourier matrix, and $F_N^*=(w^{-ij})_{ij}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.810916+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd82b4205db6bc36eaa1d5db5d6c927bdd8a4b66213fcce312a100b8e9dc8413","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:intro-SHE of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] By [AJRS22], there exists a measurable map 
    \begin{align*}
        \begin{array}{ccc}
            \mathcal{Z} :(\Xi,\mathcal{F}^{\mathrm{prod}},\mathbb{P}) &\longrightarrow &(C(\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}\times \mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0}),\; \mathcal{B}(C(\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}\times \mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0})))\\
            \;\;\xi &\longmapsto & \mathcal{Z}^{\xi}_{{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}}}({\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}},{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}};{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}},{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}})
        \end{array}
    \end{align*}
    satisfying the following properties.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textup{(Coupling of SHEs).} 
        For any fixed $(s,x)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\beta\geqslant 0$, the process $(t,y)\mapsto \mathcal{Z}_{\beta}^{\xi}(s,x;t,y)$ coincides a.s. with the unique continuous adapted mild solution of (equation e:she-intro of the paper).
        \item\textup{(Strict positivity).} Almost surely, $\mathcal{Z}^\xi_{\beta}(s,x;t,y)>0$ for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$ and $\beta\geqslant 0$.
        \item\textup{(Chapman--Kolmogorov, [AJRS22]).} 
        Almost surely, for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, all $r\in (s,t)$, and all $\beta\geqslant 0$, we have
        \begin{align}
            \mathcal{Z}^{\xi}_{\beta}(s,x;t,y) & = \int_{\mathbb{R}}
            \mathcal{Z}^{\xi}_{\beta}(s,x;r,z)\mathcal{Z}^{\xi}_{\beta}(r,z;t,y)dz.
        \end{align}
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.358160+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd895daf6ea5063d9afba86fe0d8ae4d5c9ed1b07c2ba530cf5cc3183192e688","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:severity-bridges of arXiv:2608.23860, 'Revelation Control'] The positive-harm component left uncontrolled by stop--flip risk is summarized by the integrated stopped-harm tail $H_+$ in Equation~(equation eq:stopped-harm-mass of the paper). The following declared conditions are sufficient ways to control it for a lower utility certificate.

If $Y_+\le B_+$ almost surely, then
\begin{equation}
\Delta V(S)\ge c\rho-B_+r.

\end{equation}
If instead $q>1$ and $\mathbb E[|D|^q]\le M_q$, then H\"older's inequality gives
\begin{equation}
\Delta V(S)\ge c\rho-M_q^{1/q}r^{1-1/q}.

\end{equation}
More generally, if an integrable envelope $u:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\Pr(E,\,Y_+>t)\le u(t)
\qquad\text{for all }t\ge0,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
\Delta V(S)
\ge
c\rho-\int_0^\infty u(t)\,dt.

\end{equation}
Thus a finite-sample event $\rho\ge L_\rho$, $r\le U_r$ yields certified lower bounds $cL_\rho-B_+U_r$ or $cL_\rho-M_q^{1/q}U_r^{1-1/q}$ under the corresponding declared severity class; an independently valid upper bound $U_H$ on $H_+$ yields the assumption-matched bound $cL_\rho-U_H$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.020555+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd8a55cb6a4e1e87fcd36f2f3031c1bb52a44d7c7a9c82887eac428d13ad4b75","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:C_bdds of arXiv:2605.15545, 'Crossover from subcritical to critical decay: \\ random walk, self-avoiding walk, percolat'] Let $d \ge 1$,
$x,\eta\in \mathbb{R}^d$, and let $\Lambda \in \mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$ be symmetric and positive-definite.
Suppose $\hat{b} = \sqrt{ \hat x \cdot \Lambda^{-1} \hat x} \asymp 1$ for all $\hat x$.
Then, for $d > 2$ we have
\begin{equation} 
\mathbb{C}(x;\abs \eta \hat x,\Lambda)    \asymp	B(x;\abs \eta \hat x)
\quad \text{with} \quad
B(x;\eta)=
\frac{ ( \abs \eta  \vee \abs x^{-1} )^{(d-3)/2} }{ \abs x^{(d-1)/2} }
\end{equation}
uniformly in $\eta$ and in $x\ne 0$.
If $d \le 2$ and $s_0 > 0$ is a given constant,
(equation eq:C-combo of the paper) still holds subject to the condition that $\abs \eta \abs x \ge s_0$, with constants that depends on $s_0$.","status":"unverified"}

