{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.658119+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddb570aa8e23b5ed826e783223ce4fd23da706fd0cb5c5c3b694d1ae5683b4d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 (Scaling limit of deterministic Schr\"{o}dinger bridges) of arXiv:2604.23461, 'Scaling limit of Sinkhorn-rescaled Random Matrices \\ via Stability of Static Schrödinger '] Let Assumption \ref{assumption: limit_theory} hold. For each $k$, let
		\begin{align}
			\pi_k \in \argmin_{\pi \in \Pi(\mu_{0}^k, \mu_1^{k})} D_{\textup{KL}}(\pi \Vert \mathcal{R}_k)
		\end{align}
		be the static Schr\"{o}dinger bridge with margins $\mu_0^k, \mu_1^k$ and reference measure $\mathcal{R}_k$, and let $\pi^{\r,\c;\bLambda}$ denote the SSB with the limiting margins $\mu_0, \mu_1$ and reference measure $\mathcal{R}$. Then
		\begin{align}
			d_{H}(\pi_k, \pi^{\r, \c; \bLambda})^2 \leq 8(K + 4\log\delta^{-1}) \left( \|\rho_{\r_k} - \rho_{\r}\|_{L^1} + \|\rho_{\c_k} - \rho_{\c}\|_{L^1} \right) + 4\, e^{3K}\,\delta^{-12}\, d_{H}(\mathcal{R}_k, \mathcal{R})^2.
		\end{align}
		In particular, $d_{H}(\pi_k, \pi^{\r, \c; \bLambda}) \to 0$ as $k \to \infty$, so the sequence of discrete bridges converges to the continuous SSB in the Hellinger metric. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:20:30.555821+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddb7263d61f0c21cff772234b781f424fbf14856fe29f55a2c1b58e209baa67a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2609.01826, 'An extension theory for fully fractional Schr\"odinger equations with memory'] Let $\Phi, v_0\in \mathscr S(\RNu)$. Then the solution of the equation \eqref{Lsu=F}, with $F$ given by \eqref{F}, is represented by the oscillatory integral
\begin{align}
u(x,t) & = \frac{1}{\G(s)} \int_0^t \frac{1}{\sigma^{1-s}} S(\sigma)(\Phi(\cdot,t-\sigma))(x) d\sigma
+ \frac{1}{\G(s)} \int_{t}^\infty \frac{1}{\sigma^{1-s}} S(\sigma)(\Le^s v_0(\cdot,t-\sigma))(x)  d\sigma.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:34:45.687491+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddb734d93d3f30191f701999b6faf8e258d69e2ab244138d7cecb83ad39952cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Classification of the $2n$-sum range of arXiv:2609.01926, 'On Sum Ranges for $3n$-convergence'] Let $\sum A = \lim_{k \to \infty} \sum_{n=1}^{2k} a_n$ be a series of real numbers that $2n$-converges to $x$. Then $\textnormal{SR}_2(A)$ is one of the following:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(1)] The whole of $\mathbb{R}$;
        \item[(2)] The singleton $\{x\}$;
        \item[(3)] A shifted additive subgroup of the form $r + \Gamma$, where $r \in \mathbb{R}$ and $\Gamma \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is any countable additive subgroup. Note that case (2) is a special case where $r = x$ and $\Gamma = \{0\}$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.317335+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddb834ed4c58ca3ad67154a2404e77f3226007d877d5a36380743c87e126324e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2506.20470, 'Pivot probabilities and norm effects in Gaussian elimination for $\beta$-ensembles'] Let $A$ be a $2 \times 2$ GUE matrix and GEPP pivot comparisons use the $L^1$ norm (as in LAPACK). Then the pivot probability is
    \begin{equation}
    \P(\|A_{21}\|_1 > |A_{11}|) = \P(|Z_1| + |Z_2| > \sqrt 2 |Z_3|) = \frac23
\end{equation}
for $Z_i \sim N(0,1)$ iid. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.550471+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddba2adb5bb8fa063ea24c51576fb7e4b87bd35913299e5a170d4d7378912f71","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2604.01978, 'Homogenized Transformers'] Fix $d, n\ge 2$ and $\upbeta\in[0,1]$, and assume that $\langle x_i(0),x_j(0)\rangle\ge \gamma$ for $i\neq j$ and $\gamma\in(0, 1)$. Define the empirical mean
\begin{equation*} 
m(t)\coloneqq\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n \langle x_i(t),x_j(t)\rangle.
\end{equation*}
Let $u$ be the unique solution to the  Cauchy problem for the logistic equation
\begin{equation} 
\begin{cases}
\dot{u}(t)=u(t)(1-u(t)) \\
u(0)=m(0).
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Then there exists a universal constant $C>0$ such that for every every $T>0$ and every $\delta\in(0,1)$, with probability at least $1-\delta$, the solution to \eqref{eq:Diffusive_gaussian_case} satisfies
\begin{equation}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\left|m(t)-u(t)\right|
\le Ce^{CT}\max\left(\frac{1}{d},\,d^{2}\sigma_{\bA}^{4}\upbeta^{2}\right)
+ C\sqrt{\frac{T}{d}\log\left(\frac{4}{\delta}\right)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.238713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddbc0389bc38ca1750b78b987bfcdd08e60f2a72653fcc7ff074f7a07acb8c35","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_translate of arXiv:2507.06522, 'Balanced multi-species spin glasses'] Given $p,d\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant1}$, let $N = p d$ and consider the multi-species model (equation MSKHamiltonian of the paper) with $\mathscr{S} = \{1,\dots,p\}$, $\# \mathcal{I}_{s,N} = d$ for each $s\in\mathscr{S}$, and 
\eeq{ 
\Delta^2_{s_1,\dots,s_q} = 0 \quad \text{whenever $q\ne p$}, \qquad
\Delta^2_{s_1,\dots,s_p} = \begin{cases}
\frac{p^{p+1}}{p!} &\text{if $s_1,\dots,s_p$ are distinct} \\
0 &\text{else.}
\end{cases}
}
Then the covariance function is
\eeq{ 
\xi_N(x_1,\dots,x_p) = px_1\cdots x_p, 
}
and we have the following distributional equality:
\eeq{ 
\frac{\|T\|_\mathrm{inj}}{\sqrt{d}} \stackrel{\mathrm{law}}{=} \max_{\sigma\in\Sigma_{N,\mathsf{spherical}}}\frac{H_{N}(\sigma)}{N}.
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.848368+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddbc2db1b1fb8bfdb8c4793b2be69de65425ef25a13fbca97cb1d5c378d64757","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: threshold for general initial condition of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] If $\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathcal{S}^+$ and $y_*$ is defined as in (equation eq: equilibrium threshold of the paper), then
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar y_*\left(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t\right) \geq t \quad \text{for all} \quad t \in [0, y_*) \quad \text{and} \quad \bar y_*\left(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t\right) = y_* \quad \text{for all} \quad t \geq y_*.
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.899067+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddbd6636c1b5f1fb28c43482e2e707b480ebb5e5213b563f4bff323d3d2e818f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:limitingF_SSK of arXiv:2510.26960, 'Free energy fluctuations in SK and related spin glass models: \\ A literature survey'] When no external field is present ($h=0$), the free energy of SSK exhibits the following convergence in distribution as $N\to\infty$.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item In the high temperature regime, $\beta<1$,
 \begin{equation} 
N(F_N(\beta)-F(\beta))\to\mathcal{N}(-\frac12 \sigma^2,\sigma^2)
 \end{equation} 
where
$\sigma^2=-\frac12\log(1-\beta^2)$.  

\item In the low temperature regime, $\beta>1$,
 \begin{equation} 
\frac{2N^{2/3}}{\beta-1}(F_N(\beta)-F(\beta))\to TW_1.
 \end{equation} 
\end{enumerate} 
The limiting free energy $F(\beta)$ in this setting is given by the formula
 \begin{equation} 
F(\beta)=\begin{cases}
\frac14\beta^2& \text{for }\beta\leq1,\\
\beta-\frac12\log\beta-\frac34 & \text{for }\beta\geq1.
\end{cases}
 \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.075880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddc1544fa56ef26e22ac20b2f4c6140622d9580569d11520d5469a5890fd0e84","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 7. cor: GW tree satisfies the sub-aging assumption of arXiv:2412.08236, 'Aging and sub-aging for Bouchaud trap models on resistance metric spaces'] Define a probability measure $\tilde{p} = (\tilde{p}_{k})_{k \geq 1}$ on $\mathbb{N}$ 
  by setting $\tilde{p}_{k} \coloneqq p_{k-1}$.
  Then, it holds that 
  \begin{equation}
    (T_{n}^{GW}, n^{-1/2}d_{T_{n}^{GW}}, \rho_{T_{n}^{GW}}, n^{-1} \dot{\mu}^{\#}_{T_{n}^{GW}})
    \xrightarrow{\mathrm{d}}
    (T_{2e}, d_{2e}, \rho_{2e}, \mu_{2e} \otimes \tilde{p})
  \end{equation}
  in the space $\mathfrak{M}_{c}(\markedfinMeasFunct{\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}})$.","status":"unverified"}

